window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t":"Material recipes for molecular renders","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html","x":"Every look here is the same Principled BSDF shader with different settings. Roughness, and how light gets through the surface (Alpha, Transmission, Subsurface), do most of the work. For a texture you can picture but cannot name, see Designing a look from scratch . Materials only look as good as their light; the full course is in Lighting . Every shader node Blender has, and why the recipes start from the Principled BSDF, is in Shaders: the whole menu, from zero . How the maps and models themselves are built, and the tool the sticky-slime section uses, is in Geometry Nodes, from zero ."},{"t":"How to use this guide","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#how-to-use-this-guide","x":"Every section in every part follows one pattern: objectives, the material itself, one or more exercises to do in Blender, and a short self-check with answers. Do the exercises. The numbers in the tables only become intuition once you have moved the sliders and watched the render change. Set up a lab bench once and reuse it for everything here: a new file containing a UV Sphere (Shift + A › Mesh › UV Sphere, right-click › Shade Smooth), a Cube, a Monkey (Shift + A › Mesh › Monkey, with a Subdivision Surface modifier at level 2 and Shade Smooth), and a copy of your map, all on a large plane, lit with the default rig from the Lighting part , viewport in Rendered mode with Cycles. Save it as material_lab.blend . The sphere shows highlights and the shadow edge, the cube shows flat faces and sharp edges, the monkey shows cavities and thin parts, and the map shows real geometry."},{"t":"Why your render looks slimy","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#why-your-render-looks-slimy","x":"Name the four settings that together produce a wet, gelatinous look. Predict what removing each one does before you try it. Recognize the same four ingredients in other people's renders."},{"t":"The four ingredients","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#the-four-ingredients","x":"Four things combine in that render: a mirror-smooth surface, a see-through surface, color that deepens with thickness, and a bumpy shape. None of them alone reads as slime. Together they are unmistakable. Roughness near 0. The two area lights reflect in the surface as sharp white streaks, one per ridge that happens to face a light at the mirror angle. Sharp, bright, white highlights are the single strongest cue for wetness, because dry surfaces almost never have them; a film of water is what gives a surface a polished skin. Alpha below 1. At 0.177 you see about 80% of what is behind each surface. On a folded map, what is behind is more map, then the tubes of the model, then more map again. The eye reads stacked translucent layers as gel. Layered tint. Every see-through layer multiplies its color into what is behind it and loses some brightness. Three layers of pale pink over a green ribbon add up to a dim olive. Thin edges have only one layer and stay pale, thick regions have several and go dark. That gradient from pale edge to dark body is exactly how jelly and thick syrup look. The map's own bumps. A density surface is a field of small lobes. Each lobe catches the lights from its own angle, so instead of one broad highlight you get hundreds of small ones. Smooth the geometry, or raise roughness, and the glitter becomes a single sheen. To get slime on purpose, keep all four and add a thickness tint from a Volume Absorption node (the slime recipe below does this). To lose it, raise Roughness to 0.4 or higher first; that alone removes most of the wet look, and Alpha 1 removes the rest."},{"t":"Exercise 1.1: take the slime apart","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-1-1","x":"In the Material tab, click the copy icon next to the material's name so you keep the original, and rename the copy slime_ref . Raise Roughness from 0.05 to 0.4. Watch the viewport for a few seconds while it cleans up. Put Roughness back and set Alpha to 1. Put Alpha back, select the sphere from the lab bench, give it the same material, and compare it with the map. What you should see In step 2 the streaks spread into a soft sheen and the surface stops looking wet, even though it is still see-through. In step 3 the olive murk disappears and the pink returns, and the surface reads as solid pink plastic. In step 4 the sphere shows one or two clean highlights where the map showed hundreds, which tells you the glitter comes from the geometry rather than the material."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#why-your-render-looks-slimy-check","x":"Which single setting removes most of the wet look, and why does it work? Answer Roughness. Wetness is read from sharp specular highlights, and roughness spreads them until they stop reading as reflections. The material's Base Color is pink. Why does the render show olive? Answer Each translucent layer tints and darkens what is behind it; several pink layers over the green ribbon multiply to a dim olive. Two renders use the identical material. One glitters, one shows a single highlight. What differs? Answer The geometry. Many small bumps give many small highlights; a smooth surface gives one."},{"t":"The controls that set a look","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#the-controls-that-set-a-look","x":"Locate each of the eleven controls that matter on the Principled BSDF node and in its panels. Describe what the eye sees as each control moves from low to high. Know which three controls to touch first and which to leave alone."},{"t":"Where they are","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#where-they-are","x":"In the Material tab, the Principled BSDF shows its main inputs at the top, Base Color through Alpha, and then a row of collapsible panels: Diffuse, Subsurface, Specular, Transmission, Coat, Sheen, Emission, Thin Film. In the Shader Editor the same panels fold open on the node itself. The table names the panel for each control so you can find it either way ( Principled BSDF manual ). Roughness, plus how light passes through the surface, sets most of the look; the other controls are finishing touches. Control Panel Low → high Use it for Roughness Main 0 mirror-sharp highlights → 1 no visible highlight The biggest lever. 0.3 to 0.6 suits most figures. IOR Main 1.0 no reflection → higher, more reflective Keep 1.4 to 1.5. Most real materials sit between 1.0 and 4.0. Alpha Main 0 invisible → 1 opaque Ghosted overlays. Nothing bends, so the model inside stays undistorted. Transmission Weight Transmission 0 opaque → 1 glass-like Gel, glass, liquid. Light passes through and bends what is behind. Subsurface Weight and Scale Subsurface 0 plain surface → 1 light glows inside Wax, milk, soft tissue. Scale is how far light travels, in scene units. Coat Weight and Roughness Coat 0 none → 1 full clear layer Lacquer, candy, or a wet sheen on top of any other look. Sheen Weight Sheen 0 none → 1 strong A soft, velvety glow at the edges. IOR Level Specular 0 no highlights → 0.5 default Dims highlights without changing Roughness. Emission Color and Strength Emission Strength 1 shows the exact color, unshaded Flat, illustration-style color. Thin Film Thickness Thin Film 0 none → a few hundred nm Soap-bubble iridescence (Cycles). Metallic Main 0 → 1 Real metals only. Keep 0 for biology."},{"t":"What each control looks like as it moves","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#what-each-control-looks-like-as-it-moves","x":"Roughness spreads the highlight. At 0 the lights are small, sharp mirror images; at 0.3 they are soft blobs; at 0.6 they are a faint wide sheen; at 1 the surface shows no highlight, only a lighter and a darker side. Between 0.3 and 0.6 a material reads as plastic, paint or skin, which is why that range suits most figures. IOR sets how strongly the surface reflects when seen head-on, through the Fresnel effect. Keep it near 1.5 for anything non-metal: 1.33 is water, 1.45 glass, 1.5 plastic. Values above 2 reflect so much that the color underneath disappears into mirrored surroundings, which is what happened at 71.4. Alpha fades the whole surface toward invisible without bending light. At 0.5 the object is half-transparent and anything behind it stays sharp. It is cheap, works in both engines, and is the right tool for ghosted overlays. Transmission Weight turns the surface into glass: light enters and refracts by the IOR. At 1 the Base Color becomes the tint of the glass and the object's diffuse color is gone. Roughness now controls frosting as well as highlights. Subsurface Weight and Scale let light wander below the surface before leaving. Thin parts glow when lit from behind, and the shadow edge softens. Scale is a distance in scene units; 1 to 2% of the object's width is a usual start, and the Radius sets how far each color channel travels, which is why skin defaults to a reddish radius. Coat Weight and Roughness add a clear layer with its own gloss. The body keeps its roughness; the coat adds a second, sharper highlight on top. It is how you make a matte body look wet or varnished without making the body itself glossy. Sheen Weight adds a pale glow at grazing angles, as fine fibers do. It brightens the silhouette and leaves the center alone. IOR Level scales the strength of the specular highlight without changing its size. 0.5 is physically correct for the given IOR; 0.2 mutes highlights for dusty looks; 0 removes them. Emission Color and Strength make the surface give off light. Strength 1 shows the color unshaded; 5 and above makes a visible lamp in Cycles. Thin Film Thickness adds the rainbow interference of a soap film at a few hundred nanometers, Cycles only. Metallic switches the surface to a metal model: the reflection takes on the Base Color and the diffuse color disappears. Biological surfaces stay at 0."},{"t":"Exercise 2.1: the roughness ladder","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-2-1","x":"In material_lab.blend , duplicate the sphere four times (Shift + D, then X and a distance) so five spheres sit in a row. Give each a separate material (click the number next to the material name to make it single-user), all with Base Color mid-blue and IOR 1.5. Set Roughness to 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 and 1 from left to right. Render with F12 and save the image as roughness_ladder.png . What you should see The first sphere mirrors the lights and the room, the second shows soft bright blobs, the middle one a gentle sheen, and the last two differ only slightly from each other. Keep the image; it is your reference for reading roughness in other renders."},{"t":"Exercise 2.2: Alpha against Transmission","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-2-2","x":"Two spheres, same blue material. Put a thin vertical cylinder behind them. On the first set Alpha 0.3. On the second set Transmission Weight 1 and IOR 1.45. Compare the cylinder seen through each. What you should see Through the Alpha sphere the cylinder is faint but straight. Through the Transmission sphere it is bent, flipped and magnified, and the sphere's edges show a bright rim of reflected light. That difference is the whole choice between the two controls."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#the-controls-that-set-a-look-check","x":"A render looks like a plastic toy. Which control is most likely at its default, and what range would you try? Answer Roughness, sitting at a middle value with no variation. Try moving it, or adding a coat over a rougher body, or a little noise. You need the model to stay undistorted inside a translucent map. Alpha or Transmission? Answer Alpha. Transmission refracts. What does IOR Level change that Roughness does not? Answer The brightness of the highlight, not its size. Why does a transmissive material need a paler Base Color than you expect? Answer Base Color tints every pass of light through the glass, and light passes through more than once, so a mid color darkens to a deep one."},{"t":"Recipes","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#recipes","x":"Build any of the eight looks from a fresh Principled BSDF in under a minute. Explain why each recipe uses the values it does, so you can bend it instead of copying it. Place a new look on the roughness and see-through map before building it."},{"t":"How to read a recipe","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#how-to-read-a-recipe","x":"Start from a fresh Principled BSDF, change only the settings listed, and leave everything else at its default. Then adjust by eye in Rendered view. The map below places each look by its two most important properties, which is also the quickest way to decide where a look you have in mind belongs. Two settings place most looks; slime is glossy and see-through See-through Opaque Glossy Matte Wet, glassy Frosted, ghosted Polished Soft, chalky Slime Frosted glass Ghost Glossy candy Soft wax Satin plastic Matte clay Recipe looks placed by roughness and see-through Slime and frosted glass both let light through; roughness is what separates them. The flat look is left off the map because it is unshaded, so roughness does not change it. Look Roughness Alpha Transmission Other changes Good for Matte clay 0.9 1 0 IOR Level 0.2 Clean, illustration-like figures; pairs well with ambient occlusion Satin plastic 0.45 1 0 None The safe default for most figures Glossy candy 0.4 1 0 Coat Weight 1, Coat Roughness 0.03 Eye-catching, cover-style renders Soft wax 0.5 1 0 Subsurface Weight 1, Subsurface Scale about 2% of the map's width Organic surfaces that glow softly at thin edges Ghost 0.5 0.25 0 IOR Level 0.2 Map over model: the model stays sharp and undistorted Frosted glass 0.6 1 1 IOR 1.45 Milky see-through shells Slime 0.05 1 1 IOR 1.35, Coat Weight 0.5, saturated Base Color Wet, gelatinous surfaces (steps below) Flat illustration Any 1 0 Base Color black, IOR Level 0, Emission Color set to your color, Emission Strength 1 Flat, unshaded color, like a 2D schematic To find the map's width for Subsurface Scale, select it and read N › Item › Dimensions. For the flat look, also set Color Management › View Transform to Standard so the color comes out exactly as picked. Molecular Nodes also ships ready-made materials, including Ambient Occlusion, Flat and Squishy ( materials guide ). Pick one in the style node's Material field, then open it in the Shader Editor to see how it is built."},{"t":"Why each recipe works","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#why-each-recipe-works","x":"Matte clay turns the highlight off with Roughness 0.9 and IOR Level 0.2, leaving pure shading. It looks best with ambient occlusion because, without highlights, cavities are the only remaining depth cue. Satin plastic is the Principled default with the roughness tuned. It is safe because a single medium highlight reads correctly on every shape and under every light. Glossy candy keeps a soft body (Roughness 0.4) and adds a mirror-smooth coat. The two highlights, one broad and one sharp, are what candy, ceramic glaze and car paint have in common. Soft wax lets light into the surface. Subsurface Scale at about 2% of the object's width is far enough to soften shading and light thin edges, not so far that the whole object glows. Ghost uses Alpha for see-through without refraction and dims the highlight with IOR Level 0.2 so the ghost does not sparkle over the model. Frosted glass is transmission with high roughness. The frosting is the same roughness that blurs the highlight; it blurs the view through the glass too. Slime is near-zero roughness, transmission with a water-like IOR, and a half-strength coat for a second glint; the section below adds the thickness tint. Flat illustration takes the shading out entirely: a black Base Color reflects nothing, IOR Level 0 removes highlights, and Emission at Strength 1 paints the color on unshaded."},{"t":"Exercise 3.1: three looks side by side","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-3-1","x":"On three spheres from the lab bench, build Matte clay, Satin plastic and Glossy candy, all in the same mid-red. Render once with the full rig, then once with the key light hidden (click its eye icon in the Outliner) and only the world light on. What you should see Under the full rig the three are clearly different. With only world light they look nearly the same, because the differences are all in how they reflect a bright, well-placed light. Materials are only half of a look."},{"t":"Exercise 3.2: ghost against frosted glass on the map","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-3-2","x":"Build Ghost and Frosted glass as two materials, and switch the map's style node between them. Look at the model inside in each. What you should see Through the Ghost the model is faint but exactly in place; through the frosted glass it is a blurred, slightly swimming shape. Ghost is for figures where position matters; frosted glass is for images where mood matters."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#recipes-check","x":"Which two settings differ between Satin plastic and Glossy candy? Answer Coat Weight and Coat Roughness; the body roughness is nearly the same. Why is the flat illustration's Base Color black? Answer So that no diffuse shading is added to the emission; the color comes entirely from Emission. Why does Soft wax specify Subsurface Scale relative to the object's width rather than as a fixed number? Answer Scale is a distance in scene units, so the same number means different things on a 1 m sphere and a 20 m map."},{"t":"Slime on purpose, step by step","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#slime-on-purpose-step-by-step","x":"Build a wet, gelatinous material from scratch and know what each step contributes. Add color that deepens with thickness using a volume shader. Add ripples with a procedural bump without image textures. Turn the same recipe into water, honey or tar by changing three numbers."},{"t":"The build","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#the-build","x":"Slime is a wet, see-through surface whose color deepens where it is thick. Build it in this order in the Shader Editor, checking Rendered view in Cycles after each step, because each step changes what the next one looks like. Surface. A Principled BSDF in the Surface socket, nothing in Volume yet. If the material started on a volume object, remove the Principled Volume first. Wet glints. Roughness 0.05. Coat Weight 0.5, Coat Roughness 0. The body gloss gives the broad wet sheen; the coat adds a second, sharper glint on top, which is what a thin water film over a soft gel looks like. See-through. Transmission Weight 1, IOR 1.35. Water is 1.33 and most gels are a little above it. A lower IOR bends less and gives a thinner, watery look; 1.45 looks like glass. Color. A saturated, mid-dark Base Color. Transmission tints the light passing through, and light passes through twice or more, so the color compounds; a strong color reads as jelly, a pale one as water. Color that deepens with thickness (optional). Set Base Color to near-white so the surface itself stops tinting. Add Shift + A › Shader › Volume Absorption, set its Color to the slime color, and connect its Volume output to the Material Output's Volume input. Density: start at 1 divided by the map's width in meters, then double or halve. Thick lobes go dark and saturated while thin edges stay clear, which is what distinguishes jelly from tinted glass. Ripples (optional). Add Shift + A › Input › Texture Coordinate and Shift + A › Texture › Noise Texture. Connect Object to the Noise Texture's Vector. Noise Scale: about 30 divided by the map's width in meters, so roughly 30 ripples across. Detail 3. Add Shift + A › Vector › Bump: Noise Fac into Height, Strength 0.15. Connect its Normal to both Normal and Coat Normal on the Principled BSDF so the film follows the ripples. Light for glints. One fairly small, strong key light makes sharp streaks; a large one makes broad bands. A light behind the map makes the gel glow through, which is the single most convincing addition. If the model inside looks warped, that is refraction from Transmission. For a figure, use Alpha 0.35 with Transmission 0 instead of step 3. You keep the glints and the see-through look, and the model stays undistorted; that is close to what your accidental render did."},{"t":"Variants","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#variants","x":"Variant Change from the recipe Why Clean water IOR 1.33, Base Color white, no absorption, Coat Weight 0 Water has no body color and no second film Honey IOR 1.48, Absorption Color amber at double density, Roughness 0.02 Thicker, more refractive, strongly colored Tar Absorption near black at high density, Roughness 0.1, Coat Weight 1 Opaque in all but the thinnest edges, with a hard wet shine Wet soap Add Thin Film Thickness 400 nm Rainbow interference on the surface film"},{"t":"Exercise 4.1: build and vary","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-4-1","x":"Build the full recipe on the monkey head from the lab bench, including steps 5 and 6. Render, then change only the three settings in the Honey row and render again. Place a small area light behind the head, pointing at the camera, and render a third time. What you should see The first render is a green or pink jelly with dark ears and pale brows. Honey turns the thin parts amber and the thick parts almost brown. The backlight makes the ears and brow ridge glow, and the whole head suddenly reads as translucent rather than merely shiny."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#slime-on-purpose-step-by-step-check","x":"Why set Base Color to near-white when adding Volume Absorption? Answer So the color comes only from the absorption, which depends on thickness, instead of being applied evenly at the surface as well. What does Coat Normal do that Normal alone does not? Answer It makes the coat's highlight follow the bump; without it the film stays smooth over a rippled body. Which single light change does the most for a translucent material? Answer A light behind it, so that light passes through to the camera."},{"t":"Sticky slime: where the tubes come out","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#sticky-slime-where-the-tubes-come-out","x":"Make slime cling to whatever passes through it: a wet collar, a meniscus, strands and drips. Build each effect as a node chain you can reuse on any object. Keep all four reading as one substance."},{"t":"Why the exits matter","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#why-the-exits-matter","x":"Tubes breaking through a translucent surface are what make it read as a substance rather than a shell. But real gel wets whatever leaves it, and a clean geometric intersection says \"model placed inside map\" instead of \"model emerging from slime.\" Four things fix it, and the first two do most of the work. The node chains live in Geometry Nodes; the concepts behind them are in Geometry Nodes, from zero , Chapter 7 of which walks the same chains with more explanation."},{"t":"1. The wet collar","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#1-the-wet-collar","x":"A tube that just left slime is glossy and darker for a short distance, then dry. Store a wet value on the model that is 1 at the surface and fades to 0 a short way out, then let the material read it. In the model's Geometry Nodes tree, after the style node: Object Info (Object: the map, Relative) › Geometry → Geometry Proximity (Target Element: Faces), Target input. Geometry Proximity › Distance → Map Range (From Min 0, From Max 0.2, To Min 1, To Max 0, Clamp on). From Max is how far the wetness reaches; 0.2 m is about 2 nm at the Blender 5 world scale. Map Range › Result → Store Named Attribute (Domain: Point, Data Type: Float, Name wet ), with the style's geometry through the Geometry socket, then on to Group Output. In the model's material: Attribute (Name wet ) › Fac → Coat Weight. Coat Roughness 0.02. Attribute wet › Fac → Factor of a Mix Color whose A is the chain color (Attribute Color ) and whose B is the same color through Hue/Saturation/Value at Value 0.5; the Mix result → Base Color. Wet surfaces are darker. Attribute wet › Fac → Map Range (0 to 1 into 0.5 to 0.15) → Roughness."},{"t":"2. The meniscus","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#2-the-meniscus","x":"Slime climbs a little way up anything that passes through it. Blender 5's SDF nodes make this a smooth union with a fillet, which is a meniscus in the mathematical sense. In the map's tree, after the Density Style Surface: Density Style Surface › Geometry → Mesh to SDF Grid (Voxel Size 0.02, Band Width 3). Object Info (Object: the model, Relative) › Geometry → Mesh to SDF Grid with the same Voxel Size. The model must be outputting a mesh style (cartoon, ribbon or sticks), not points. Both grids → SDF Grid Boolean (Union). SDF Grid Boolean → SDF Grid Fillet (Iterations 3). This rounds the concave crease where tube meets surface into a curve that rises up the tube; each iteration rounds it a little further, and a coarser voxel size makes each step larger. SDF Grid Fillet → Grid to Mesh (Threshold 0) → Set Material (the slime material) → Group Output. The result includes the tubes themselves, now wrapped in slime. To show the model's own material on the tubes, keep the original model object visible as well; the slime skin over it is very thin and the model's material shows through where the tubes leave the slime, or use the Alpha-based ghost version of the slime on the union mesh. The quick alternative when the SDF chain is too heavy: lower the map threshold slightly so the surface swells around the tubes, then add a Metaball (Shift + A › Metaball › Ball) with the slime material at each exit. Metaballs merge smoothly into each other and into nothing else, so they fake a meniscus from the outside."},{"t":"3. Strands","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#3-strands","x":"The stretchy threads between a tube and the surface are curves with a radius. Shift + A › Curve › Bézier. In Edit Mode, place one end on the tube just outside the surface and the other on the slime surface a little way off, and pull the middle control handles downward so the curve sags. Object Data Properties › Geometry › Bevel › Depth 0.01, Resolution 6, and the slime material. For a pulled look rather than a hung one, taper the middle: in Geometry Nodes on the curve, Spline Parameter › Factor → Float Curve (a dip to 0.3 at the center, 1 at both ends) → Set Curve Radius, then Curve to Mesh with a Curve Circle profile. Three or four strands per exit at different lengths read better than one. Duplicate with Shift + D and vary the sag."},{"t":"4. Drips","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#4-drips","x":"Elongated metaballs hanging below the lowest exits: Shift + A › Metaball › Ellipsoid, scaled tall, with a small Ball metaball at the lower tip. Metaballs of one object merge, so the tip blends into the ellipsoid as a droplet. Give the metaball object the slime material. To animate a falling drop, keyframe the tip ball's position downward; the ellipsoid stretches with it until they separate."},{"t":"Keeping it one substance","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#keeping-it-one-substance","x":"Give the map, the union mesh, the strands and the drips the same slime material, including its Volume Absorption, so color deepens with thickness in all of them. Then put a light behind the scene: thin gel glows when lit from behind, and the strands and drips are where it shows."},{"t":"Exercise S.1: wet collar only","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-s-1","x":"Build chain 1 on the model and render with the slime material on the map. Set From Max to 0.05, then 1.0, and render each. What you should see At 0.05 only a thin dark gloss ring at each exit; at 0.2 a convincing wet collar that fades over a tube's length; at 1.0 the whole model looks dipped. The length of the collar is how recently the tube left the slime."},{"t":"Exercise S.2: the meniscus by SDF","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-s-2","x":"Build chain 2 with Fillet Iterations 0, render, then 3, then 10. What you should see At 0 the surface meets the tubes at a sharp crease; at 0.05 the crease rounds into a small climbing curve; at 0.15 the slime mounds up around every tube. Somewhere between the last two is what wet gel does."},{"t":"Exercise S.3: one strand","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-s-3","x":"Build one tapered strand from step 3 and render it with a backlight. What you should see A thread that glows where it is thin, which is the single most convincing detail in the whole effect."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#sticky-slime-where-the-tubes-come-out-check","x":"Which node measures how far each point of the model is from the map surface? Answer Geometry Proximity, with the map's geometry as its target. Why does a wet surface need to be darker as well as glossier? Answer Water on a surface reduces diffuse reflection, so wet things look darker; gloss alone reads as plastic. What does SDF Grid Fillet do to the union of two shapes? Answer Rounds the concave crease between them into a smooth curve, which is a meniscus. Why do metaballs suit slime? Answer They merge smoothly into each other, so drops and collars blend without seams."},{"t":"Edge-glow ghost","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#edge-glow-ghost","x":"Drive a shader input with a node instead of a fixed number. Use the Layer Weight node to make a property depend on viewing angle. Build a see-through map that keeps a crisp outline."},{"t":"The idea","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#the-idea","x":"This makes the map nearly clear where it faces you and solid at its silhouette. The model reads through it, while the map keeps a crisp outline. It works because Alpha is a socket, not just a number: anything that outputs a value can drive it, and the Layer Weight node outputs a value that depends on the angle between the surface and the camera. Facing is 0 where the surface points straight at the camera and rises to 1 where it turns edge-on. At the silhouette every surface is edge-on, so Facing is highest there. Feeding it to Alpha makes the faces transparent and the outline opaque."},{"t":"The build","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#the-build-2","x":"Start from the Ghost recipe: Roughness 0.5, IOR Level 0.2. Add Shift + A › Input › Layer Weight. Set Blend to 0.3. Blend controls how quickly Facing rises toward the edge: low values keep the center clear and make the rim narrow, high values widen the rim. Add a Map Range node (Shift + A, then type Map Range). Set From Min 0, From Max 1, To Min 0.05, To Max 0.9. This rescales Facing so the faces are not fully invisible and the rim is not fully opaque. Connect Layer Weight's Facing output to Map Range's Value, and Map Range's Result to the Principled BSDF's Alpha. Tune by eye: raise To Max for a stronger outline, raise Blend for a wider rim, and raise To Min if the faces vanish completely. For a glowing rim on a dark background, also connect Map Range's Result to Emission Strength and set Emission Color to the map's color. The rim then emits as well as reflects, which reads as an X-ray or a hologram."},{"t":"Exercise 5.1: watch Facing","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-5-1","x":"Temporarily connect Layer Weight's Facing output straight to the Principled BSDF's Base Color. Orbit the camera around the sphere and the map in Rendered view. What you should see Black in the center of every surface, white at every edge, moving as you orbit. This is the value you are feeding to Alpha; seeing it directly makes the rest of the setup obvious. Reconnect Base Color afterwards."},{"t":"Exercise 5.2: invert it","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-5-2","x":"Swap the Map Range's To Min and To Max so faces are opaque and edges fade out. What you should see The map's silhouette dissolves and only the surfaces facing you remain, the opposite effect. This is the same trick used to fade objects at their edges into a background."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#edge-glow-ghost-check","x":"What value does Facing give at the exact silhouette? Answer 1, because the surface is edge-on to the camera there. What would happen with Blend at 1? Answer Facing rises very slowly, so almost the whole surface stays near 0 and only the last sliver of the edge becomes opaque. Why use Map Range instead of connecting Facing directly to Alpha? Answer To set the lowest and highest alpha the surface reaches, so the faces never fully disappear and the rim never becomes a hard white line."},{"t":"Setting up a recipe on a Molecular Nodes object","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#setting-up-a-recipe-on-a-molecular-nodes-object","x":"Put any recipe onto a Molecular Nodes map or model through the style node. Keep the per-chain colors that Molecular Nodes computes while using your own material. Compare two materials on the same object without rebuilding either."},{"t":"How materials reach a Molecular Nodes object","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#how-materials-reach-a-molecular-nodes-object","x":"A Molecular Nodes object is not a plain mesh with a material slot. Its geometry is generated by a node tree, and the style node at the end of that tree has a Material field; whatever material is in that field is applied to the geometry it generates. The material slots in the Material tab still exist, but the style node overrides them. So the workflow is: build the material in the Shader Editor, then choose it in the style node's Material field. Use a surface material. Principled BSDF in Surface, nothing in Volume. A map is a volume object, so Material › New starts with a Principled Volume, which renders as fog. Set Surface to Principled BSDF and the Volume field to Remove. Assign it in the style node. In the Geometry Nodes workspace, set the density style node's Material field to your material. The viewport updates immediately in Rendered mode. Keep Molecular Nodes' colors. The per-chain or per-atom colors live in a mesh attribute named Color , filled by the Color nodes in the Geometry Nodes tree. Add Shift + A › Input › Attribute, set its Name to Color , and connect its Color output to Base Color, or to Emission Color for the flat look. Without this, the style node's Color swatch and the color nodes before it have no effect, because your material sets Base Color to a constant. Compare recipes. Duplicate the material with the copy icon beside its name, rename the copy, change it, and switch between the two in the style node's Material field. Each switch takes a few seconds to resolve in Rendered view. Find nodes fast. In any node editor, press Shift + A and start typing a node's name. Render engine. Cycles renders Transmission, Subsurface, Coat, Thin Film and Volume Absorption accurately. EEVEE is faster but approximates them. If Alpha looks wrong in EEVEE, try the material's Settings › Render Method › Blended. Preview. Judge materials in Rendered view (Z › Rendered). Material Preview uses a built-in studio light, not your lights, and it hides most of what a material does under your actual rig."},{"t":"Exercise 6.1: chain colors through your own material","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-6-1","x":"Load a model from the PDB tab with Cartoon style and the default coloring, so chains differ in color. Build a Glossy candy material and assign it in the style node. The whole model turns one color. Add the Attribute node with Name Color and connect its Color output to Base Color. What you should see The chain colors return, now with the candy coat on top. Change a color in the Geometry Nodes tree and it flows through to the render, because the material reads the attribute rather than a constant."},{"t":"Exercise 6.2: the fog mistake, on purpose","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#exercise-6-2","x":"On the map, click Material › New and look at the Surface and Volume fields before changing anything. Render once as is, then set Surface to Principled BSDF and remove the Volume, and render again. What you should see The first render is a cloud; the second is a surface. Knowing exactly what the fog looks like means you will recognize it instantly the next time a map renders wrong."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#setting-up-a-recipe-on-a-molecular-nodes-object-check","x":"You changed the color swatch on the Density Style Surface node and nothing happened. Why? Answer Your material sets Base Color to a constant; it needs an Attribute node reading Color for the swatch to reach it. Where is the one place a material must be chosen for a Molecular Nodes object? Answer The Material field on the style node in the Geometry Nodes tree. Why does a new material on a map start as fog? Answer The map is a volume object, so Blender creates a Principled Volume by default."},{"t":"Sources","w":"Recipes","u":"material-recipes.html#sources","x":"Principled BSDF (Blender Manual) Materials (Molecular Nodes documentation)"},{"t":"Designing a look from scratch","w":"Designing a look","u":"designing-a-look.html","x":"The Recipes part gives fixed settings for named looks. This part covers the other case: a texture you can picture but cannot name. Describe what you see in five questions, each of which maps to one group of controls, then build the material in that order with the render open."},{"t":"The method","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 1","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-1","x":"State the five questions from memory and name the control group each one sets. Describe a real material in a photo using the five questions. Explain why the questions are asked in this order."},{"t":"Five questions","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 1","u":"designing-a-look.html#five-questions","x":"Each question isolates one thing the eye reads: the highlight, what happens below the surface, what sits on top of it, the small-scale relief, and the color. They are asked in the order light meets the material: first how it bounces off, then whether it enters, then what lies over the surface, then the fine shape of that surface, then how color varies across it. Answer them in order and you have a material. Five questions turn a texture in your head into settings Ask about the texture Then set 1. How does light bounce off it? Sharp glints, a soft sheen, or no highlight at all Roughness, IOR Level 0 is a mirror; 1 hides every highlight 2. Does light go into or through it? Solid, see-through, bending, or glowing from inside Alpha, Transmission, Subsurface Pick one; mixing them is rare 3. Is there a layer on top? Varnish, a wet film, fuzz or dust, an oil slick Coat, Sheen, Thin Film Added on top of whatever is below 4. Does it have fine relief? Grain, pores or ripples too small to model Bump from a Noise texture Object coordinates, so no UVs needed 5. Does the color change across it? By viewing angle, thickness, grain or data Layer Weight, Absorption, Attribute Feed Base Color, Alpha or Emission Work top to bottom, one question at a time, checking the render after each. The five questions and the controls each one sets The tinted boxes are all on the Principled BSDF, except Absorption (a Volume Absorption node) and the Bump, Layer Weight and Attribute nodes, which plug into it. The first two answers decide most looks. Questions 3 to 5 are refinements, and you can skip any that does not apply. Two habits make the method work. Judge from a reference, a photo of the real thing or a render you want to match, because the picture in memory is vaguer than it feels. And change one setting at a time with Rendered view open, so you can tie each change to what it did."},{"t":"Why the order matters","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 1","u":"designing-a-look.html#why-the-order-matters","x":"Each answer changes what the next one looks like. A coat (Question 3) over a rough body looks nothing like the same coat over a glossy one, so the body's roughness (Question 1) must be settled first. Bump (Question 4) multiplies whatever gloss is already there. Color variation (Question 5) is invisible until the shading that reveals it exists. Working out of order means tuning a layer, then changing the layer under it, then tuning the first layer again."},{"t":"Exercise 1.1: describe before you build","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 1","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-1-1","x":"Find three photos: a wet stone, a block of cheese, and a frosted glass bottle. For each, write one line per question: how light bounces, whether it goes in, what sits on top, what relief it has, how the color varies. Only then open Blender and compare your lines with the Recipes part and the feeling-words table in Chapter 6 . What you should find The stone is a soft body with a sharp film on top; the cheese has no highlight but a faint glow in thin slices, which is subsurface; the bottle is transmission with roughness. Writing the lines first turns a vague impression into a build list."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 1","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-1-check","x":"Which two questions decide most of a look? Answer How light bounces off the surface, and whether it goes into or through it. Why is relief asked after the layer on top? Answer Because the bump has to be connected to the right normal input, and whether the coat follows the bump or stays smooth depends on the look of the coat. What is wrong with building from the picture in your head rather than a photo? Answer The memory is vaguer than it feels, so you cannot tell whether a change moved toward it or away."},{"t":"Questions 1 and 2: the base layer","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 2","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-2","x":"These two answers set the body of the material. Get them right before touching anything else. Read the roughness of a real surface from the edge of its highlight. Tell Alpha, Transmission and Subsurface apart from a photo, and pick one. Know the IOR of the four materials you will meet most."},{"t":"Question 1. How does light bounce off it?","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 2","u":"designing-a-look.html#question-1-how-does-light-bounce-off-it","x":"Find the brightest spot on the reference, where the light reflects, and look at its edge. The edge of the highlight is roughness made visible: a sharp edge means a smooth surface, a soft edge means a rough one. Small, sharp, with the room reflected in it : wet, glassy, polished. Roughness 0 to 0.15. You can often see the shape of the window or the lamp in the highlight. A soft bloom that fades out : satin, plastic, skin. Roughness 0.3 to 0.6. The highlight is a bright region, not a picture of anything. No spot, only a lighter side and a darker side : chalk, clay, paper. Roughness 0.8 to 1. The surface is lit, not reflective. Two refinements. IOR Level (Specular panel) scales how bright the highlight is without changing its size; drop it to 0.2 for powdery surfaces whose highlights are faint even when smooth. And the highlight is white on everything except metal, so Metallic stays 0; a colored highlight in a photo means metal, nothing else. On a bumpy surface such as a density map, low roughness becomes many tiny glints, the glitter from the Recipes part . A single smooth glint needs a smooth mesh; otherwise raise Roughness or put the gloss in a Coat (Question 3), whose Coat Roughness can stay low because the coat's highlight is small relative to the body's."},{"t":"Question 2. Does light go into or through it?","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 2","u":"designing-a-look.html#question-2-does-light-go-into-or-through-it","x":"Look at a thin edge, or at a part held against the light. Nothing gets through : solid. Alpha 1, Transmission 0, Subsurface 0. You see through it and what is behind stays undistorted (a ghost, a cutaway): Alpha below 1. The object is faded, not glassy. You see through it and what is behind bends or swims (glass, water, jelly): Transmission Weight 1 and an IOR. Water is 1.33, glass 1.45, plastic and most gels about 1.5. Roughness now also sets how frosted it is, because it blurs transmitted light as well as reflected light. Nothing gets through, but thin parts glow and the shading is soft (wax, milk, skin, grapes): Subsurface Weight 1, with Scale about 1 to 2% of the object's width. Hold a grape up to a lamp and the far side glows; that is the test. Pick one of the three. Stacking them is rarely what you saw, and it is where murky renders come from: Alpha over Transmission doubles the see-through and halves the brightness, and Subsurface under Transmission has nothing to scatter."},{"t":"Exercise 2.1: read roughness from photos","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 2","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-2-1","x":"Collect five product photos: a phone, a leather bag, a ceramic mug, a rubber tire, a sheet of paper. For each, write a roughness number from the highlight's edge before checking. Build each on a sphere and compare with the photo. What you should find The phone sits near 0.05, the mug near 0.15 (glaze), the bag around 0.4, the tire near 0.7, the paper near 1. If your guesses were within 0.15, you can read roughness."},{"t":"Exercise 2.2: three see-through spheres","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 2","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-2-2","x":"Three spheres, one with Alpha 0.3, one with Transmission 1 at IOR 1.45, one with Subsurface Weight 1 and Scale 0.02 on a 1 m sphere. Put a small area light behind them and a cylinder behind that. What you should see The Alpha sphere shows the cylinder faintly and straight; the Transmission sphere bends it and shows bright rims; the Subsurface sphere hides it but glows at its edges from the backlight. Those three images are the three answers to Question 2."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 2","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-2-check","x":"A highlight shows a sharp reflection of a window. Roughness range? Answer 0 to 0.15. A material shows the object behind it bent and flipped. Which control, and what else must be set? Answer Transmission Weight 1, plus an IOR; roughness then also controls frosting. What single observation separates subsurface from a solid matte surface? Answer Thin parts glow when lit from behind. Why not combine Alpha 0.5 with Transmission 1 for an extra-clear look? Answer Each adds see-through and removes brightness; together they make murk, not clarity."},{"t":"Questions 3 and 4: what sits on the surface","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 3","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-3","x":"Many materials are two things, a body and a thin film over it, and many have texture too small to model. Both are added on top of the base from Chapter 2 . Tell a highlight that belongs to a film from one that belongs to the body. Add a coat, a sheen or a thin film and know what each does to the base. Add procedural relief with a Noise Texture and a Bump node, and scale it to the object. Explain why molecular surfaces cannot take an image texture directly."},{"t":"Question 3. Is there a layer on top?","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 3","u":"designing-a-look.html#question-3-is-there-a-layer-on-top","x":"Look at whether the highlight sits on the color, like a film, or in it. A film shows a sharp, colorless reflection while the color under it stays soft; a glossy body shows one highlight that is part of the color. Clear varnish or a wet film : Coat Weight 1, or 0.5 for damp. Coat Roughness 0 to 0.1. The body keeps its own roughness underneath, so a matte body under a glossy coat reads as wet clay or candy. Coat IOR stays at 1.5 for water and lacquer. Coat Tint colors the film; leave it white unless the film is itself colored, like a varnish on wood. Fuzz, dust, velvet : Sheen Weight 0.5 to 1 with Sheen Roughness high. It shows as a pale glow along the edges, where fibers are seen side-on. Sheen Tint sets the glow's color; white reads as dust, the body color as velvet. Oil slick or soap bubble : Thin Film Thickness 300 to 600 nm, Cycles only. The rainbow comes from interference in a film a few hundred nanometers thick, which is why the number is in real nanometers. A film changes the base in one way you should expect: the coat reflects a few percent of the light before it reaches the body, so the body color darkens slightly when a coat is added. Raise Base Color brightness a little to compensate."},{"t":"Question 4. Does it have fine relief?","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 3","u":"designing-a-look.html#question-4-does-it-have-fine-relief","x":"Grain, pores and ripples are built into the material rather than modeled. Use a procedural texture, not a photo: molecular surfaces have no UV map, which is the 2D layout an image needs to be pinned to a 3D surface. Procedural textures are formulas evaluated at each point in space, so they need only a position, which every surface has. Shift + A › Input › Texture Coordinate, and Shift + A › Texture › Noise Texture (Voronoi for a cellular pattern, Wave for stripes). Connect Object to Vector. Object coordinates follow the object if it moves and stay the same size if the scene scales. Shift + A › Vector › Bump. Connect Noise Fac to Height, and Bump Normal to the Principled BSDF's Normal. Add Coat Normal too if the film should follow the grain; leave it off for a smooth film over a rough body. Noise Scale sets how many ripples fit in one meter, so an object 0.2 m wide with Scale 100 gets about 20 across. Detail 2 to 5 adds finer ripples on top of the main ones. Bump Strength 0.05 to 0.3; above 0.5 the shading breaks into obvious artifacts because bump cannot cast shadows. Bump changes the shading, not the outline. For relief that shows at the silhouette, use Displacement in the material's Settings panel (Cycles) and more geometry. Bump also feeds Question 1: a glossy surface over bump sparkles, so keep Roughness moderate when you add it, or let the coat carry the gloss while the body stays rough."},{"t":"Exercise 3.1: film or body?","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 3","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-3-1","x":"Two spheres with Roughness 0.6 and a saturated color. On the first, lower Roughness to 0.05. On the second, leave Roughness at 0.6 and set Coat Weight 1, Coat Roughness 0.02. Render. What you should see Both have a sharp highlight, but the first looks like glossy plastic and the second like wet clay or glazed ceramic. In the second the color under the highlight stays soft. That difference is what Question 3 asks you to spot in a photo."},{"t":"Exercise 3.2: scale the grain","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 3","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-3-2","x":"On the monkey head, add the Noise into Bump chain with Strength 0.2. Render at Noise Scale 5, 50 and 500. What you should see At 5 the head looks dented, at 50 it looks like orange peel, at 500 it looks sandy. The look of relief is its size relative to the object, which is why the chapter gives Scale as ripples per meter rather than a single number."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 3","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-3-check","x":"What stays unchanged when you add a coat to a rough body? Answer The body's own roughness; the coat adds a second, sharper highlight over it. Why is Thin Film Thickness given in nanometers? Answer The colors come from interference in a real film of that thickness. Why can a map not take an image texture directly? Answer It has no UV map to pin the image to; procedural textures only need a position. What does bump never do, however strong? Answer Change the silhouette or cast shadows; that needs displacement."},{"t":"Question 5: does the color change across it?","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 4","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-4","x":"Flat, even color is rare on real things. Four kinds of variation come up, each with its own node, and all of them end in a Mix Color node (Shift + A › Color › Mix Color). The Mix node takes two colors, A and B, and a Factor from 0 to 1: at 0 it outputs A, at 1 it outputs B, in between a blend. Everything in this chapter is a way of producing that Factor. By viewing angle (rim light, velvet, pearl): Shift + A › Input › Layer Weight. Its Facing output is 0 where the surface points at you and 1 at the edges. Connect it to the Factor of a Mix between two colors, or straight to Alpha for the edge-glow ghost in the Recipes part . Pearl is a light color in A and a tinted one in B; velvet is dark A and the body color in B. By thickness (gel, colored glass, deep water): Volume Absorption in the Material Output's Volume socket, as in the slime recipe. Thick parts go dark and saturated, thin edges stay clear. This needs no Mix node, because the renderer measures the thickness itself. By grain (mottling, marbling): the Noise Texture from Chapter 3 into a Mix with Factor 0.1 to 0.2, between the base color and a slightly darker or warmer one. Reusing the same noise ties the color speckle to the bump, so the dark flecks sit in the dents, which is how real grain looks. A Color Ramp between the noise and the Factor sharpens the pattern into patches. By data (chain, B-factor, map value): Shift + A › Input › Attribute, with the attribute's name, into a Color Ramp (Shift + A › Converter › Color Ramp) and then into Base Color. Molecular Nodes stores its per-atom colors in Color , ready to use without a ramp. Numeric attributes such as b_factor need the ramp to become colors. For a density map, the Sample Nearest Attribute node in Geometry Nodes transfers colors from the model onto the surface, so the map takes the chain colors of what it encloses ( CryoEM Maps tutorial ). Variation is what separates a render from a plastic toy. Even a 10% noise mix or a slight angle shift makes a surface read as real, because real surfaces are never one color. Recognize the four kinds of color variation on real surfaces. Build each with the Mix Color node and the right driver. Color a density map from the model inside it."},{"t":"Exercise 4.1: pearl","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 4","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-4-1","x":"On a sphere with Roughness 0.3, add Layer Weight (Blend 0.4) and a Mix Color with pale pink in A and pale blue in B. Connect Facing to Factor and the Mix result to Base Color. Set Coat Weight 1. What you should see Pink in the center shifting to blue at the rim, moving as you orbit. Swap the colors and the shift reverses. This is the whole of iridescent paint."},{"t":"Exercise 4.2: color the map from the model","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 4","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-4-2","x":"Follow the Sampling Colors section of the CryoEM Maps tutorial: duplicate the model, remove its style so it outputs atoms, and feed it to a Sample Nearest Attribute node in the map's tree. In the map's material, read Color with an Attribute node into Base Color. What you should see The map surface takes the chain colors of the atoms beneath it, with soft boundaries where chains meet. Change the chain coloring on the model copy and the map follows."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 4","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-4-check","x":"What does a Mix Color node output at Factor 0.25? Answer Mostly A, a quarter of the way toward B. Which kind of variation needs no Mix node at all, and why? Answer Thickness, via Volume Absorption; the renderer measures the path length itself. Why reuse the bump's noise for the color speckle? Answer So dark flecks and dents coincide, as they do on real grain."},{"t":"Build order and a test rig","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 5","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-5","x":"Build the material in the order of the questions, on a sphere next to the real object, under the lights you will render with. Set up a swatch scene that shows every property of a material at once. Follow a build order that never requires undoing a previous step. Keep material versions safely and render a final check."},{"t":"The procedure","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 5","u":"designing-a-look.html#the-procedure","x":"Reference. Find a photo of the thing you are picturing and write down three words for it (wet, dark, grainy, say). The words become your answers to the five questions. Keep the photo open beside Blender; you will look at it twenty times. Test sphere. Shift + A › Mesh › UV Sphere, right-click › Shade Smooth, placed beside the map and given the same material. A sphere shows the highlight, the shadow edge and the rim at once; the map shows how the material behaves on real geometry. The monkey head from the lab bench adds cavities and thin parts, which matter for subsurface and occlusion. Rendered view, real lights. Press Z › Rendered with the final lights on. Material Preview uses a built-in studio light that will mislead you on roughness and transparency, because its HDRI gives every surface something bright to reflect. Base first. Base Color and Roughness only. Stop when the sphere's highlight matches the reference. Nothing after this step can fix a wrong roughness; it can only hide it. Then, in order: Alpha, Transmission or Subsurface; Coat or Sheen; bump; color variation. Check after each. If a step does not improve the look, undo it rather than compensating elsewhere, because compensation is how materials end up with six settings fighting each other. Keep versions. Duplicate the material with the copy icon beside its name and rename it, for example map_wet_v2 . Click the shield icon on a material you are not using, or Blender discards it when you save the file, since it keeps only materials with a user. Final check. Render with F12 at the output size. Fine bump and small glints change character with resolution and sample count, and the viewport preview is at a lower resolution than the final image."},{"t":"The material and its light","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 5","u":"designing-a-look.html#the-material-and-its-light","x":"A material and its lighting are one thing. Gloss needs something to reflect: a large area light gives a broad highlight, a small one a sharp dot, and a lit world gives the reflections something to show. When a glossy material looks dead, the fix is usually the light, not the material. The Lighting part covers this in full; for material work, the default rig there is enough."},{"t":"Exercise 5.1: a complete build","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 5","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-5-1","x":"Choose one feeling word from Chapter 6 that is not in the Recipes part , for example rubbery. Find a photo. Write the three words. Build on the sphere following steps 4 and 5, saving a version after each step. Render the final version next to the photo. What you should see Four or five saved versions, each one step further along, and a final sphere that matches the photo's highlight and body. If a later version looks worse than an earlier one, that step was the wrong answer to its question; go back to the earlier version and answer differently."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 5","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-5-check","x":"Why is Material Preview the wrong place to judge roughness? Answer Its studio HDRI gives every surface a bright reflection, so even rough materials look lively. What happens to a material with no users when you save? Answer Blender discards it unless the shield (fake user) is set. If adding a coat made the material worse, what should you do? Answer Remove the coat, not adjust something else to compensate."},{"t":"Feeling words and the settings behind them","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 6","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-6","x":"When the picture in your head comes with a word, this table turns the word into a starting point. The middle column is the important one: it says what the eye is reading, which is what you check against the reference. Words combine: wet clay is the chalky row plus the wet row, and dusty rubber is the rubbery row with the dry row's sheen. Feeling What the eye is reading Start from Wet A sharp, bright highlight sitting on top of the body color Coat Weight 1, Coat Roughness 0, body Roughness unchanged Dry, dusty No highlight, slightly pale edges Roughness 0.9, IOR Level 0.2, Sheen Weight 0.3 Chalky, clay No highlight, soft shading Roughness 1, IOR Level 0.1, or the Ambient Occlusion material Plasticky One medium highlight, perfectly even color Roughness 0.4 and nothing else; this is the default look, which is why renders start out plastic Rubbery A broad dim highlight, a faint edge sheen Roughness 0.6, IOR Level 0.3, Sheen Weight 0.2 Waxy, soft Light bleeds through thin parts; shadows soften Subsurface Weight 1, Scale 1 to 2% of width Glassy See-through with sharp reflections; the background bends Transmission 1, Roughness 0, IOR 1.45 Frosted, milky See-through but blurred Transmission 1, Roughness 0.4 to 0.7 Jelly, gummy See-through, colored, deeper where thick, wet Transmission 1, IOR 1.35, Roughness 0.05, Volume Absorption Ghostly See-through without bending, faint Alpha 0.2 to 0.4, IOR Level 0.2 Velvety Dark in the middle, glowing at the rim Sheen Weight 1, Roughness 0.8, Layer Weight into a Mix Pearly, iridescent Color shifts with angle Coat Weight 1 with Thin Film 400 nm, or Layer Weight into a two-color Mix Metallic A colored highlight, dark reflections Metallic 1, Roughness 0.3; rare in biology Flat, diagrammatic No shading at all Emission Strength 1 with your color, Base Color black, IOR Level 0 Grainy, mottled Fine speckle in both shading and color Noise into Bump at 0.1 to 0.3 and into a Mix at Factor 0.1 Translate an adjective into a starting set of values. Combine two adjectives into one material. Spot which row a render you admire belongs to."},{"t":"Combining rows","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 6","u":"designing-a-look.html#combining-rows","x":"Each row changes a different group of controls, so two rows rarely conflict. Add the second row's settings to the first's and resolve any overlap by asking which of the two the eye reads first. Wet velvet, for instance, keeps the velvet's sheen and high roughness and adds the wet row's coat; the coat's sharp highlight wins where the two overlap, which is also what a wet velvet looks like."},{"t":"Exercise 6.1: two-word materials","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 6","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-6-1","x":"Build three materials from pairs of rows: wet clay, dusty plastic, frosted jelly. For each, write down which row's settings you kept where the two overlapped. What you should see Wet clay is a matte body with a sharp coat highlight; dusty plastic is a satin body with a pale rim; frosted jelly is a rough transmission with absorption. If any looks like a single row, the second row's settings were overwritten rather than added."},{"t":"Exercise 6.2: classify a render","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 6","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-6-2","x":"Pick three renders from the Molecular Nodes examples page or any structural biology cover you admire. Assign each to one or two rows of the table and name the settings you believe were used. What you should find Most published renders are satin plastic or matte clay with ambient occlusion; the striking ones add one more row, usually wet or glassy, to one object only."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 6","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-6-check","x":"Which column of the table should you compare with the photo? Answer What the eye is reading. Why do two rows rarely conflict? Answer Each changes a different group of controls. A render has colored highlights. Which row, and what does that tell you? Answer Metallic; colored highlights only come from metal."},{"t":"Worked examples","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 7","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-7","x":"Follow the five questions end to end on two different subjects. See how a single observation in the reference decides a single setting. Practice the method on your own subject."},{"t":"A wet river pebble","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 7","u":"designing-a-look.html#a-wet-river-pebble","x":"The picture: dark gray, smooth but not polished, one bright sharp glint, a little paler at grazing angles, a faint speckle. Bounce. The glint is sharp, but the stone under it is only semi-gloss, so the glint belongs to a water film and not to the stone. Body Roughness 0.5; Coat Weight 1, Coat Roughness 0.02. If the stone were dry, there would be no coat and the roughness alone would set the look. Into or through. Solid, and thin edges do not glow. Alpha 1, Transmission 0, Subsurface 0. Layer. Already added in step 1, because the sharp glint told us about the film before the question came up. This happens often; the questions are a checklist, not a strict sequence of discoveries. Relief. Faint speckle. Noise Texture with Scale 200 for a 0.2 m pebble, Detail 5, into Bump at Strength 0.05, connected to Normal only, so the water film stays smooth over the grain. Color. Paler at grazing angles comes from the film reflecting the sky. Layer Weight with Blend 0.5, Facing into the Factor of a Mix between dark gray and mid gray, into Base Color. The same noise into a second Mix at Factor 0.1 adds the speckle to the color. If the result looks like plastic, lower Coat Weight to 0.6. If it looks dusty, lower the body Roughness. If the speckle reads as dirt rather than grain, lower the second Mix's Factor."},{"t":"A map as a frosted sugar shell around the model","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 7","u":"designing-a-look.html#a-map-as-a-frosted-sugar-shell-around-the-model","x":"The picture: the model visible as a soft shape inside a milky, slightly glossy shell. Bounce. A soft highlight: Roughness 0.4. Into or through. See-through with blur: Transmission 1, IOR 1.45. The roughness from step 1 now also does the frosting; there is no separate frosting control. Layer. None. Sugar has no film. Relief. The map's own surface already has it; adding bump would double it. Color. Milkier where the shell is thick: Volume Absorption with a pale version of the shell color, Density 2. A pale color is important; a saturated one turns the shell into colored glass rather than sugar. The model inside will be refracted. If it needs to stay sharp for the figure, swap step 2 for Alpha 0.4 and accept a flatter shell."},{"t":"A dry chalk cast","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 7","u":"designing-a-look.html#a-dry-chalk-cast","x":"The picture: a plaster model, matte white, slightly warmer in the hollows, no highlight anywhere. Bounce. No highlight: Roughness 1, IOR Level 0.1. Into or through. Solid, but very thin edges of plaster do glow faintly: Subsurface Weight 0.3, Scale 1% of width. A small weight is enough. Layer. Faint chalk dust on the surfaces: Sheen Weight 0.3, white tint. Relief. Fine, even grain: Noise Scale 400 per meter, Bump Strength 0.1. Color. Warmer in the hollows: an Ambient Occlusion node's Color output into the Factor of a Mix between white and a warm gray, into Base Color. This is a good default for figure panels where color is carried by labels rather than the surface."},{"t":"Exercise 7.1: your own subject","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 7","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-7-1","x":"Choose an object within reach: a mug, a piece of fruit, a key. Write the five answers as in the examples, with a number or a node for each. Build it on the sphere and the monkey head, and photograph the object under a desk lamp for comparison. What you should find The fruit will need subsurface and a sheen; the mug a coat over a matte body; the key a metallic base. Every object you can hold is one of the rows in Chapter 6 plus one or two refinements."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 7","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-7-check","x":"In the pebble, why does the coat get the sharp roughness and not the body? Answer The glint belongs to the water film; the stone under it is semi-gloss. In the sugar shell, what controls the frosting? Answer The same Roughness that blurs the highlight, since roughness blurs transmission too. Why does the chalk cast use a small Subsurface Weight rather than 1? Answer Plaster only glows at its thinnest edges; full subsurface would make it look like wax."},{"t":"When it looks wrong","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 8","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-8","x":"Most failures trace back to one of the five questions answered twice, or to the light rather than the material. Before changing anything, decide which: put the material on the test sphere under the lab-bench rig, and if the sphere looks right, the problem is the geometry or the lighting of the real scene. Symptom Likely cause Fix Glitter, pinpoint sparkles Roughness near 0 on a bumpy mesh Roughness 0.3 or more, or move the gloss into a Coat with Coat Roughness 0.1 Looks like a plastic toy Even roughness and color, one medium highlight Bump at 0.1, noise into a Mix on Base Color at 0.1, or a coat over a rougher body Dull and lifeless Roughness too high, weak key light, nothing for reflections to show Roughness 0.5, a larger and brighter key light, World Strength 0.3 Murky, dark Two see-through effects stacked, or a dark Base Color on a transmissive material Pick one of Alpha, Transmission, Subsurface; lighten Base Color (transmissive materials need a paler color than you expect) Fog or smoke instead of a surface A volume shader on a surface object Surface: Principled BSDF; Volume: Remove Colors washed out or shifted AgX view transform, or highlights too strong View Transform Standard, IOR Level down No depth, parts look pasted on Light from every direction, no occlusion Lower World Strength, add a key light, or use the Ambient Occlusion material Black object No material in the style node, a misspelled Attribute name, or black Base Color with Emission 0 Assign the material; check the Attribute name is Color ; set a color Edits change nothing Solid viewport, or editing a different material than the one in the style node Z › Rendered; check the style node's Material field Noise and white speckles in the render Glossy surface, small bright light, transmission A larger light, more samples with Denoise on, and Render › Light Paths › Clamping › Indirect Light 10 The model inside is warped Transmission refracts Alpha instead, or Transmission with IOR 1.0, which passes light without bending it Diagnose a bad render from its symptom rather than by trial and error. Separate material problems from lighting problems."},{"t":"Exercise 8.1: break it on purpose","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 8","u":"designing-a-look.html#exercise-8-1","x":"Take a working satin plastic sphere and, one at a time, produce five of the symptoms in the table: glitter, plastic toy, murk, fog, black object. For each, write down the one setting you changed, then undo it. What you should find Every symptom has a single cause you can name. Once you have made each mistake deliberately, you will recognize it in a render within seconds."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Designing a look, Chapter 8","u":"designing-a-look.html#chapter-8-check","x":"A render is grainy with white speckles. Material or lighting? Answer Usually lighting: small bright lights on glossy surfaces. Enlarge the lights, then raise samples. The map renders as smoke. Which socket is wrong? Answer The Volume socket holds a volume shader; the Surface socket is empty or should hold the Principled BSDF. The sphere looks fine but the map looks wrong with the same material. Where is the problem? Answer In the geometry or the scene lighting, not the material."},{"t":"Lighting, from zero to designing your own setups","w":"Lighting","u":"lighting.html","x":"Named setups like three-point and clamshell are answers to a few underlying questions about direction, softness and contrast. This part teaches the questions first, then the named answers, then how to invent an answer of your own, such as a glowing ribbon wrapped around the subject."},{"t":"How light behaves in a render","w":"Lighting, Chapter 1","u":"lighting.html#chapter-1","x":"Every light, whatever its name, is five properties, and every surface shows those properties in two ways. Once you can see a photo in these terms, you can rebuild its lighting. List the five properties of any light and say what each one changes in the image. Explain why the same lamp can be a hard light or a soft light. Tell shading from reflection on a surface, and know which properties each one shows."},{"t":"The five properties of any light","w":"Lighting, Chapter 1","u":"lighting.html#the-five-properties-of-any-light","x":"Direction. Where the light sits relative to the camera and the subject, measured as an angle around the subject (front, side, back) and an angle above it. Direction decides where shadows fall and which planes are bright, which is how the eye reads shape. A surface facing the light is bright; one turned 60° from it receives half as much; one turned 90° receives none. That falloff across a curved surface is the shading gradient. Size, relative to distance. A light that looks large from the subject's position wraps around it: shadows have soft edges and highlights are broad. A light that looks small gives crisp shadows and small, sharp highlights. The same lamp is soft up close and hard far away, because what matters is the angle it covers as seen from the subject. A 1 m panel at 1 m covers about 53°; at 10 m it covers about 6°. The sun is enormous but far, so it covers half a degree and is a hard light; an overcast sky covers the whole dome and is the softest light there is. Intensity. How bright it is at the subject. For a point, spot or area light this falls with the square of distance: twice as far, a quarter of the light. That is why 500 W at 50 m rendered black in your scene. Intensity on its own changes only brightness; everything else about the image stays the same, which is why exposure can be set last. Color. Warm or cool, and how saturated. Two lights of different colors on the same object split it into a warm side and a cool side, which is a cheap way to add depth. For figures, color in the lights competes with color in the data, so lights stay white. Shape. A disk, a long strip, a ring, a window. The shape is invisible in diffuse shading, but it is exactly what glossy surfaces reflect, and it decides what highlights look like."},{"t":"The two ways a surface shows light","w":"Lighting, Chapter 1","u":"lighting.html#the-two-ways-a-surface-shows-light","x":"Shading. Matte surfaces scatter light in every direction, so each part of the surface is bright in proportion to how squarely it faces the light. Shading carries the form: the gradient from lit side to shadow side is what tells the eye a sphere is round. Shading sees direction, size and intensity, but not shape, because every direction from the light arrives mixed together. Reflection. Glossy surfaces return a picture of the light. The highlight on a wet pebble is a small image of the lamp, and the long streak along a glass bottle is the image of a strip light. Reflection sees shape and size above all. A glossy object in a scene with a single tiny light and nothing else shows a single pinpoint and looks dead, however bright the light is, because there is nothing else for it to reflect. The practical rule that follows: light matte things by thinking about shadows, and light glossy things by thinking about what the surface will reflect. Most subjects are both, so a good setup does both. A density map with a satin material needs a soft key for its form and a large, well-placed bright shape for its sheen."},{"t":"Adding and moving things, while you learn","w":"Lighting, Chapter 1","u":"lighting.html#adding-and-moving-things-while-you-learn","x":"Every exercise in this part places objects and lights. Each move below is a key pressed with the mouse over the viewport; then move the mouse, or type a number for an exact amount. To do this Press Notes Add an object or light Shift + A It appears at the 3D cursor. The Adjust Last Operation panel, bottom left, sets its size and place right after Put the 3D cursor back at the center Shift + S › Cursor to World Origin Shift + right-click places it anywhere Move, rotate, scale G, R, S Click or Enter keeps the change; right-click or Esc cancels it Lock to one axis G, R or S, then X, Y or Z G Z moves only up and down; G Shift + Z moves in X and Y but never in Z An exact amount type a number, then Enter G Z 2 Enter is 2 m up; R X 90 Enter is 90° about X; a minus sign reverses Fine control, snapping hold Shift, or Ctrl, while moving Shift slows the mouse; Ctrl snaps to grid steps Type the values N › Item Location, Rotation and Scale fields for the selected object Reset Option + G, Option + R, Option + S Clears location, rotation or scale Duplicate Shift + D Then move it; right-click leaves the copy in place Select click; Shift + click; A; Option + A One; add or remove one; all; none Frame the selection numpad period, or View › Frame Selected Home frames everything Front, side, top, camera numpad 1, 3, 7, 0 No numpad: the ` key left of 1 opens a view menu, or click the X, Y, Z balls of the gizmo Isolate numpad slash, or View › Local View Shows only the selection until you press it again Undo, redo Cmd + Z, Shift + Cmd + Z On a MacBook trackpad, two fingers orbit, Shift and two fingers pan, and a pinch zooms. Preferences › Input › Emulate Numpad makes the number row act as the numpad keys above."},{"t":"Exercise 1.1: the softness test","w":"Lighting, Chapter 1","u":"lighting.html#exercise-1-1","x":"In the lab bench, keep one area light (Disk) 2 m from the sphere and set Size to 0.1 m. Look at the edge of the sphere's shadow on the plane. To build it from an empty scene, first Shift + S › Cursor to World Origin. Then Shift + A › Mesh › Plane and S 10 Enter; Shift + A › Mesh › UV Sphere, G Z 1 Enter, and right-click › Shade Smooth; Shift + A › Light › Area, Shape set to Disk in the Light properties, then G X 1.2 Enter and G Z 2.6 Enter. That puts the light 2 m from the sphere's center, off to one side, so its shadow falls beside the sphere where you can see it. Set Size to 1 m, then 3 m, watching the shadow edge and the highlight each time. Put Size back to 1 m and move the light to 6 m away, raising Power until the sphere is as bright as before. To move it along the same line, open the sidebar (N) › Item and type Location X 3.6 and Z 5.8: that is 6 m from the sphere's center in the same direction. Expect about 9 times the Power, since light falls off with the square of distance (Chapter 8 ). What you should see At 0.1 m the shadow edge is a sharp line and the highlight is a dot. At 3 m the shadow fades over a wide band and the highlight is a broad patch. At 6 m away with Size 1 m the shadow is nearly as sharp as with the 0.1 m light up close: softness is size divided by distance."},{"t":"Exercise 1.2: shading against reflection","w":"Lighting, Chapter 1","u":"lighting.html#exercise-1-2","x":"Two spheres: one with Roughness 1, one with Roughness 0.05. One area light. Change the light's Shape from Disk to Rectangle 2 m by 0.1 m, then back. What you should see The matte sphere does not change. The glossy one shows a disk, then a long bar, in its surface. The matte sphere reads direction and size; the glossy one reads shape."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Lighting, Chapter 1","u":"lighting.html#chapter-1-check","x":"Which property of a light does a matte surface never reveal? Answer Shape. A light is moved from 2 m to 4 m from the subject. What happens to its brightness at the subject, and to its softness? Answer Brightness drops to a quarter; the light becomes harder, because it covers a smaller angle. Why does a glossy sphere lit by one tiny bright light look dead? Answer It shows one pinpoint and nothing else, because there is nothing else bright to reflect."},{"t":"Blender's light sources","w":"Lighting, Chapter 2","u":"lighting.html#chapter-2","x":"Blender has four light objects, a world background, and any mesh with an Emission shader. Each is a different trade of control against realism ( Light Objects ). Add a light with Shift + A › Light; its settings live in the Object Data Properties tab, the green bulb icon. Source What it models Key settings Best for Point A bulb radiating in all directions Power (W), Radius (bigger is softer), Soft Falloff Bare bulbs, candles, quick fills; rarely a key light Sun Parallel light from infinitely far; position does not matter, only rotation Strength (W/m², 1000 for clear sky), Angle (0.5° is the real sun; larger is softer) Even light over a whole scene, orthographic figures, a cheap hard key Spot A point light with a cone Power, Radius, Spot Size (cone angle), Blend (edge softness), Show Cone Pools of light, gobos, picking out one domain Area A glowing panel: the softbox of 3D Power, Shape (Rectangle, Square, Disk, Ellipse), Size, Spread (narrower is more directional) Nearly all key, fill and rim lights for still images World Light from every direction: a color or an HDRI photo of a real environment Color, Strength; an Environment Texture for HDRIs Ambient fill, realistic reflections on glossy surfaces Emissive mesh Any object with an Emission shader Emission Strength and Color in its material; any shape you can model Shaped highlights: strips, rings, ribbons, logos reflected in the subject Choose the right light object for a job and set its three or four important parameters. Turn any mesh into a light and hide it from the camera. Restrict a light to specific objects with light linking."},{"t":"Settings shared by all light objects","w":"Lighting, Chapter 2","u":"lighting.html#settings-shared-by-all-light-objects","x":"Power is radiant power, not bulb wattage. The manual's reference points: an 800-lumen LED bulb is about 2 W, a fluorescent tube 4.5 W as a rectangle area light. Studio renders use far higher values than these because the lights sit far from the subject. Chapter 8 gives the arithmetic. Exposure on each light multiplies its power by 2 to that value, so +1 doubles it, +3 is eight times, and −2 quarters it. It is a convenient coarse dial when Power would need large numbers. Temperature sets a natural color by blackbody temperature in kelvin: 3200 K is warm tungsten, 5600 K is daylight, 7000 K is bluish shade. Color multiplies on top for tints that are not natural light. Normalize keeps total power constant when you resize a light, so a bigger light becomes softer but not brighter. Turn it off if you want a bigger light to also be brighter, the way a real softbox is. Radius or Size is softness. Size is relative to distance: an area light 1 m wide at 1 m from the subject is soft; the same light at 10 m is hard. Cast Shadow and the Cycles-only Multiple Importance and Cast Shadow options are left on; turning shadows off makes a light pass through objects, which is occasionally useful for a fill that must not add a second shadow."},{"t":"The area light in detail","w":"Lighting, Chapter 2","u":"lighting.html#the-area-light-in-detail","x":"The area light is the one you will use for nearly everything, so its extra settings deserve a closer look. Shape sets what glossy surfaces will see: Disk and Square reflect as a soft blob, Rectangle and Ellipse as a bar whose aspect you choose. Size, or Size X and Y, sets both softness and the shape of the reflection. Spread, from 180° down to 1°, narrows the cone the light emits into: at 180° it is a bare panel, at 30° it behaves like a softbox with a grid, lighting the subject without spilling onto the backdrop. The viewport shows the panel as an outline with a line indicating its direction; the yellow dot at the end of that line can be dragged to aim it."},{"t":"Emissive meshes","w":"Lighting, Chapter 2","u":"lighting.html#emissive-meshes","x":"An Emission shader on any mesh turns it into a light. This is how shaped highlights are made: a long thin plane becomes a strip, a torus becomes a ring, a bent curve with Bevel Depth becomes a glowing ribbon. Three things to know. Cycles samples them as real lights, with soft shadows and reflections, and treats their Strength exactly like Power on a light object spread over the mesh's area. EEVEE shows them as glowing surfaces and reflects them in glossy materials, but does not use them to cast shadows or light diffuse surfaces unless an Irradiance Volume light probe is baked, so for EEVEE pair each emissive mesh with a real light. And they appear in the render unless you hide them from the camera: Object Properties › Visibility › Ray Visibility, untick Camera, leaving Glossy on so the reflection survives (Cycles)."},{"t":"Light linking","w":"Lighting, Chapter 2","u":"lighting.html#light-linking","x":"In Cycles and EEVEE, a light can be told to affect only certain objects, and shadow linking which objects block it ( Light Linking ). Select the light, open the Shading panel in its Object Properties, create a light linking collection, and drag objects into it from the Outliner. Included objects are the only ones the light touches; excluded objects are the only ones it skips. A rim light that touches only the map, not the model, is one click away; emissive meshes support this in Cycles only. It breaks physics on purpose, which is why it is powerful: a fill for the model that does not flatten the map, a rim for the map that does not flare on the model."},{"t":"Exercise 2.1: the same light four ways","w":"Lighting, Chapter 2","u":"lighting.html#exercise-2-1","x":"Light the sphere with one Point light at 2 m, Radius 0, Power 100 W. Change its type to Spot and set Spot Size 30°, Blend 0.2. Then to Sun, Strength 3, Angle 0.5°. Then to Area, Disk, Size 1 m, Power 100 W. Render each. What you should see The point gives a hard shadow and a dot highlight; the spot gives the same with a pool of light on the plane; the sun gives parallel shadows that do not change if you move the light; the area gives a soft shadow and a broad highlight. Those four images are the four light objects."},{"t":"Exercise 2.2: an emissive strip","w":"Lighting, Chapter 2","u":"lighting.html#exercise-2-2","x":"Add a Plane, scale it to 3 m by 0.2 m, and give it an Emission material with Strength 20. Stand it beside the glossy sphere, untick Camera under Ray Visibility, and render in Cycles. What you should see A long bright bar reflected in the sphere, with the plane itself absent from the image. Rotate the plane and the bar moves. This is the basis of Chapter 5 ."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Lighting, Chapter 2","u":"lighting.html#chapter-2-check","x":"Which light object's position does not matter? Answer Sun; only its rotation counts. What does Spread do on an area light? Answer Narrows the cone it emits into, like a grid on a softbox, so light reaches the subject without spilling. Why pair an emissive mesh with a real light in EEVEE? Answer EEVEE reflects emissive meshes but does not use them to cast shadows or light diffuse surfaces. A rim light is flaring on the model but is wanted on the map. What feature solves it without moving the light? Answer Light linking: include only the map."},{"t":"The three decisions behind every setup","w":"Lighting, Chapter 3","u":"lighting.html#chapter-3","x":"Strip any named setup down and it is three choices about the main light, plus two about everything else. Make these deliberately and the names take care of themselves. Place a key light by what it should reveal, not by habit. Set softness and contrast on purpose, and name the mood each choice gives. Check separation and reflections before calling a setup finished."},{"t":"Decision 1: where the key light comes from","w":"Lighting, Chapter 3","u":"lighting.html#decision-1-where-the-key-light-comes-from","x":"The key is the one light that defines the form. Its direction is the single most important choice in the image, because it decides which planes are bright and where the shadows fall, and those two things are what the eye uses to reconstruct shape. Front (near the camera): flat, few shadows, shows color and detail but hides form. Fine for a documentation figure, dull for anything else. Camera flash is front light. 45° to the side and 45° above : the classic portrait angle, because it puts a shadow on every plane that turns away and leaves the rest lit. Shape reads immediately. This is the default for a reason, and the starting point for nearly every setup in Chapter 4 . 90° to the side : half lit, half dark. Dramatic and very revealing of surface texture; every bump casts a shadow, so it is also how photographers check a surface for flaws. Behind (back light or rim): the subject goes dark with a bright edge. Shows outline and separation, not form. Used as a second light, rarely alone. Above or below : top light reads as natural sun or an overhead lamp and makes deep eye-socket shadows on faces; light from below reads as unnatural, because nothing in nature lights from below, which is why horror films use it. In Blender, set this by placing the key and giving it a Track To constraint aimed at the subject, then move it until the shadows fall where you want them. The angle above matters as much as the angle around: a key at 45° around but level with the subject gives long horizontal shadows that confuse the shape."},{"t":"Decision 2: how soft","w":"Lighting, Chapter 3","u":"lighting.html#decision-2-how-soft","x":"Softness is size relative to distance, and it sets the mood more than any other choice. Hard (small or distant light): crisp shadow edges, small bright highlights, a sunny or theatrical feel, and every surface flaw on display. Hard light is honest and unflattering. Soft (large or close light): shadow edges fade over a wide band, highlights are broad and gentle, the look is calm and flattering. Most product and portrait photography is soft, which is why softboxes exist. To change softness without changing brightness, resize the area light with Normalize on. To judge it, look at the edge of the subject's shadow on a backdrop, or at the edge of the terminator, the line where a sphere turns from lit to shadow. A soft light also fills its own shadows a little, because light from the far edge of a large panel reaches around the subject."},{"t":"Decision 3: how much contrast","w":"Lighting, Chapter 3","u":"lighting.html#decision-3-how-much-contrast","x":"Contrast is the ratio between the lit side and the shadow side, and it is controlled by how much fill light reaches the shadows. Low contrast (shadows nearly as bright as the lit side, a ratio near 2:1): open, clean, informational. High-key images live here. Medium (shadows about a third of the lit side, near 3:1, the usual starting point): natural, dimensional. High contrast (shadows nearly black, 8:1 and beyond): moody, dramatic, low-key. Fill comes from a second, larger, dimmer light on the opposite side, from the world background, or from a bright surface that bounces the key light back. Fill should never cast a visible second shadow; if it does, it is too small or too strong. The cleanest fill is the largest: the world light, or an area light several times the key's size at a fraction of its power."},{"t":"Two more: separation and reflections","w":"Lighting, Chapter 3","u":"lighting.html#two-more-separation-and-reflections","x":"Separation. A subject the same brightness as its background disappears into it. Either light the subject brighter than the background, or add a rim light from behind to draw a bright edge. With a transparent background, separation is still a question of whether the far edges of the subject are visible against whatever it will sit on; a dark rim edge on a white poster reads as a line, a bright rim on a dark slide reads as a glow. Reflections. Decide what the glossy parts will reflect: a big soft panel gives one broad sheen, a strip gives a line that traces the contour, a dark world gives nothing. Chapter 5 builds on this."},{"t":"Exercise 3.1: the key around the clock","w":"Lighting, Chapter 3","u":"lighting.html#exercise-3-1","x":"One area light on the monkey head, Track To constraint on, at 45° above. Render with the light at 0°, 45°, 90°, 135° and 180° around the head, measured from the camera. What you should see At 0° a flat, detailed face; at 45° a clearly shaped one; at 90° a half-moon; at 135° a dark face with one lit cheek; at 180° a silhouette with a bright outline. Label the five renders and keep them."},{"t":"Exercise 3.2: contrast ladder","w":"Lighting, Chapter 3","u":"lighting.html#exercise-3-2","x":"Key at 45°/45°, Power fixed. Add a fill at twice the key's size on the opposite side. Render with the fill at 0, a quarter, a half and equal to the key's power. What you should see The shadow side rises from black to nearly as bright as the lit side, and the image moves from dramatic to flat. Somewhere between a quarter and a half is where a figure usually wants to sit."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Lighting, Chapter 3","u":"lighting.html#chapter-3-check","x":"Which direction of key light reveals surface texture most? Answer 90° to the side. Why does the angle above the subject matter as much as the angle around it? Answer It sets whether shadows fall down and across or straight sideways; level light confuses shape. A fill light casts a second shadow. What are the two fixes? Answer Make it larger, or make it dimmer; both make its shadow disappear. What is the cheapest fill in Blender? Answer The world light: large, even and free."},{"t":"The named setups","w":"Lighting, Chapter 4","u":"lighting.html#chapter-4","x":"Each name is a fixed answer to the three decisions of Chapter 3 . The table gives the geometry, what it does to the image, and when to reach for it. Angles are measured from the camera's line of sight; \"above\" means the light sits higher than the subject. Three-point lighting: key for form, fill for contrast, rim for separation Subject Camera Key Soft, 45° off the axis, the brightest light Fill Larger, a third as bright, opposite side Rim Behind, opposite the key; draws the edge Plan view from above Three-point setup, seen from above The key is the only light that decides the form. Everything else in the plan modifies its result: the fill lifts the shadows it makes, and the rim adds the edge it cannot reach. Setup Geometry Effect Use when Single key, 45/45 (Rembrandt in portraits) One soft area light 45° to the side, 45° above, a little world light Strong form, one clean shadow side, a classic look You want shape to read and have one subject Split One key at 90° to the side Half lit, half dark; texture jumps out Showing surface detail; dramatic cover-style images Butterfly (Paramount) Key directly in front, high, pointing down Symmetric, a shadow directly below features; flattering, glamorous Symmetric subjects seen from the front Three-point Key at 45/45; fill opposite, larger, a third as bright; rim behind, opposite the key, aimed at the edge Form from the key, open shadows from the fill, a bright outline from the rim The default for anything that must look finished; the setup from the Recipes part Clamshell Two soft lights stacked on the camera axis, one above pointing down, one below pointing up, the lower one weaker Nearly shadowless, even, glowing; highlights top and bottom Beauty and product shots; smooth, glossy subjects; when you want no mood, only polish High key Soft key, strong fill, bright background, low contrast everywhere Clean, open, bright; nothing hidden Documentation figures, white-background posters Low key Hard or narrow key, little or no fill, dark background Mostly shadow, a few lit planes; dramatic Hero images, title slides, cover art Studio product Two or three large area lights (a big top softbox plus side strips) around a gradient backdrop; the lights are placed for reflections more than for shading Long clean highlights tracing the edges, controlled reflections Glossy or glassy subjects; the \"advertising\" look Rim or kicker only One or two lights behind and to the sides, nothing in front A glowing outline on a dark body Silhouettes, showing the overall shape of a complex, a dramatic second panel HDRI No lights; an environment photo in the World Real-world reflections and soft ambient light; the sun in the photo acts as a key Fast realistic reflections on metal and glass; a natural base to add one light to Sun and sky A Sun light plus a Sky Texture in the World Hard shadows, blue fill from the sky, outdoor look Scenes meant to feel outdoors; rarely for molecules Build three-point, clamshell and studio product lighting from the description alone. Say what each named setup does to the image and pick one for a given job. Recognize the setups in photographs and in other people's renders."},{"t":"Reading the table","w":"Lighting, Chapter 4","u":"lighting.html#reading-the-table","x":"The names come from portrait and film work, where the subject is a face. For a molecule, read \"subject\" as the part of the structure you want the viewer to look at first, and aim the key there. Fill ratios are a starting point. A fill at a third of the key's power is \"medium contrast\" in Chapter 3 ; halve it for drama, double it for a documentation figure. Studio product lighting is the one that most people mean by \"looks professional\": large lights, placed where their reflections land on the subject's edges, and a backdrop that fades from light to dark so the subject separates on both sides."},{"t":"Three setups built step by step","w":"Lighting, Chapter 4","u":"lighting.html#three-setups-built-step-by-step","x":"Three-point. Key: Area Disk, Size equal to the subject's width, 45° left of the camera and 45° above, Track To the subject, Power until the lit side is just under white. Fill: Area Disk, twice the size, 45° right, slightly below the camera height, a third of the key's power. Rim: Area Rectangle, narrow and tall, behind the subject and to the right, aimed at the subject's edge, roughly the key's power. World gray at 0.2. Clamshell. Remove the fill and rim. Move the key to directly in front, 30° above the camera, pointing down. Add a second Area Disk directly in front, 30° below the camera, pointing up, at half the key's power. Both large, both close. The subject glows with almost no shadow; highlights appear above and below every rounded form. Studio product. Three Area lights: a large Rectangle above the subject pointing down (the top softbox), and two tall narrow Rectangles to the left and right, slightly behind the subject, pointing inward (the edge strips). Set Spread to 60° on each so they do not spill onto the backdrop. A large plane behind the subject with a Gradient Texture into its Emission, bright at the bottom and dark at the top, as the backdrop. Then move the strips while watching the glossy parts until each edge shows one clean line of reflection."},{"t":"Exercise 4.1: build all three","w":"Lighting, Chapter 4","u":"lighting.html#exercise-4-1","x":"On the glossy candy sphere and the map, build three-point, then clamshell, then studio product, saving each as a collection ( Rig_ThreePoint , Rig_Clamshell , Rig_Studio ). A collection is a folder of objects in the Outliner: select a rig's lights and their aim Empty, press M › New Collection, and type the name. The checkbox beside the collection then switches the whole rig on or off, and once the file is saved, File › Append › that file › Collection brings the rig into any other file. Render each. Switch between them by hiding and showing the collections. What you should see Three-point gives a shaped subject with an edge; clamshell gives a soft, glowing, nearly shadowless subject; studio gives long clean highlights along the edges of the glossy parts and a graded background. You now own three rigs you can append into any file."},{"t":"Exercise 4.2: identify setups","w":"Lighting, Chapter 4","u":"lighting.html#exercise-4-2","x":"Find five product photos and five portraits online. For each, name the setup from the position of the highlights in the eyes or on the product, and the shape of the shadows. What you should find Catchlights in the eyes show the number and shape of the lights directly, one above and one below for clamshell, one at 45° for the classic single key. Product highlights show the strips and softboxes as bars and blobs. You are reading the lighting plan off the image."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Lighting, Chapter 4","u":"lighting.html#chapter-4-check","x":"Which setup is nearly shadowless, and what geometry gives it that? Answer Clamshell: two soft lights stacked on the camera axis, so every shadow is filled by the other light. In studio product lighting, what are the lights placed for? Answer Their reflections on the subject's edges, more than for shading. How do catchlights tell you a setup? Answer Each light appears as a reflection in the eye at the position it occupies."},{"t":"Inventing a setup","w":"Lighting, Chapter 5","u":"lighting.html#chapter-5","x":"People who make striking product renders are not choosing from a list of named setups. They are placing reflections. Once you see lighting that way, the glowing ribbon stops being a trick and becomes an obvious move. Design lighting by deciding what each glossy surface will reflect. Build a glowing ribbon and other shaped lights, and hide them from the camera. Use black cards and flags to take light away. Start a reference habit that turns admired images into reusable setups."},{"t":"Think in reflections, not lamps","w":"Lighting, Chapter 5","u":"lighting.html#think-in-reflections-not-lamps","x":"A glossy surface is a curved mirror. Every bright shape in the scene appears on it, stretched by the curvature, and every dark region appears as a dark patch. So the question a product photographer asks is not \"where should the lamp go?\" but \"what do I want to see reflected in each part of the object, and where must a bright shape sit so that it lands there?\" The geometry is the law of reflection: a highlight appears where the surface normal points halfway between the camera and the light. On a sphere that puts the reflection of a light at a fixed spot for each light position, which is why moving a light slides the highlight across the sphere. On a long cylinder, a strip light parallel to the axis reflects as a line down its whole length, because every point along that line has the same halfway normal. A long strip light beside a bottle reflects as a thin bright line that runs the full height of the bottle, which is why bottles in advertisements have those clean vertical highlights. A large softbox above a car reflects as a broad bright band across the roof and hood, which is what makes the paint read as glossy. A dark card held near a chrome object reflects as a dark patch that gives the chrome contrast, and a chrome object in a uniformly bright room looks white, not metallic. The working method is to put the camera in place, switch the viewport to Rendered, put a glossy test material on the subject, then move lights while watching the reflections, not the shading. A light that is right for the reflection is often a bad angle for shading, which is why product setups use several lights and why light linking exists."},{"t":"The glowing ribbon","w":"Lighting, Chapter 5","u":"lighting.html#the-glowing-ribbon","x":"The ribbon wrapped around a product is a shaped light: an emissive mesh whose reflection traces the product's contour, often animated so the highlight sweeps along the surface. To build one: Shift + A › Curve › Bézier. Tab into Edit Mode and shape the curve around the subject at the height where you want the highlight; select an end point and press E to extrude more points. Keep the curve a little larger than the subject so it does not intersect it. In the curve's Object Data Properties › Geometry › Bevel, set Depth to a few centimeters so the curve becomes a tube. Resolution 8 keeps it round. A flat ribbon instead of a tube: add a Plane, scale it long and thin, and give it a Curve modifier pointing at the Bézier. Give it a material: Surface › Emission, with a color and a Strength of 10 to 50. Emission alone, not Principled, so it does not catch light from elsewhere and so it has no shading of its own. Object Properties › Visibility › Ray Visibility: untick Camera if the ribbon itself should not appear, and leave Glossy on so its reflection does. In EEVEE, add a thin area light alongside it, because EEVEE does not treat emissive meshes as real lights. Dial Strength until the reflection is clearly brighter than the surroundings without blowing out to white; check in Rendered view with Film › Transparent off so you can see what the object reflects. To animate, keyframe the curve's rotation (hover over the Rotation field and press I) at frame 1 and again, rotated 360°, at frame 120. Or move an Empty along the curve with a Follow Path constraint and parent a short emissive segment to it, so a single bright spot travels the ribbon. The same idea in other shapes: a ring light reflects as a circular halo in anything spherical; two strips on opposite sides give a symmetric pair of edge lines; a gradient panel (an emissive plane with a Gradient Texture into Emission Color) reflects as a soft fade across a flat surface, which is how car paint is made to look deep."},{"t":"Negative fill, flags and gobos","w":"Lighting, Chapter 5","u":"lighting.html#negative-fill-flags-and-gobos","x":"Lighting is also about taking light away. A black plane placed beside the subject (any mesh with a black diffuse material) reflects darkness into glossy parts and blocks fill from reaching matte parts, which restores contrast under a soft setup. Photographers call it negative fill. A flag is the same plane placed between a light and the part of the subject it should not reach, which is how a key is kept off the background. A gobo is a plane with holes, or a spot light with a Blend of 0 shining through a cutout, that projects patterns of light and shadow. Both are ordinary meshes in Blender; there is nothing to enable, and they can be hidden from the camera with Ray Visibility like the ribbon."},{"t":"Where ideas come from","w":"Lighting, Chapter 5","u":"lighting.html#where-ideas-come-from","x":"The designs you see on YouTube come from product photography, where strip lights, ring lights and gradient backdrops have been standard for decades. Looking at advertisements for watches, bottles and cars, and tracing where each highlight must come from, teaches more than any list of setups. Three habits make this work: Collect reference renders and photos you like, and for each one name the key direction, softness and what the glossy parts reflect, using Chapters 1 and 3 . Rebuild one reference per week in a scratch file with a sphere, a cube and a torus. The setups transfer to any subject. Change one light at a time and watch what it does; a setup you understand is one you can modify, which a downloaded preset is not."},{"t":"Exercise 5.1: place a reflection","w":"Lighting, Chapter 5","u":"lighting.html#exercise-5-1","x":"Glossy candy sphere, camera fixed. Decide where on the sphere you want a highlight: say, upper left, a third of the way in from the edge. Add an area light and move it until the highlight sits exactly there. Then change the light to a Rectangle 2 m by 0.1 m and rotate it until the reflection is a vertical bar. What you should see The highlight lands where you decided, and then becomes a bar. You placed a reflection by moving its source, which is the whole skill."},{"t":"Exercise 5.2: the ribbon","w":"Lighting, Chapter 5","u":"lighting.html#exercise-5-2","x":"Build the ribbon from the steps above around the map, hide it from the camera, and render in Cycles. Animate it one full turn over 120 frames and render a few frames. What you should see A bright line tracing the map's contour, moving along it as the ribbon turns, with the ribbon itself invisible."},{"t":"Exercise 5.3: steal a setup","w":"Lighting, Chapter 5","u":"lighting.html#exercise-5-3","x":"Find a watch advertisement. Count the highlights on the bezel and the glass. Rebuild them on a torus and a disk using strips and a softbox. What you should find Usually one large soft source above and two thin strips to the sides; the \"expensive\" look is three well-placed reflections and a dark surround, nothing more."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Lighting, Chapter 5","u":"lighting.html#chapter-5-check","x":"Where does a highlight appear on a glossy surface? Answer Where the surface normal points halfway between the camera and the light. Why use Emission rather than Principled on the ribbon? Answer So it emits only and does not pick up shading or reflections of its own. What does negative fill do? Answer Takes light away: reflects darkness into glossy parts and blocks fill from matte parts, restoring contrast. Why do product setups often use several lights when one would shade the object well? Answer Each light is placed for one reflection; the shading is a side effect."},{"t":"Lighting for scientific figures","w":"Lighting, Chapter 6","u":"lighting.html#chapter-6","x":"A figure has a different job from an advertisement: the reader must be able to see every domain, compare panels, and trust the colors. That rules out most of the drama in Chapter 4 and leaves a small set of priorities. State the five priorities that separate figure lighting from cinematic lighting. Build and save a figure rig that gives identical light in every panel. Aim a rig at the part of a structure the figure is about."},{"t":"The priorities","w":"Lighting, Chapter 6","u":"lighting.html#the-priorities","x":"Shape must read everywhere. No part of the structure should fall into black shadow. Medium contrast at most, with a fill at half the key rather than a third, and World Strength around 0.3 so cavities stay visible. A domain hidden in shadow is a domain the reader cannot check. No color cast. Lights stay white (Temperature 5600 K or Color pure white), the world light neutral gray. Colored lights change the chain colors the legend depends on, and a warm key over a blue chain makes a purple that is in neither the legend nor the data. Same light in every panel. Build the rig once, save it, and append it into each figure file, so two structures side by side differ only in the structure. Light the structure, not the camera: if the rig is parented to the camera, every panel is lit from the same screen direction, which is what you usually want for comparisons; if it is parented to the structure, rotating the structure rotates the light with it and the shading stays fixed to the molecule. Depth without darkness. Ambient occlusion, a soft contact shadow, and a slight rim do the work that deep shadows do in cinematic images. The Ambient Occlusion material in Molecular Nodes gives this at almost no cost, and the Fast GI option in Cycles' Light Paths adds occlusion to any material. Transparent background. Film › Transparent, so the figure sits on the page without a box. Remember that whatever the world color is still lights and reflects in the subject, so keep it neutral."},{"t":"A default rig","w":"Lighting, Chapter 6","u":"lighting.html#a-default-rig","x":"Key: Area, Disk, Size equal to the width of the subject, 45° to the camera's left and 45° above, Track To the subject. Fill: Area, Disk, twice the key's size, 45° to the right, slightly below the camera's height, half the key's power. Rim: Area, Rectangle, narrow and tall, behind the subject on the key side, at about the key's power, light-linked to the subject if the model and map should get different edges. World: neutral gray, Strength 0.3. Power: whatever makes the brightest part of the subject just short of white, found with Film › Exposure rather than by editing all three lights. For orthographic panels and schematic styles, a single Sun light from the camera's upper left with Angle 10° plus the world light is simpler and gives identical shading at every scale, because a sun's brightness does not depend on distance."},{"t":"Rigs that follow the camera","w":"Lighting, Chapter 6","u":"lighting.html#rigs-that-follow-the-camera","x":"Parent the three lights and their target Empty to the camera (select the lights, then the camera last, Ctrl + P › Object). Now every camera angle gets the same lighting from the viewer's point of view: the key always comes from the upper left of the frame. This is the right choice for a multi-panel figure with different orientations, because the reader's eye expects light from a consistent direction across the page."},{"t":"What to show first","w":"Lighting, Chapter 6","u":"lighting.html#what-to-show-first","x":"Aim the key at the part of the structure the figure is about: the epitope, the interface, the ligand. The eye goes to the brightest, highest-contrast region, so if the interesting part sits in fill light while a loop on the far side catches the key, the figure points the wrong way. Rotate the rig, not the structure, so that the orientation stays consistent with the other panels. A second, subtle way to point: a slightly stronger rim on the far side of the region of interest draws its outline against the rest."},{"t":"Exercise 6.1: build and save the figure rig","w":"Lighting, Chapter 6","u":"lighting.html#exercise-6-1","x":"Build the default rig around the map, parent it to the camera, and put the lights and the Empty in a collection named Lights_Figure . Save the file. Open a new file, import a different structure, and File › Append › your file › Collection › Lights_Figure . Render both. What you should see Identical lighting on two different structures, from the same screen direction. That is what consistency across panels means in practice."},{"t":"Exercise 6.2: point the figure","w":"Lighting, Chapter 6","u":"lighting.html#exercise-6-2","x":"On a two-chain structure, render with the key aimed at chain A, then at chain B, without moving the camera. What you should see The chain that catches the key is the one the eye goes to first. The figure's subject changed without any change to the structure or the camera."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Lighting, Chapter 6","u":"lighting.html#chapter-6-check","x":"Why do figure lights stay white? Answer Colored light shifts the chain colors the legend depends on. What does parenting the rig to the camera guarantee? Answer That every panel is lit from the same screen direction regardless of the structure's orientation. Where do depth cues come from when deep shadows are not allowed? Answer Ambient occlusion, a soft contact shadow and a light rim."},{"t":"A build procedure","w":"Lighting, Chapter 7","u":"lighting.html#chapter-7","x":"Light one source at a time, on a neutral material, and judge each addition before the next. Lights added all at once cannot be told apart afterwards, and the usual result of adding four lights together is three of them fighting. Light a scene one source at a time so every light's contribution is known. Use clay override, solo viewing and exposure as diagnostic tools. Save a rig so the next project starts lit."},{"t":"The procedure","w":"Lighting, Chapter 7","u":"lighting.html#the-procedure","x":"Lock the camera first. Lighting depends on the viewpoint; a rim that works from one angle is a flare from another. Frame the shot, then do not touch the camera until the lights are done. Pin the view with N › View › Lock Camera to View unticked, so an accidental orbit does not move it. Start in the dark. World Strength 0, every existing light disabled (the eye icon in the Outliner for the viewport, the camera icon for renders). Rendered view should be black. If it is not, something is emitting; find it before continuing. Use a clay material while placing lights. Put a mid-gray Principled BSDF with Roughness 0.5 on everything, or set Material Override in View Layer Properties (Cycles). Color and gloss hide lighting mistakes; gray clay shows them. The override is one dropdown and leaves your real materials untouched. Add the key. Area, Disk. Give it a Track To constraint aimed at an Empty on the subject (Ctrl + T › Track To with the Empty selected last), then move the light with G and watch the shadow side. Set its size for the softness you want, then its Power until the brightest part is just below white. Everything after this is relative to the key. Judge, then add the fill. Only if the shadow side needs opening. Larger than the key, opposite it, start at half the key's Power. If a second shadow appears, enlarge the fill or move it closer to the camera axis. Add the rim. Behind the subject, opposite the key. Hide the fill and key for a moment (click their eye icons) to see exactly what the rim alone draws, then bring them back. A rim that draws a line wider than a few percent of the subject is too strong or too far forward. Now turn on the world at 0.2 to 0.3. It lifts everything a little and gives glossy parts something to reflect. Switch to the real materials and revisit the reflections: move the key or add a strip until the glossy parts show a bright shape where you want it. This is the step where the material and the light are tuned together. Set exposure once. Render Properties › Film › Exposure moves everything together; use it instead of editing three powers. Render a test at half resolution (Output Properties › Resolution % at 50) with 64 samples. Noise, fireflies and over-bright highlights show up here before you commit to a full render. Save the rig. Put the lights and their target Empty in a collection named Lights_Figure , save the file, and append that collection into later projects (File › Append › the .blend › Collection)."},{"t":"Viewport habits","w":"Lighting, Chapter 7","u":"lighting.html#viewport-habits","x":"Dragging the yellow dot at the end of a light's direction line aims it without a constraint. N › View › Lock Camera to View, with the viewport in camera view, lets you orbit the camera to check that a rim is not flaring into the lens; untick it afterwards. The Outliner's eye icons solo a light in the viewport, which is faster than editing powers to see one light's contribution. And the Viewport Shading popover in Rendered view has Scene Lights and Scene World toggles: turning them off shows the materials under Blender's studio light, which tells you whether a problem is in the rig or in the materials."},{"t":"Exercise 7.1: light the map from black","w":"Lighting, Chapter 7","u":"lighting.html#exercise-7-1","x":"Follow the eleven steps on the map with the clay override on, taking a screenshot after steps 4, 5, 6 and 7. Lay the four screenshots side by side. What you should see Form appears at step 4, the shadows open at step 5, an edge appears at step 6, and the whole image lifts slightly at step 7. If any step made the image worse, that light is wrong; fix it before the next."},{"t":"Exercise 7.2: the override test","w":"Lighting, Chapter 7","u":"lighting.html#exercise-7-2","x":"On a finished scene, switch Material Override on and off. What you should see If the clay version looks well lit and the real version does not, the materials need work. If the clay version looks badly lit, the rig needs work. The test separates the two in one click."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Lighting, Chapter 7","u":"lighting.html#chapter-7-check","x":"Why start from a black scene? Answer So that each light's contribution is seen alone as it is added. What is the purpose of the clay material? Answer Color and gloss hide lighting mistakes; neutral gray shows shading and shadows plainly. When should Film › Exposure be set? Answer Last, after the ratios between lights are right; it scales everything together."},{"t":"Units, exposure and color","w":"Lighting, Chapter 8","u":"lighting.html#chapter-8","x":"Blender's lights are physical, which makes their numbers feel arbitrary until you know what they measure. Convert between light power, distance and brightness at the subject. Use Film › Exposure correctly and know what it does not change. Choose a color temperature and a view transform for figures."},{"t":"Watts and the inverse square","w":"Lighting, Chapter 8","u":"lighting.html#watts-and-the-inverse-square","x":"Point, spot and area lights are set in watts of radiant power, the energy they emit as visible light, not the electrical rating printed on a bulb ( Light Objects ). A bright household bulb is about 2 to 3 W in these units. Studio renders use 100 to 5,000 W because the lights are large and far away, and because the light reaching the subject falls with the square of the distance: Distance from subject Light reaching it, relative to 1 m 1 m 1 2 m 1/4 5 m 1/25 10 m 1/100 50 m 1/2,500 Moving a light twice as far needs four times the power for the same brightness. Sun lights escape this: their Strength is in W/m² at the subject, 1000 for clear sky and 500 for overcast, and distance does not matter. Area lights also spread their power over their surface, so with Normalize on, doubling the size keeps the total the same and makes each part of the panel dimmer. Molecular Nodes imports at 1 nm per meter, so a 20 nm structure is 20 m across. Lights that fit the scene end up 20 to 50 m away and need thousands of watts, or an Exposure of +5 to +8 on each light. Neither is wrong; it is the arithmetic above. A quick rule: multiply the power you would use at 1 m by the square of the distance in meters."},{"t":"Exposure","w":"Lighting, Chapter 8","u":"lighting.html#exposure","x":"Render Properties › Film › Exposure scales the whole image by 2 to that value, the way a camera's exposure does. Set the light ratios first, then use exposure to place the brightest part of the subject just below white. Changing exposure does not change noise or the balance between lights, and it does not change which highlights clip, because clipping is decided by the view transform after exposure is applied. Per-light Exposure, by contrast, changes one light's share of the ratio."},{"t":"Color temperature","w":"Lighting, Chapter 8","u":"lighting.html#color-temperature","x":"A light's Temperature setting gives natural colors without picking them by hand: 2700 K candle-warm, 3200 K tungsten, 5600 K daylight, 6500 K overcast, 8000 K blue shade. A warm key with a cool fill is a standard way to separate planes, but for figures keep both at 5600 K so the material colors stay true. The world light's color counts too; a bluish world is a cool fill."},{"t":"Color management","w":"Lighting, Chapter 8","u":"lighting.html#color-management","x":"The View Transform in Render Properties › Color Management decides how bright values become pixels. AgX, the default, compresses highlights gracefully and desaturates bright colors, which makes glossy surfaces look natural but shifts saturated chain colors. Standard maps values directly, keeps colors exact, and clips highlights to white. For figures where the legend colors must match, use Standard and control highlights with light power. The Look setting adds contrast curves on top; leave it at None for figures. Filmic, the older transform, is still available and sits between the two. The World's Strength also matters more than its small number suggests, because it arrives from every direction at once. At Strength 1 with a light gray color it alone lights a scene adequately; 0.2 to 0.3 is a fill, not a key."},{"t":"Exercise 8.1: the inverse square by hand","w":"Lighting, Chapter 8","u":"lighting.html#exercise-8-1","x":"One point light at 1 m from the sphere, Power 10 W. Note how bright the sphere is. Move the light to 2 m. Raise Power until the sphere matches. Then 4 m. What you should find About 40 W at 2 m and about 160 W at 4 m. The numbers quadruple each time the distance doubles."},{"t":"Exercise 8.2: AgX against Standard","w":"Lighting, Chapter 8","u":"lighting.html#exercise-8-2","x":"Render a chain-colored model under the figure rig with View Transform AgX, then Standard. Use the eyedropper in the Image Editor (right-click and hold on a pixel) to read the color of a chain in each. What you should see Under AgX the chain is paler and slightly shifted from the color set in the material; under Standard it matches, and the brightest highlights are clipped flat white. For a legend, Standard is right; for a cover, AgX."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Lighting, Chapter 8","u":"lighting.html#chapter-8-check","x":"A light at 3 m needs how much more power than at 1 m for the same brightness? Answer Nine times. What does Film › Exposure leave unchanged? Answer The ratios between lights and the amount of noise. Which view transform preserves the material's colors exactly? Answer Standard. Why is the world light stronger than its number suggests? Answer It arrives from every direction at once rather than from one panel."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","w":"Lighting, Chapter 9","u":"lighting.html#chapter-9","x":"Most lighting problems are one of three kinds: a light in the wrong place, a light the wrong size for its distance, or a setting such as visibility, exposure or view transform. The table sorts the common symptoms into those kinds. Symptom Likely cause Fix Everything is dark Lights too far for their power; world at 0 Raise light Exposure by +3 at a time, or move lights closer; then set Film › Exposure Flat, no shape Key too close to the camera axis, or fill as strong as the key Move the key to 45°/45°; halve the fill Harsh, every bump casts a shadow Key too small or too far Enlarge the key (Normalize on) or move it closer Two shadows Fill too strong or too small Enlarge the fill, lower its power, move it nearer the camera axis Shadow side is black No fill, world at 0 World Strength 0.3 or a large fill at half the key Glossy parts look dead or black Nothing bright for them to reflect Add a large area light or a strip where its reflection lands; raise World Strength Highlights blow out to white Light too strong or too small for the gloss Lower Power, enlarge the light, or switch View Transform to AgX Grainy render, bright speckles Small bright lights with glossy surfaces Enlarge lights, raise Max Samples, keep Denoise on, set Light Paths › Clamping › Indirect Light to 10 Rim light flares the image Rim aimed into the lens Move it further behind the subject, narrow it, or light-link it to the subject only Colors look wrong A colored light or world, or AgX desaturation Lights white at 5600 K, world gray, View Transform Standard Changes do nothing Viewport in Solid or Material Preview; light hidden for render Z › Rendered; check the camera icon in the Outliner Emissive ribbon shows no reflection in EEVEE EEVEE does not sample emissive meshes as lights Add a thin area light along the ribbon, or render in Cycles Emissive ribbon appears in the frame Ray Visibility › Camera still on Untick Camera in Object Properties › Visibility (Cycles) Lighting differs between panels Rig rebuilt per file Append one saved Lights_Figure collection into every file Match a lighting symptom to its cause without trial and error. Know which problems are fixed by moving a light, which by resizing it, and which by changing a setting."},{"t":"Exercise 9.1: diagnose from screenshots","w":"Lighting, Chapter 9","u":"lighting.html#exercise-9-1","x":"Take the four screenshots from Exercise 7.1 and, for each, cover the lights in the viewport and look only at the render. Write down which lights are on and where they are, from the shadows and highlights alone, then uncover and check. What you should find By the fourth screenshot you can name every light from its evidence. Reading a render this way is the same skill as diagnosing a bad one."},{"t":"Exercise 9.2: make the mistakes","w":"Lighting, Chapter 9","u":"lighting.html#exercise-9-2","x":"Starting from a correct figure rig, produce five rows of the table on purpose: flat, two shadows, dead gloss, blown highlights, flaring rim. Fix each using only the fix in the table. What you should see Each fix returns the image to the correct version with one change. Mistakes you have made deliberately are the ones you recognize fastest."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Lighting, Chapter 9","u":"lighting.html#chapter-9-check","x":"The image is too dark and raising Film › Exposure makes it grainy. What is wrong? Answer The lights are too weak for their distance; raise their power or move them closer, so the renderer has more light to sample. Everything looks flat. Which decision from Chapter 3 is most likely wrong? Answer The key direction: too close to the camera axis, or the fill as strong as the key. The glossy map shows no sheen under a strong key. What is missing? Answer Something large and bright for it to reflect: a bigger key or a brighter world."},{"t":"Sources","w":"Lighting, Chapter 9","u":"lighting.html#sources","x":"Light Objects , Light Linking (Blender Manual)"},{"t":"Shaders: the whole menu, from zero","w":"Shaders","u":"shaders.html","x":"This part is a short book. Chapters 1 and 2 explain what a shader is and the handful of things light can do at a surface. Chapters 3 to 6 go through every shader node Blender offers and how they combine. Chapter 7 explains why the Principled BSDF is where to start, and the rest covers what feeds a shader, the engines, stylized looks, and a chooser."},{"t":"What a shader is","w":"Shaders, Chapter 1","u":"shaders.html#chapter-1","x":"A shader is a rule that tells the renderer what a surface does with the light that reaches it. Everything else in this part is detail on that one sentence. Use the words material, shader and texture correctly and know which one you are editing. Name the three sockets of the Material Output and what plugs into each. Describe, in one paragraph, how a renderer turns a shader into pixels and why renders start noisy."},{"t":"Three words that get mixed up","w":"Shaders, Chapter 1","u":"shaders.html#three-words-that-get-mixed-up","x":"Material is what you assign to an object. It is a container: a node tree plus a few settings. One object can carry several materials in slots, one per face region. In the Outliner, materials appear under the mesh data, and in the Blender File view they are listed as data-blocks that exist independently of any object. Shader is a node inside that tree that outputs a shader socket, the green one. It describes a way of scattering light. Most are named BSDF, for bidirectional scattering distribution function: given a direction light arrives from and a direction it leaves in, the function says how much goes that way. \"Bidirectional\" means it works the same backwards, which is what lets a renderer trace rays from the camera instead of from the lights. Diffuse, Glossy and Principled are all BSDFs. Texture is a pattern of values across the surface, from an image or a formula. Textures feed numbers and colors into shader inputs; they do not scatter light themselves. A Noise Texture into Roughness is a texture driving a shader. So a material is a tree whose last node is a shader, and whose other nodes supply the shader's inputs. When someone says \"the texture looks wrong\" they usually mean the material; when they say \"change the shader\" they mean swapping the final node."},{"t":"The Material Output node","w":"Shaders, Chapter 1","u":"shaders.html#the-material-output-node","x":"Every material ends at a Material Output node with three sockets: Surface takes a shader and describes the skin of the object. This is where almost all work happens. Volume takes a volume shader and describes the inside: fog, smoke, or the colored interior of a gel. It is evaluated along the path light takes through the object, so it has no effect on a flat plane with no interior. Displacement takes a vector that moves the surface itself, for relief that changes the silhouette. Nothing happens unless the material's Settings allow displacement (Chapter 9 ). The node also has a Target menu (All, Cycles, EEVEE) that lets one material carry different trees for different engines. Only one output node is active at a time; the active one has a highlighted header."},{"t":"How the renderer uses it","w":"Shaders, Chapter 1","u":"shaders.html#how-the-renderer-uses-it","x":"In Cycles, the renderer fires rays from the camera into the scene. Where a ray hits a surface, it asks the material's shader: what fraction of light from each direction reaches this ray? The shader answers by sampling, that is, by picking one plausible direction for the incoming light according to its own distribution, and the renderer follows a new ray that way to find what light arrives from there. One sample is one guess. Thousands of such samples per pixel average into the final color, which is why renders start noisy and clean up over time, and why materials whose distributions are narrow and bright, like a glossy surface under a small light, are the noisiest: most samples miss the light. EEVEE instead draws the surface the way a game does, in one pass with approximations: shadows from shadow maps, reflections from screen-space data and probes, subsurface as a blur. It asks the shader the same question but takes shortcuts in the answer. The shader nodes are the same; only the honesty of the answer differs (Chapter 10 ). Two consequences shape how you work. First, a shader is evaluated per sample at a specific point, so any input can vary across the surface: plug a texture into Roughness and each point gets its own. Second, the renderer trusts the shader. If a shader says a surface reflects 150% of the light it receives, Cycles renders that, and the image glows unphysically. The built-in shaders are written to conserve energy; combining them badly (Chapter 6 ) is how you break that."},{"t":"Exercise 1.1: find the three words in the interface","w":"Shaders, Chapter 1","u":"shaders.html#exercise-1-1","x":"Select the sphere, open the Material tab, and name the material lab_sphere . Open the Shader Editor (Shading workspace). Identify the Material Output, the shader node connected to Surface, and add a Noise Texture connected to Roughness. Switch the Material Output's Target to EEVEE and back to All. What you should see The material name at the top of the tab, the node tree below it, and the Noise Texture driving only roughness while the color stays constant. You have edited a material, a shader and a texture, and you know which was which."},{"t":"Exercise 1.2: watch sampling","w":"Shaders, Chapter 1","u":"shaders.html#exercise-1-2","x":"In Rendered view with Cycles, set Render Properties › Sampling › Viewport › Max Samples to 1, then 16, then 256. What you should see At 1 sample the image is a field of noise with the shapes barely visible; at 16 it is recognizable but grainy; at 256 it is clean. Each pixel is averaging more guesses."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 1","u":"shaders.html#chapter-1-check","x":"What does the Volume socket do on an object with no interior, like a single plane? Answer Nothing; volume shaders act along the path through the inside. Why does a glossy surface under a small light render noisier than a matte one? Answer Its distribution is narrow, so most samples miss the light and only a few are bright. Which word names a node that outputs the green socket? Answer Shader."},{"t":"Six things light can do at a surface","w":"Shaders, Chapter 2","u":"shaders.html#chapter-2","x":"Every shader node is a combination of six behaviors. Learn the six and the node list in the next chapters becomes a set of labels rather than a list to memorize. Name the six behaviors and give a real-world example of each. Explain energy conservation and why a physically based shader cannot be \"too bright\". Read any shader node's name as a combination of the six."},{"t":"The six","w":"Shaders, Chapter 2","u":"shaders.html#the-six","x":"Diffuse reflection. Light enters the top layer, bounces around among pigment particles, and leaves in a random direction. The result is the flat, matte color of chalk, paper and clay. It depends on how squarely the surface faces the light and not at all on where the viewer stands: walk around a sheet of paper and its brightness does not change. This view-independence is why diffuse surfaces are the easiest to render and the hardest to make look interesting. Specular reflection. Light bounces off the surface like a mirror. On a perfectly smooth surface it leaves at exactly the mirror angle; on a rough one the surface is a field of tiny mirrors (microfacets) at slightly different tilts, so the reflection spreads into a blurred highlight. Roughness is the width of that spread: 0 is a mirror, 1 is so scattered that no highlight is visible. Unlike diffuse, specular depends on where you stand, which is why highlights move when you move. Fresnel. How much light reflects specularly depends on the angle you look from. Glass reflects about 4% head-on and nearly 100% at a grazing angle, which is why a lake mirrors the far shore but not the stones at your feet. The index of refraction (IOR) sets the head-on value: 1.33 for water, 1.45 for glass, 1.5 for most plastics, and the head-on reflectance is ((n−1)/(n+1))², which for 1.5 is 4%. Every physically based shader has Fresnel built in; it is what makes edges brighter than centers and what makes a plastic sphere's rim catch the light. Transmission. Light passes into the object. If the object is clear it is refracted, bending at the surface by an amount set by the IOR, which is why a straw looks broken in a glass of water. Roughness applies here too: rough transmission is frosted glass. Light that enters must also leave, through a second surface, so a transmissive object needs a closed mesh with correct normals to render properly. Scattering inside. Light enters, bounces around below the surface, and leaves somewhere else. Over a short distance that is subsurface scattering, the soft glow of wax, milk and skin. Over a long distance, through a thin medium, it is a volume: fog, smoke, colored liquid. Absorption, where the medium swallows some wavelengths along the way, is what makes thick jelly darker than thin jelly, and it is why red wine is pink in a thin glass and nearly black in a bottle. Emission. The surface gives off its own light. It needs no lamp, and in Cycles it lights other objects. Emission is the only behavior that adds energy; the other five only redistribute what arrived."},{"t":"Two rules that bind them","w":"Shaders, Chapter 2","u":"shaders.html#two-rules-that-bind-them","x":"Energy conservation. What reflects, transmits and absorbs must add up to what arrived. A real surface cannot reflect 60% diffusely and 60% specularly at the same time. Physically based shaders divide the light between behaviors, so raising one lowers the others. That is why the Principled BSDF's Metallic input removes the diffuse color as it rises, why Transmission at 1 leaves no diffuse at all, and why a white Base Color at 1.0 is slightly unrealistic: nothing in nature reflects 100%, and fresh snow manages about 90%. Layering. Behaviors stack in a definite order. A clear coat sits over the base; sheen sits over the coat; the base is one of diffuse, metal, subsurface or transmission, or a blend. Light that the top layer reflects never reaches the layer below, so a strong coat slightly darkens the body. The Principled BSDF is exactly this stack with a dial on each layer (Chapter 7 ). With these six words, read any node name: Glossy is behavior 2 alone, Glass is 2 plus 4 with Fresnel deciding the split, Subsurface Scattering is 5, Emission is 6, and Principled is all of them in their correct order. Every shader node is one of six behaviors; Principled stacks four of them Principled BSDF Reflect, transmit, scatter and emit, layered in physical order, each with its own dial Reflect Diffuse BSDF Glossy BSDF Metallic BSDF Toon BSDF Sheen BSDF Specular BSDF (EEVEE) Transmit Transparent BSDF Refraction BSDF Glass BSDF Translucent BSDF Scatter inside Subsurface Scattering Volume Absorption Volume Scatter Principled Volume Volume Coefficients Emit Emission Background (world) Combine Mix Shader: blend two by a factor Add Shader: sum two, for emission on top Special Holdout: cuts a transparent hole Ray Portal BSDF: teleports rays Hair BSDF, Principled Hair BSDF Blender's shader nodes grouped by behavior The four behavior groups under the Principled bar are what it contains. Combine and Special sit apart because they do not scatter light themselves: they route it."},{"t":"Exercise 2.1: see the six on real objects","w":"Shaders, Chapter 2","u":"shaders.html#exercise-2-1","x":"Gather a sheet of paper, a spoon, a glass of water, a candle, a bar of soap, and a phone screen that is on. Assign each to the behavior it shows most, and look for the second behavior in each (the paper's faint sheen, the spoon's slight diffuse color where it is scratched). What you should find Paper is diffuse, the spoon is specular with metal tint, the water is transmission with Fresnel at the rim, the candle is subsurface, the soap is subsurface with a weak coat, the screen is emission. Nothing shows only one behavior."},{"t":"Exercise 2.2: Fresnel on a sphere","w":"Shaders, Chapter 2","u":"shaders.html#exercise-2-2","x":"Sphere with a Glossy BSDF at Roughness 0.1, white, and Shade Smooth on (in Object Mode, right-click the sphere in the viewport › Shade Smooth). Add a Diffuse BSDF (Shift + A › Shader › Diffuse BSDF) with a dark Color, and a Mix Shader (Shift + A › Shader › Mix Shader). Wire Diffuse BSDF › BSDF → the Mix Shader's first Shader input, Glossy BSDF › BSDF → its second, and Mix Shader › Shader → Material Output › Surface. Then add Shift + A › Input › Fresnel and connect Fresnel › Factor to the Mix Shader's Factor. Render, then set the Fresnel node's IOR to 1.1, then 2.5. What you should see At 1.5 a bright rim and a dark center with a small highlight; at 1.1 almost no reflection anywhere; at 2.5 strong reflection across the whole sphere. IOR sets how far in from the edge the reflection reaches."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 2","u":"shaders.html#chapter-2-check","x":"Which behavior does not depend on where the viewer stands? Answer Diffuse reflection. What is the head-on reflectance of a surface with IOR 1.5? Answer About 4%. Why does raising Metallic on the Principled BSDF make the diffuse color disappear? Answer Energy conservation: the light claimed by the metal reflection is no longer available for diffuse. Which behavior adds energy to the scene rather than redistributing it? Answer Emission."},{"t":"The reflection shaders","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#chapter-3","x":"These nodes do one thing each: send light back out without entering the object. They are the atoms that the Principled BSDF is built from, and they are what you reach for when you want exactly one behavior, cheaply and under full control ( Shader nodes ). Describe what each reflection shader models, what its inputs do, and when it beats the Principled BSDF. Build a plastic by hand from Diffuse and Glossy and understand why it matches the Principled default. Pick the right microfacet distribution and know when anisotropy applies."},{"t":"Diffuse BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#diffuse-bsdf","x":"Matte reflection only: behavior 1 from Chapter 2 , nothing else. Inputs are Color, Roughness and Normal. Roughness here is not the specular roughness of other nodes: at 0 the node is the standard Lambert model, and higher values switch to the Oren-Nayar model, which flattens the shading the way rough materials like clay and concrete do, brightening the terminator slightly. There is no highlight, no Fresnel and no edge brightening, so a Diffuse surface looks like chalk under any light. It renders faster than any other shader and converges with less noise, which makes it the right choice for large background objects, for clay test renders while lighting, and as one half of a Mix when you want to add gloss separately with a Glossy node."},{"t":"Glossy BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#glossy-bsdf","x":"Specular reflection only: a mirror blurred by Roughness. Color tints the reflection, which is what a metal does and a plastic does not; a plastic's highlight is white, so Glossy with a colored tint is already halfway to a metal. Distribution chooses the microfacet model: GGX is the default and has long highlight tails that look natural; Multiscatter GGX adds the light that bounces between microfacets, which plain GGX loses, so rough surfaces stay bright instead of darkening; Beckmann has tighter highlights; Ashikhmin-Shirley is an older model kept for compatibility. Anisotropy stretches the highlight along a direction, as on brushed metal, a vinyl record or a cooking pot, and Rotation turns that direction; it needs a Tangent input or a UV map to know which way the brushing runs. On its own, Glossy with a white color at Roughness 0 is a perfect mirror; mixed with Diffuse by a Fresnel factor, it is the classic hand-built plastic."},{"t":"Metallic BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#metallic-bsdf","x":"A conductor model: specular reflection with the physically correct way metals tint their reflections. Metals reflect their color most strongly at normal incidence and shift toward white at grazing angles, the opposite of the colored-edge behavior of a Glossy node with a tint. The node offers either artist-friendly Base Color plus Edge Tint, or the physical n and k constants published for real metals (gold, copper, aluminium have tables). It replaces the Glossy-with-color habit for real metals. For biology it is a curiosity; for gold nanoparticles or an electrode it is the right node."},{"t":"Toon BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#toon-bsdf","x":"A diffuse or glossy reflection with a hard edge: Size sets how wide the lit region is and Smooth how soft its boundary. In Diffuse mode it gives a flat lit zone and a flat shadow zone; in Glossy mode it gives a hard-edged highlight disc. It gives cel-shaded, flat-banded lighting without any node tricks, in both engines, and it reacts to your real lights, so moving the key moves the band. It ignores Fresnel and is not energy-conserving in the physical sense, which is the point. Chapter 11 uses it."},{"t":"Sheen BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#sheen-bsdf","x":"The reflection of fine fibers standing up from a surface, which catch light at grazing angles: velvet, felt, peach fuzz, and dust on anything. It is bright at the edges and dark in the middle, the opposite of Glossy. Roughness sets how soft the rim is; Distribution offers Ashikhmin for the classic velvet look and Microfiber for a more physically based one. It is rarely used alone; usually it rides on top of another shader through a Mix, or through the Sheen panel of the Principled BSDF. Earlier Blender versions called it Velvet."},{"t":"Specular BSDF (EEVEE only)","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#specular-bsdf-eevee-only","x":"An alternative to the Principled BSDF that takes a Specular Color instead of an IOR, matching the specular-glossiness workflow of some game engines and older texture sets. Cycles ignores it and renders the object black. Unless you are matching a texture set authored for that workflow, there is no reason to use it, and the Principled BSDF's IOR Level and Tint inputs cover the same ground."},{"t":"Reading the family","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#reading-the-family","x":"Diffuse and Glossy are the two halves of almost every non-metal in the world; a Mix of the two with a Fresnel or Layer Weight factor was how every material was built before the Principled BSDF existed, and seeing it built that way once makes the Principled node's inputs obvious. Metallic is the conductor version of Glossy. Toon and Sheen are the stylized and the fuzzy special cases, and Specular is a compatibility node. The Add menu lists only the shaders the current render engine supports: Toon, Sheen and the two hair shaders appear only while the engine (Render tab › Render Engine) is Cycles, and Specular only while it is EEVEE."},{"t":"Exercise 3.1: plastic by hand","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#exercise-3-1","x":"On a sphere: Diffuse BSDF (Color mid-red, Roughness 0) into the first socket of a Mix Shader; Glossy BSDF (Color white, Roughness 0.3, GGX) into the second; Fresnel (IOR 1.5) into Fac; Mix into Surface. On a second sphere: a Principled BSDF with the same red, Roughness 0.3, IOR 1.5. Render them side by side, then set the Glossy's Distribution to Multiscatter GGX and its Roughness to 0.8. What you should see The two spheres are nearly identical, which tells you what the Principled default contains. At Roughness 0.8 the plain GGX version goes visibly darker than the Multiscatter one, which is the lost inter-facet light."},{"t":"Exercise 3.2: brushed metal","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#exercise-3-2","x":"A Metallic BSDF on the cube, Base Color gold, Roughness 0.3. Then a Glossy BSDF with gold Color, Anisotropy 0.8, Rotation 0 and 0.25. What you should see The Metallic cube has gold centers and whiter edges; the Glossy cube has gold throughout; and the anisotropic version stretches each highlight into a streak that turns 90° when Rotation changes by 0.25."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 3","u":"shaders.html#chapter-3-check","x":"Which shader's Roughness input does not control a highlight, and what does it do instead? Answer Diffuse BSDF; it flattens the shading toward the Oren-Nayar model. Why does a Glossy node with a colored tint look wrong for most metals? Answer Real metals go whiter at grazing angles; a tinted Glossy stays colored everywhere. What is wrong with using Specular BSDF in a scene that will be rendered in Cycles? Answer Cycles does not support it; the object renders black. Which distribution keeps rough surfaces from darkening? Answer Multiscatter GGX."},{"t":"The transmission, scattering and special shaders","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#chapter-4","x":"These nodes let light into the object, or do something other than scatter it. Tell Transparent, Refraction, Glass and Translucent apart and choose the right one. Set up subsurface scattering with the right method and scale. Use Emission as a light, Holdout as a mask, and know what the remaining nodes are for."},{"t":"Transparent BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#transparent-bsdf","x":"Light passes straight through without bending or tinting, as if the surface were not there. Color multiplies what passes, so a red Transparent acts like a colored gel. It is not glass: no reflection, no refraction, no Fresnel rim. Its uses are cutouts (a leaf texture's alpha into a Mix between Transparent and the leaf material), ghosted overlays, and fading objects in and out. Because the ray continues undisturbed, Cycles counts transparent surfaces separately from other bounces: Light Paths › Max Bounces › Transparent sets how many layers a ray may pass through, and a stack of ghosted maps can hit that limit and turn black. The Principled BSDF's Alpha input is a built-in Mix with Transparent, so you rarely add this node by hand any more."},{"t":"Refraction BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#refraction-bsdf","x":"Transmission only: light enters and bends by the IOR, with Roughness frosting it. There is no reflection at all, so a Refraction surface alone looks wrong, like glass with its highlights erased. It exists as a building block, to be mixed with Glossy by a Fresnel factor, and it is useful on its own in one case: an object seen from inside, or a flat window pane where the reflection is handled by a separate surface."},{"t":"Glass BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#glass-bsdf","x":"Refraction plus Glossy, with Fresnel deciding the split automatically: reflective at grazing angles, transmissive head-on. This is real glass, water and clear plastic in one node, with Color, Roughness and IOR. It is the node to use for a glass object when you do not need the Principled BSDF's other layers, and it converges a little faster. Glass needs a closed mesh with outward normals: a single plane with Glass is seen as entering an infinitely thick slab and renders dark. Thin-walled glass, like a bubble or a window, is available through the Thin Wall option of the Principled node rather than here."},{"t":"Translucent BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#translucent-bsdf","x":"Diffuse transmission: light enters, scatters, and exits the far side with no memory of its direction, as through paper, a leaf or a lampshade. It is like Diffuse but for the back face: the side away from the light glows evenly. A Mix of Diffuse and Translucent is the standard leaf and curtain material. It differs from subsurface scattering in that light does not travel sideways inside the object, so a Translucent sphere lit from the left glows on its right side but shows no softening of the shadow edge."},{"t":"Subsurface Scattering","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#subsurface-scattering","x":"Light enters, wanders below the surface over a distance set by Radius and Scale, and leaves somewhere nearby. This softens shading, lets thin parts glow when lit from behind, and gives wax, marble, milk and skin their look. Radius is three numbers, one per color channel, which is how skin gets its reddish glow: red travels further than green and blue. Scale multiplies all three. Random Walk is the accurate method and the default; it traces real paths inside the mesh and needs a closed mesh. Christensen-Burley is a faster approximation that can be less noisy in dense media and tolerates open meshes. The Principled BSDF contains the same model in its Subsurface panel; the standalone node is for mixing by hand. IOR and Anisotropy refine the Random Walk model; the defaults are right for most organic materials."},{"t":"Emission","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#emission","x":"The surface gives off light of a Color at a Strength. Strength 1 means the surface shows exactly its color regardless of lighting, which is the flat-illustration look. Higher strengths make it a lamp: in Cycles an emissive mesh lights its surroundings and reflects in glossy objects, which is the basis of the glowing-ribbon technique in the Lighting part . Strength is per unit area, so a large emissive plane at Strength 1 can light a room while a small one at Strength 1 is barely a glow. Emission is the one shader you can legitimately Add to another, since it contributes light rather than redistributing it."},{"t":"Background","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#background","x":"The shader of the World, not of objects: a Color and Strength that fill the environment, or an Environment Texture for an HDRI. You meet it in the World tab's node tree. It is where the ambient light and the reflections of the surroundings come from, and it can be made to show one thing to the camera and another to reflections with a Light Path node (Chapter 6 )."},{"t":"Holdout","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#holdout","x":"Renders as a transparent hole in the image, with the alpha channel cut out, as if the object masked the film. It is used for compositing: an object that will be replaced by live footage, or a shape to punch out of a render so something else can be placed there in Illustrator. With Film › Transparent off, a holdout renders black; with it on, the hole is transparent."},{"t":"Ray Portal BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#ray-portal-bsdf","x":"A Cycles-only effect: a ray that hits the surface continues from a different position and direction that you supply. It makes portals, mirrors that show another room, and impossible spaces. For scientific work it has no use, but it explains why the list is longer than you expected."},{"t":"Hair BSDF and Principled Hair BSDF","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#hair-bsdf-and-principled-hair-bsdf","x":"Shaders for hair strands: a cylinder model with separate reflection and transmission lobes, and a principled version that takes melanin values to color the hair. They apply to curve objects rendered as hair. Molecules have no hair, but ribbon and tube representations are meshes, not curves, so these are not involved."},{"t":"Exercise 4.1: the four see-throughs","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#exercise-4-1","x":"Four spheres over a checkered plane, lit by the figure rig: Transparent (white), Refraction (IOR 1.45), Glass (IOR 1.45), Translucent (white). Render. What you should see The Transparent sphere is invisible except for its shadow; the Refraction sphere shows the checkerboard bent and flipped but has no highlights and a dull rim; the Glass sphere shows the same bend plus bright highlights and a reflective rim; the Translucent sphere is a soft white ball glowing on the side away from the light. Four nodes, four distinct answers to Question 2 of the design method."},{"t":"Exercise 4.2: scatter on the monkey","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#exercise-4-2","x":"Subsurface Scattering on the monkey head, Random Walk, Radius 1, 0.2, 0.1, Scale 0.02, with a small area light behind the head. Render at Scale 0.02, 0.1 and 0.5. What you should see At 0.02 the ears glow red at their thin edges; at 0.1 the whole head looks like wax; at 0.5 it looks like a lamp shade with the shape nearly lost. Scale is the distance light travels, and the look is that distance relative to the object."},{"t":"Exercise 4.3: a holdout mask","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#exercise-4-3","x":"Put a Holdout material on the cube, place it in front of the map, and render with Film › Transparent on. Open the PNG in an image editor. What you should see A cube-shaped transparent hole cut through the map, which is how a figure element is reserved for an overlay drawn elsewhere."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 4","u":"shaders.html#chapter-4-check","x":"Which node bends light but has no reflection? Answer Refraction BSDF. Why does a single plane with a Glass BSDF render dark? Answer Glass expects a closed mesh; a lone plane is seen as the entry into an infinite slab. What distinguishes Translucent from Subsurface Scattering in a render? Answer Translucent glows on the far side only; subsurface also softens the shadow edge and lets light travel sideways. Why can Emission be added to another shader when Diffuse cannot? Answer Emission creates light; Diffuse redistributes it, and adding two redistributions reflects more than arrived."},{"t":"Volume shaders","w":"Shaders, Chapter 5","u":"shaders.html#chapter-5","x":"Surface shaders describe a skin. Volume shaders describe what happens to light while it travels through the inside of an object, and they plug into the Material Output's Volume socket instead of Surface. An object can have both: a glass surface and an absorbing interior is colored glass. A volume needs an inside, which means a closed mesh; the renderer steps through the interior along each ray, which is why volumes are slower and why Render Properties › Volumes has its own Step Rate and Max Steps. Explain what a volume shader does differently from a surface shader and where it plugs in. Set Density by the size of the object, using the units it is measured in. Render a density map as a cloud and as a tinted solid, and know when each is appropriate."},{"t":"Volume Absorption","w":"Shaders, Chapter 5","u":"shaders.html#volume-absorption","x":"The medium swallows light as it passes, by Color and Density. The further light travels, the more is lost, so thick regions go dark and thin ones stay clear. Color here is the color that is kept, not the color absorbed: a green Volume Absorption makes a green liquid, because it absorbs red and blue. This is the node for tinted gels, colored glass, and the slime recipe's thickness-dependent color. It is cheap to render because it never scatters: each ray simply dims as it crosses the object."},{"t":"Volume Scatter","w":"Shaders, Chapter 5","u":"shaders.html#volume-scatter","x":"The medium bounces light sideways, which is what makes fog, smoke and milk visible from the side and gives them their glow. Density sets how much; Anisotropy sets whether light continues forward (positive, like mist lit from behind that glows around the sun) or scatters evenly (0) or back toward the light (negative). Scattering is expensive to render, because every bounce is another path to follow, and it is the main source of noise in volumetric renders. Cycles limits volume bounces separately under Light Paths › Max Bounces › Volume; the default is 0, which means single scattering only and is usually enough."},{"t":"Principled Volume","w":"Shaders, Chapter 5","u":"shaders.html#principled-volume","x":"Absorption and scattering together, plus emission and blackbody color for fire, with inputs named for simulation attributes (density, temperature, flame). It is the all-in-one for smoke and fire caches, and it is what Blender assigns by default when you click New on a volume object, which is why a density map first renders as fog. For a map you want as a surface, remove it and use a surface shader (Chapter 9 ); for a map you want shown as a true cloud, keep it and set Density Attribute to the name of the map's grid so brightness follows the data."},{"t":"Volume Coefficients","w":"Shaders, Chapter 5","u":"shaders.html#volume-coefficients","x":"The same physics as Absorption and Scatter expressed directly as the coefficients scientists use: absorption, scattering and emission per unit length, as a color each. It is the node for matching measured optical properties of a medium, and for building a volume from literature values rather than by eye."},{"t":"Density and units","w":"Shaders, Chapter 5","u":"shaders.html#density-and-units","x":"Density is per meter of travel. A density of 1 means that over one meter about 63% of the light is absorbed or scattered, because the fraction that survives is e to the minus density times distance. Over two meters 86% is gone; over 0.1 m only 10%. Molecular Nodes scenes use 1 m per nanometer, so an object 20 m across needs densities around 0.1 to 0.5 for a visible gradient, and a density of 2 makes it nearly opaque. When a volume looks solid black, the density is too high for the object's size; when it is invisible, too low. A good first guess is 2 divided by the object's width in meters, which makes the thickest part lose about 86%."},{"t":"Volumes and density maps","w":"Shaders, Chapter 5","u":"shaders.html#volumes-and-density-maps","x":"A cryo-EM map is itself a volume, a grid of values. Molecular Nodes gives you three ways to show it. The style nodes turn it into a surface at a threshold, which is the usual figure and the most honest, because the threshold is stated. A Principled Volume with Density driven by the map renders it as a cloud whose brightness follows the map value, which shows the whole density distribution at once without a cutoff; it is good for showing where density is weak or ambiguous. And a surface shader plus a Volume Absorption inside gives a solid shell that darkens with thickness, which is an aesthetic choice rather than a representation of the data. All three are legitimate; the first is what reviewers expect, and the other two need a sentence in the legend."},{"t":"Exercise 5.1: density by size","w":"Shaders, Chapter 5","u":"shaders.html#exercise-5-1","x":"A cube 1 m across with a Glass surface and Volume Absorption, Color blue, Density 1. Render. Scale the cube to 5 m and render again. Then set Density to 0.2 and render once more. What you should see The 1 m cube is a clear blue; the 5 m cube at Density 1 is nearly black; at 0.2 it matches the first. Density times size is what the eye sees."},{"t":"Exercise 5.2: the map three ways","w":"Shaders, Chapter 5","u":"shaders.html#exercise-5-2","x":"Render the map with the Density Style Surface at its usual threshold. Replace the style with the raw volume: in the Geometry Nodes tree connect Group Input straight to Group Output, and give the object a Principled Volume with Density Attribute set to the grid name (check the Spreadsheet's Volume Grids list) and Density 5. Return to the surface style, and add Volume Absorption to its material at Density 0.1. What you should see A solid surface; a glowing cloud brightest where the map is dense; and a surface with darker thick regions. Keep the three images with a note of which is which; they are three different claims about the data."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 5","u":"shaders.html#chapter-5-check","x":"What fraction of light survives 1 m of travel at Density 1? Answer About 37%. A volume renders solid black. First change? Answer Lower the density; it is too high for the object's size. Which volume node is cheap and which is expensive to render, and why? Answer Absorption is cheap, since rays only dim; Scatter is expensive, since every bounce spawns new paths. Why does a density map start as fog when you click New? Answer It is a volume object, so Blender gives it a Principled Volume by default."},{"t":"Combining shaders","w":"Shaders, Chapter 6","u":"shaders.html#chapter-6","x":"Two nodes combine shaders, and the whole craft of hand-built materials is in what you feed their factor. Use Mix Shader with a varying factor to blend two materials by angle, pattern or data. Know the one legitimate use of Add Shader and why the others break energy conservation. Use Light Path to show different things to the camera and to reflections."},{"t":"Mix Shader","w":"Shaders, Chapter 6","u":"shaders.html#mix-shader","x":"Takes two shaders and a Fac between 0 and 1, and blends them: at 0.3 the surface behaves like the first shader 70% of the time and the second 30%. Energy is conserved, because the two are weighted, not added. A constant Fac is a crude blend; the interesting cases drive Fac with something that varies: Fresnel node (Shift + A › Input › Fresnel) with an IOR: 0 head-on rising to 1 at the edges, in the physically correct curve. Diffuse into the first socket, Glossy into the second, Fresnel into Fac, and you have rebuilt a plastic by hand. Its one weakness is on rough surfaces, where a true Fresnel is too strong; the Principled BSDF corrects for that internally. Layer Weight gives the same idea with an artist's Blend slider instead of an IOR, plus a Facing output that is the simpler linear version. It is easier to tune and good enough for most looks. Blend 0.5 is roughly an IOR of 1.5. A texture : a Noise Texture's Fac blends two materials in patches; an image's alpha cuts one material out of another; a Color Ramp between either sharpens or softens the boundary. A Color Ramp set to Constant interpolation turns a soft noise into hard-edged islands of the second material. An attribute : a Named Attribute or Attribute node, such as a per-atom value from Molecular Nodes, blends shaders by data, so one chain can be glossy and another matte on the same object. A boolean selection stored in Geometry Nodes becomes a 0 or 1 factor directly. Light Path : its Is Camera Ray output is 1 only for rays coming directly from the camera. Mix a Transparent shader in when Is Camera Ray is 0 and an object becomes invisible to reflections and shadows while still visible to the camera, or the reverse, which is how emissive ribbons are hidden from the lens while their reflections stay. Is Shadow Ray does the same for shadows, and Is Glossy Ray for what mirrors see. Mixes nest. A Mix of a Mix is a three-layer material, and the Principled BSDF is, internally, exactly such a nest. Nesting order follows the physical order: the outermost Mix is the top layer."},{"t":"Add Shader","w":"Shaders, Chapter 6","u":"shaders.html#add-shader","x":"Sums two shaders with no weighting. This breaks energy conservation whenever both redistribute incoming light: Diffuse plus Glossy added together reflects more than arrives and renders too bright, with a glow that no exposure setting can fix because the material itself is producing it. The legitimate use is adding Emission to any shader, because emission creates light rather than redistributing it, and adding Transparent to a volume-like effect. If you find yourself reaching for Add to make a surface look richer, use Mix and raise a light instead."},{"t":"Order and layering","w":"Shaders, Chapter 6","u":"shaders.html#order-and-layering","x":"In a Mix, order does not matter beyond which socket gets Fac 0 and which gets 1. But physically, layers do have an order: a coat sits on top of a body, and light the coat reflects never reaches the body. A hand-built Mix cannot express that; it only interpolates, so a 50% mix of coat and body sends half the light to each as if they sat side by side. That is one reason to prefer the Principled BSDF when you want a coat, a sheen or a thin film over a base: its layers are stacked in the physical order and the energy passed down is reduced correctly."},{"t":"Shader to RGB (EEVEE only)","w":"Shaders, Chapter 6","u":"shaders.html#shader-to-rgb-eevee-only","x":"Converts the result of a shader back into a color, so you can post-process the shading: push it through a Color Ramp to get cel-shading bands, or multiply it with a texture. Cycles cannot do this, because a Cycles shader is sampled rather than evaluated to one color. It is the main tool for stylized looks in Chapter 11 ."},{"t":"A worked hand-built material","w":"Shaders, Chapter 6","u":"shaders.html#a-worked-hand-built-material","x":"To see all of this once: Diffuse BSDF (Color gray, Roughness 0.4) into Mix Shader socket 1, Glossy BSDF (Color white, Roughness 0.3) into socket 2, Layer Weight Facing into Fac with Blend 0.5, Mix into Surface. Render it next to a Principled BSDF with the same gray and Roughness 0.4. They look nearly identical, and every input you then discover on the Principled node, Coat, Sheen, Subsurface, Transmission, is one more Mix layer in this same pattern."},{"t":"Exercise 6.1: two materials by data","w":"Shaders, Chapter 6","u":"shaders.html#exercise-6-1","x":"On a two-chain model, build a material with Mix Shader: a matte Principled in socket 1, a glossy candy Principled in socket 2. Add an Attribute node reading chain_id into a Math node (Compare, Equal to 1) and connect its output to Fac. What you should see One chain matte, the other candy, on the same object with one material. Change the compared value and the other chain switches."},{"t":"Exercise 6.2: visible but not reflected","w":"Shaders, Chapter 6","u":"shaders.html#exercise-6-2","x":"On a bright red sphere beside a glossy black sphere, build a Mix Shader: Transparent in socket 1, the red Principled in socket 2, Light Path › Is Camera Ray into Fac. Render, then swap the two sockets. What you should see First, the red sphere is visible to the camera but absent from the black sphere's reflection and casts no shadow; then the reverse, a reflection and a shadow of a sphere that is not there. This is the trick behind invisible ribbons and invisible flags."},{"t":"Exercise 6.3: break energy conservation","w":"Shaders, Chapter 6","u":"shaders.html#exercise-6-3","x":"Add Shader with Diffuse white and Glossy white at Roughness 0.3. Render next to a Mix of the same two at Fac 0.5. What you should see The Add version glows brighter than any white object under the same light could, and a plane of the same material would be brighter than the light reaching it. The Mix version looks like ordinary white plastic."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 6","u":"shaders.html#chapter-6-check","x":"What does a Mix Shader output at Fac 1? Answer Entirely the second shader. Why can a hand-built Mix not reproduce a coat correctly? Answer It interpolates side by side; a real coat sits on top and removes light before it reaches the body. Name the one shader you can Add without breaking energy conservation. Answer Emission. Which Light Path output lets an object appear only in reflections? Answer Is Camera Ray, used to mix in Transparent for camera rays."},{"t":"Principled BSDF: the assembled stack","w":"Shaders, Chapter 7","u":"shaders.html#chapter-7","x":"The Principled BSDF is Chapters 3 , 4 and 6 pre-wired: the single-behavior shaders stacked in physical order, with one input per layer and energy conservation handled for you ( Principled BSDF ). It follows the design that Disney introduced for film production in 2012 and that most renderers and file formats now share, which is why its inputs look the same in Blender, Substance, Unreal and glTF. Draw the Principled BSDF's layer stack from memory and say which input controls each layer. Give four reasons to start every material from it. Name the cases where a different node is the better tool."},{"t":"The layers, bottom to top","w":"Shaders, Chapter 7","u":"shaders.html#the-layers-bottom-to-top","x":"Base. A blend of four bases: diffuse, metal, subsurface and transmission. Metallic, Subsurface Weight and Transmission Weight are the dials that shift the base between them; whatever is not claimed by those three is diffuse. Base Color feeds all four. The Diffuse panel's Roughness is the Oren-Nayar flattening from Chapter 3 . Specular. The Fresnel-controlled gloss over the base, with IOR setting the head-on strength, Roughness its blur, IOR Level a scale on the strength, Tint a color for the reflection, and Anisotropic for stretched highlights. Metals use this same layer for their colored reflection. Emission. Light added from the base, under the coat, so a lit screen behind a varnish is modeled correctly: the varnish still reflects the room over the glowing screen. Coat. A second clear specular layer over everything, with its own Roughness, IOR, Tint and Normal, so a wet film can be smooth over a rough body. Coat Weight 0 removes it entirely, which keeps the default material cheap. Sheen. Fibers and dust on the very top, bright at grazing angles. Thin Film over the specular layers, for iridescence; it modifies the specular reflection's color by wavelength rather than adding a layer of its own. Alpha is applied outside the stack: it mixes the whole thing with a Transparent shader. Thin Wall switches the stack to a two-sided slab with no thickness, for leaves and paper, which makes transmission and subsurface behave as if the object were a sheet."},{"t":"Why to start here","w":"Shaders, Chapter 7","u":"shaders.html#why-to-start-here","x":"One node covers nearly everything. Plastic, metal, glass, skin, wax, rubber, paint, frosted and clear, flat and iridescent are all reachable by moving dials, which means one habit to learn instead of twenty, and one node to read in anyone else's file. It cannot break physics by accident. Raising one layer lowers the others, so a Principled material never reflects more light than it receives. A hand-built Add network can, and a hand-built Mix cannot stack layers correctly. The defaults are a believable material. A fresh Principled node at Roughness 0.5 and IOR 1.5 is an ordinary matte plastic that renders sensibly under any light, which is the right starting point for Question 1 in the design method. It is the industry's shared language. The same inputs exist in glTF, USD, Substance, Unreal and most renderers, so textures, tutorials and exported assets line up with it. A material built from it survives export to another program; a hand-built tree does not. It works in both engines. Cycles renders every layer accurately; EEVEE renders all of them with approximations. Hand-built trees often include nodes one engine ignores."},{"t":"When to leave it","w":"Shaders, Chapter 7","u":"shaders.html#when-to-leave-it","x":"Flat or cel-shaded figures. Emission alone, or Toon, or Shader to RGB in EEVEE give the look directly (Chapter 11 ). Speed on large, simple objects. Diffuse alone converges faster for backgrounds and clay tests, because Cycles has fewer lobes to sample. Effects that are not materials. Holdout, Ray Portal and Background are their own nodes by necessity. Hair. Principled Hair BSDF, on curve objects. Fully custom layering. When a coat must be rough and a body smooth, Principled does it; when you need three coats of different colors, or a blend that depends on data per atom in ways the dials do not express, a Mix network with the atoms from Chapters 3 and 4 is the honest tool, and you will understand it because it is the same stack built by hand. The practical rule across the whole guide follows from this: start every material as a Principled BSDF, answer the five questions of the Designing a look part on its dials, and add other shader nodes only when a dial does not exist for what you see."},{"t":"Exercise 7.1: one dial at a time","w":"Shaders, Chapter 7","u":"shaders.html#exercise-7-1","x":"A fresh Principled BSDF on the sphere, mid-green. Render it. Change one input at a time, rendering after each and resetting before the next: Metallic 1; Transmission 1; Subsurface Weight 1 with Scale 0.02; Coat Weight 1; Sheen Weight 1; Emission Strength 2 with the same green; Thin Film Thickness 400; Alpha 0.3. What you should see Eight distinct materials from one node, and in each case the rest of the look unchanged. Keep the eight renders as a contact sheet; it is the Principled BSDF in one image."},{"t":"Exercise 7.2: find the layer in the stack","w":"Shaders, Chapter 7","u":"shaders.html#exercise-7-2","x":"Base Color black, Roughness 0.2, Coat Weight 1, Coat Tint deep red, Emission white at Strength 1. What you should see A white glow seen through a red-tinted gloss, with white highlights on top. Emission sits under the coat, and the coat's tint colors it; the coat's own highlight stays white. The order of the layers is visible in a single render."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 7","u":"shaders.html#chapter-7-check","x":"Which three inputs decide what the base layer is made of? Answer Metallic, Subsurface Weight and Transmission Weight; the remainder is diffuse. Where does Emission sit in the stack, and why does it matter? Answer Under the coat, so a varnish still reflects over a glowing surface. Which input is applied outside the stack? Answer Alpha, as a mix with Transparent. Give one case where a hand-built Mix network is the better choice. Answer Several differently colored coats, or layer logic driven by per-atom data that the dials cannot express."},{"t":"What feeds a shader","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#chapter-8","x":"A shader's inputs are sockets, and anything that outputs a number, color or vector can drive one. The Shift + A menu in the Shader Editor groups those sources into Input, Texture, Color, Vector and Converter. This chapter is a guided tour of the ones that matter. Socket colors tell you the type: gray is a number, yellow a color, blue a vector, green a shader. Connecting mismatched types converts automatically (a color into a number takes its brightness), which is convenient and occasionally surprising. Choose the right coordinate system for a texture on a molecular surface. Turn any value, procedural, image or attribute, into a usable input with Color Ramp, Math and Map Range. Read an unfamiliar node tree by following links backward from the output."},{"t":"Input nodes: facts about the point being shaded","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#input-nodes-facts-about-the-point-being-shaded","x":"Texture Coordinate gives positions to feed textures: Generated (0 to 1 across the object's bounding box, so a texture stretches to fit any object), Object (the object's own axes, in meters, unaffected by scale changes elsewhere, so a pattern stays the same physical size), UV (the unwrapped layout, which molecular meshes do not have), Normal, Camera (relative to the camera, for effects fixed to the view) and Window (screen space). For procedural textures on molecules, use Object. Attribute reads a named attribute from the mesh: Color from Molecular Nodes, or any value stored by its Geometry Nodes tree. The Type menu chooses whether the name refers to a mesh attribute, an object property or an instancer attribute; Geometry is right for Molecular Nodes data. Geometry gives the Normal, the Position, whether this is a Backfacing side, and Pointiness (a cheap curvature that darkens crevices and highlights ridges, useful for edge wear and for making cavities read). Fresnel and Layer Weight give view-angle factors (Chapter 6 ). Ambient Occlusion returns how enclosed a point is, for darkening cavities without lighting changes; this is what the Molecular Nodes AO material uses. Distance sets how far it looks for occluders; Inside and Only Local change what counts. Light Path tells which kind of ray is asking: camera, shadow, reflection, transmission, and how many bounces deep. It is the basis of \"visible but not reflective\" tricks. Object Info gives the object's Color, Location, Random (a different number per object, for varying copies) and Index. Wireframe is 1 on edges and 0 elsewhere, for rendering a mesh's wire as a material; Size sets the line width. Camera Data gives the view distance, for depth cueing (Chapter 11 )."},{"t":"Textures: patterns across the surface","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#textures-patterns-across-the-surface","x":"Procedural textures are formulas: Noise (cloudy variation at any scale, with Detail for octaves, Roughness for how much the fine detail contributes, Lacunarity for the gap between octaves and Distortion for swirl), Voronoi (cells, for scales and crackle; the Feature menu picks the cell centers, edges or distances), Wave (stripes and rings, bent by Distortion), Musgrave and Gabor (terrain-like and oriented noise), Checker, Brick, Gradient, Magic, White Noise. They have no resolution and no seams, and they need only a coordinate vector, which is why they suit molecules. They can also be evaluated in 4D, with a W input that lets you animate them without moving them. Image Texture samples a picture and needs UV coordinates, or a projection (Box projection in the node's Projection menu tiles the image from six directions without UVs, with a Blend across the seams). Environment Texture is the same for HDRI backgrounds. IES Texture gives a light a measured real-world beam profile. Sky Texture generates a sky. Point Density renders points as a volume. A texture's output is usually a Fac (grayscale) and a Color. Fac feeds Roughness, Fac of a Mix, Bump Height, Alpha; Color feeds Base Color. The Color output of a Noise is three different noises in the three channels, which is handy when you want three independent variations from one node."},{"t":"Color utilities","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#color-utilities","x":"Color Ramp maps a 0 to 1 value to a gradient you draw: it is the way a data attribute becomes a color scale, and the way noise becomes two sharply divided colors (set Interpolation to Constant). Drag the stops to set thresholds; the Color Mode menu lets you interpolate through HSV for rainbow scales. Mix Color blends two colors by a factor, in any blend mode (Mix, Multiply, Screen, Overlay) as an image editor would. Multiply is how a chain color is applied over a grayscale pattern. Hue/Saturation/Value , RGB Curves , Bright/Contrast , Gamma , Invert adjust a color in place, which saves rebuilding a ramp when a scale is nearly right."},{"t":"Converters: numbers","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#converters-numbers","x":"Math does arithmetic on a value: multiply a noise by 0.3 before it drives Roughness, or clamp it, or compare it to a threshold to get a 0 or 1 mask. Map Range rescales one range to another, the clean way to turn 0 to 1 into 0.2 to 0.6, with a Clamp option and smooth-step modes for soft thresholds. Separate Color and Combine Color split a color into channels and rebuild it, useful for using one channel of an attribute as a factor. Clamp , Float Curve , Vector Math and the rest follow the same pattern; Float Curve is a drawable transfer function, useful for tuning a data scale by eye."},{"t":"Bump and Displacement","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#bump-and-displacement","x":"Bump takes a Height value and produces a Normal that makes the surface shade as if it had that relief. Strength scales it, Distance sets how tall a unit of height is, and Invert flips it. It changes shading only. Chain a second Bump into the first's Normal input to add two scales of relief. Normal Map does the same from a tangent-space normal image, the game-asset workflow, which needs UVs. Displacement (Vector › Displacement) makes a vector for the Material Output's Displacement socket, which in Cycles can move the geometry itself when the material's Displacement setting is Displacement and Bump or Displacement Only (Chapter 9 ). Real displacement needs dense geometry to show, which is what Adaptive Subdivision provides."},{"t":"Reading a complex tree","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#reading-a-complex-tree","x":"Follow the green shader links backward from the Material Output to find the shader, then follow each of its inputs leftward to its source. Most impressive materials are one Principled BSDF with five or six of these feeder chains, each a texture or attribute, through a Math or Color Ramp, into one dial. Ctrl + Shift + click on any node (with the Node Wrangler add-on enabled) previews that node's output on the surface, which is the fastest way to see what a chain is producing."},{"t":"Exercise 8.1: three coordinate systems","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#exercise-8-1","x":"A Checker Texture into Base Color on the cube, with Scale 4. Connect Texture Coordinate › Generated, render; then Object, render; then scale the cube to twice its size and render both again. What you should see With Generated, 4 checks fit the cube at both sizes, so they double in size with it; with Object, the checks stay the same physical size and there are twice as many on the larger cube. Object is what you want when a texture represents a physical pattern."},{"t":"Exercise 8.2: a data color scale","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#exercise-8-2","x":"On a model with the b_factor attribute, Attribute (Geometry type, Name b_factor ) › Fac into a Map Range (From Min and Max set to the attribute's range, read from the Spreadsheet) into a Color Ramp (blue to red) into Base Color. What you should see A B-factor coloring like ChimeraX's, built from three nodes, with the ramp's stops under your control."},{"t":"Exercise 8.3: preview a chain","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#exercise-8-3","x":"Enable Node Wrangler in Preferences › Add-ons. Build a Noise into Color Ramp (Constant) into a Mix, and Ctrl + Shift + click each node in turn. What you should see The noise as gray clouds, then as hard islands, then as two colors. Previewing each stage is how you debug a tree that is not doing what you expect."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 8","u":"shaders.html#chapter-8-check","x":"Which coordinate system keeps a texture the same physical size when the object is scaled? Answer Object. What does the Fac output of a Noise Texture feed, typically? Answer Roughness, Bump Height, Alpha or a Mix factor. How do you turn a soft noise into hard-edged patches? Answer A Color Ramp with Constant interpolation. What is the difference between Bump and Displacement in the final image? Answer Bump changes shading only; displacement moves the geometry and changes the silhouette."},{"t":"The Material Output and material settings","w":"Shaders, Chapter 9","u":"shaders.html#chapter-9","x":"Beyond the node tree, a material has a settings panel and a place in the object's slot list. These decide how the tree is applied. Manage material slots, users and single-user copies without losing work. Set the material settings that decide transparency, backfaces and displacement. Give one material different trees for Cycles and EEVEE."},{"t":"Material slots","w":"Shaders, Chapter 9","u":"shaders.html#material-slots","x":"An object holds a list of materials in the Material Properties tab, and each face is assigned to one slot. In Edit Mode, select faces, pick a slot and click Assign to put them on it; Select and Deselect find the faces of a slot. For a Molecular Nodes object the style node's Material field supplies the material for the geometry it generates, so set it there rather than in the slot list; the slot list is for meshes you model yourself. Clicking New makes a fresh material for the active slot; the dropdown reuses an existing one; the number next to the name is how many objects share it, and clicking it makes a single-user copy so edits stop affecting the others. A material with zero users is discarded when the file is saved unless its shield icon (fake user) is set; the Blender File view in the Outliner lists every material and its user count."},{"t":"Surface, Volume, Displacement","w":"Shaders, Chapter 9","u":"shaders.html#surface-volume-displacement","x":"The three sockets of the Material Output (Chapter 1 ). A volume object given a new material gets a Principled Volume in Volume and nothing in Surface. A mesh gets a Principled BSDF in Surface and nothing in Volume. For a density map rendered as a surface, the material must have the Surface filled and the Volume empty; the style node turns the map into a mesh, and the volume shader would otherwise render it as fog. Displacement is ignored until the Settings panel enables it."},{"t":"Settings panel","w":"Shaders, Chapter 9","u":"shaders.html#settings-panel","x":"Render Method (EEVEE): Dithered renders transparency as a noise pattern that resolves with samples and sorts correctly whatever the overlap; Blended renders it as true blending, smoother for glass and ghosts but prone to sorting errors where transparent surfaces overlap, because EEVEE must guess their order. If Alpha looks wrong in EEVEE, switch this. Backface Culling : hides the back side of faces in the viewport and render. Useful for a cutaway shell seen from outside; harmful on a map, whose inside you often see through holes. Backface Culling Shadow does the same for shadows only. Transparent Shadows (EEVEE): whether an Alpha surface casts a lighter shadow or a solid one. Displacement (Cycles): Bump Only uses the Displacement socket as a bump map; Displacement Only moves the geometry; Displacement and Bump does both, which is the usual choice since bump adds the fine detail that the displaced mesh is too coarse to show. Real displacement needs a Subdivision Surface modifier set to Adaptive in the object's Modifier properties (available when Feature Set is Experimental) for enough geometry. Pass Index : a number that a Material Index compositor pass can read to mask this material in post-processing, for example to recolor only the map in Photoshop. The object has a separate Pass Index for the Object Index pass. Light Probe and Shadow settings (EEVEE): how the material interacts with EEVEE's approximations; leave at defaults unless a shadow looks wrong."},{"t":"Viewport Display","w":"Shaders, Chapter 9","u":"shaders.html#viewport-display","x":"The color, metallic and roughness shown in Solid view, independent of the node tree. Setting these lets Solid mode show chain colors while you work, without the cost of Rendered view. Solid view's shading popover must be set to Material color for them to show; Attribute color shows the mesh's own color attribute instead, which for a Molecular Nodes object is the Color attribute, so Solid mode can show chain colors with no material at all."},{"t":"The Target menu","w":"Shaders, Chapter 9","u":"shaders.html#the-target-menu","x":"On the Material Output, Target lets a material carry two trees, one tagged for Cycles and one for EEVEE, for materials that use engine-specific nodes (Shader to RGB in EEVEE, Ray Portal in Cycles). Add two Material Output nodes, set each one's Target, and the renderer picks the one that matches. Nodes that one engine cannot evaluate are simply skipped there, so a Shader to RGB chain renders black in Cycles unless a Cycles-targeted output exists."},{"t":"Exercise 9.1: slots and users","w":"Shaders, Chapter 9","u":"shaders.html#exercise-9-1","x":"Give the cube two slots: in Edit Mode select the top face, add a slot, assign a red material to it, and leave the rest gray. Duplicate the cube. On the copy, change the red material's color. Undo, click the user-count number next to the material's name on the copy, and change the color again. What you should see The first change recolors both cubes, because they share the material; after making it single-user, only the copy changes. This is the cause of most \"I changed one object and another changed too\" surprises."},{"t":"Exercise 9.2: EEVEE transparency sorting","w":"Shaders, Chapter 9","u":"shaders.html#exercise-9-2","x":"Two overlapping planes with Alpha 0.5 materials, one red, one blue, in EEVEE. Render with Render Method Blended, then Dithered. What you should see With Blended, the overlap may show the wrong plane in front as you orbit; with Dithered, the order is always right but the surfaces look grainy until the samples accumulate."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 9","u":"shaders.html#chapter-9-check","x":"Why is a material you built sometimes gone when you reopen a file? Answer It had no users and no fake user, so Blender discarded it on save. Which Displacement setting is usual for real relief, and what modifier does it need? Answer Displacement and Bump, with an Adaptive Subdivision Surface modifier. How can Solid mode show chain colors with no material at all? Answer Set its color mode to Attribute, which shows the mesh's Color attribute."},{"t":"Render engines","w":"Shaders, Chapter 10","u":"shaders.html#chapter-10","x":"The same material renders differently in Blender's three engines, because each answers the shader's question with a different amount of honesty. Explain how a path tracer and a rasterizer differ in what they do with a shader. List what EEVEE approximates and what only Cycles can do. Choose an engine for a task and set its two or three important settings."},{"t":"Cycles","w":"Shaders, Chapter 10","u":"shaders.html#cycles","x":"A path tracer: it follows light physically, with real reflections, refraction, soft shadows, subsurface scattering and volumes, and it converges toward the correct image as samples accumulate. Every shader in this part works in Cycles, and Ray Portal, Thin Film, light linking for emissive meshes and true caustics exist only here. The cost is time and noise: a clean still needs hundreds of samples, and glossy or transmissive materials under small lights need more. Denoising hides most of the noise. The settings that matter: Device (GPU), Max Samples (256 for drafts, 1024 for finals with hard materials), Denoise (on, OpenImageDenoise), and Light Paths › Max Bounces, where Transparent must exceed the number of ghosted layers a ray can cross and Glossy affects how deep reflections of reflections go. For a figure that will be printed, Cycles is the default."},{"t":"EEVEE","w":"Shaders, Chapter 10","u":"shaders.html#eevee","x":"A rasterizer, in the family of game engines, rewritten for Blender 4.2 with ray-traced reflections, virtual shadow maps and a new shadow and light system. It renders in seconds and the viewport is interactive, which makes it ideal for designing materials and lighting even when the final render is Cycles. Its approximations matter in a few places: subsurface scattering is a screen-space blur rather than real transport; refraction looks through a screen-space copy of the scene, so objects behind glass can vanish at the frame edge; transparency needs the Render Method choice of Chapter 9 ; emissive meshes glow but do not cast light unless a probe or a real light helps; Shader to RGB and Specular BSDF work only here. Its settings that matter: Sampling › Render samples (64 is plenty), Raytracing on for reflections, Shadows › Resolution Scale, and Film › Transparent. For a presentation animation or a quick draft, EEVEE is the right engine; for a figure with glass or jelly, check it in Cycles before trusting it."},{"t":"Workbench","w":"Shaders, Chapter 10","u":"shaders.html#workbench","x":"The Solid viewport's renderer, available as a final engine. It ignores node trees entirely and uses the Viewport Display color or an attribute color, a studio light or flat lighting, cavity shading and outlines. For a schematic figure where every chain is a flat color with an outline, Workbench with Flat lighting, Cavity on and Outline on produces it in a fraction of a second, with no materials to build. It also renders the Color attribute of a Molecular Nodes object directly when Color is set to Attribute."},{"t":"Choosing","w":"Shaders, Chapter 10","u":"shaders.html#choosing","x":"Need Engine Publication still, any material Cycles Glass, gel, subsurface, volumes shown accurately Cycles Interactive material and lighting design EEVEE in the viewport, then Cycles Animation for a talk EEVEE Flat, outlined schematic Workbench, or EEVEE with Shader to RGB Cel shading with Color Ramp bands EEVEE The engine is set in Render Properties, and the viewport's Rendered mode uses whichever engine is selected, so switching to EEVEE while working and back to Cycles to finish is one dropdown. Materials built with the Principled BSDF carry over without change; materials with engine-specific nodes need the Target menu of Chapter 9 ."},{"t":"Exercise 10.1: the same scene three ways","w":"Shaders, Chapter 10","u":"shaders.html#exercise-10-1","x":"The lab bench with the frosted-glass sphere and the subsurface monkey. Render in Cycles (256 samples, denoised), EEVEE (64 samples, Raytracing on) and Workbench (Flat, Cavity, Outline). Note the render time of each. What you should see Cycles and EEVEE agree on the satin objects and disagree on the glass and the subsurface, where EEVEE is smoother but less convincing; Workbench shows flat colors with outlines and no materials at all. Times should differ by orders of magnitude."},{"t":"Exercise 10.2: find the bounce limit","w":"Shaders, Chapter 10","u":"shaders.html#exercise-10-2","x":"Stack five ghost-material planes (Alpha 0.3) in front of the map in Cycles. Set Light Paths › Max Bounces › Transparent to 2 and render, then 8. What you should see At 2, the back planes and the map go black where more than two transparent surfaces overlap; at 8 everything shows. Every black patch in a ghosted render is a bounce limit."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 10","u":"shaders.html#chapter-10-check","x":"Which engine evaluates a shader by sampling, and which by approximation in one pass? Answer Cycles samples; EEVEE rasterizes with approximations. Name two effects that exist only in Cycles. Answer Any two of: Ray Portal, Thin Film, light linking for emissive meshes, true caustics. A ghosted map shows black patches in Cycles. Which setting? Answer Light Paths › Max Bounces › Transparent. When is Workbench the right final engine? Answer For flat, outlined schematic figures with no materials needed."},{"t":"Stylized and flat shading for figures","w":"Shaders, Chapter 11","u":"shaders.html#chapter-11","x":"Photorealism is one choice. Many of the best structure figures are deliberately not realistic: flat colors, soft bands, dark outlines, no highlights. Each of those looks is a specific shader setup, and most of them render faster than realism because there is less light to simulate. Build flat, banded and outlined looks and know which engine each needs. Shade with ambient occlusion alone, with no lights in the scene. Add depth cueing in the compositor or in the material."},{"t":"Flat color","w":"Shaders, Chapter 11","u":"shaders.html#flat-color","x":"An Emission shader at Strength 1 shows the exact color with no shading at all. Combine it with Freestyle or Line Art outlines (below) and you have a 2D-illustration style. On a Principled BSDF the same thing is Base Color black, IOR Level 0, Emission Color set to the color, Emission Strength 1, which keeps the option of adding a little shading back by raising Base Color. With View Transform set to Standard, the rendered pixel is exactly the color you typed, which matters when a legend must match."},{"t":"Toon BSDF bands","w":"Shaders, Chapter 11","u":"shaders.html#toon-bsdf-bands","x":"The Toon BSDF gives two-tone lighting directly: Size sets the width of the lit zone and Smooth the softness of its edge. Mix a Toon Diffuse with a Toon Glossy for a cel-shaded highlight. It works in both engines and reacts to your real lights, so moving the key moves the band. A Size of 0.3 and Smooth of 0.05 gives a classic comic look; Smooth 0 gives a hard edge that may alias, so keep it slightly above 0."},{"t":"Shader to RGB cel shading (EEVEE)","w":"Shaders, Chapter 11","u":"shaders.html#shader-to-rgb-cel-shading-eevee","x":"The most controllable stylized look. Diffuse BSDF into Shader to RGB, into a Color Ramp set to Constant interpolation with two or three stops, into Emission, into the Material Output. The shading is quantized into the bands you draw on the ramp, and the colors of the bands are yours. Add the chain color by multiplying it in with a Mix Color (Multiply) before Emission, so the ramp sets brightness and the attribute sets hue. This only renders in EEVEE; give the material a Cycles-targeted Material Output with a plain Principled BSDF if the file might be rendered there."},{"t":"Outlines","w":"Shaders, Chapter 11","u":"shaders.html#outlines","x":"Freestyle (Render Properties › Freestyle) draws lines on silhouettes and creases at render time in either engine, with thickness and color set in View Layer Properties › Freestyle Line Style. Its Edge Types let you choose silhouettes only, or add creases and borders. It is the simplest way to a comic outline around every chain. Grease Pencil Line Art (Shift + A › Grease Pencil › Scene Line Art) builds the same lines as editable strokes, which can be styled further, thinned at the ends, or kept while the 3D view changes. Inverted hull : a Solidify modifier with Flip Normals and a black Emission material in its material slot, with Backface Culling on, gives a thick outline that works in EEVEE at interactive speed. Thickness is the Solidify modifier's Thickness, negative so it grows outward. Layer Weight into Emission gives a soft glowing rim rather than a hard line, as in the edge-glow ghost of the Recipes part ."},{"t":"Ambient occlusion as the only shading","w":"Shaders, Chapter 11","u":"shaders.html#ambient-occlusion-as-the-only-shading","x":"A favorite for structure figures: a flat color darkened only where the surface is enclosed, so grooves and interfaces read without any directional light. Ambient Occlusion node › Color into Emission, or simply the Molecular Nodes Ambient Occlusion material, whose Distance input sets how deep the darkening reaches. Multiply the chain color in with a Mix Color. Pair it with Film › Transparent and a white background, and the figure needs no lights at all; the whole render is a few seconds."},{"t":"Depth cueing","w":"Shaders, Chapter 11","u":"shaders.html#depth-cueing","x":"Fading distant parts toward the background, as ChimeraX and PyMOL do, is not a shader setting in Blender. It is a compositor step: enable the Z or Mist pass in View Layer Properties, and in the Compositor mix the image toward the background color by that pass. Mist has a Start and Depth in World Properties › Mist Pass that set where the fade begins and how far it runs. The same result in the material is possible with Camera Data › View Z Depth into a Map Range into a Mix with the background color, which keeps it per-material and lets the map fade while the model does not. All of these looks can be combined with realistic ones in the same image: a photoreal map with a flat, outlined model inside it, or a cel-shaded protein against a realistic membrane. Materials are per object, so the choice is per object too."},{"t":"Exercise 11.1: four looks on one model","w":"Shaders, Chapter 11","u":"shaders.html#exercise-11-1","x":"On a chain-colored model, render four times: flat Emission, Toon bands, Shader to RGB cel shading (EEVEE) with three bands, and ambient-occlusion-only. Add Freestyle outlines to the last two. What you should see Four figure styles from one structure, each a different claim about what matters, from pure color map to shape with depth. Keep the four as references for choosing a style per figure."},{"t":"Exercise 11.2: depth cue the map only","w":"Shaders, Chapter 11","u":"shaders.html#exercise-11-2","x":"Put the Camera Data › View Z Depth › Map Range › Mix chain into the map's material, fading toward white, and leave the model's material alone. What you should see The far side of the map fades into the background while the model stays crisp, which draws the eye to the model without hiding the map."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Shaders, Chapter 11","u":"shaders.html#chapter-11-check","x":"Which stylized technique works in Cycles and which only in EEVEE? Answer Toon and Emission work in both; Shader to RGB only in EEVEE. How is chain color combined with an occlusion or band pattern? Answer A Mix Color in Multiply mode: the pattern sets brightness, the attribute sets hue. Where does depth cueing live in Blender? Answer The compositor, using the Mist or Z pass, or a Camera Data chain in the material."},{"t":"The chooser, a capstone, and a glossary","w":"Shaders, Chapter 12","u":"shaders.html#chapter-12","x":"Go from a stated need to a node or setting in one lookup. Build one complete, publication-ready render that uses material from every chapter."},{"t":"I want this; use that","w":"Shaders, Chapter 12","u":"shaders.html#i-want-this-use-that","x":"I want Use Note An ordinary solid material of any kind Principled BSDF Set Base Color and Roughness first Real glass or water Principled with Transmission 1, or Glass BSDF Glass BSDF is simpler and a little faster A see-through overlay that does not bend what is behind Principled with Alpha below 1 Or Transparent BSDF in a Mix Wax, milk, skin, a soft glow in thin parts Principled with Subsurface Weight 1 Scale about 1 to 2% of object width A wet or lacquered finish over any body Principled Coat panel Coat Roughness 0 to 0.1 Velvet, fuzz, dust Principled Sheen panel, or Sheen BSDF Bright edges, dark center Gold, silver, copper Metallic BSDF, or Principled with Metallic 1 Base Color sets the metal's tint A colored interior that darkens with thickness Volume Absorption in the Volume socket Plus a surface shader in Surface Fog, smoke, a cloud rendering of a map Principled Volume, Density from the map Cycles; expensive A flat, unshaded color Emission at Strength 1 Exact color, no lights needed A light source shaped like an object Emission at Strength 10 to 50 Hide from camera with Ray Visibility Cel-shaded bands Toon BSDF, or Shader to RGB into a Color Ramp (EEVEE) An outline around every object Freestyle, Line Art, or an inverted hull Not a shader; a render or modifier setting Shading only in crevices, no directional light Ambient Occlusion into Emission, or the MN AO material A mask or a hole in the image Holdout For compositing Chain or data colors on any shader Attribute node ( Color ) into Base Color, optionally through a Color Ramp Two materials on one object by region Mix Shader with a texture or attribute as Fac Fast clay for lighting tests Diffuse BSDF, gray Or Material Override in Cycles"},{"t":"Capstone exercise: one figure from every chapter","w":"Shaders, Chapter 12","u":"shaders.html#capstone-exercise-one-figure-from-every-chapter","x":"Build a single figure of your EGFR map and model that uses, and that you can point to in the file: A material on the model built from a Principled BSDF with chain colors read from the Color attribute (Chapters 7 and 8 ). A material on the map built from the five questions, with a surface shader, no volume shader, and one procedural bump (Chapters 1 , 4 and 8 , and the Designing a look part ). One hand-built Mix: a rim glow on the map driven by Layer Weight (Chapter 6 ). The figure rig from the Lighting part , parented to the camera, with one emissive strip hidden from the camera that adds a reflection on the model ( Lighting part , Chapters 4 and 6 here). Film › Transparent, View Transform Standard, rendered in Cycles at 256 samples with Denoise on (Chapters 9 and 10 ). A second version of the same file rendered as a flat, outlined schematic in Workbench or EEVEE (Chapters 10 and 11 ). What you should have at the end: two renders of the same scene, one realistic and one schematic, and a .blend file in which every material, light and setting is something you built on purpose and can explain. If a reviewer asks why the map looks the way it does, every answer is a chapter in this guide."},{"t":"Check yourself: the whole part","w":"Shaders, Chapter 12","u":"shaders.html#chapter-12-check","x":"A colleague's material renders black in Cycles and fine in EEVEE. Three possible causes? Answer Specular BSDF, Shader to RGB, or a Material Output targeted to EEVEE with no Cycles output. You want the map colored by the model inside it, glossy, with a thickness tint. Which nodes? Answer Sample Nearest Attribute in Geometry Nodes, Attribute into Base Color, Principled with a coat, Volume Absorption in the Volume socket. Why does the guide say to start every material from the Principled BSDF? Answer It stacks the six behaviors in physical order with energy conservation handled, its defaults are believable, it works in both engines, and it is the shared language of other tools. What two things must be true of a mesh for Glass or Random Walk subsurface to render correctly? Answer Closed, with outward-facing normals."},{"t":"Glossary","w":"Shaders, Chapter 12","u":"shaders.html#glossary","x":"Albedo : the fraction of light a surface reflects diffusely; Base Color in practice. Alpha : opacity, 1 opaque, 0 invisible, applied without refraction. Anisotropy : a highlight stretched along a direction, as on brushed metal; in volumes, a preference for forward scattering. BSDF : bidirectional scattering distribution function, the rule a shader node encodes for how light leaving in one direction depends on light arriving from another. Coat : a clear glossy layer over the base material. Diffuse : reflection scattered in all directions, giving matte color. Fresnel : the increase in specular reflection toward grazing angles, governed by IOR. IOR : index of refraction, which sets both how light bends on entering a material and how strongly it reflects head-on; 1.33 water, 1.45 glass, 1.5 plastic. Microfacet : the model of a rough surface as many tiny mirrors; Roughness is the spread of their angles. Normal : the direction a surface faces at a point; Bump and Normal Map alter it to fake relief. Roughness : the blur of specular reflection and transmission, 0 sharp, 1 fully scattered. Sheen : reflection from surface fibers, bright at grazing angles. Specular : mirror-like reflection, blurred by roughness. Subsurface scattering (SSS) : light that enters, travels below the surface, and exits nearby. Transmission : light passing through the material, bending by the IOR. Volume : a medium that absorbs or scatters light along its path, described per meter."},{"t":"Sources","w":"Shaders, Chapter 12","u":"shaders.html#sources","x":"Shader nodes , Principled BSDF (Blender Manual) Light Objects , Light Linking (Blender Manual) Materials (Molecular Nodes documentation)"},{"t":"Geometry Nodes, from zero","w":"Geometry Nodes","u":"geometry-nodes.html","x":"Materials decide how a surface looks; Geometry Nodes decides what the surface is. Every Molecular Nodes object is a Geometry Nodes tree, so learning it is learning how your maps and models are built, and it is what the sticky slime in the Recipes part is made of. This part goes from what a node tree is, through attributes and fields, to volumes and SDFs, and ends with the slime project built chain by chain. Notation. Shift + A › Geometry › Read is a menu path. Node › Socket names one output of a node, so Separate XYZ › Z is the Z output of a Separate XYZ node. An arrow, → , is a link you draw from one socket to the next."},{"t":"What Geometry Nodes is","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-1","x":"Explain what a Geometry Nodes modifier does and why it never damages the original data. Find your way around the Geometry Nodes workspace and the Group Input and Group Output nodes. Open any Molecular Nodes object and see it as a tree you can read."},{"t":"A pipeline on a modifier","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes.html#a-pipeline-on-a-modifier","x":"Geometry Nodes is a modifier. Like a Subdivision Surface or a Solidify modifier, it sits in the Modifier Properties tab, takes the object's geometry in, and hands transformed geometry out to the viewport and the renderer. The difference is that what it does is not fixed: it is a tree of nodes you build. The original mesh is never touched; the tree runs again every time something it depends on changes, which is why you can drag a threshold slider and watch a density surface reshape itself. Read a tree from left to right. Group Input on the left is where the object's own data enters. Each node takes what arrives, does one operation, and passes the result on. Group Output on the right is what Blender shows. Anything not connected to Group Output does not exist as far as the viewport is concerned, which is a useful way to park work in progress. The same tree can be applied to different objects, and the values you expose on Group Input appear as sliders in the modifier panel, so a tree is also a tool: build it once, reuse it on every map."},{"t":"The workspace","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes.html#the-workspace","x":"The Geometry Nodes workspace tab at the top of Blender gives the standard layout: the 3D viewport top right, the node editor across the bottom, and the Spreadsheet top left. The Spreadsheet shows the actual numbers of a geometry, one row per vertex or point and one column per attribute, and it is the single most useful debugging tool, because it shows what the data is rather than what you think it is. The menu at its top left chooses which geometry it shows: Evaluated is the modifier's final result, Original is the object's data before any modifier (what Group Input receives), and Viewer Node is whatever is connected to a Viewer node. Selecting a node in the editor does not change what it shows; to inspect a point in the tree, connect that point to a Viewer. In the node editor: Shift + A adds nodes, and typing immediately after Shift + A searches them by name. Drag from a socket to connect; drag a link into empty space to disconnect. Ctrl + right-drag across links cuts them. Tab enters a node group. Home frames the whole tree. N opens the sidebar, where the Group tab lists the tree's exposed inputs and outputs. Ctrl + Shift + click on a node connects it to a Viewer so the viewport and Spreadsheet show that node's result. Sockets are typed and colored: green for geometry, gray for a number, yellow for a color, blue for a vector, pink for a boolean, orange for an object or collection, and a diamond shape means a field (Chapter 3 ). A link between mismatched types converts where it can and refuses where it cannot."},{"t":"Why every Molecular Nodes object is one","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes.html#why-every-molecular-nodes-object-is-one","x":"Molecular Nodes does not model a cartoon. It imports the atoms as points, with the data from the file stored on them as attributes, and adds a Geometry Nodes modifier whose tree reads those attributes and builds the cartoon, the sticks or the surface. A density map imports as a volume with the map values in a grid, and its tree turns the grid into a surface at a threshold. Click any Molecular Nodes object with the Geometry Nodes workspace open and you will see the tree: Group Input, a color node, a style node, Group Output. Everything in the Molecular Nodes menu is a node group designed to fit into that pattern, and everything in this part applies to it."},{"t":"Which tree you are looking at","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes.html#which-tree-you-are-looking-at","x":"The node editor shows one tree: the one in the active modifier of the active object. Its breadcrumb at the top left reads object › modifier › node group, and the Modifier tab (the wrench) shows the same group in each Geometry Nodes modifier's field. That field is the only link between an object and a tree: pick a different group there, or click New, and the object runs that one instead. A group with a user count of 0 beside its name belongs to no object and is gone after the file is saved and reopened, so point the modifier back at a Molecular Nodes tree before saving if you replaced it by accident. To give a new object its own tree, select it, Add Modifier › Geometry Nodes, and click New in the empty field; rename the group at once (double-click its name) so that Geometry Nodes.001 and .002 do not pile up. Tab's meaning depends on where the mouse is: over the viewport it toggles Edit Mode, where X opens a menu for deleting vertices of the active object rather than the object itself; over the node editor it enters and leaves a node group. If the viewport header reads Edit Mode and you did not ask for it, press Tab again."},{"t":"Exercise 1.1: a tree from nothing","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-1-1","x":"Add a Cube. In Modifier Properties, Add Modifier › Geometry Nodes, and click New. Open the Geometry Nodes workspace. Between Group Input and Group Output, add Shift + A › Mesh › Operations › Subdivide Mesh (Level 2), then Shift + A › Geometry › Operations › Transform Geometry (Scale 2). Disconnect the Transform node's output from Group Output, then reconnect it. What you should see The cube gains edges, then doubles in size, and disappears entirely while the output is disconnected. Apply nothing; the cube in Edit Mode is still eight vertices."},{"t":"Exercise 1.2: open a Molecular Nodes tree","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-1-2","x":"Select your map and your model in turn with the Geometry Nodes workspace open. Set the Spreadsheet's menu (top left) to Original and read the row count and the column names. Then Ctrl + Shift + click the style node, which connects it to a Viewer and switches the menu to Viewer Node, and read them again. What you should see The model's Group Input is a few thousand points with columns like chain_id and b_factor ; after the style node it is a mesh with far more vertices and a Color column. The map's Group Input is a volume with a grid; after the style node it is a mesh. Each node changed the kind of data as well as its shape."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-1-check","x":"What happens to geometry that is not connected to Group Output? Answer It is not shown; it exists only in the tree. Where do you see the actual numbers a node produces? Answer The Spreadsheet, after connecting the node to a Viewer with Ctrl + Shift + click; selecting the node is not enough. Which Spreadsheet setting shows the data Group Input receives? Answer Original. What does a Molecular Nodes model object contain before its tree runs? Answer Points, one per atom, with the file's data stored as attributes."},{"t":"Geometry and attributes","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-2","x":"Name the five geometry types and the domains each one has. Read and write attributes with Named Attribute and Store Named Attribute. List the attributes Molecular Nodes puts on a model and know which domain each lives on. Check the world scale of your file."},{"t":"The geometry types","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes.html#the-geometry-types","x":"A green socket can carry any of five kinds of geometry, and one geometry can contain several at once (a mesh plus some instances, say). Nodes act on the kinds they understand and pass the others through. Mesh : vertices, edges and faces, with face corners as a fourth level. Everything renderable as a surface is a mesh by the time it reaches Group Output. Curves : control points joined into splines, with a radius per point. They render only after Curve to Mesh or with a profile; Molecular Nodes builds cartoons by making curves through the backbone and sweeping a profile along them. Point cloud : points with a radius and no connections. A Molecular Nodes model is a mesh with only vertices, which behaves like points; Mesh to Points converts it to a true point cloud for instancing. Instances : references to a piece of geometry placed many times, each with a transform. Cheap to display and render; Realize Instances turns them into real geometry when a later node needs to edit them. Volume : a grid of values in space, either as a Blender volume object or as named grids inside geometry. A density map is this. Grids have no vertices; they are converted to a mesh at a threshold (Chapter 6 )."},{"t":"Domains","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes.html#domains","x":"An attribute lives on one domain of its geometry: Point (vertices or points), Edge, Face, Face Corner, Spline (for curves), Instance, or Layer (for Grease Pencil). position is on Point. A face's shade-smooth flag is on Face. Molecular Nodes stores its atom data on Point, one row per atom. When a node needs an attribute on a different domain, Blender interpolates: reading a Point attribute on Face averages the face's corners. Store Named Attribute lets you pick the domain explicitly, and the Spreadsheet has a tab per domain so you can see where a column lives."},{"t":"Reading and writing attributes","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes.html#reading-and-writing-attributes","x":"Named Attribute (Shift + A › Geometry › Read › Named Attribute) reads an attribute by name, as a field of the chosen type: Float for b_factor , Integer for chain_id , Color for Color , Vector for position . An attribute that does not exist reads as zero, and the node shows a warning, which is the first thing to check when a chain does nothing. Store Named Attribute (Shift + A › Geometry › Write) writes a value into an attribute on a domain, creating it if needed. Geometry passes through. This is how a computed value, such as a distance, becomes something the material can read with its Attribute node. Capture Attribute stores a value under an anonymous name for use later in the same tree, which Chapter 3 explains. Attributes are also visible in the Data tab of the Properties editor under Attributes, where they can be added, removed and renamed by hand."},{"t":"What Molecular Nodes gives you","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes.html#what-molecular-nodes-gives-you","x":"On a model, on the Point domain ( Attributes ): Attribute Type Meaning position Vector Atom coordinates, scaled by the world scale Color Color The current per-atom color, written by the Color nodes chain_id Int Chain, as an integer index; the names are in the Molecular Nodes panel res_id Int Residue number res_name Int Residue type as an integer code atomic_number Int Element b_factor Float B-factor, or pLDDT for AlphaFold models vdw_radii Float Van der Waals radius in world units sec_struct Int Secondary structure class is_backbone , is_side_chain , is_alpha_carbon Bool Backbone classification entity_id , model_id , occupancy , charge and others Various From the file, where present Strings cannot be attributes, so chain and residue names are stored as integers, and the Molecular Nodes select nodes translate between the two. On a map, the data is a grid named after the file, not a Point attribute. After the Density Style Surface it is a mesh with a Color attribute and nothing else, which is why coloring a map needs data sampled from the model (Chapter 5 )."},{"t":"World scale","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes.html#world-scale","x":"Molecular Nodes scales coordinates on import. In Blender 5 the scale is 0.1, so 1 nm equals 1 m and a 15 nm complex is 15 m across; in earlier versions it was 0.01, so 1 Å equaled 1 cm and the same complex was 1.5 m. Every distance in this guide, from Subsurface Scale to the wet collar's reach, depends on it. Check once per file: select the model, press N › Item and read Dimensions, then compare with the real size."},{"t":"Exercise 2.1: read the Spreadsheet","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-2-1","x":"Select the model and set the Spreadsheet's menu to Original, which shows the atoms exactly as Group Input receives them. Show only one residue with a row filter. The Spreadsheet cannot sort, but it can filter: with the mouse over the Spreadsheet press N to open its sidebar, add a row filter with the + button, set its column to res_id , Equal To, 19, and make sure the funnel icon (Use Filter) in the Spreadsheet's header is on. Ctrl + Shift + click the style node to view its output, then switch the domain list on the left from Vertex to Face. What you should see A handful of rows for residue 19, one per atom, each with its element and chain. After the style node, the Vertex tab still has the atom columns, because Style Cartoon copies them onto the cartoon's vertices, but the Face tab has different, face-level columns. Where a column appears tells you which domain it lives on."},{"t":"Exercise 2.2: write an attribute and read it in a material","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-2-2","x":"Add a Store Named Attribute node (Domain Point, Float, Name height ) and drop it onto the link between the style node and Join Geometry so it splices in. Its Geometry output must continue on to Group Output; a Store node on a side branch writes to a copy that is thrown away. Add a Position node and a Separate XYZ node, and draw Position → Separate XYZ's Vector input. Separate XYZ › Z is now each vertex's height in meters. Rescale it to 0 to 1 so the material needs no numbers. Add an Attribute Statistic node (Float, Point): style node's Geometry → its Geometry, Separate XYZ › Z → its Attribute. Add a Map Range: Separate XYZ › Z → Value, Attribute Statistic › Min → From Min, Attribute Statistic › Max → From Max, To Min 0, To Max 1. Then Map Range › Result → Store Named Attribute's Value. The Spreadsheet's Vertex tab now shows a height column running from 0 to 1. Make a material for the test. A Molecular Nodes object has no material in its slots, because the style node assigns it, so the Material tab only offers New: click New, double-click the name and rename it height_test , then on the style node click its Material field (it shows Default) and pick height_test . Open the Shader Editor: click the Editor Type menu in the top-left corner of the node editor and choose Shader Editor, or click the Shading workspace tab at the top of the window. With the model selected, the Shader Editor's header shows height_test . Press Shift + A, type Attribute to add one, and set its Name to height . Add a Color Ramp the same way, then draw Attribute › Fac → Color Ramp's Fac, and Color Ramp › Color → Principled BSDF's Base Color. Turn the Viewer off (the icon in its header) and view in Material Preview or Rendered. Solid mode never shows a shader. What you should see The model colored by height. The whole route from a geometry node to a pixel is Store Named Attribute on one side and Attribute on the other."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-2-check","x":"Which domain do Molecular Nodes atom attributes live on? Answer Point. Why are chain names stored as integers? Answer Geometry Nodes cannot store strings as attributes. A Named Attribute node reads all zeros. What is the likeliest cause? Answer The name is misspelled or the attribute is on a different geometry than the one connected. How do you confirm the world scale of a file? Answer N › Item › Dimensions on the model, compared with its real size."},{"t":"Fields","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-3","x":"Fields are the idea that makes Geometry Nodes different from a flowchart, and the one that confuses everyone at first. Once it is clear, every node in Chapter 4 reads the same way. Explain what a field is and recognize one by its diamond socket. Use Position, Index, Normal and Named Attribute as fields inside Math and Compare nodes. Drive a Selection input with a comparison. Explain why Capture Attribute exists and when you need it. Bring data from one geometry onto another with Sample Index, Sample Nearest and Geometry Proximity."},{"t":"A field is a function, not a number","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#a-field-is-a-function-not-a-number","x":"A gray socket carrying a number can carry two different things. A circle socket carries one value: a single roughness, a single scale. A diamond socket carries a field: a rule that produces a value for each element of whatever geometry it is eventually evaluated on. The Position node outputs a field; it does not output one position, it outputs \"the position of this element,\" and the node that consumes it, say Set Position, evaluates it once per vertex. So a Math node fed by Position → Separate XYZ › Z does not compute one number; it computes \"Z of this point\" for every point of the geometry the result is applied to. Which geometry? The one connected to the node that finally consumes the field. The field itself is geometry-free until then, which is why the Spreadsheet shows nothing for it and why the same chain can be reused on a different mesh. A diamond socket with a dot in it means the field is currently just a single value; a plain diamond means it depends on per-element data. Hover over any socket for a tooltip saying which."},{"t":"The built-in fields","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#the-built-in-fields","x":"Position : the location of the element, in the object's local space. Index : the element's number, 0 upward, in the order the geometry stores it. Molecular Nodes stores atoms in file order, so Index on the atoms is the atom's serial position. Normal : the direction a face or point faces. Named Attribute : any stored attribute, by name, including everything in the table in Chapter 2 . ID , Radius , Spline Parameter and the rest are the same idea for their domains. Any Math, Vector Math, Compare, Map Range, Color Ramp or Mix node accepts fields and stays a field, so you can write expressions: \"points whose b_factor is above 60,\" \"the distance of this point from the origin,\" \"a color from res_id .\""},{"t":"Why Position has no input","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#why-position-has-no-input","x":"The Spreadsheet is the right picture of a field. Each row is one element, and Position is one of its columns. A node with no geometry input and a diamond output is a question each element answers about itself: Position asks \"where am I?\", so it needs no wire telling it which geometry to read. A chain such as Position → Separate XYZ › Z → Map Range is a formula written once, height = (Z − Min) / (Max − Min), that nobody has run yet. Separate XYZ and Map Range only extend the formula. It runs the moment it reaches a node with a Geometry input beside the field input: Store Named Attribute, Attribute Statistic, Set Position, or a Selection socket on Delete Geometry. That node supplies the rows and computes the formula for each one. This is why Store Named Attribute must sit after Style Cartoon in Exercise 2.2 : the geometry flowing into it decides whose positions are read. Dashed links carry a formula; solid links carry one number. In the height chain, Z → Map Range and Map Range → Store are dashed. Min and Max arrive on solid links, because Attribute Statistic collapses the whole column into single numbers. Map Range mixes the two: a value that changes per row and two constants. The same Position can answer differently for two consumers. Feed Attribute Statistic the atoms (Group Input › Atoms) and feed the cartoon mesh to Store Named Attribute: Min and Max come from atom positions, while the written values come from cartoon vertices. Node What it asks each element Position Where am I? Normal Which way does the surface face here? Index Which row am I? Named Attribute What is in my column called X? ( chain_id , res_id , b_factor ) Spline Parameter How far along my curve am I, 0 to 1? Random Value A random number, different for each element Sample Grid What value does a grid hold at my position? (Chapter 7 , project 2) Scene Time and Object Info also have no geometry input, but they give one answer for everything, so they are single values, not fields. Some nodes read Position without a wire: Set Position, Geometry Proximity, Sample Nearest and Sample Grid use each element's own position when their position socket is left empty. That is how chain 1 of the slime measures distance from every cartoon vertex with no Position node in sight. The Shader Editor works the same way. Texture Coordinate and Geometry have no inputs either; they answer per shading point instead of per vertex."},{"t":"Selections","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#selections","x":"Most nodes have a Selection input, a boolean field that defaults to true everywhere. Connect a Compare node (Shift + A › Utilities › Math › Compare) and the node acts only where the comparison is true: Named Attribute chain_id → Compare (Equal, B = 0) → Selection on a Delete Geometry node deletes chain 0 only. Molecular Nodes' Select nodes are prebuilt comparisons of exactly this kind, which is why they plug into the Selection socket of every style node."},{"t":"Capture Attribute: freezing a field","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#capture-attribute-freezing-a-field","x":"A field is evaluated on the geometry at the point where it is consumed. If the geometry changes between where you set up the field and where you use it, the field evaluates on the new geometry, which is usually not what you meant. The standard example: after Instance on Points and Realize Instances, Index and Random Value count the vertices of every sphere instead of the atoms, so a random color per atom turns into a speckle per vertex. Capture Attribute (Shift + A › Attribute) evaluates the field on the geometry passing through it right now and carries the result forward as an anonymous attribute, so later nodes read the frozen values: Instance on Points copies it onto each instance, and Realize Instances onto every vertex of that instance. The rule of thumb: when a value should be computed on the geometry before a node that changes the geometry, capture it before that node. Fields wired into a node's own inputs need no capture: Instance on Points evaluates its Selection, Rotation and Scale on the points it receives."},{"t":"Data from another geometry","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#data-from-another-geometry","x":"Fields read from the geometry they are evaluated on. To read from a different geometry, three nodes bridge the gap: Sample Index (Shift + A › Geometry › Sample): read the value of an attribute at a given index on another geometry. Molecular Nodes uses it internally to copy atom data onto the cartoon it builds. Sample Nearest : for each element here, find the nearest element of another geometry and return its index; chain it into Sample Index to read that element's attribute. Older Molecular Nodes versions packaged this pair as a Sample Nearest Attribute node; with or without it, the pair is how a map borrows the model's colors. Geometry Proximity : for each position here, the closest point on another geometry and the distance to it. It is a distance field to any object, which is the basis of the wet collar in the slime project. In every case the other geometry arrives through an Object Info node (Shift + A › Input › Scene › Object Info), with Relative ticked so the two objects' transforms are reconciled."},{"t":"Exercise 3.1: see a field evaluate","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-3-1","x":"On the subdivided cube from Chapter 1 , add Set Position between the Subdivide and the Transform nodes. Position → Separate XYZ › Z → Math (Multiply, 0.5) → Combine XYZ (into Z only) → Set Position › Offset. Ctrl + Shift + click the Math node, and look at the Spreadsheet. What you should see The top of the cube lifts and the bottom sinks, by an amount that depends on each vertex's own height; and the Spreadsheet's Viewer column lists one number per vertex, which is the field evaluated."},{"t":"Exercise 3.2: a selection on the model","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-3-2","x":"On the model, before the style node, add Delete Geometry. Named Attribute (Float, b_factor ) → Compare (Greater Than, B 80) → Selection. What you should see The most mobile atoms vanish from the cartoon, and the threshold is a single number you can drag. Swap Delete Geometry for Separate Geometry and both halves are available to style differently."},{"t":"Exercise 3.3: capture before you instance","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-3-3","x":"Mesh to Points on the model's atoms (Group Input › Atoms, or Set Color's output so the chain colors come along) → Instance on Points with an Icosphere: Mesh to Points' output → its Points socket, and an Ico Sphere (Shift + A › Mesh › Primitives, Radius 0.08, Subdivisions 2) → its Instance socket, so one sphere lands on every atom. Position → Separate XYZ › Z → Map Range (To Min 0.5, To Max 2) → the Scale socket on the instancer, Instance on Points, so each sphere is sized by its atom's height. From Min and From Max come from an Attribute Statistic (Float, Point) with Mesh to Points' output in its Geometry and the same Z in its Attribute; with nothing in Geometry both read 0, and every sphere gets To Min. Add Realize Instances after Instance on Points, then Store Named Attribute (Float, Point, Name rand) with a Random Value (Float) as its Value. Spheres made in the tree have no material of their own, so give them one: click New in the Material tab and rename it, then add Set Material (Shift + A › Material) after Store with that material picked. In the Shader Editor (Editor Type menu at the node editor's top-left, as in Exercise 2.2 ), read rand in a material: Attribute › Fac → Color Ramp → Base Color. Then insert a Capture Attribute (Point) between Mesh to Points and Instance on Points, drag Random Value › Value into its empty bottom socket to add a capture item, and connect that item's output, on the node's right side, to Store's Value instead. What you should see After step 1 every sphere is sized by its atom's height, with no capture, because Instance on Points evaluates its Scale field on the points it receives. In step 2 the uncaptured version speckles every sphere, because after Realize Instances the random number is drawn once per sphere vertex; the captured version paints each sphere one solid color, because the number was drawn once per atom and carried onto every vertex of its sphere. The same trap catches a selection placed after instancing. Separate Geometry, Delete Geometry and Set Position on the Point domain reach inside each instance and evaluate on the instanced mesh itself. Named Attribute b_factor → Compare → Separate Geometry after Instance on Points therefore reads 0 on the sphere's own vertices: Greater Than removes every sphere and Less Than keeps them all, whatever the threshold. Set the domain to Instance, where Instance on Points has copied b_factor onto each instance, or wire the comparison into Instance on Points' Selection socket so the unwanted atoms never get a sphere."},{"t":"Exercise 3.4: four more formulas on the height chain","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-3-4","x":"Each variant reuses the tree from Exercise 2.2 . Change only what is named, keep Attribute Statistic and Map Range, and watch the height column and the material update. Side to side: connect Sepfarate XYZ › X instead of Z to both Map Range's Value and Attribute Statistic's Attribute. B-factor, like spectrum b in PyMOL: replace Position → Separate XYZ with Named Attribute (Float, Name b_factor ), feeding both nodes. Check first that b_factor appears among the Spreadsheet's columns on the Viewer output. Which way the surface faces: replace Position with Normal and keep Separate XYZ › Z. Upward-facing parts read 1 and downward-facing parts −1 before Map Range rescales them. Core versus surface: distance from the center only works for a sphere, so measure to the surface instead. Add Style Surface on Group Input › Atoms as a branch that goes nowhere else, then Geometry Proximity with Target Element set to Faces and that surface as Target; leave Sample Position empty. Its Distance is each vertex's depth below the nearest surface, including the lining of a channel or cavity. Feed Distance into Attribute Statistic and Map Range in place of Z. An SDF grid holds true distances only within its band (3 voxels for Points to SDF Grid), so Proximity is the exact route. What you should see A gradient across the model in each case: left to right, by refinement B-factor, by facing, and from the surface inward. In variant 4 the exposed loops are dark and the buried core bright whichever way the model is turned, because a depth does not depend on the view, and the lining of any channel through the model is dark as well."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-3-check","x":"What is the difference between a circle socket and a diamond socket? Answer A circle carries one value; a diamond carries a field, a rule evaluated per element. On which geometry is a field evaluated? Answer The geometry connected to the node that consumes it. What does Capture Attribute protect you from? Answer A field evaluating on later, changed geometry instead of the geometry you meant. Which node gives a distance from every point of one object to the surface of another? Answer Geometry Proximity."},{"t":"The core vocabulary","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-4","x":"Geometry Nodes has several hundred nodes. These twenty cover nearly every tree you will read or build. Each is listed by the menu path under Shift + A, with search by name as the faster route. Node Menu Takes Gives Use it for Set Position Geometry › Write Geometry, Position or Offset field Moved geometry Deforming, pushing along normals, jitter Transform Geometry Geometry › Operations Geometry, Translation, Rotation, Scale Moved geometry Placing a part, scaling a whole map Join Geometry Geometry Several geometries One geometry Combining two styles, adding a collar to a map Separate Geometry Geometry › Operations Geometry, Selection Selected and Inverted Two styles for two parts of one model Delete Geometry Geometry › Operations Geometry, Selection The rest Hiding solvent, trimming a map Set Material Geometry › Material Geometry, Selection, Material Geometry with material Assigning your slime material after a join Mesh to Points Mesh › Operations Mesh Point cloud Preparing atoms for instancing Instance on Points Instances Points, Instance geometry, Scale, Rotation Instances Spheres on atoms, arrows on residues Realize Instances Instances Instances Real mesh Before any node that edits instance geometry Curve Line, Curve Circle, Bézier Segment Curve › Primitives Points or parameters A curve Strands, profiles for sweeps Set Curve Radius Curve › Write Curve, Radius field Curve Tapering strands Curve to Mesh Curve › Operations Curve, Profile curve Mesh Turning a strand or a backbone into a tube Spline Parameter Curve › Read Curve (field) Factor 0 to 1 along the spline Driving taper or color along a strand Points to Volume, Volume to Mesh Point, Volume Points and radius; volume and threshold Volume; mesh Blobby envelopes around atoms Mesh to SDF Grid, Grid to Mesh Volume Mesh, voxel size; grid SDF grid; mesh The meniscus chain (Chapter 6 ) SDF Grid Boolean, SDF Grid Fillet Volume › Operations Two grids; a grid and Iterations Combined grid Smooth unions Geometry Proximity Geometry › Sample Target geometry, Source Position Position and Distance fields Wetness by distance to the map Sample Nearest, Sample Index Geometry › Sample Another geometry, an attribute Index; the value at an index Borrowing the model's color for the map Named Attribute, Store Named Attribute Geometry › Read, Write Name; name, value, domain Field; geometry with attribute Reading chain_id ; writing wet Object Info Input › Scene An object Its geometry, location, rotation Bringing the map into the model's tree Three habits make the table usable. First, Geometry passes through almost every node, so chains are built by inserting a node onto an existing link (drop it on the link and it splices in). Second, the gray inputs are fields, so any of them can be driven by a computation rather than a number. Third, a node that does not do what you expect is usually receiving the wrong geometry type: a Set Curve Radius on a mesh, a Realize Instances on nothing instanced. Recognize the twenty nodes that do most of the work in any tree. Say, for each, what goes in, what comes out, and what you would use it for on a molecule."},{"t":"Exercise 4.1: spheres on atoms, by hand","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-4-1","x":"Start clean: hide your other molecules (the eye icons in the Outliner) and import the structure again with Molecular Nodes, from its tab in the viewport sidebar (N), the way you imported it the first time; any style will do, since you will delete it. The new object comes with its own node tree: check that the breadcrumb at the top of the node editor names the new object. In that tree, delete the style node, which leaves Set Color's Atoms output free. Set Color › Atoms → Mesh to Points' Mesh, then Mesh to Points → Instance on Points with Shift + A › Mesh › Primitives › Ico Sphere (Radius 1, Subdivisions 2) as the Instance. With a radius of 1, the Scale you give each copy is its radius in scene units. Named Attribute (Float, vdw_radii ) → Scale on the instancer, so every sphere takes its element's van der Waals radius; Molecular Nodes stores it in scene units, 0.17 for carbon. For ball-and-stick proportions, put Math (Multiply, 0.3) in between. Then Realize Instances → Set Material → Group Output. What you should see A space-filling model built from four nodes, with atom radii by element. This is what Molecular Nodes' Style Spheres does inside, plus color."},{"t":"Exercise 4.1, part 2: one look per element","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-4-1-part-2","x":"Molecular Nodes stores each atom's element as atomic_number : 1 hydrogen, 6 carbon, 7 nitrogen, 8 oxygen, 16 sulfur. Instance on Points and Realize Instances carry it onto every vertex of that atom's sphere, so Set Material nodes after Realize Instances can choose a material by element. Check which elements your model has. With the Viewer on Realize Instances' output, add a Spreadsheet row filter on atomic_number , Equal To 1. Cryo-EM models refined in Phenix usually carry no hydrogens, so expect no rows; to have them, add hydrogens in ChimeraX ( addh ), save the model, and import that file. Make one material per element. In the Material tab press + to add a slot, click New and rename it; repeat for atom_C (light gray, Roughness 0.6), atom_O (red), atom_N (blue), atom_S (yellow) and atom_H (white). The slots make each material reachable from the Shader Editor's Slot menu. Give them different Roughness or Coat values too, and the elements differ in finish as well as color. After Realize Instances, chain five Set Material nodes. The first sets atom_C with nothing in Selection, so every sphere starts as carbon. Each of the others sets one element, with Selection = Compare (Integer, Equal, B = that element's number) whose A comes from one shared Named Attribute (Integer, atomic_number ). Optional: to hide hydrogens, as most figures do, put Delete Geometry before Mesh to Points with Selection = Compare (Integer, Equal, A = atomic_number , B = 1). What you should see The space-filling model in the standard CPK colors, carbon gray, oxygen red, nitrogen blue, sulfur yellow, with hydrogens white and visibly smaller, since their van der Waals radius is 1.2 Å against carbon's 1.7 Å. A later Set Material overrides an earlier one only where its selection is true, which is why carbon goes first as the catch-all. One material instead of five: in a single material, Attribute ( atomic_number ) › Factor → Map Range (From Min 0, From Max 20, To Min 0, To Max 1) → Color Ramp set to Constant, with a stop just below each element's value: white at 0.03, gray at 0.28, blue at 0.33, red at 0.38, yellow at 0.78. With Constant interpolation each atom takes the stop at or below its value, and placing the stops slightly below keeps rounding from dropping an atom into the previous color. This colors by element but gives every element the same finish, which is what separate materials add."},{"t":"Exercise 4.2: a tube from scratch","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-4-2","x":"Curve Line (Start 0,0,0, End 0,0,1) → Resample Curve (Count 32) → Set Curve Radius with Spline Parameter › Factor → Float Curve (a dip in the middle) → Curve to Mesh with a Curve Circle (Radius 0.02) as Profile → Set Material. What you should see A tube that is thin in the middle, which is the strand from the slime project. Replace the Curve Line with a Bézier Segment and it sags."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-4-check","x":"Which node must come before editing the mesh of instanced spheres? Answer Realize Instances. Which node brings another object's geometry into a tree? Answer Object Info. What is the quickest way to insert a node into an existing chain? Answer Drop it onto the link. Which pair of nodes writes a computed value for the material to read? Answer Store Named Attribute in the tree, Attribute in the material."},{"t":"Reading and editing a Molecular Nodes tree","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-5","x":"Molecular Nodes' node groups are ordinary Geometry Nodes groups with a consistent design, so once you know the pattern, every tree it makes is readable ( Fetch from the PDB ). Read the default model tree and the default density tree node by node. Give different chains different styles and materials in one object. Color a density surface from the model it encloses. Expose a value to the modifier panel so it can be changed without opening the tree."},{"t":"The model tree","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes.html#the-model-tree","x":"Group Input carries the atoms as points with their attributes. The first node is a Color node, for example Color Common or Color Chain, which writes the Color attribute by element or by chain; it changes data, not geometry. The last node is a Style node, Style Cartoon, Style Ribbon, Style Surface, Style Sticks, Style Ball and Stick or Style Spheres, which builds new geometry from the atoms and copies Color onto it. Group Output shows the style's result. Every style node has a Selection socket, so a Select node (Select Chain, Select Res ID and the rest, under Shift + A › Molecular Nodes › Select) plugged into it limits the style to part of the model, and a Material socket, which is how the Recipes part assigns your materials. To add nodes from the Molecular Nodes library use Shift + A › Molecular Nodes, which has submenus for Style, Select, Color, Animate, Topology, Density and Utilities. Each is a node group; Tab opens it to see how it is built, and this part's chapters are enough to read what you find inside."},{"t":"The density tree","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes.html#the-density-tree","x":"Group Input carries the volume. A Density Style Surface, Wire or ISO Surface node turns it into a mesh at the Threshold and gives it a color and material. Group Output shows the mesh. There are no atom attributes anywhere in this tree, which is why the map's material has only the style's Color to work with unless you sample from the model."},{"t":"Two styles in one object","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes.html#two-styles-in-one-object","x":"The style must be the last thing applied to a piece of geometry, because its output is a mesh and no longer atom-like. To show cartoon for one chain and sticks for another, run two style nodes in parallel from the same Color node, each with its own Select node on Selection, and merge them with Join Geometry before Group Output. Each branch can carry its own material. The same pattern gives ribbons for the backbone and ball-and-stick for a ligand: Select Res Name picks the ligand into one branch, and its inverse (the Select node's Inverted output, or a Boolean Math Not) picks the rest into the other."},{"t":"Coloring a map from the model","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes.html#coloring-a-map-from-the-model","x":"The map mesh has no chain data, so it borrows the model's: for every vertex of the surface, find the nearest atom and copy its Color . Current Molecular Nodes versions no longer list a Sample Nearest Attribute node under Density, so build the same thing from Blender's own nodes in the map's tree. Object Info (Relative on) brings in the model. Sample Nearest (Point) takes the model's geometry and returns, for each surface vertex, the index of the nearest atom. Sample Index (Color, Point) takes the model's geometry, Named Attribute (Color, Color ) as its Value and Sample Nearest › Index as its Index, and returns that atom's color. Store Named Attribute (Color, Point, Name Color ) writes it onto the surface, between the Density Style node and Join Geometry. The model object should ideally be outputting atoms, not a cartoon, so that every bit of density takes the color of the atom inside it, side chains and glycans included; the CryoEM Maps tutorial duplicates the model and removes the style on the copy ( CryoEM Maps , which still shows the older node). On that copy, also mute the Color Element node (M), so every atom carries its chain color: Color Element paints nitrogens blue and oxygens red, and a surface sampled from them comes out speckled. Sampling the cartoon object directly also works and needs no copy, because the cartoon's vertices carry the chain colors of their alpha carbons; but the cartoon holds only the backbone, so density over side chains, glycans and ligands takes the color of the nearest backbone, which at a chain interface can be the neighboring chain's. The material then reads Color with an Attribute node, as in the Recipes part ."},{"t":"Exposing inputs","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exposing-inputs","x":"Any value in a tree can become a slider on the modifier panel: drag a link from the node's input socket to the empty socket at the bottom of Group Input, and name it in the N panel's Group tab. A density threshold, a wetness reach or a strand radius exposed this way can be adjusted from the Modifier Properties without opening the node editor, and keyframed there for animation. Group Input sockets also accept an Object, which is how a tree can be pointed at a different map without editing it."},{"t":"Exercise 5.1: two styles, two materials","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-5-1","x":"On the model, add a second style node, Style Ball and Stick, fed from the same Color node, with Select Chain set to one chain on its Selection; set the cartoon's Select Chain to the inverse. Join Geometry both into Group Output. Give the cartoon branch the satin plastic material and the ball-and-stick branch glossy candy. What you should see One chain as colored sticks with glossy balls, the rest as a satin cartoon, in one object."},{"t":"Exercise 5.2: expose the threshold","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-5-2","x":"On the map, drag the Density Style Surface's Threshold input to Group Input and name it Contour . In Modifier Properties, change Contour and watch the surface. What you should see The contour level as a slider on the modifier, which is how you would keyframe a contour sweep for a talk."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-5-check","x":"Why must a style node be last in its branch? Answer Its output is a mesh, not atoms; later Molecular Nodes nodes expect atoms. How do two styles end up in one object? Answer Parallel branches from one Color node, joined by Join Geometry. Why does the map need Sample Nearest and Sample Index to show chain colors? Answer Its mesh carries no atom data; the pair copies Color from the nearest atom of the model. How do you make a tree value adjustable from the modifier panel? Answer Connect it to a new Group Input socket and name it."},{"t":"Volumes, grids and SDFs","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-6","x":"A density map is a grid, so this chapter is the one that explains what your maps are and what the SDF chain in the slime project does. Explain what a grid is, and the difference between a density grid and an SDF grid. Convert between meshes and grids in both directions and choose a voxel size. Build smooth unions, offsets and fillets with the SDF nodes."},{"t":"What a grid is","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes.html#what-a-grid-is","x":"A grid is a value at every point of a regular 3D lattice of voxels. A cryo-EM map is a grid of density values; the Density Style node draws the surface where the value crosses the threshold. A signed distance field, SDF, is a grid of a different quantity: at every voxel, the distance to the nearest surface, negative inside and positive outside. Its surface is where the value crosses zero. SDFs are what make shape operations easy: union is the minimum of two distances, intersection the maximum, offset adds a constant, and smoothing filters the values. Meshes make these operations hard; grids make them one node each. In Blender 5, grids travel inside geometry alongside meshes and curves, with a name each. Get Named Grid and Store Named Grid read and write them; Grid Info reports voxel size and bounds; the Spreadsheet's Volume Grids tab lists them. A Molecular Nodes map carries one grid named after the file."},{"t":"Converting","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes.html#converting","x":"Grid to Mesh (Shift + A › Volume › Operations): a surface at a threshold, with Adaptivity to reduce triangles on flat regions. For a density grid the threshold is the contour level; for an SDF it is 0. This is what the Density Style Surface does inside. Mesh to SDF Grid : a closed mesh becomes an SDF grid. Voxel Size sets resolution; Band Width, in voxels, is how far from the surface distances are stored. A mesh with holes gives a grid with leaks. Mesh to Density Grid : a mesh becomes a filled density grid, for use with scatter and fog rather than shape operations. Points to SDF Grid : points with radii become a blobby SDF, which is the metaball effect as a grid; useful for a cloud of atoms as one surface. Points to Volume and Volume to Mesh : the older, simpler pair, which still works and is what Molecular Nodes' Style Surface uses for the molecular surface. Field to Grid : any field evaluated on a lattice becomes a grid, which is how a formula or a distance becomes a volume."},{"t":"Shape operations on SDFs","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes.html#shape-operations-on-sdfs","x":"SDF Grid Boolean : Union, Intersection or Difference of two grids. Both need the same voxel size. SDF Grid Fillet : rounds the concave creases of a grid a little further with each of its Iterations, which is what turns a union into a smooth blend and gives the slime a meniscus. The rounding is measured in voxels, so a coarser Voxel Size gives a larger fillet for the same Iterations. SDF Grid Offset : grows or shrinks the surface by a distance, the grid version of a Solidify or a shell. SDF Grid Mean and Median , Laplacian : smoothing filters, each a different flavor of blur on the distances, for taking the voxel steps out of a surface or the noise out of a map-derived one. Grid Dilate & Erode , Prune Grid , Clip Grid : housekeeping, for trimming a grid to a box or removing stray voxels."},{"t":"Choosing a voxel size","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes.html#choosing-a-voxel-size","x":"Voxel size is the resolution of everything after it. Too large and the surface shows steps and loses detail; too small and the grid is slow and heavy, since halving the voxel size multiplies the voxel count by eight. A rule for a meniscus: voxel size about a tenth of the tube width, so a 0.1 m tube uses 0.01 m voxels. For an envelope around a whole map, a fiftieth of the map's width is plenty. Match the voxel size on every grid that meets in a Boolean."},{"t":"The map's own threshold","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes.html#the-map-s-own-threshold","x":"The contour level on a density map is a threshold on density; the SDF nodes need a distance. To treat a map as a shape, first make a mesh from it at the contour level (the style node does this), then Mesh to SDF Grid. Going the other way, Grid to Mesh on the raw density grid at a chosen threshold reproduces the style node without Molecular Nodes, which is useful when you want the map in a tree with no add-on dependency."},{"t":"Exercise 6.1: smooth union","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-6-1","x":"A Cube and a UV Sphere overlapping, both in one tree via Object Info. Mesh to SDF Grid on each (Voxel Size 0.02), SDF Grid Boolean (Union), Grid to Mesh. Insert SDF Grid Fillet before Grid to Mesh with Iterations 0, 3 and 10. What you should see A cube and sphere fused with a sharp crease, then a soft crease, then a melted join. That melt is the meniscus of the slime project."},{"t":"Exercise 6.2: atoms as one blob","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-6-2","x":"On the model, Mesh to Points → Points to SDF Grid (Radius 0.08, Voxel Size 0.02) → Grid to Mesh → Set Material. What you should see A space-filling envelope built from one grid, smoother than instanced spheres. Raise Radius and the envelope swells into a molecular surface."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-6-check","x":"What value does an SDF store at each voxel? Answer The signed distance to the nearest surface: negative inside, positive outside. What threshold draws the surface of an SDF grid? Answer Zero. Why must two grids share a voxel size before a Boolean? Answer The operation compares them voxel by voxel. What does SDF Grid Fillet do? Answer Rounds concave creases, further with each iteration, turning a union into a smooth blend."},{"t":"Worked projects: the sticky slime and local resolution","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-7","x":"This chapter builds two projects with everything from Chapters 2 to 6 . The first is the sticky slime: the Recipes part gives its settings, this chapter the reasoning, chain by chain. Build it on your map and model with the slime material from the Recipes part already assigned. The second, after the slime exercises, paints cryoSPARC's local resolution onto the map surface and onto the model. Build the four effects of the sticky slime as node chains and explain every node in them. Combine data from two objects in one tree. Leave the result adjustable from the modifier panel. Paint cryoSPARC's local resolution onto a map surface and onto the model built into it with Sample Grid."},{"t":"Chain 1: the wet collar (model tree)","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chain-1-the-wet-collar-model-tree","x":"The goal is a per-vertex number on the cartoon, 1 where it touches the slime and 0 a short way out, that the material reads. Object Info , Object: the map, Relative on. This brings the map's evaluated geometry, which is the surface the style node made, into the model's tree, in the model's coordinate space. Geometry Proximity , Target Element: Faces. Target: the Object Info geometry. Its Distance output is a field: for every element it is evaluated on, the distance to the nearest point of the map surface. Map Range , From 0 to 0.2, To 1 to 0, Clamp on. Turns distance into wetness: 1 at contact, 0 at 0.2 m and beyond. The From Max is the reach; expose it to Group Input as Wet reach . Store Named Attribute , inserted on the link after the style node, Domain Point, Float, Name wet , Value from the Map Range. Because the geometry passing through is the cartoon, the proximity field is evaluated on the cartoon's vertices, which is what you want. The material then reads wet with an Attribute node into Coat Weight, a darkening Mix and Roughness, as the Recipes part lists. If wet reads as zero in the material, the Store node is before the style node rather than after it, so the attribute was written on atoms the style discarded."},{"t":"Chain 2: the meniscus (map tree)","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chain-2-the-meniscus-map-tree","x":"The goal is a surface that is the slime and the tubes fused, with the crease between them rounded. Density Style Surface as before, its output mesh going on to the next node. Mesh to SDF Grid , Voxel Size 0.02, Band Width 3. The slime surface as distances. Object Info , Object: the model, Relative on, into a second Mesh to SDF Grid with the same Voxel Size. The model must be outputting closed mesh geometry: cartoon, ribbon or sticks are closed tubes; Style Spheres as points is not a mesh. SDF Grid Boolean , Union. The minimum of the two distances, which is both shapes as one. SDF Grid Fillet , Iterations 3. Rounds the concave crease where the tubes enter the slime into a curve that climbs the tube. Expose Iterations as Meniscus . Grid to Mesh , Threshold 0, Adaptivity 0.2 to keep the mesh light. Set Material with the slime material, then Group Output. The union mesh wraps the tubes in a thin slime skin everywhere, not only at the exits. Two ways to handle it: keep the original model object visible so its own material shows through the skin, which works because the skin is thin and the slime is translucent; or limit the union to the exits by inserting Delete Geometry on the model's mesh before its Mesh to SDF Grid, deleting where Geometry Proximity to the slime surface is greater than the meniscus reach, so only the parts of the tubes near the surface are fused in."},{"t":"Chain 3: strands (a new object)","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chain-3-strands-a-new-object","x":"Strands are curves with a tapered radius, swept into tubes. On a new Empty mesh object with a Geometry Nodes modifier: Bézier Segment , Start and End exposed as From and To , with Start Handle and End Handle pulled downward for sag. Resample Curve , Count 32, so the taper has enough points. Spline Parameter › Factor → Float Curve shaped as a dip to 0.3 at the center → Set Curve Radius . Curve to Mesh , Profile Curve: a Curve Circle (Resolution 8, Radius 0.01). Set Material : slime. To place strands, set From to a point on a tube just outside the surface and To to a point on the slime. For several strands, instead of duplicating objects, feed the whole chain a set of points: Points (Count 4) with randomized positions near an exit, Instance on Points with the strand as the instance, and Realize Instances; each strand then needs its own sag, which a Random Value into the handle offsets gives."},{"t":"Chain 4: drips","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chain-4-drips","x":"Drips are easiest as a metaball object, outside Geometry Nodes, because metaballs merge by themselves. In Geometry Nodes the equivalent is Points to SDF Grid on two points, one large radius and one small below it, Grid to Mesh: the two blobs merge into a drop shape because an SDF of points is the metaball function. Animate the lower point downward and the drop stretches and separates, which is what Points to SDF Grid gives for free."},{"t":"Putting it together","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#putting-it-together","x":"Four objects: the model with chain 1, the map with chain 2, a strand object with chain 3, and drips. One slime material on everything except the model, which has the chain-colored material reading wet . Three exposed values, Wet reach , Meniscus and Contour , on the modifier panels. A backlight. That is the entire effect, and every node in it is one you can now explain."},{"t":"Exercise 7.1: the whole chain, with the Viewer","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-7-1","x":"Build chain 1 and, after each node, Ctrl + Shift + click it and read the Spreadsheet's Viewer column. What you should see At the Proximity node, distances in meters; at the Map Range, values between 0 and 1 with the vertices near the map at 1; at the Store node, a wet column in the attribute list. If a column is missing, the previous node is where the chain broke."},{"t":"Exercise 7.2: the meniscus with a limited union","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-7-2","x":"Build chain 2 with the Delete Geometry limit described above, exposing the reach. What you should see The slime surface rises up each tube for a short distance and the tubes beyond it keep their own material, with no thin skin over the whole model. Change the reach and the collar of fused slime grows and shrinks."},{"t":"Project 2: local resolution from cryoSPARC","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#project-2-local-resolution-from-cryosparc","x":"The goal is the figure ChimeraX makes with color sample : the map surface colored by local resolution, and the same colors on the model built into that map. cryoSPARC's Local Resolution Estimation job writes *_map_locres.mrc , a map on the same grid as the half maps in which each voxel holds a resolution in Å instead of a density. (Local Filtering writes a filtered density, which is not the file to sample.) In Geometry Nodes the Sample Grid node reads that map at any position, and because the result is stored as an attribute, one chain serves both the surface and the cartoon."},{"t":"Step 1: three imports in one frame","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#step-1-three-imports-in-one-frame","x":"Import the density ( *_map_sharp.mrc , or the unsharpened map) through the Molecular Nodes Density panel with Center Density off and Invert Data off. This object's tree makes the isosurface. Import the local resolution map ( *_map_locres.mrc ) the same way, with the same two settings. Its tree will try to contour resolution values as if they were density, which means nothing, so hide the object with its eye icon in the Outliner. It only needs to exist so its grid can be read. Import the model with Centre off. A model refined into this map in Phenix already sits in the map's coordinate frame, so with nothing recentered the three objects overlap in the viewport. If the model sits outside the density, one import was centered: import it again rather than moving objects by hand. Find the grid's name: select the locres object and choose Volume Grids in the Spreadsheet's domain list. A map imported with Molecular Nodes has one grid, named density ."},{"t":"Step 2: resolution onto the map surface","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#step-2-resolution-onto-the-map-surface","x":"In the density object's tree, after the style node that makes the mesh (Style Density Surface): Object Info (Relative) with Object set to the locres object → Get Named Grid (Float, Name density ); search the Add menu for \"named grid\". It takes the volume at its Geometry input and outputs the grid. Sample Grid (Float, Interpolation Trilinear): Get Named Grid › Grid → Grid. Leave Position empty; it defaults to Position, so each element reads the resolution where it sits. Value is a field: \"the resolution in Å at this vertex.\" Map Range: Sample Grid › Value → Value, From Min 3.0, From Max 6.0, To Min 0, To Max 1, Clamp on. Type the range the color bar will span, in Å, taken from the job's reported range. Do not use Attribute Statistic here: a fixed range keeps panels comparable and matches a printed color bar. Store Named Attribute (Float, Point, Name locres ): the style node's mesh → Geometry, Map Range › Result → Value, in line before Group Output. Check with the Viewer on Store's output, Vertex tab: a locres column from 0 to 1 that varies across the surface. The same number everywhere means Sample Grid is returning a constant: the grid name is wrong, the two maps do not overlap, or Object Info points at the wrong object. World scale needs no correction, because the grid's values are resolutions, not coordinates, and both maps were scaled alike."},{"t":"Step 3: the material","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#step-3-the-material","x":"Duplicate height_test from Exercise 2.2 and rename it locres . In the Shader Editor change the Attribute node's Name to locres . On the Color Ramp make the left stop blue (0, the best resolution), add a white stop at 0.5, and make the right stop red (1, the worst). Assign it through the density style node's Material field. The convention in most papers is blue for high resolution and red for low; keep the same ramp and the same two numbers in every panel of a figure."},{"t":"Step 4: the same values on the model","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#step-4-the-same-values-on-the-model","x":"In the model's tree, after Style Cartoon, repeat step 2 word for word: Object Info to the locres object → Get Named Grid → Sample Grid → Map Range with the same 3.0 and 6.0 → Store Named Attribute locres on the cartoon mesh. Each cartoon vertex reads the resolution at its own position, so a flexible domain comes out red on both the map and the model. Assign the same locres material through Style Cartoon's Material field. This works for the reason in Chapter 3 : Sample Grid has no geometry input of its own, and the geometry that reaches Store Named Attribute decides where the samples are taken. Swap the surface mesh for the cartoon and the sampling chain does not change."},{"t":"Variants","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#variants","x":"Per residue instead of per vertex: after Sample Grid, Accumulate Field (Float) with the value as Value and Named Attribute ures_id as Group ID gives each residue's Total; ures_id is unique across chains, where res_id repeats. A second Accumulate Field with Value 1 and the same Group ID gives the count. A Math node set to Divide gives the mean, and every vertex of a residue carries it. Model in weak density: sample the density grid instead of the locres grid (Object Info to the density object). Parts of the model sitting in weak density go dark, which is measure mapvalues in ChimeraX. Color bar: put the Color Ramp in a node group (Ctrl + G) and use it in a second material on a plane, with Texture Coordinate › Generated → Separate XYZ › X as the ramp's Fac. Render the plane beside the figure, labeled with the two numbers from Map Range, or draw the bar in Illustrator with those numbers. Outside the mask: where the job's mask ends, the locres map holds a fill value rather than an estimate, and those vertices land at one end of the ramp. If a fringe of the surface shows a solid end color, read its value with the Viewer, then cut it with Delete Geometry using a Compare on the same Sample Grid value as the selection."},{"t":"Exercise 7.3: the map by local resolution","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-7-3","x":"Build steps 1 to 3 on your sharpened map and its locres map, with the Viewer on Store Named Attribute's output. Set Interpolation to Nearest first, then to Trilinear. What you should see With Nearest, patches of flat color the size of a voxel; with Trilinear, smooth blends between them. The best-resolved core sits at the blue end and the periphery and any flexible domain at the red end, in the same places ChimeraX colors them."},{"t":"Exercise 7.4: the model by local resolution","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-7-4","x":"Build step 4 on the model. Duplicate the locres material for the map, set its Alpha to 0.3 (Render Method Blended in EEVEE), and render the surface over the cartoon with both colored by locres . What you should see The cartoon's colors match the surface around it, because both read the same voxels. Where they disagree, the model has drifted out of the map's frame; check the Center settings of the imports."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-7-check","x":"Why must Store Named Attribute come after the style node in chain 1? Answer So the attribute is written on the cartoon's vertices rather than on atoms the style discards. What does Relative on Object Info do? Answer Puts the other object's geometry into this object's coordinate space, so distances and unions line up. Why does the union wrap the whole model in slime, and what limits it? Answer The Boolean fuses all of the model's mesh; deleting the parts far from the surface before the SDF step limits it to the exits. Which single node produces a metaball-like merge of points? Answer Points to SDF Grid. Why does the local resolution chain use typed numbers in Map Range instead of Attribute Statistic? Answer So every panel and the color bar share one scale; the statistic would change the scale with each map. Why can one Sample Grid chain color both the surface and the cartoon? Answer Sample Grid is a field: the geometry reaching Store Named Attribute decides where it is evaluated."},{"t":"Working habits and debugging","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-8","x":"Find where a tree breaks in under a minute. Keep trees readable and reusable with frames and node groups. Keep heavy trees responsive, and bake what need not recompute."},{"t":"Debugging in order","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes.html#debugging-in-order","x":"Look at the socket colors and warnings. A red link is a type mismatch; a node with a warning triangle has a tooltip saying what is wrong, most often a missing attribute name or the wrong geometry type. View each node. Ctrl + Shift + click walks the Viewer along the chain. The viewport shows that node's geometry and the Spreadsheet shows its data. The first node whose output is not what you expect is the bug; everything before it is fine. Read the Spreadsheet, not the viewport. Row counts tell you whether a selection deleted everything or nothing; the domain tabs tell you whether an attribute landed on points or faces; the Viewer column shows a field's actual numbers. Check which geometry a field is evaluated on. If the values look right in the Spreadsheet but wrong in the result, the field is being consumed after a node that changed the geometry; capture it earlier (Chapter 3 ). Check the modifier order. Geometry Nodes runs in the modifier stack's order; a Subdivision Surface above it changes what Group Input receives. Mute, do not delete. M mutes a node so the geometry passes through unchanged, which isolates its effect without rebuilding anything."},{"t":"Keeping trees readable","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes.html#keeping-trees-readable","x":"Frames (Shift + A › Layout › Frame, or select nodes and Ctrl + J) box a chain with a label. One frame per effect, named for what it does, means a tree you can read in a year. Node groups (select nodes, Ctrl + G) collapse a chain into one node with its own inputs and outputs. The wet-collar chain as a group called Wetness with one Reach input is reusable on any object, and editing the group updates every copy. Tab enters a group, Tab exits. Reroutes (Shift + right-drag across a link) tidy long links. Name every exposed input in the N panel's Group tab, with a tooltip and a sensible min and max, so the modifier panel reads like a tool. Mark assets. Right-click a node group in the Blender File view and Mark as Asset, and it appears in the Asset Browser for drag-and-drop into any file, which is how Molecular Nodes itself ships its nodes."},{"t":"Performance","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes.html#performance","x":"The timing overlay (the node editor's Overlays popover, Timings) shows how long each node takes. The expensive ones are almost always voxel conversions at small voxel sizes, Realize Instances on many instances, and Mesh Boolean. Keep instances as instances until the last possible node; a million spheres as instances are cheap, realized they are a million meshes. Lower voxel size only where it shows. Build at a coarse size and halve it for the final render. Use Delete Geometry early to drop what the rest of the tree does not need, such as solvent atoms or the far side of a map. Bake (Shift + A › Geometry › Operations › Bake) stores a node's result on disk so it is not recomputed; put one after the SDF chain once the meniscus is settled, and the rest of the tree stays interactive."},{"t":"Zones, briefly","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes.html#zones-briefly","x":"Two node pairs form loops. A Simulation Zone carries geometry from one frame to the next, so what leaves the zone at frame 10 enters it at frame 11; it is how particles, growth and drips that accumulate are built, and it needs the timeline to play. A Repeat Zone runs its contents a fixed number of times in one evaluation, for iterative smoothing or building a chain of segments. Neither is needed for anything in this guide, but they are what you will meet next in other people's trees."},{"t":"Exercise 8.1: break and find","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-8-1","x":"In the wet-collar chain, misspell the attribute name in the material's Attribute node, then in the Store node, then switch Object Info's object to the wrong one. For each, find the break using only the ordered list above. What you should see The first two show as a warning on the Named Attribute or a missing Spreadsheet column; the third shows as a Proximity distance that is wrong everywhere. Each takes one Viewer click to locate."},{"t":"Exercise 8.2: make it a tool","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes.html#exercise-8-2","x":"Select the four nodes of the wet-collar chain, Ctrl + G, name the group Wetness , expose Reach and the target object as group inputs, and Mark as Asset. Apply it to a second model from the Asset Browser. What you should see One node on the second model's tree that does the whole job, with a Reach slider and an object picker. That is the difference between a chain and a tool."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-8-check","x":"What is the fastest way to find which node breaks a chain? Answer Walk the Viewer along it with Ctrl + Shift + click and read the Spreadsheet. What does muting a node do? Answer Passes geometry through unchanged, so you can isolate the node's effect. When should instances be realized? Answer As late as possible; realized instances are heavy. What does a Bake node save you? Answer Recomputing an expensive result every time something upstream changes."},{"t":"The chooser, a glossary and sources","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 9","u":"geometry-nodes.html#chapter-9","x":""},{"t":"I want this; use that","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 9","u":"geometry-nodes.html#i-want-this-use-that","x":"I want Use A value from the file on every atom Named Attribute with the name from the Chapter 2 table A computed value the material can read Store Named Attribute, then Attribute in the material Only some atoms A Select node, or Compare on an attribute, into a Selection socket Two representations in one object Two style branches joined with Join Geometry A sphere or arrow on every atom Mesh to Points, Instance on Points, Realize Instances only if needed The map colored by the model Sample Nearest → Sample Index on the model's atoms via Object Info, stored as Color Distance from each point to another object Geometry Proximity on Object Info's geometry Depth below the surface, any shape Geometry Proximity, Faces, with a Style Surface branch as Target A smooth blob around points Points to SDF Grid, Grid to Mesh Two shapes fused with a rounded join Mesh to SDF Grid on each, SDF Grid Boolean, SDF Grid Fillet, Grid to Mesh A tapered tube between two points Bézier Segment, Resample, Set Curve Radius, Curve to Mesh A slider on the modifier panel Connect the input to Group Input and name it A reusable tool Ctrl + G into a node group, Mark as Asset The value a field actually has Ctrl + Shift + click the node and read the Spreadsheet An expensive result frozen A Bake node after it Something that changes over frames A Simulation Zone The map or model colored by local resolution Sample Grid on the locres grid via Object Info and Get Named Grid, then Store Named Attribute"},{"t":"Glossary","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 9","u":"geometry-nodes.html#glossary","x":"Attribute : a named value stored on each element of a domain. Bake : storing a node's result on disk so it is not recomputed. Capture Attribute : freezing a field's values on the current geometry for use later in the tree. Domain : the level an attribute lives on: Point, Edge, Face, Face Corner, Spline, Instance. Field : a rule evaluated once per element of the geometry that consumes it; shown as a diamond socket. Grid : values on a regular lattice of voxels; a density map is one. Group Input, Group Output : where geometry enters and leaves a tree. Instance : a reference to geometry placed many times, cheap until realized. Node group : a tree collapsed into one node with its own sockets. Realize : convert instances into real editable geometry. Sample Grid : a field that reads a grid's value at each element's own position; the node behind local resolution coloring. SDF : signed distance field; a grid of distances to a surface, negative inside. Selection : a boolean field that limits where a node acts. Spreadsheet : the editor that shows a geometry's elements and attributes as a table. Viewer : the node that sends a geometry and a field to the viewport and Spreadsheet for inspection. Voxel size : the resolution of a grid, in scene units. World scale : the factor Molecular Nodes applies to coordinates on import."},{"t":"Sources","w":"Geometry Nodes, Chapter 9","u":"geometry-nodes.html#sources","x":"Geometry Nodes (Blender Manual), including Fields and Attributes Molecular Nodes attributes , Fetch from the PDB and CryoEM Maps (Molecular Nodes documentation)"},{"t":"Geometry Nodes for art","w":"Geometry Nodes for art","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html","x":"The same nodes that draw a cryo-EM map draw the paper spirals in the manual's Geometry Nodes introduction. This part turns the Geometry Nodes part around: the data comes from formulas instead of files, the goal is a feeling instead of a fact, and surprises are kept rather than debugged. Work through it in a new, empty scene. Notation. As in the Geometry Nodes part : Shift + A › menu is a path, Node › Socket names an output, and → is a link you draw. Every exercise starts from Add › Mesh › Plane with a new Geometry Nodes modifier and Group Input disconnected, unless it says otherwise."},{"t":"What changes when the goal is a picture","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-1","x":"Say what procedural art is and why a node tree suits it. Name the five parts every piece is built from. Adopt the working habit: expose, scrub, keep."},{"t":"The same tool, turned around","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#the-same-tool-turned-around","x":"In the Geometry Nodes part a tree starts with atoms from a file and ends in a faithful picture of them. Here the tree starts with nothing and ends in a shape that never existed, and the nodes are the same. A Molecular Nodes tree is a generator, a distribution and a style; a piece of art is the same three with the file replaced by a formula. Three things change. The data comes from primitives and math, so there is nothing to be faithful to. Randomness is a material, not a bug: a seed slider is often the best control in the tree. And judging the result means looking, not checking a column in the Spreadsheet."},{"t":"The five parts of any piece","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#the-five-parts-of-any-piece","x":"Part What it does Typical nodes Generator Makes something from nothing Grid, Cube, Curve Circle, Spiral, Points Distribution Decides where things go Distribute Points on Faces, Points with a formula, Curve to Points Repetition Copies one thing many times Instance on Points, Duplicate Elements Variation Makes the copies differ Index, Random Value, Noise Texture, Map Range Surface Gives it color and light Set Material, Store Named Attribute read by a material Most pieces are these five in that order, and most troubleshooting is asking which part is missing. A grid of identical cubes lacks variation; a noise landscape with nothing on it lacks repetition."},{"t":"The working habit","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#the-working-habit","x":"Expose every number you touch twice to the modifier panel and name it. Scrub it with the mouse while watching the viewport, and when something good happens by accident, duplicate the object (Shift + D) before you change anything else. A piece is usually chosen from a dozen accidents, not designed in advance. Keep Material Preview on, with a plain studio HDRI from the viewport shading popover, so color reads from the start. Save often: a scrubbed slider is easy to lose."},{"t":"Exercise 1.1: read the manual's spiral","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-1-1","x":"Open the Blender manual's Geometry Nodes introduction and look at the paper-spiral image without reading. Name its five parts in a sentence each: what generates, what distributes, what repeats, what varies, and what the surface does. What you should see A spiral curve swept into a ribbon (generator), a scatter of points (distribution), one ribbon copied onto each (repetition), rotation, size and color differing per copy (variation), and a matte pastel material under soft light (surface). Chapter 8 builds exactly this."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 1","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-1-check","x":"What replaces the file in an art tree? Answer A generator: a primitive, a formula, or a scatter of points. Which of the five parts is missing from a field of identical spheres? Answer Variation. What is the first thing to do when an accident looks good? Answer Duplicate the object before changing anything else. Why does the Spreadsheet matter less here? Answer The result is judged by looking; there is no reference to check against."},{"t":"Something from nothing","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-2","x":"Start a tree on an object that contributes no geometry. Use mesh primitives, curve primitives and the Points node as generators. Build the Spiral node into a coil and expose its controls."},{"t":"An object that gives nothing","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#an-object-that-gives-nothing","x":"Every tree needs an object to live on, but the object's own mesh need not be used. Add › Mesh › Plane, add a Geometry Nodes modifier, and in the tree disconnect Group Input from Group Output. Whatever you plug into Group Output replaces the plane. The object is now a container for the tree, and its origin is where the tree's (0, 0, 0) sits."},{"t":"Mesh primitives","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#mesh-primitives","x":"Shift + A › Mesh › Primitives holds Grid, Cube, UV Sphere, Ico Sphere, Cylinder, Cone and Mesh Circle. Each takes dimensions and vertex counts, and most output a UV Map field you can store for texturing. Grid is the workhorse: a flat sheet to scatter on, displace, or instance over. Ico Sphere at Subdivisions 1 is the cheap low-poly ball of a thousand pieces."},{"t":"Curve primitives","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#curve-primitives","x":"Shift + A › Curve › Primitives holds Curve Circle, Curve Line, Arc, Star, Quadrilateral, Bézier Segment, Quadratic Bézier and Spiral. A curve draws nothing until something gives it a surface (Chapter 5 ), so a curve primitive is a path, not a shape. Curve Circle and Quadrilateral double as profiles: the cross-section swept along another curve. The Spiral node is the one behind the manual's picture. Resolution is points per turn, Rotations the number of turns, Start Radius and End Radius the size at each end, and Height the rise over the whole length. Height 0 gives a flat coil, Height 2 a spring. Reverse flips the winding."},{"t":"Points from a count","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#points-from-a-count","x":"The Points node makes N points at one position; with a field in Position it makes N points wherever the formula says, which is how Chapter 4 draws a sunflower. Mesh to Points turns any mesh's vertices into points, and Distribute Points on Faces scatters them over faces (Chapter 3 )."},{"t":"Exercise 2.1: a coil","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-2-1","x":"Add Spiral (Resolution 32, Rotations 3, Start Radius 0.1, End Radius 1, Height 0) → Curve to Mesh's Curve, and Curve Circle (Radius 0.05) → Curve to Mesh's Profile Curve. Curve to Mesh → Group Output. Set Height to 1, then Rotations to 8, then Resolution to 6. Connect Rotations and End Radius to Group Input, name them, and scrub them from the modifier panel. What you should see A flat coiled tube, then a spring, then a tight spring, then a chunky hexagonal one when Resolution drops. The modifier panel shows two sliders that reshape it live."},{"t":"Exercise 2.2: a sheet and a ball","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-2-2","x":"Add Grid (Size 4 × 4, Vertices 40 × 40) and view it with Shade Smooth off. Replace it with Ico Sphere at Subdivisions 1, then 3. Join both with Join Geometry and move the sphere up 1 m with Transform Geometry. What you should see A flat lattice, a twenty-sided ball, a smoother ball, and finally the ball floating over the sheet, all from one object with no mesh of its own."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 2","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-2-check","x":"Why disconnect Group Input? Answer So the plane's own mesh plays no part; the tree builds everything. What does a curve primitive look like in a render? Answer Nothing, until Curve to Mesh or another node gives it a surface. What does Spiral's Height do? Answer Lifts the end of the coil above the start; 0 keeps it flat. Where is the tree's origin? Answer At the object's origin, the orange dot."},{"t":"Repetition","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-3","x":"Scatter points by density or by a formula. Copy a shape onto points with Instance on Points, and vary which shape with Pick Instance. Decide when instances must be realized."},{"t":"Points to put things on","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#points-to-put-things-on","x":"Distribute Points on Faces (Shift + A › Point) scatters points across any mesh. Random mode takes Density and Seed. Poisson Disk mode adds Distance Min, so no two points sit closer than that, which is what a tidy scatter needs. Its Rotation output aligns each point with the face normal, so things stand up on a slope. Mesh to Points puts one point on every vertex, and Curve to Points spaces them along a curve. Points with a formula in Position puts them wherever the math says."},{"t":"Instance on Points","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#instance-on-points","x":"Instance on Points (Shift + A › Instances) takes Points, Instance (the thing to copy), Rotation and Scale, and makes one lightweight copy per point. The copies share one mesh, so a million instances cost little until Realize Instances turns them into real geometry. Rotation and Scale are fields evaluated per point, which is where Chapter 4 's variation plugs in. Pick Instance, with a collection from Collection Info (Separate Children on) as the Instance, chooses one child per point by Instance Index. Index → Math Modulo 3 cycles through three shapes; Random Value (Integer, 0 to 2) picks at random."},{"t":"Realize or not","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#realize-or-not","x":"Leave instances as instances for speed and for the Object Info › Random trick in Chapter 6 . Realize when a later node needs real vertices: Set Position on the copies' points, Mesh Boolean, Merge by Distance, or any mesh-level smoothing. Realize Instances is where memory jumps, so put it last and only when needed."},{"t":"Exercise 3.1: a field of coils","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-3-1","x":"Grid (Size 6 × 6, Vertices 2 × 2) → Distribute Points on Faces (Poisson Disk, Distance Min 0.6, Density Max 10, Seed 0) → Instance on Points' Points. The coil from Exercise 2.1 (Curve to Mesh's output) → Instance on Points' Instance. Scrub Seed, then Distance Min. What you should see A few dozen coils in a tidy scatter, re-rolled on each Seed change, sparser as Distance Min grows. All identical for now; Chapter 4 fixes that."},{"t":"Exercise 3.2: three shapes by lot","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-3-2","x":"Make a collection called Shapes with a small Cube, Cone and Ico Sphere in it, each at the origin, and hide the collection in the Outliner. Collection Info (Separate Children on, Reset Children on) → Instance on Points' Instance, Pick Instance on, Random Value (Integer, Min 0, Max 2) → Instance Index. Replace Random Value with Index → Math Modulo 3. What you should see The scatter from 3.1 with a random mix of three shapes, then a strict repeating order of cube, cone, sphere."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 3","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-3-check","x":"What does Poisson Disk give that Random does not? Answer A minimum spacing between points. Why are a million instances cheap? Answer They share one mesh; only a transform is stored per copy. When must you realize? Answer When a later node needs the copies' own vertices, such as Set Position or a Boolean. What does Pick Instance need to work? Answer A geometry with several children, from Collection Info with Separate Children on, and an Instance Index."},{"t":"Variation","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-4","x":"Drive scale, rotation, position and color from Index, Random Value and Noise Texture. Write a formula into Points' Position and draw a sunflower. Keep variation adjustable with a seed and a strength."},{"t":"Three sources of difference","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#three-sources-of-difference","x":"Index is orderly: it counts 0, 1, 2 along the points, so Index → Map Range gives a gradient and Index → Math Modulo gives a repeat. Random Value is disorderly: a different number per element, re-rolled by Seed. Noise Texture is smooth: nearby points get similar values, so clumps and waves appear rather than static. Most pieces mix one orderly and one smooth source."},{"t":"Where variation plugs in","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#where-variation-plugs-in","x":"Scale on Instance on Points: Random Value (Float, 0.6 to 1.4), or Noise Texture › Fac → Map Range. Rotation: Random Value (Vector, Min 0 and Max 6.283 on Z only) → Instance on Points' Rotation, which spins each copy about its axis. Position: Set Position with Offset = Noise Texture › Color minus 0.5, scaled, which jitters or warps. Color: Store Named Attribute (Color, Point) on the points before instancing, read in the material (Chapter 6 ). Noise Texture's Scale is features per meter; Detail adds finer ripples and Roughness sets their strength. Set the node to 4D and feed W from Scene Time › Seconds for noise that drifts (Chapter 7 )."},{"t":"A formula in Position","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#a-formula-in-position","x":"Points (Count 400) with a field in Position places each point by a formula of its Index. The sunflower, or phyllotaxis, pattern is angle = Index × 2.39996 radians (the golden angle) and radius = 0.08 × √Index. Combine XYZ (radius × cos angle, radius × sin angle, 0) → Position."},{"t":"Exercise 4.1: make the coils differ","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-4-1","x":"On the field from Exercise 3.1 , Random Value (Float, 0.5 to 1.5, Seed 1) → Instance on Points' Scale. Random Value (Vector, Min (0, 0, 0), Max (0, 0, 6.283)) → Rotation. Noise Texture (Scale 0.4) › Fac → Map Range (0 to 1 → 0.3 to 1.6) → Scale, replacing step 1. What you should see Coils of random size, then also randomly turned, then sizes that swell and shrink in smooth patches across the field instead of at random."},{"t":"Exercise 4.2: a sunflower","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-4-2","x":"Points (Count 400) → Instance on Points with Ico Sphere (Radius 0.05) as Instance. Index → Math Multiply 2.39996 → Math Cosine and Math Sine. Index → Math Square Root → Math Multiply 0.08. Multiply the radius by each, Combine XYZ → Points' Position. Index → Map Range (0 to 400 → 0.3 to 1.2) → Scale. Expose Count and the 0.08. What you should see The seed head of a sunflower, spheres growing toward the rim, with the familiar crossing spirals. Change 2.39996 to 2.4 and the spirals become straight arms: the pattern depends on the golden angle to the fourth decimal."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 4","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-4-check","x":"Which source gives clumps rather than static? Answer Noise Texture; nearby points get similar values. How do you spin instances at random about Z only? Answer Random Value, Vector, with Min and Max zero on X and Y and 0 to 6.283 on Z, into Rotation. Where does the sunflower's formula live? Answer In Points' Position, as a field of Index. What makes a repeat rather than a gradient from Index? Answer Math Modulo."},{"t":"Curves as a pen","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-5","x":"Give a curve a body with Curve to Mesh and profiles. Shape it along its length with Spline Parameter, Set Curve Radius and Set Curve Tilt. Cut and grow it with Trim Curve."},{"t":"Profiles","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#profiles","x":"Curve to Mesh sweeps a profile curve along a path. Curve Circle gives a tube, Curve Line gives a ribbon, Quadrilateral gives a strip with thickness, and Star gives a ridged cord. The profile's own size sets the body's size, so a thin ribbon is a Curve Line 0.3 long and a thick one a Quadrilateral 0.02 wide and 0.3 tall. Fill Caps closes the ends of a closed profile. Which way the profile stands depends on the path's normals. Add Set Curve Normal set to Z Up before Curve to Mesh and the orientation stops depending on the curve's twist. If a strip then lies flat where it should stand, swap the profile's Width and Height."},{"t":"Along the length","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#along-the-length","x":"Spline Parameter › Factor runs 0 to 1 along each spline, and nodes that take a field use it to vary with length. Set Curve Radius with Factor → Float Curve scales the profile for a taper, as in the slime strands. Set Curve Tilt with Factor → Math Multiply 6.283 twists the profile one full turn over the length, which turns a ribbon into a helix. Resample Curve first, with Count 64 or more, so the twist has points to show on."},{"t":"Cutting and bending","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#cutting-and-bending","x":"Trim Curve keeps the part between Start and End, as factors 0 to 1; driving End with time grows a line (Chapter 7 ). Fillet Curve rounds the corners of a polyline, which is how a Star becomes a flower. Set Spline Type to Bézier or NURBS smooths a jagged primitive. Curve to Points places things along the path; Curve Length and Sample Curve read it."},{"t":"Exercise 5.1: the paper ribbon","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-5-1","x":"Spiral (Resolution 48, Rotations 2.5, Start Radius 0.05, End Radius 0.8, Height 0) → Resample Curve (Count 200) → Set Curve Normal (Z Up) → Curve to Mesh's Curve. Quadrilateral (Rectangle, Width 0.02, Height 0.35) → Curve to Mesh's Profile Curve. Set Shade Smooth → Group Output. Insert Set Curve Tilt before Curve to Mesh: Spline Parameter › Factor → Math Multiply 0.5 → Tilt. What you should see A strip of paper rolled into a flat spiral, standing on its edge, the thickness visible on the outer end; after step 3 the strip leans as it unrolls, like a loosened roll of quilling paper."},{"t":"Exercise 5.2: a twisted cord","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-5-2","x":"Curve Line (Start (0, 0, 0), End (3, 0, 0)) → Resample Curve (Count 128) → Set Curve Tilt (Spline Parameter › Factor → Math Multiply 12.566 → Tilt) → Curve to Mesh with Star (Points 5, Inner Radius 0.05, Outer Radius 0.1) as the profile. Replace Set Curve Tilt with Set Curve Radius (Factor → Float Curve, 1 at both ends and 0.3 in the middle). What you should see A five-ridged cord twisting twice along its length, then an untwisted cord pinched in the middle."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 5","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-5-check","x":"What decides the thickness of a swept body? Answer The profile curve's own size. Which field runs 0 to 1 along a spline? Answer Spline Parameter › Factor. Why resample before tilting? Answer A twist shows only where the curve has points. What node grows a line over time? Answer Trim Curve with End driven by Scene Time."},{"t":"Color and light for a piece","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-6","x":"Give each copy its own color, three ways. Build a palette rather than picking colors one by one. Light a piece for mood instead of for measurement."},{"t":"Color per copy","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#color-per-copy","x":"The quickest way needs no attribute: in the material, Object Info › Random → Color Ramp → Base Color. In Cycles each instance draws a different random number, so a field of coils gets a scatter of ramp colors from one node. It only works on instances left unrealized, and it cannot be steered. The controlled way stores the color in the tree. Before Instance on Points, Store Named Attribute (Color, Point, Name tint ) on the points, fed by whatever you like: Index → Map Range → Color Ramp for a gradient across the field, Random Value → Color Ramp for a scatter, Noise → Color Ramp for patches. Instance on Points carries point attributes onto the copies, and Realize Instances carries them onto the realized mesh. In the material, Attribute (Name tint ) › Color → Base Color. The third way is per element inside one mesh: Store Named Attribute on the points of a displaced grid, read the same way, which paints a landscape by height or slope."},{"t":"A palette","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#a-palette","x":"Set a Color Ramp to Constant interpolation and give it four or five stops: that is a palette. Any 0 to 1 input then picks one of the colors, never a mix, which is what makes a piece look designed rather than rendered. Pastel palettes (low saturation, high value) on a dark background are the look of most procedural work; one accent color on a tenth of the copies, chosen by Random Value, is usually enough."},{"t":"The surface of paper and plastic","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#the-surface-of-paper-and-plastic","x":"The Recipes part 's rows still apply. Matte paper is Roughness 0.6, Specular IOR Level 0.3 and a touch of Sheen; soft plastic is Roughness 0.4 with Subsurface 0.05 so edges glow; clay is Roughness 0.8. Keep Metallic at 0 unless the piece is about metal. Set Shade Smooth on the instance before instancing, once, rather than on the realized mesh."},{"t":"Light for mood","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#light-for-mood","x":"The Lighting part 's scientific rig is deliberately neutral. For a piece, start from its high-key setup and break two rules on purpose: let the key be warm (4500 K) and the fill cool (7500 K), and let the shadows go dark. A large Area key 30 degrees above the camera and a World from a studio HDRI at Strength 0.5 carry most pieces. Add Depth of Field on the camera (F-Stop 2, focus on the nearest coil) for the miniature look of the manual's image."},{"t":"Exercise 6.1: three ways to color the field","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-6-1","x":"On the field from Exercise 4.1 , give the coil's material Object Info › Random → Color Ramp (two pastel stops) → Base Color, and render in Cycles. Replace it: Store Named Attribute (Color, Point, tint ) on the points before Instance on Points, with Noise Texture › Fac → Color Ramp (Constant, four stops) as the value; Realize Instances after instancing; Attribute tint → Base Color. Change the Color Ramp to Linear interpolation. What you should see A random scatter of two tints; then four flat colors in patches that follow the noise, with realized geometry in the Spreadsheet; then smooth blends between the four."},{"t":"Exercise 6.2: mood","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-6-2","x":"Light the field with one Area key (Size 3 m, 45 degrees from above, 4500 K), a World HDRI at 0.5, and a dark gray ground plane. Add Depth of Field, F-Stop 2, with the nearest coil as the focus object. What you should see Warm-lit coils with soft shadows, the far ones melting into blur: the same scene as the manual's picture, on your own objects."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 6","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-6-check","x":"Why does Object Info › Random stop working after Realize Instances? Answer Realized copies are one mesh in one object, so there is one random number. How does a stored point color reach an instance's material? Answer Instance on Points carries point attributes onto the copies, Realize Instances carries them onto the mesh, and Attribute reads them. What does Constant interpolation on a Color Ramp give? Answer A palette: one of the stops, never a blend. Which rule of the scientific rig is broken for mood? Answer Neutral color; a warm key and cool fill are allowed."},{"t":"Motion","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-7","x":"Drive any value with Scene Time. Grow, spin and breathe a piece. Build a first Simulation Zone that accumulates."},{"t":"Time as an input","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#time-as-an-input","x":"Scene Time (Shift + A › Input › Scene) outputs Seconds and Frame, single values that change every frame. Any socket fed from it animates with no keyframes: Spiral's Rotations from Seconds, a Transform Geometry rotation from Seconds × 0.5, Noise Texture's W from Seconds so patches drift. Keyframing an exposed modifier input (hover it, press I) works too, and gives easing in the Graph Editor."},{"t":"Three motions","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#three-motions","x":"Grow: Trim Curve with End = Scene Time › Seconds → Map Range (0 to 4 → 0 to 1), so a line draws itself over four seconds. Spin: Rotate Instances with Scene Time › Seconds → Rotation on Z, which turns every copy in place. Breathe: Instance on Points' Scale from Math Sine of (Seconds + Index × 0.3), remapped to 0.7 to 1.3, so a wave of size runs across the field."},{"t":"A Simulation Zone, briefly","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#a-simulation-zone-briefly","x":"A Simulation Zone (Shift + A › Simulation) feeds its output back as the next frame's input, so the tree can accumulate. Inside the zone, Set Position with a small Offset from Noise moves points a little each frame, and because the input is last frame's output, the points wander instead of jittering. Play the timeline to see it; return to frame 1 to reset. It is the door to particles, growth and cloth, and Chapter 8 of the Geometry Nodes part says more."},{"t":"Exercise 7.1: draw the spiral","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-7-1","x":"Spiral (Rotations 4) → Trim Curve (End = Scene Time › Seconds → Map Range (0 to 5 → 0 to 1)) → Curve to Mesh with Curve Circle (Radius 0.03). Set the scene's End frame to 150 and press Space over the viewport. What you should see The coil drawing itself from the center outward over five seconds, finished at frame 120."},{"t":"Exercise 7.2: wander","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#exercise-7-2","x":"Points (Count 200) with Random Value (Vector, −2 to 2) → Position → a Simulation Zone; inside, Set Position with Offset = Noise Texture (4D, W from Scene Time › Seconds) › Color minus 0.5, scaled by 0.05 → the zone's output. After the zone, Instance on Points with a small Ico Sphere. Play. What you should see Two hundred specks drifting in slow currents, each path smooth, never snapping back; stop and return to frame 1 and they jump home."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 7","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-7-check","x":"What does Scene Time output? Answer Seconds and Frame, as single values. Which node makes a curve draw itself? Answer Trim Curve with End driven by time. Why does the Simulation Zone wander rather than jitter? Answer Its input each frame is the previous frame's output, so the offsets add up. How is a scrubbed value animated with easing instead? Answer Keyframe the exposed modifier input with I."},{"t":"Three worked pieces","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-8","x":"Each piece lists its full chain once, with the chapter that explains each step, then what you should see. Build them in order; each reuses the one before."},{"t":"Piece 1: the paper spirals from the manual","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#piece-1-the-paper-spirals-from-the-manual","x":"Ribbon (Chapter 5 ): Spiral (Resolution 48, Rotations 2.5, Start Radius 0.05, End Radius 0.8, Height 0) → Resample Curve (200) → Set Curve Normal (Z Up) → Set Curve Tilt (Spline Parameter › Factor → Multiply 0.4) → Curve to Mesh with Quadrilateral (0.02 × 0.35) → Set Shade Smooth. Scatter (Chapter 3 ): Grid (8 × 8, 2 × 2 vertices) → Distribute Points on Faces (Poisson Disk, Distance Min 1.0, Density Max 4) → Instance on Points with the ribbon. Variation (Chapter 4 ): Random Value (Float, 0.5 to 1.3) → Scale; Random Value (Vector, 0 to 6.283 on Z) → Rotation. Color (Chapter 6 ): Random Value (Float) → Color Ramp (Constant, five pastel stops) → Store Named Attribute (Color, Point, tint ) on the points before instancing; Realize Instances; material Attribute tint → Base Color, Roughness 0.6. Camera from above at 60 degrees, one warm Area key, HDRI at 0.5, Depth of Field at F-Stop 2.8. What you should see: a tabletop of rolled paper strips in five colors, each a different size and turn, leaning as they unroll, the far ones softening into blur. Scrub Distance Min down to 0.6 for the overlapping crowd of the manual's picture."},{"t":"Piece 2: a phyllotaxis flower","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#piece-2-a-phyllotaxis-flower","x":"Points (Count 300) with Position from the sunflower formula of Chapter 4 , radius 0.08 × √Index. Petal: Curve Line (Start (0, 0, 0), End (0.6, 0, 0)) → Resample Curve (16) → Set Curve Radius (Spline Parameter › Factor → Float Curve: 0 at the start, 1 at 0.6, 0 at the end) → Curve to Mesh with Curve Circle (Radius 0.08, Resolution 8) → Set Shade Smooth. Instance on Points with the petal. Rotation: Align Rotation to Vector (Axis X) with Vector = Position, so every petal points away from the center; then Rotate Rotation (Local) by an Euler whose Y is Index → Map Range (0 to 300 → −0.8 to 0), so inner petals tilt up. Scale: Index → Map Range (0 to 300 → 0.2 to 1). Color: Index → Map Range (0 to 300 → 0 to 1) → Color Ramp (Linear, yellow at 0 to red at 1), stored as tint before instancing, realized, read by the material. What you should see: a flower seen from above, small upright petals at the center opening into large flat ones at the rim, color running from the center out, every petal sitting on one of the crossing spirals."},{"t":"Piece 3: a low-poly island","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#piece-3-a-low-poly-island","x":"Ground: Grid (10 × 10, 60 × 60 vertices) → Set Position with Offset = Combine XYZ (0, 0, Z), where Z = Noise Texture (Scale 0.3, Detail 2) › Fac → Map Range (0 to 1 → −0.5 to 1.5). A second Noise Texture at Scale 2, × 0.1, added to Z gives small bumps. Shade Flat. Water: Grid (10 × 10, 2 × 2) raised to Z 0.2 with Transform Geometry, a blue glossy material, joined with Join Geometry. Trees: Distribute Points on Faces on the ground (Poisson Disk, Distance Min 0.25, Density Max 30), with Selection = Position → Separate XYZ › Z → Compare Greater Than 0.4, so nothing grows below the waterline, and Density Factor = Noise Texture (Scale 1) › Fac so trees cluster. Instance on Points with a tree (Cone, Radius 0.12, Depth 0.4, on a Cylinder, Radius 0.02, Depth 0.15, joined and Shade Flat); Rotation from the Distribute node's Rotation output; Scale from Random Value (0.6 to 1.2). Color: Store Named Attribute height (the displaced Z) on the ground's points; its material reads it into a Color Ramp (Constant: sand, grass, dark green, gray, white). Light: Sun at 35 degrees, 5500 K, Strength 3; World sky at 0.3; camera at 45 degrees. What you should see: a faceted island with sand at the shore, green slopes, gray peaks and white tops, forested in clumps above the water, lit by one sun with crisp shadows: the low-poly look in one tree."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 8","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-8-check","x":"In piece 1, why store the color before Instance on Points? Answer So each point's color rides onto its copy; after instancing, the points no longer exist. In piece 2, what makes petals point outward? Answer Align Rotation to Vector with each point's Position as the vector. In piece 3, why does the Distribute node's Rotation output matter? Answer It stands each tree on the slope's normal instead of straight up. Which pieces need Realize Instances, and why? Answer Pieces 1 and 2, for the stored color; piece 3 does not, because its trees share one plain material."},{"t":"Chooser, glossary and sources","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 9","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#chapter-9","x":""},{"t":"I want this, use that","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 9","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#i-want-this-use-that","x":"I want Use A shape from nothing Mesh Primitives or Curve Primitives, with Group Input disconnected Things scattered evenly Distribute Points on Faces, Poisson Disk Points placed by a formula Points with a field of Index in Position One thing copied everywhere Instance on Points Several things mixed Collection Info, Pick Instance, Instance Index Copies that differ at random Random Value into Scale, Rotation or a stored color Copies that differ in patches Noise Texture into the same sockets A gradient across the piece Index or Position → Map Range A path with a body Curve to Mesh with a profile A ribbon that twists Set Curve Tilt from Spline Parameter A line that draws itself Trim Curve, End from Scene Time A flat palette Color Ramp, Constant interpolation Color per copy, quick Object Info › Random in the material, Cycles Color per copy, controlled Store Named Attribute before instancing, Realize, Attribute in the material Motion with no keyframes Scene Time into any socket Motion that accumulates Simulation Zone The blocky low-poly look Low vertex counts and Shade Flat"},{"t":"Glossary","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 9","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#glossary","x":"Generator : a node that makes geometry from numbers alone: a primitive, Points, or a formula. Golden angle : 2.39996 radians, 137.5 degrees; the turn between successive seeds in phyllotaxis. Instance : a lightweight copy that shares one mesh; cheap until realized. Palette : a Color Ramp set to Constant, so inputs pick colors instead of blending them. Pick Instance : choosing one of several children per point by Instance Index. Poisson Disk : a scatter with a minimum spacing between points. Profile : the cross-section curve that Curve to Mesh sweeps along a path. Realize : turn instances into real geometry, carrying their attributes onto it. Scene Time : the node that outputs the current Seconds and Frame. Seed : the integer that re-rolls a random node; the same seed gives the same result. Simulation Zone : a region whose output becomes the next frame's input. Tilt : rotation of a curve's profile about the curve's own direction. Trim : keeping the part of a curve between two factors."},{"t":"Sources","w":"Geometry Nodes for art, Chapter 9","u":"geometry-nodes-for-art.html#sources","x":"Blender 5.2 manual: Geometry Nodes . The introduction page carries the paper-spiral image. Blender 5.2 manual: Fields . In Blender, F1 over any node opens its manual page."},{"t":"Molecular Nodes as a second ChimeraX","w":"Molecular Nodes","u":"molecular-nodes.html","x":"In ChimeraX a selection is a line you type: select /A:300-350 & ~backbone . In Molecular Nodes the same selection is a boolean field, True or False for every atom, and each node under Molecular Nodes › Select writes one piece of that line. This part goes through them: what each is for, how to use it, and for every one the plain Geometry Nodes it is built from, so you can read any of them by pressing Tab on it and build your own when none fits. It assumes Chapters 1 to 3 of the Geometry Nodes part : attributes, the Spreadsheet, fields and selections. Notation. As in the other parts: Shift + A › menu is a path, Node › Socket names an output, → is a link you draw. \"Under the hood\" names the plain nodes that do the same job as a Molecular Nodes node. Select any Molecular Nodes node and press Tab to see its real contents, Tab again to come back out, and don't edit inside: the groups are shared by every tree that uses them."},{"t":"What a selection is here","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-1","x":"See a selection as a True/False column in the Spreadsheet. Know the four places a selection plugs in. Read the integer codes that stand in for chain, residue and atom names. Recognize the anatomy shared by every Select node."},{"t":"A column of True and False","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"molecular-nodes.html#a-column-of-true-and-false","x":"Every node in the Select menu outputs a boolean field: a rule that, evaluated on the atoms, gives each atom True or False. Ctrl + Shift + click a Select node and the Spreadsheet shows that column, while the viewport paints True atoms white and False ones black. ChimeraX's green outline and this column are the same thing. A selection carries no atoms of its own. Like every field ( Geometry Nodes part , Chapter 3 ), it is evaluated on whatever geometry reaches the node that consumes it. The same Select Res ID node picks residue 50 on the atoms, or on a cartoon mesh that carries res_id ."},{"t":"Where a selection goes","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"molecular-nodes.html#where-a-selection-goes","x":"Socket What happens to unselected atoms ChimeraX A style node's Selection Discarded; the style is built from the rest hide , show , cartoon , surface Set Color › Selection Keep their color color Separate Atoms › Selection Go to the Inverted output two selections Delete Geometry › Selection (plain node) Removed delete Any other boolean socket takes a selection too. Style Cartoon's Helix Shape or a Switch node can be driven by one, so one chain gets cylinder helices and another ribbons."},{"t":"Names are integers","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"molecular-nodes.html#names-are-integers","x":"Geometry Nodes cannot store text on atoms, so Molecular Nodes stores every name as an integer. chain_id : chains sorted alphabetically and numbered from 0. entity_id : the same for entities. res_name : a fixed table, with ALA 0, ARG 1, ASN 2, ASP 3, CYS 4, GLU 5, GLN 6, GLY 7, HIS 8, ILE 9, LEU 10, LYS 11, MET 12, PHE 13, PRO 14, SER 15, THR 16, TRP 17, TYR 18, VAL 19, DNA bases 30 to 33, RNA 40 to 43 and unknown −1. atom_name : another table, N 1, CA 2, C 3, O 4, CB 5, CG 6 and on through the side chains, OXT 38, nucleic names from 50. sec_struct : 0 not protein, 1 helix, 2 sheet, 3 loop. res_id and atomic_number are what they say. Several booleans are precomputed on import and sit on every atom: is_alpha_carbon , is_backbone , is_side_chain , is_solvent , is_peptide , is_nucleic , is_lipid , is_hetero and is_carb . Read any code off the Spreadsheet. The Select nodes with checkboxes hide the codes behind names, and the generated chain node shows your real chain letters. The full tables are in the Molecular Nodes reference linked in Chapter 7 ."},{"t":"The anatomy of a Select node","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"molecular-nodes.html#the-anatomy-of-a-select-node","x":"Nearly every node in the Select menu has the same shape: inputs And (default True) and Or (default False) at the top, outputs Selection and Inverted at the bottom, and its own criterion between them. And means \"only where this other selection is also true\"; Or means \"or where this other selection is true\". That is how Select nodes chain into one another without a Boolean Math node between them. Under the hood: Named Attribute (the attribute in question) → Compare, or nothing for a stored boolean → Boolean Math (And) with the And input → Boolean Math (Or) with the Or input → Selection; Boolean Math (Not) on that → Inverted. Press Tab on Is Alpha Carbon and that is what you find: one Named Attribute reading is_alpha_carbon and three Boolean Math nodes."},{"t":"Exercise 1.1: look inside","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-1-1","x":"In your model's tree, add Shift + A › Molecular Nodes › Select › Is Alpha Carbon. Select it, press Tab, read the nodes, press Tab again. Ctrl + Shift + click the node, open the Spreadsheet's Vertex tab, and filter the Viewer column to True. What you should see A Named Attribute and three Boolean Math nodes inside; in the Spreadsheet, one True per residue, a few percent of the atoms, and in the viewport one white dot per residue on a black model."},{"t":"Exercise 1.2: the same selection, raw","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-1-2","x":"Named Attribute (Integer, res_id ) → Compare (Integer, Equal, B 50) → Style Spheres › Selection, on a branch from Set Color › Atoms, joined to the cartoon with Join Geometry. Set the style's Sphere menu to Mesh, since Point Cloud renders only in Cycles. Replace the two plain nodes with Select Res ID (Res ID 50). What you should see The same handful of spheres both times, one residue's atoms in each chain, sitting on the cartoon. The Molecular Nodes node saved a node and added And, Or and Inverted for free."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 1","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-1-check","x":"What does a Select node output? Answer A boolean field: True or False per atom, evaluated where it is used. What happens to unselected atoms at a style node's Selection socket? Answer They are discarded before the style is built. Why does res_name read 11 for a lysine? Answer Names are stored as integers from a fixed table, and LYS is 11. What do the And and Or sockets do? Answer Combine this node's selection with another one, without a separate Boolean Math node."},{"t":"Selecting by name: chain, residue, atom","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-2","x":"Pick chains, entities, residue ranges, residue types and atom names. Use the precomputed booleans for backbone, side chain, solvent, hydrogens and glycans. Translate the common ChimeraX specifiers."},{"t":"Chains and entities","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chains-and-entities","x":"Molecular Nodes generates a Select Chain_ node for each imported structure, named after it, with one checkbox per chain labeled with the real chain letter; find it by typing \"Select Chain\" into Shift + A's search. Select Entity_ does the same by entity, which groups identical copies: in a dimer with two receptor chains, one entity ticks both. Under the hood: Index Switch (Boolean) with chain_id as the index and the checkboxes as its items, which is why the boxes come in chain_id order. If the generated node is missing, the raw path is Named Attribute (Integer, chain_id ) → Compare (Equal), with the code read from the Spreadsheet. ChimeraX: /A ."},{"t":"Residues by number","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"molecular-nodes.html#residues-by-number","x":"Select Res ID takes one number. Select Res ID Range takes Min and Max, inclusive. Select Res ID String takes text like 1-20, 45, 100-120 , the way you'd write :1-20,45,100-120 . Remember that res_id restarts in each chain, so residue 50 selects residue 50 of every chain; put a chain selection into the And socket to pin it down. Under the hood: Compare (Equal) for one residue; two Compares (Greater Equal and Less Equal) into Boolean Math (And) for a range; String nodes that split the text at commas and dashes for the string version."},{"t":"Residues by type","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"molecular-nodes.html#residues-by-type","x":"Select Res Name has a checkbox per amino acid, with DNA and RNA bases in their own panels. Under the hood: Index Switch (Boolean) indexed by res_name , like the chain node but with a fixed table. For a residue not in the list (a modified residue, a ligand), use the raw Compare on res_name or is_hetero below. ChimeraX: :LYS,ARG ."},{"t":"Atoms by name and element","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"molecular-nodes.html#atoms-by-name-and-element","x":"Menu Atom Name gives a dropdown of atom names (N, CA, C, O, CB and the rest) and outputs the Selection together with the integer code, so it doubles as a lookup for Chapter 1 's table. Is Alpha Carbon is the shortcut for CA. Is Backbone and Is Side Chain read the precomputed booleans; Is Side Chain's Include CA counts the alpha carbon as side chain, which keeps side-chain sticks attached to the backbone. Select Atomic Number takes one element's number, Select Element has a checkbox per element, and Is Hydrogen is atomic number 1. Under the hood: Menu Switch (Integer) feeding a Compare; Named Attribute (Boolean) for the Is nodes; Index Switch by atomic_number for Select Element. ChimeraX: @CA , backbone , sidechain , C , H ."},{"t":"Molecule types and the booleans with no node","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"molecular-nodes.html#molecule-types-and-the-booleans-with-no-node","x":"Is Peptide , Is Nucleic , Is Lipid and Is Solvent read their booleans. Select Nucleic Type outputs is_purine and is_pyrimidine. Select Attribute reads any boolean attribute by name, and is the only way to reach two useful ones: is_hetero , everything the file marked HETATM (ligands, ions, waters, glycans), and is_carb , the glycans. Under the hood: Named Attribute (Boolean) with the Name you typed. ChimeraX: protein , nucleic , solvent , ligand , ~protein ."},{"t":"Exercise 2.1: one chain, one domain","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-2-1","x":"Select Chain_, with only the receptor chain ticked → Style Cartoon › Selection. Select Res ID Range (Min 310, Max 481: domain III of EGFR) with the first node's Selection in its And socket → a second Style Cartoon with a different material, joined to the first. What you should see Only the receptor, then domain III highlighted within it. Untick the chain in the first node and the highlight follows the residue numbers into EGF and the Fab, which is what the And socket prevents."},{"t":"Exercise 2.2: glycans as sticks","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-2-2","x":"Select Attribute (Name is_carb ) → Style Sticks › Selection, on a branch from Set Color › Atoms. Join Geometry with the cartoon. Change the Name to is_hetero . What you should see The N-linked glycans as sticks on the receptor surface; then also any waters and ions, since is_hetero is everything that was HETATM."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 2","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-2-check","x":"Why does Select Res ID 50 light up more than one residue? Answer res_id restarts in every chain; add a chain selection in And. Where do the chain letters in Select Chain_ come from? Answer The import: the node is generated per structure and indexed by chain_id . How do you select glycans? Answer Select Attribute with is_carb . What does Include CA on Is Side Chain do? Answer Counts the alpha carbon as part of the side chain, so sticks stay attached."},{"t":"Selecting by structure and property","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-3","x":"Select by secondary structure, computing it when the file has none. Select by any number with the plain Compare node. Grow a selection to whole residues or along bonds."},{"t":"Secondary structure","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"molecular-nodes.html#secondary-structure","x":"Is Helix , Is Sheet and Is Loop read sec_struct (1, 2 and 3). Under the hood: Named Attribute (Integer, sec_struct ) → Compare (Equal). A PDB or mmCIF from the databank carries secondary structure in its header, but a freshly built cryo-EM model often doesn't; then every atom reads 0 or 3 and the cartoon is all loop. Topology DSSP (Shift + A › Molecular Nodes › Topology) computes sec_struct from the backbone geometry and writes it back. Place it right after Group Input and the cartoon, the color nodes and the Is nodes all see it. ChimeraX: helix , strand , coil , dssp ."},{"t":"Any number","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"molecular-nodes.html#any-number","x":"There is no Select B-factor node because none is needed. Named Attribute (Float, b_factor ) → Compare (Float, Greater Than, B 100) is the whole thing, and the same two nodes select by occupancy , charge , mass , or anything you stored yourself ( height or locres from the Geometry Nodes part ). For a range, two Compares into Boolean Math (And); for a tolerance, Compare's Equal mode with its Epsilon. ChimeraX: ::bfactor>100 ."},{"t":"Whole residues","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"molecular-nodes.html#whole-residues","x":"A proximity or B-factor cut usually catches part of a residue. Select Res Whole (Expand on) makes a residue all True if any of its atoms is. Under the hood: Boolean Any grouped by ures_id , which is Accumulate Field counting the Trues per group and testing for more than zero; ures_id rather than res_id , because ures_id is unique across chains. ChimeraX: select up , or ending a selection with : ."},{"t":"Along bonds","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"molecular-nodes.html#along-bonds","x":"Select Bonded grows a selection by Depth bonds along the edges. Its Bonded output gives the new atoms without the originals, for coloring neighbors differently. Under the hood, one step is: Edge Vertices reads both ends of every bond, Sample Index tests the selection at each end, Boolean Math (Or) marks the bond touched, and Evaluate on Domain brings \"any touched bond\" back to the atoms; a Repeat Zone runs that Depth times. Expand Boolean (Molecular Nodes › Utilities) is the index-wise cousin: it grows a run of Trues by n points either side along the atom order, which follows the sequence."},{"t":"Exercise 3.1: color by secondary structure","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-3-1","x":"Color Secondary Structure (Molecular Nodes › Color) → Set Color › Color, in place of the chain colors. If everything comes out the Loop color, insert Topology DSSP after Group Input. Is Sheet → Style Spheres (Sphere Mesh, Scale 0.3) › Selection, joined with the cartoon. What you should see Helices, sheets and loops in three colors, then small spheres on the sheet residues only; domain III of EGFR is mostly sheet and the Fab nearly all of it."},{"t":"Exercise 3.2: poorly ordered residues","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-3-2","x":"Named Attribute (Float, b_factor ) → Compare (Greater Than, the top quarter of your model's range; read Min and Max off an Attribute Statistic) → Select Res Whole (Expand on) → Style Sticks › Selection, joined with the cartoon. Mute Select Res Whole (M). What you should see Whole residues as sticks where the map is weakest, usually at the periphery and in loops; muted, the sticks fray into partial residues."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 3","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-3-check","x":"Why can Is Helix, Is Sheet and Is Loop all give False everywhere? Answer sec_struct is missing or 0; run Topology DSSP. Which node selects by B-factor? Answer The plain Compare on Named Attribute b_factor . What is Select Res Whole made of? Answer Boolean Any over ures_id : a residue is True if any of its atoms was. What is in Select Bonded's Bonded output? Answer The grown part only, without the starting atoms."},{"t":"Selecting by space","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-4","x":"Select inside a sphere or a box you can grab and move. Select atoms near other atoms, within one object or across two. Turn a spatial selection into a clipping box or an interface."},{"t":"A sphere or a box you can move","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"molecular-nodes.html#a-sphere-or-a-box-you-can-move","x":"Select Sphere selects atoms within an Empty's radius. Adding the node creates an Empty called Empty_Sphere ; its Scale is the radius in scene units (0.1 per Å), so grab (G) and scale (S) the Empty in the viewport and the selection follows. Under the hood: Object Info (Location, Scale) → Vector Math (Distance) from Position → Compare (Less Than) against the scale. Select Cube does the same with a box that can also rotate. Under the hood: Object Info › Transform → Invert Matrix → Transform Point puts each atom in the box's own frame, and three Compares test that |x|, |y| and |z| are all at most 1. Its Inverted output is a clipping tool: into a style's Selection, everything inside the box disappears, like ChimeraX clip but with six faces you can turn."},{"t":"Atoms near other atoms","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"molecular-nodes.html#atoms-near-other-atoms","x":"Select Proximity is zone . Target Atoms is the geometry to measure from; Distance (A) is the cutoff in Å, so no world-scale arithmetic; Subset narrows the target to a selection of it; Expand grows the result to whole residues. Under the hood: Separate Geometry keeps the Subset of the target, Geometry Proximity measures each atom's distance to what is left, Compare (Less Equal) tests the cutoff converted to scene units, and Boolean Any by ures_id does Expand. This node has no And or Or sockets; combine its Selection through another node's And socket or a Boolean Math. Two patterns. Within one object: Target Atoms ← Group Input › Atoms and Subset ← a Select Chain_ ticking the Fab chains gives everything within the cutoff of the Fab, including the Fab itself at distance zero; feed the result into a Select Chain_ (receptor ticked) › And to keep the receptor side. Across objects: Target Atoms ← Object Info (Relative) of the other model, which is how a ligand from one file selects its pocket in another. ChimeraX: select /A & /H,L @<5 , select zone ."},{"t":"Exercise 4.1: the epitope","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-4-1","x":"Select Proximity: Target Atoms ← Group Input › Atoms, Subset ← Select Chain_ with the Fab heavy and light chains ticked, Distance 4.5, Expand on. Its Selection → a second Select Chain_ (receptor ticked) › And. That node's Selection → Style Sticks on a branch, joined with the cartoon, and → Set Color › Selection with one bright color, placed before the styles. What you should see The receptor residues in contact with Fab108 as whole-residue sticks, recolored, on the cartoon. Lower Distance to 3.5 and only the tightest contacts remain; turn Expand off and the sticks break into partial residues."},{"t":"Exercise 4.2: a box as a clipping plane","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-4-2","x":"Select Cube → Inverted → Style Cartoon › Selection. Move (G) and scale (S) Empty_Cube until it covers half the model. Rotate the Empty (R) by 30 degrees. What you should see The cartoon cut away wherever the box sits, with a clean edge that follows the box's rotation."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 4","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-4-check","x":"What sets Select Sphere's radius? Answer The Empty's Scale, in scene units. Why does a within-object Select Proximity include the target itself? Answer Target atoms are at distance zero from themselves; an And with the other side excludes them. Which node is ChimeraX zone ? Answer Select Proximity. How does Select Cube handle a rotated box? Answer It transforms each atom into the box's frame with the inverted matrix before comparing."},{"t":"Combining and applying selections","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-5","x":"Combine selections with Boolean Math and the And/Or sockets. Apply them to styles, colors and separations, and join the results. Expose a selection as a checkbox on the modifier panel."},{"t":"Boolean algebra","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"molecular-nodes.html#boolean-algebra","x":"Boolean Math (Shift + A › Utilities › Math) is the full algebra: And, Or, Not, Not And, Nor, Xor, and Subtract, which is A and not B. In ChimeraX terms, & , | , ~ and & ~ . The And and Or sockets on Select nodes cover the common two-term cases without it. Read a chain of Select nodes from the top: each node's output is (its own criterion And its And socket) Or its Or socket. For anything with parentheses, draw Boolean Math nodes so the tree looks like the expression; it is easier to check by eye than a chain of sockets."},{"t":"Applying","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"molecular-nodes.html#applying","x":"Three habits. One: a style's Selection discards, so \"these as sticks and everything as cartoon\" is two style nodes on two branches from the same atoms, joined with Join Geometry. Two: Set Color's Selection does not discard, so coloring happens before styling, on the atoms, and every style downstream inherits the Color . Three: Separate Atoms splits the atoms into Atoms and Inverted geometries and also outputs Index, each atom's index before the split, for when a later node must refer back to the whole structure. Separate Polymers is the same split run on is_peptide , then is_nucleic , with the rest as Other. Under the hood: Separate Geometry, with a Capture Attribute of Index just before it."},{"t":"Checkboxes on the modifier","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"molecular-nodes.html#checkboxes-on-the-modifier","x":"Drag any Select node's And input to Group Input's empty socket and it becomes a checkbox on the modifier panel; drag a Distance or a Res ID and it becomes a slider. A Switch node (Boolean) choosing between two selections, driven by such a checkbox, flips a whole figure between two states without opening the tree."},{"t":"Exercise 5.1: a selection with parentheses","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-5-1","x":"Build \"chain A and (LYS or ARG) and not backbone\": Select Chain_ (one chain ticked) › Selection → Select Res Name (LYS and ARG ticked) › And. Its Selection → Boolean Math (Subtract) › A; Is Backbone › Selection → B. The result → Style Sticks › Selection, on a branch joined with the cartoon. What you should see The basic side chains of one chain as sticks, their backbone atoms excluded; tick GLU and ASP too and the salt-bridge partners join them."},{"t":"Exercise 5.2: the figure","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-5-2","x":"Color: Set Color with Random Color for everything, then a second Set Color with Select Chain_ (receptor ticked) in Selection and a flat gray, so only the receptor goes gray. Styles: Style Cartoon for all; Style Surface with Select Chain_ (Fab chains) in Selection and a material at Alpha 0.3; Style Sticks for Exercise 4.1 's epitope; Style Sticks for is_carb . Four branches, one Join Geometry. Expose the epitope's Distance and the surface's chain checkboxes to the modifier panel. What you should see A gray receptor with colored EGF, a translucent antibody surface, contact residues and glycans as sticks, and two controls on the modifier panel that tune it without the node editor."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 5","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-5-check","x":"How do you write A and not B? Answer Boolean Math set to Subtract, or an And socket fed B's Inverted. Why color before styling? Answer Set Color keeps unselected atoms; every style downstream inherits Color . What is Separate Atoms' Index output for? Answer Referring back to an atom's index in the whole structure after the split. How does a selection become a checkbox? Answer Connect its And input to Group Input."},{"t":"The rest of the ChimeraX toolbox","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-6","x":"Color by chain, entity, rainbow, secondary structure and any attribute. Compute what the file lacks: secondary structure, bonds. Know the six style nodes and the sockets that matter. Measure, center, explode and morph with the utility nodes."},{"t":"Coloring","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"molecular-nodes.html#coloring","x":"All color nodes output a Color field for Set Color , which under the hood is Store Named Attribute writing Color on the Point domain with a Selection. Color Chain_ and Color Entity_ are generated per structure with one swatch per chain or entity (Index Switch of colors by chain_id ). Random Color , the node in your default tree, seeds Random Value with an ID and builds an HSL color. Color Rainbow runs a hue along each chain or the whole structure (the Factor menu), from each atom's relative position in its group: ChimeraX rainbow . Color Secondary Structure is three swatches by sec_struct . Color Attribute Map is color byattribute : Name, Min, Max and two or three colors; under the hood it is Named Attribute → Map Range → Mix Color, the chain you built by hand for height , in one node. Color pLDDT is that node preset for AlphaFold models. Color Res Name , Color Common and Color Element map res_name or atomic_number to swatches, and Color Goodsell darkens the non-carbon atoms for the Goodsell look."},{"t":"Reading attributes","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"molecular-nodes.html#reading-attributes","x":"Molecular Nodes › Attributes holds a reader per attribute: Chain ID, Residue ID, Residue Name, Atom Name, B Factor, URes ID, Secondary Structure, Entity ID, VDW Radii. Each is a Named Attribute with the name typed and the type set, plus an Index input for reading another atom's value. Use them for the same reason as the Select nodes: no typos, no wrong data type."},{"t":"Computing what the file lacks","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"molecular-nodes.html#computing-what-the-file-lacks","x":"Topology DSSP computes sec_struct ( dssp ). Find Bonds adds edges by distance for files without bonds, and Break Bonds removes them by a cutoff or a selection ( bond , ~bond ). Set URes ID recomputes ures_id after you delete atoms. Backbone Positions gives every residue's N, CA, C and O positions as fields, for drawing your own geometry along the backbone."},{"t":"The six styles","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"molecular-nodes.html#the-six-styles","x":"Style ChimeraX Sockets that matter Style Spheres show atoms , style sphere Sphere: Point Cloud is free but renders only in Cycles; Instances or Mesh for EEVEE. Scale multiplies vdw_radii Style Ball and Stick style ball Bond Find for files without bonds; Bond Split for double bonds; Bond Scale in Å Style Sticks style stick Scale is the stick radius in Å Style Ribbon cartoon style tube Peptide Radius; Backbone Smoothing Style Cartoon cartoon Peptide Shape; Helix Shape, for cylinder helices; Sheet Arrows; Loop Radius; Color Blur Style Surface surface Quality; Scale and Offset grow the surface; Separate By and Group ID make one surface per chain, like surface /A ; Color Source picks the alpha carbon or the nearest atom Every style's Selection discards, and every style's Material socket is where the Recipes part 's materials go."},{"t":"Measuring, centering, exploding, morphing","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"molecular-nodes.html#measuring-centering-exploding-morphing","x":"Centre on Selection moves the atoms so a selection's centroid sits at the origin ( cofr , view ); a Group ID centers each chain separately. Split to Centred Instances turns each Group ID (a chain, with chain_id ) into an instance at its own centroid, so a Scale on the instances explodes the complex apart ( tile ). Field Remap and Normalize Field rescale a field between a min and a max, grouped if you like: the Attribute Statistic and Map Range chain of the Geometry Nodes part in one node. Group Info and Group Parameter give each atom its residue's size, first and last index, and relative position, with ures_id as Group ID. Offset Float and its siblings read a value at Index plus an offset: the next residue's value, for differences along the chain. Sample Mix Vector mixes positions between two geometries by a Factor, which is a morph between two conformations ( morph ) once the two models have matching atoms. For a distance, Geometry Proximity from one selection to another with a Viewer on its Distance output is distance , read off the Spreadsheet."},{"t":"Exercise 6.1: color by B-factor in one node","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-6-1","x":"Color Attribute Map (Name b_factor , Min and Max from your model, A blue, B red, Intermediate on and white) → Set Color › Color. What you should see The B-factor coloring of the Geometry Nodes part , with two numbers on one node instead of three nodes, and the same blue-to-red convention as spectrum b ."},{"t":"Exercise 6.2: one surface per chain","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"molecular-nodes.html#exercise-6-2","x":"Style Surface with Separate By set to chains, or Group ID ← Chain ID (Attributes), Quality 6, and a material at Alpha 0.4. What you should see A surface that stops at each chain boundary instead of one skin over the complex, so the Fab and the receptor read as separate bodies pressed together."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 6","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-6-check","x":"Which node is color byattribute bfactor ? Answer Color Attribute Map. Which sphere mode shows in EEVEE? Answer Instances or Mesh; Point Cloud is Cycles only. How do you get one surface per chain? Answer Style Surface with Separate By, or Group ID from chain_id . What does Field Remap replace? Answer Attribute Statistic plus Map Range."},{"t":"From ChimeraX to nodes","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"molecular-nodes.html#chapter-7","x":""},{"t":"The translation table","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"molecular-nodes.html#the-translation-table","x":"ChimeraX Molecular Nodes select /A Select Chain_ with A ticked select :100-200 Select Res ID Range select :100,105,110-120 Select Res ID String select :LYS,ARG Select Res Name select @CA Is Alpha Carbon, or Menu Atom Name select backbone , sidechain Is Backbone, Is Side Chain select protein , nucleic , solvent Is Peptide, Is Nucleic, Is Solvent select ligand Select Attribute is_hetero , with Is Solvent's Inverted in And glycans Select Attribute is_carb select H Is Hydrogen select helix , strand , coil Is Helix, Is Sheet, Is Loop select ::bfactor>80 Named Attribute b_factor → Compare select /A & /H,L @<5 Select Proximity (Target Group Input, Subset chains H and L) → Select Chain_ A › And select up Select Res Whole, Select Bonded ~select , select ~sel Inverted output, or Boolean Math Not A & B , A | B , A & ~B And socket, Or socket, Boolean Math Subtract hide X , show X Inverted, or the selection, into a style's Selection color /A red Set Color with Select Chain_ in Selection rainbow Color Rainbow color byattribute bfactor Color Attribute Map color bychain , color byhetero Color Chain_; Color Element after a chain color dssp Topology DSSP surface /A Style Surface with Separate By, or Group ID from chain_id transparency 60 the material's Alpha clip Select Cube › Inverted into a style's Selection cofr sel , view sel Centre on Selection tile Split to Centred Instances, then Scale the instances morph Sample Mix Vector between two models distance Geometry Proximity › Distance, read in the Spreadsheet bond , ~bond Find Bonds, Break Bonds"},{"t":"Glossary","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"molecular-nodes.html#glossary","x":"Selection : a boolean field on atoms, True or False each, evaluated where it is consumed. And, Or sockets : inputs on most Select nodes that combine another selection with this node's, without Boolean Math. Inverted : the output that is the Not of Selection. Code : the integer standing in for a name; chain_id from 0 alphabetically, res_name and atom_name from fixed tables. Generated node : a node built per imported structure and named after it: Select Chain_, Select Entity_, Color Chain_, Color Entity_. ures_id : residue number unique across the whole structure; res_id restarts in each chain. is_hetero , is_carb : precomputed booleans for HETATM records and for carbohydrates, reached with Select Attribute. Expand : grow an atom-level selection to whole residues, in Select Res Whole and Select Proximity. Target Atoms, Subset : in Select Proximity, the geometry measured from and the part of it that counts. Under the hood : the plain Geometry Nodes doing the same job as a Molecular Nodes group; Tab on the node shows the real ones."},{"t":"Sources","w":"Molecular Nodes, Chapter 7","u":"molecular-nodes.html#sources","x":"Select nodes , the Molecular Nodes reference for every node in Chapters 1 to 5 . Selections tutorial , the PyMOL comparison and Boolean Math examples. Attributes and data tables , every attribute and the integer codes for residue and atom names. Color nodes , Style nodes , Topology nodes , Geometry nodes and Utilities / Fields , for Chapter 6 . Generated node groups , the per-structure Select Chain_ and Color Chain_ nodes. In Blender, Tab on any Molecular Nodes node opens the group itself; the Molecular Nodes documentation describes the sockets, and the group shows the construction."},{"t":"Handling objects and modeling","w":"Objects and modeling","u":"objects-and-modeling.html","x":"This part starts where you are: you can add a plane and move, turn and scale it. The first half, Chapters 1 to 6 , covers what you will use every day on figures: selecting, placing things exactly, relating objects to each other, editing a mesh, modifiers, and curves, text and the camera. The second half, Chapters 7 to 9 , is modeling for its own sake, a cartoon house and the road to an anime character, and ends with what carries back to science. Notation. Keys are pressed with the mouse over the 3D viewport. On a Mac, Alt is the Option key, and Ctrl is Control, not Command; only undo, save, copy and paste use Command. A menu path follows in parentheses the first time a key appears, and F3 searches every command by name. Without a number pad, the View menu and the grave accent key, left of 1, reach the same views. Shift + A › menu is a path, as in the other parts."},{"t":"Objects: select, hide, name, organize","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 1","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-1","x":"Tell the active object from the selected ones, and know why the difference matters. Select, hide and isolate objects quickly. Keep a scene findable with names and collections."},{"t":"Selected and active","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 1","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#selected-and-active","x":"Clicking an object selects it and makes it active. Shift + click adds more: all of them are selected, drawn with an orange outline, and the last one clicked is active, drawn in a lighter yellow-orange. The Properties editor always shows the active object. Commands that involve several objects treat the active one specially: it becomes the parent, the object the others are joined into, or the source that settings are copied from. So the click order matters, and that object goes last."},{"t":"Selecting","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 1","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#selecting","x":"To select Do this One object; one more or one fewer Click; Shift + click Everything; nothing A; Alt + A A region Drag a box in empty space with the Select Box tool, or press B and drag The opposite of what is selected Ctrl + I Every light, camera or mesh Select › Select All by Type Something buried behind other things Click its name in the Outliner, or Alt + click in the viewport for a list of what lies under the mouse"},{"t":"Hiding and isolating","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 1","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#hiding-and-isolating","x":"H hides the selection and Alt + H shows everything again. Numpad / (View › Local View › Toggle Local View) shows only the selection until you press it again, the quickest way to work on one object in a crowded scene. In the Outliner, the eye icon hides an object in the viewport only; the camera icon decides whether it renders. An object can be hidden from you and still render, which is the usual cause of a mystery shape in a render."},{"t":"Names and collections","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 1","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#names-and-collections","x":"F2 renames the active object; so does a double-click on its name in the Outliner. Name things for what they are ( key light , sweep , EGFR atoms ): node pickers, the Outliner's filter and you six weeks from now all go by name. A collection is a folder. M moves the selection into a collection or a new one, the checkbox beside a collection switches all of it off, and dragging in the Outliner rearranges. For figures, four collections cover most scenes: subject, lights, backdrop, and helpers that never render."},{"t":"Finding any command","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 1","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#finding-any-command","x":"F3 opens a search over every command: type shade smooth or origin , then press Enter. Hover over any button and press F1 to open its page in the manual."},{"t":"Exercise 1.1: tidy your scene","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 1","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-1-1","x":"In the scene with your model, rename the lights, cameras and planes with F2. Select the lights and press M › New Collection, named lights ; do the same for the planes, as backdrop . Click the model, press numpad /, orbit around it, and press numpad / again. Select the two planes: click one, then Shift + click the other. Press H, render a still (Render › Render Image), then press Alt + H. What you should see The Outliner reads like a list of parts. In local view the model stands alone on gray. In step 3 the planes vanish from the viewport and still appear in the render, because H does not touch the camera icon."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 1","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-1-check","x":"Which object does the Properties editor show? Answer The active one, the last clicked. You select a light, then Shift + click an Empty, and parent. Which is the parent? Answer The Empty: the active object, clicked last. An object you hid with H appears in the render. Why? Answer H hides in the viewport only; the camera icon in the Outliner controls rendering."},{"t":"Placing things exactly","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 2","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-2","x":"Lock a move to an axis and type its amount. Choose what a rotation or scale happens around, with the pivot and the 3D cursor. Snap one thing onto another. Say what an origin is and when to apply a transform."},{"t":"G, R and S in full","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 2","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#g-r-and-s-in-full","x":"G moves, R rotates and S scales. Move the mouse, then click or press Enter to keep the change, or right-click to cancel it. During any of them, X, Y or Z locks to that axis, and Shift + X, Y or Z locks to the other two. Pressing the axis key twice uses the object's own axis in place of the world's, so G Z Z moves a tilted lamp along its own beam. Typing a number sets the amount: G X 2 Enter, R Z 45 Enter, S 0.5 Enter. Holding Ctrl snaps to round steps, and Shift slows the mouse for fine control. The N panel's Item tab holds the same values as fields you can type into, plus Dimensions, the object's real size in meters."},{"t":"The pivot: what things turn around","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 2","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#the-pivot-what-things-turn-around","x":"R and S act around the pivot point, chosen in the Pivot menu in the middle of the viewport header (the period key opens it under the mouse). Median Point, the default, is the middle of the selection. Individual Origins turns or scales each object about itself. 3D Cursor uses the cursor, which is how you orbit one thing around another: with the cursor on the subject and the pivot set to 3D Cursor, R Z swings a light around the subject and keeps it aimed there."},{"t":"The 3D cursor and snapping","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 2","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#the-3d-cursor-and-snapping","x":"The 3D cursor, the red and white ring, is a point you place. New objects appear on it, and it can be the pivot. Shift + right-click puts it under the mouse, and Shift + S opens the Snap menu: Cursor to World Origin, Cursor to Selected, Selection to Cursor. Cursor to Selected on one object, then Selection to Cursor on another, puts the second exactly on the first. For snapping during a move, hold Ctrl, or switch on the magnet in the header. The menu beside the magnet picks the target: Increment for grid steps, or Vertex, Edge and Face to land on other geometry. Face, with Align Rotation to Target ticked, sets an object down on a surface the right way up, such as a prop on a floor or a marker on a membrane."},{"t":"Origins, and why Apply exists","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 2","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#origins-and-why-apply-exists","x":"Every object has an origin, the small orange dot. Location is where the origin sits, and the object turns and scales about it. Object › Set Origin moves the origin without moving the mesh: Origin to Geometry centers it, and Origin to 3D Cursor puts it wherever you need a hinge. Location, Rotation and Scale are a layer on top of the stored mesh. S in Object Mode changes Scale, not the mesh. Tools that measure in the object's own units, such as Bevel, Solidify and most modifiers, then come out stretched, and the status bar warns of non-uniform scale. Ctrl + A › Scale (Object › Apply › Scale) writes the current size into the mesh and resets Scale to 1; nothing visibly moves. Scaling in Edit Mode changes the mesh itself and never needs applying. Do not apply transforms or move origins on Molecular Nodes objects: their mesh is the atoms, and an untouched transform is what keeps a model and its map aligned."},{"t":"Exercise 2.1: orbit a light around a subject","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 2","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-2-1","x":"On the lab bench (a sphere on a plane with one area light), click the sphere and press Shift + S › Cursor to Selected. Click the light, set the Pivot menu to 3D Cursor, and press R Z 45 Enter, then R Z -90 Enter. Set the pivot back to Median Point. What you should see The light swings 45 degrees around the sphere, then 90 degrees back the other way, always pointing at the sphere, with the highlight sliding across the sphere as it goes."},{"t":"Exercise 2.2: see what Apply fixes","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 2","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-2-2","x":"Add a cube and press S X 4 Enter in Object Mode. Add a Bevel modifier (Modifier tab › Add Modifier › Generate › Bevel) with Amount 0.2 m and Segments 4. Look at a corner from above, then press Ctrl + A › Scale. What you should see Before, each rounded edge is four times longer along X than along Y, a stretched curve. After, the corners are even quarter circles and Scale reads 1, 1, 1."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 2","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-2-check","x":"What does G Z Z do that G Z does not? Answer It moves along the object's own Z axis, not the world's. How do you swing a light around the subject? Answer Cursor on the subject, pivot set to 3D Cursor, then R Z. Why does a bevel look stretched on a scaled object? Answer It measures in the object's own units; apply the scale first. What does Set Origin › Origin to 3D Cursor change? Answer Where the object's origin is, not where its mesh is."},{"t":"Copies and relationships","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 3","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-3","x":"Choose between a copy and a linked copy. Join meshes into one object and split them apart. Make objects move together by parenting, with an Empty as the handle. Keep a light or camera aimed with Track To."},{"t":"Two kinds of copy","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 3","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#two-kinds-of-copy","x":"Shift + D duplicates: a new object with its own mesh. Alt + D makes a linked duplicate: a new object that shares the first one's mesh, so reshaping either in Edit Mode reshapes both, while each keeps its own place, size and modifiers. A number beside a mesh's or material's name is its user count, how many things share it; clicking the number gives this object a copy of its own. For thousands of copies, use instances ( Geometry Nodes part )."},{"t":"Join and separate","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 3","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#join-and-separate","x":"Ctrl + J joins the selected objects into the active one: one object, one origin, one modifier stack. In Edit Mode, P › Selection splits the selected faces off as a new object, and P › By Loose Parts splits every disconnected piece. Join what should be edited as one mesh. For things that should only travel together, parent them."},{"t":"Parenting and Empties","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 3","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#parenting-and-empties","x":"Select the children, then the parent last, and press Ctrl + P › Object. The children now follow the parent's moves, turns and scaling, and can still be moved on their own. Alt + P clears the link. In the Outliner, children nest under their parent. An Empty (Shift + A › Empty › Plain Axes) has no geometry and never renders, which makes it the standard handle. Parent three lights to an Empty at the subject, and R Z on the Empty turns the whole rig. Parent the camera to another, keyframe the Empty's rotation, and you have a turntable."},{"t":"Aiming with Track To","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 3","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#aiming-with-track-to","x":"Select the light or camera, Shift + click its target, and choose Object › Track › Track To Constraint (Ctrl + T). It now points at the target wherever either goes. An Empty as the target gives you an aim point you can drag. Alt + T clears it."},{"t":"Exercise 3.1: a rig on a handle","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 3","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-3-1","x":"On the lab bench, put an Empty at the sphere: click the sphere, press Shift + S › Cursor to Selected, then Shift + A › Empty › Plain Axes. Select every light, Shift + click the Empty last, and press Ctrl + P › Object. Select the Empty alone and press R Z 90 Enter, then G X 3 Enter. What you should see The lights swing a quarter turn around the sphere together, then the whole rig slides 3 m along X, away from the sphere."},{"t":"Exercise 3.2: copy or linked copy","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 3","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-3-2","x":"Select the sphere and press Shift + D, then X 3 Enter. Select the original again and press Alt + D, then X -3 Enter. Select the original, press Tab, then A and S 0.5 Enter, and press Tab again. What you should see The original and the Alt + D copy both shrink to half size, because they share one mesh. The Shift + D copy stays as it was."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 3","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-3-check","x":"Which copy changes when you edit the original's mesh? Answer The Alt + D linked duplicate. Join or parent a door to a house you will keep editing? Answer Parent: they move together and stay separate objects. What makes an Empty a good handle? Answer It has no geometry, never renders, and anything can be parented to it. What does Track To do? Answer Keeps the object pointed at a target as either one moves."},{"t":"Edit Mode: the mesh inside the object","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 4","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-4","x":"Enter Edit Mode on purpose and say what it changes. Select vertices, edges, faces and loops. Use the eight core tools. Fix shading with smooth, flat and normals."},{"t":"Two modes","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 4","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#two-modes","x":"Object Mode moves whole objects. Tab enters Edit Mode on the selected object, where you reshape the mesh it is made of: vertices (points), edges (lines between two vertices) and faces (surfaces bounded by three or more edges). What you do there changes the stored mesh. The viewport header names the mode; if G suddenly drags a single corner, you are in Edit Mode. One rule for your own work: a Molecular Nodes object's mesh is its atoms and bonds, so stay out of Edit Mode on molecules."},{"t":"Selecting parts","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 4","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#selecting-parts","x":"The three buttons at the left of the header choose vertex, edge or face selection (1, 2 and 3 on the number row, unless Emulate Numpad is on). Click, Shift + click, A and Alt + A work as on objects. Alt + click on an edge selects its whole edge loop, the line of edges running around the shape. L, with the mouse over a piece, selects everything connected to it. Ctrl + numpad plus and minus grow and shrink the selection (Select › Select More/Less). In solid view a box selection catches only what you can see; Alt + Z turns on X-ray so it reaches through."},{"t":"The eight tools","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 4","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#the-eight-tools","x":"Tool Key What it does A typical use Extrude E Pulls the selection out and builds new faces behind it A wall from an edge, a chimney from a face Inset I Shrinks a face inward, leaving a border A window frame, a panel Bevel Ctrl + B Replaces an edge with a slanted or rounded strip Softened corners, the sweep's fold Loop cut Ctrl + R Adds a ring of edges around the shape More detail where it is needed Knife K Draws new edges click by click; Enter confirms A cut that follows no loop Merge M Fuses the selected vertices into one Closing a roof to a ridge Fill F Makes an edge or a face between the selected parts Closing a hole Delete, dissolve X Delete leaves a hole; Dissolve removes and heals the surface Removing a loop you no longer want After any tool, a panel at the bottom left of the viewport holds its numbers: width, segments, number of cuts. Set them there if you have no scroll wheel. G, R and S act on the selection as they do on objects, and G pressed twice slides a vertex or an edge along the surface."},{"t":"Soft selection","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 4","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#soft-selection","x":"O switches on Proportional Editing: moving one vertex drags its neighbors along, less with distance. During the move, scroll (or press Page Up and Page Down) to change the radius, drawn as a circle. A flat grid becomes a hill this way, and a membrane gets its curve."},{"t":"Smooth, flat and normals","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 4","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#smooth-flat-and-normals","x":"A mesh is flat faces. Shade Smooth (right-click in Object Mode) blends the lighting across them so they read as one curved surface. Shade Flat shows the faces. Shade Auto Smooth, in the same menu, smooths gentle angles and keeps sharp ones crisp. Each face also has a front, given by its normal. If part of a mesh renders dark or a bevel turns inside out, the normals disagree: in Edit Mode press A, then Shift + N (Mesh › Normals › Recalculate Outside). Face Orientation, in the Overlays menu, paints fronts blue and backs red."},{"t":"Exercise 4.1: a tray from a cube","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 4","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-4-1","x":"Add a cube and press Tab. Choose face selection, click the top face, and press I 0.2 Enter, then E -1.5 Enter. Press Ctrl + R and move the mouse over a side until the yellow ring runs horizontally around the cube. Click, then right-click to leave the ring centered. Press S 1.15 Enter. Choose edge selection, Alt + click the outer top rim, and press Ctrl + B, then click. Set Width to 0.05 m and Segments to 3 in the panel at the bottom left. Press Tab. What you should see A hollow tray with walls 0.2 m thick and a floor near the bottom, a belt bulging slightly around its middle, and a rounded top rim that catches a highlight."},{"t":"Exercise 4.2: a hill by soft selection","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 4","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-4-2","x":"Add Shift + A › Mesh › Grid, and in the panel at the bottom left set X and Y Subdivisions to 30 and Size to 10 m. Press Tab, choose vertex selection, and click one vertex near the middle. Press O, then G Z. Scroll until the circle covers about a third of the grid, type 2 and press Enter. Press Tab and right-click › Shade Smooth. What you should see A smooth hill 2 m high whose width follows the circle. With O off, the same move pulls up a single spike."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 4","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-4-check","x":"What are the three kinds of parts in a mesh? Answer Vertices, edges and faces. How do you select a whole edge loop? Answer Alt + click one of its edges. Delete or dissolve, to remove a loop without leaving a hole? Answer Dissolve. Part of a mesh renders dark after editing. What do you try first? Answer A, then Shift + N, to recalculate the normals."},{"t":"Modifiers: edits you can change later","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 5","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-5","x":"Add, order and apply modifiers. Know the eight used most. Shape a smooth form from a simple cage with Subdivision Surface."},{"t":"What a modifier is","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 5","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#what-a-modifier-is","x":"A modifier is an operation stored on the object and recomputed live. The mesh you edit stays simple, and what you see and render is the modifiers' result. Geometry Nodes is a modifier whose operation you write yourself, and Molecular Nodes is one of those. Add modifiers in the Modifier tab (the wrench) › Add Modifier. They run from top to bottom, so order matters: Mirror above Subdivision Surface gives one smooth whole, and the reverse leaves a crease down the middle. The icons on a modifier's header switch it on in Edit Mode, the viewport and renders. The arrow menu on the header › Apply writes the result into the mesh for good, which you rarely need. Modifier What it does Use it for Subdivision Surface Splits every face and rounds the shape; Ctrl + 1, 2 or 3 adds it at that level Smooth forms from a few faces Mirror Builds the other half across an axis Anything symmetric: model one half Solidify Gives a surface a thickness A sheet into a slab: a wall, a membrane Bevel Rounds the edges sharper than an angle Corners that catch light; the sweep Array Repeats the object in a row Steps, fences, a lattice Boolean Cuts another object's shape out of this one, or adds it Windows, cutaways Simple Deform Bends, twists, tapers or stretches the whole mesh A curled strip, a twisted column Decimate Reduces the number of faces A heavy surface that slows the viewport"},{"t":"Subdivision and where the edges are","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 5","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#subdivision-and-where-the-edges-are","x":"Subdivision Surface rounds everything, including corners you wanted crisp. Edges that sit close together stay sharper, so you steer the rounding with loop cuts: a Ctrl + R loop slid toward a corner tightens it. A simple cage, a Mirror and a Subdivision Surface is how most organic models are built, characters included."},{"t":"Exercise 5.1: from cube to drum","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 5","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-5-1","x":"Add a cube and press Ctrl + 2. Right-click › Shade Smooth. Press Tab, then Ctrl + R with the ring running horizontally. Click, slide the ring up near the top face, and click again. Add a second loop near the bottom the same way. What you should see The cube turns into a ball. With the two loops, its top and bottom flatten while its sides stay round, a drum shape, and sliding a loop (G pressed twice) changes how tight the rim is."},{"t":"Exercise 5.2: a live cutaway","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 5","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-5-2","x":"Add a UV sphere. Add a cube and press G X 1 Enter, then G Z 1 Enter, so it covers one upper quarter of the sphere. Select the sphere and add Modifier tab › Add Modifier › Generate › Boolean, set to Difference, with the cube picked in its Object field. Select the cube. In the Object tab › Viewport Display, set Display As to Wire, and switch off its camera icon in the Outliner so it never renders. Move the cube with G. What you should see A bite out of the sphere in the cube's shape, with flat cut faces, that follows the cube as it moves."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 5","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-5-check","x":"Why does modifier order matter? Answer Each works on the result of the one above it. What does Ctrl + 2 do in Object Mode? Answer Adds a Subdivision Surface modifier at level 2. How do you keep an edge crisp under Subdivision Surface? Answer Put a loop cut close to it. Where does a Boolean's cutter come from? Answer Another object, picked in the modifier's Object field."},{"t":"Curves, text and the camera","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 6","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-6","x":"Draw a tube with a curve. Add text that faces the camera. Frame a shot three ways and choose a lens."},{"t":"Curves","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 6","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#curves","x":"Shift + A › Curve › Bézier adds a line defined by a few control points. In Edit Mode, G moves a point, E extrudes a new one from an end, and each point's two handles set how the line bends through it. A curve has no thickness until you give it one: in the Object Data tab (the green curve icon) › Geometry › Bevel, Depth makes it a tube and Fill Caps closes its ends. The ribbon light of the Lighting part is such a tube. The curve stays editable, so a linker, a disordered tail or the shaft of a bent arrow can be redrawn at any time; for an arrowhead, add a Cone at the end. Object › Convert › Mesh turns a finished curve into an ordinary mesh."},{"t":"Text","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 6","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#text","x":"Shift + A › Text adds a text object. Tab lets you type into it, with Backspace to delete, and Tab again leaves. Its Object Data tab (the letter a) sets Font, Size and Alignment, and Geometry › Extrude gives the letters depth. Labels for a figure usually belong in Illustrator, over the render, where they stay sharp and editable. Put text in the scene when it must sit in 3D: hidden behind the model at some angles, or moving in an animation. To keep it facing the viewer, add a Copy Rotation constraint (Object Constraints tab › Add Object Constraint › Copy Rotation) with the camera as its Target."},{"t":"The camera","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 6","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#the-camera","x":"Numpad 0 looks through the active camera (View › Cameras › Active Camera). There are three ways to aim it: Navigate the viewport to a view you like, then choose View › Align View › Align Active Camera to View (Ctrl + Alt + numpad 0). The camera jumps to where your eye was. In camera view, tick N › View › View Lock › Camera to View, then orbit and zoom as usual; the camera follows. Untick it when you are done. In camera view, treat the camera as an object: G moves the frame, G Z Z moves the camera forward and back, and R rolls it. In the camera's Object Data tab, Focal Length sets the lens: 50 mm is neutral, 85 to 135 mm flattens perspective and suits molecules, and below 35 mm exaggerates depth. Type › Orthographic removes perspective altogether, with Orthographic Scale as the zoom; use it when panels must be comparable. Output tab › Format sets the frame's Resolution. With several cameras, select one and press Ctrl + numpad 0 (View › Cameras › Set Active Object as Camera)."},{"t":"Exercise 6.1: three lenses","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 6","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-6-1","x":"Frame your model with the first method. Set Focal Length to 24 mm, 50 mm and 135 mm in turn, using G Z Z each time to bring the model back to the same size in the frame. Set Type to Orthographic and adjust Orthographic Scale until the model fills the frame. What you should see At 24 mm the near parts of the model loom and the far parts shrink. At 135 mm the model looks flatter and more compact. In orthographic, parallel helices stay parallel and nothing changes size with depth."},{"t":"Exercise 6.2: a tube you can redraw","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 6","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-6-2","x":"Add Shift + A › Curve › Bézier and set Geometry › Bevel › Depth to 0.1 m. Press Tab, select an end point, then press E, move and click. Repeat twice more. Move one point with G, then turn its handles with R. Press Tab and right-click › Shade Smooth. What you should see A smooth tube through five points that re-flows as a point or a handle moves."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 6","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-6-check","x":"What gives a curve thickness? Answer Bevel Depth, in its Object Data tab. When does text belong in the scene and not in Illustrator? Answer When it must be hidden by the model at some angles, or move in an animation. Which command moves the camera to your current view? Answer Align Active Camera to View, Ctrl + Alt + numpad 0. Which lens for figure panels that must be comparable? Answer Orthographic."},{"t":"Project: a cartoon house","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 7","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-7","x":"The second half starts here. The house uses nearly every tool of Chapters 2 to 5 once and takes an evening. Save your work, then start a new file (File › New › General), which gives you a cube, a light and a camera."},{"t":"The body and the roof","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 7","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#the-body-and-the-roof","x":"Select the cube and press Tab. Press A, then G Z 1 Enter, so the cube stands on the floor with the object's origin at its base. Press S Y 1.5 Enter: the body is now 2 m wide, 3 m long and 2 m high. Choose face selection, click the top face, and press E 1.2 Enter, then S X 0 Enter. The new top collapses to a line, the ridge. Press A, then M › By Distance to fuse the doubled vertices. Click one sloping roof face and Shift + click the other. Press Shift + D, then right-click to leave the copy in place, then P › Selection. Press Tab. In the Outliner, click the new object and rename it roof (F2). Add a Solidify modifier with Thickness 0.12 m and Offset 1. Press Tab, A, S 1.15 Enter, Tab: the roof now overhangs the walls."},{"t":"Window, door and chimney","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 7","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#window-door-and-chimney","x":"Select the body and press Tab. Press Ctrl + R over a long side wall with the yellow ring running top to bottom; click, right-click, and set Number of Cuts to 2 in the panel at the bottom left. Do the same with the ring running horizontally. The wall is now nine faces. Choose face selection, click the middle face, and press I 0.06 Enter, then E -0.08 Enter. Press Tab. Add a cube. In N › Item, set Dimensions to 0.7, 0.08, 1.3 and Location to 0, -1.5, 0.65: a door on the front wall. Add another with Dimensions 0.35, 0.35, 1.2 and Location 0.5, 0.6, 3.0: a chimney. Select both and press Ctrl + A › Scale."},{"t":"Finishing","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 7","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#finishing","x":"Select the body and add a Bevel modifier with Amount 0.03 m and Segments 2. Then select the door, the chimney and the roof, Shift + click the body last, and in the Bevel modifier's arrow menu choose Copy to Selected. Give each object a material with a plain Base Color (Material tab › New): cream walls, a red roof, a brown door, a gray chimney. For the window, select the body, press Tab, select the recessed face, add a second slot with + in the Material tab, make a blue material in it, and press Assign. Select the roof, door and chimney, Shift + click the body last, and press Ctrl + P › Object. Move the body and the house goes with it. What you should see: a gabled house with an overhanging roof, a recessed blue window, a door and a chimney, every edge slightly rounded so that it catches a highlight. For the flat storybook look, give it the toon material and outline of the Shaders part , Chapter 11 , and light it with one sun."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 7","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-7-check","x":"Why move the cube up in Edit Mode and not in Object Mode? Answer So the origin stays at the base, and Location Z 0 puts the house on the floor. What turned the flat top into a ridge? Answer Scaling the top face to zero along X, then merging the doubled vertices. Why apply scale on the door and chimney before copying the Bevel to them? Answer Setting Dimensions changed their Scale, which would stretch the bevel. How does one mesh carry two colors? Answer Two material slots, with the second assigned to chosen faces in Edit Mode."},{"t":"An anime character: the road map","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 8","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-8","x":"A character is the hardest common hobby project because it stacks several separate skills, each one a chapter's worth of practice. Nothing in it is beyond the tools of this part. It is the same extrude, loop cut and modifier, used a few thousand times with judgment about where the edges go. The stages, in the order people learn them: Stage What you learn Blender tools 1. Reference and blocking Working from front and side drawings; roughing out proportions with simple shapes Shift + A › Image › Reference, primitives, Mirror 2. Poly modeling Shaping a low-poly cage for the head, then the body Mirror and Subdivision Surface, extrude, loop cut, proportional editing 3. Topology Edge loops that circle the eyes, the mouth and the joints, so the mesh bends well Knife, loop cut, edge slide, merge 4. Sculpting, an alternative start Shaping as if in clay, then rebuilding a clean mesh over the sculpt Sculpt Mode, then retopology with face snapping 5. Hair and clothes Hair as tapered strands or flat cards; clothes as shells lifted off the body Curves with a taper, Solidify 6. UV unwrapping Flattening the surface so a 2D image can wrap around it Mark Seam, Unwrap, the UV Editing workspace 7. Texturing Painting the eyes, the face and the shading tints The Texture Paint workspace, the Image Texture node 8. Toon shading and outline The anime look: two flat tones and a drawn line Shader to RGB with a Color Ramp in EEVEE; an outline from Solidify with flipped normals, or Line Art 9. Rigging A skeleton that moves the mesh Armature, the Rigify add-on, Ctrl + P › With Automatic Weights 10. Weights and expressions How strongly each bone pulls each vertex; face shapes for speech and emotion Weight Paint mode, Shape Keys 11. Posing and animation Keyframes on bones Pose Mode, I to insert a keyframe, the Graph Editor"},{"t":"A sensible order of projects","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 8","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#a-sensible-order-of-projects","x":"The house of Chapter 7 : hard surfaces, no symmetry and no smoothing. A small prop built with Mirror and Subdivision Surface, such as a mushroom, a mug or a sword: stages 1 and 2 in an hour or two. A simple stylized head with a toon material: stages 1 to 3 and stage 8, where most of the anime look is decided. A full character, one stage at a time, following one complete course made for it instead of many short videos. Blender Studio's Stylized Character Workflow covers the whole path, sculpting first; it was recorded in Blender 2.8 and most of it needs a subscription, with a free YouTube version listed on the same page. Expect the house in an evening and a first full character over weeks of regular practice. Stages 6, 7, 9 and 10 are where most beginners stall. They are also the stages that matter least for scientific work, so there is no need to reach them before the rest pays off."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 8","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-8-check","x":"Which two modifiers does character modeling lean on? Answer Mirror and Subdivision Surface. What is topology, in one line? Answer Where the edge loops run, chosen so the mesh bends and shades well. Which stage gives the anime look? Answer Toon shading and outline, stage 8."},{"t":"What carries over to science","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 9","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-9","x":"Most of it does. Once the molecule is in it, a molecular figure is a scene like any other: it needs staging, props, clean geometry and a camera, and those are general skills. What hobby modeling will not teach is the part unique to this work, data stored on geometry, which is the subject of the Geometry Nodes and Molecular Nodes parts. Skill Where it shows up in molecular rendering Exact transforms, the pivot, the 3D cursor, Empties Light rigs that orbit the subject, turntables, camera rigs for matching panels Origins and Apply Bevels and modifiers that behave on backdrops and props Edit Mode: extrude, loop cut, bevel Sweeps, a membrane slab, a schematic receptor or cell surface, arrows Subdivision and proportional editing A curved membrane patch, a vesicle, a cell outline Boolean, Solidify, Array A cutaway through a surface or a map, a bilayer of real thickness, a repeated lattice Curves Linkers and disordered tails drawn by hand, arrows, glowing ribbons, camera paths Per-face materials, toon shading, outlines The flat illustrated style of journal schematics Lenses and composition Every figure Keyframes and shape keys Morphs between conformations, assembly animations Rigging with bones Rarely: hinge motions are usually done with morphs or Geometry Nodes UV unwrapping and texture painting Seldom: molecular surfaces take their color from attributes and procedural textures Sculpting Sometimes: organelles and cell-scale context"},{"t":"Exercise 9.1: a membrane under the receptor","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 9","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#exercise-9-1","x":"Add a plane and press Tab. Press A, then S 25 Enter, then right-click › Subdivide, and set Number of Cuts to 30 in the panel at the bottom left. Choose vertex selection and click a vertex near the middle. Press O, then G Z. Scroll until the circle covers about half the plane, type -3 and press Enter. Press Tab. Add a Solidify modifier with Thickness 4 m, then a Subdivision Surface modifier at level 2, and right-click › Shade Smooth. A bilayer is about 4 nm thick, and 1 nm is 1 m at the Molecular Nodes scale. Move the slab under your model with G Z, and give it the soft wax material from the Recipes part . What you should see A slab with rounded edges and a broad shallow dip, as thick against the receptor as a real bilayer would be, reading as one soft translucent body under the ectodomain."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 9","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-9-check","x":"Which hobby skills matter least for molecular figures? Answer UV unwrapping, texture painting and rigging. Why make the slab 4 m thick? Answer A bilayer is about 4 nm, and Molecular Nodes imports 1 nm as 1 m. What does this work need that hobby modeling does not teach? Answer Attributes and Geometry Nodes: data stored on geometry."},{"t":"Chooser, glossary and sources","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 10","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#chapter-10","x":""},{"t":"I want this, use that","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 10","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#i-want-this-use-that","x":"I want to Use Move along one axis by an exact amount G, the axis key, a number, Enter Orbit one thing around another 3D cursor on the center, pivot set to 3D Cursor, R Put one object exactly on another Shift + S: Cursor to Selected, then Selection to Cursor Set an object down on a surface Snapping to Face with Align Rotation to Target Work on one object alone Numpad / Stop a bevel or modifier from stretching Ctrl + A › Scale Change where an object pivots Object › Set Origin Move several things as one Parent them to an Empty Keep a light or camera aimed Track To A copy that stays in sync Alt + D Round a whole shape Subdivision Surface, Ctrl + 2 Round only the edges The Bevel modifier Give a sheet thickness Solidify Cut a window or a cutaway Boolean set to Difference A tube along a path A curve with Bevel Depth The camera where my eye is Ctrl + Alt + numpad 0 A command I have forgotten F3"},{"t":"Glossary","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 10","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#glossary","x":"Active object : the last one clicked; the one the Properties editor shows and that multi-object commands center on. Apply : write a transform or a modifier's result into the mesh and reset it. Collection : a folder of objects in the Outliner. Edge loop : a line of edges running around a shape; Alt + click selects one. Empty : an object with no geometry, used as a handle or a target. Modifier : a stored, live operation on an object's mesh. Normal : the direction a face's front points. Origin : the point an object's Location refers to, and that it turns about. Parent : an object whose transform its children follow. Pivot : the point a rotation or scale happens around. Proportional Editing : moving a selection so that its neighbors follow, less with distance. Topology : how a mesh's edges and faces are laid out."},{"t":"Sources","w":"Objects and modeling, Chapter 10","u":"objects-and-modeling.html#sources","x":"Bevel Modifier , Blender manual. Stylized Character Workflow , Blender Studio. In Blender, F1 over any button opens its page in the manual."},{"t":"Art direction: the whole picture","w":"Art direction","u":"art-direction.html","x":"The earlier parts each teach one group of controls: the Recipes part and the Designing a look part a material, the Lighting part a rig, the Objects part a camera. A picture uses all of them at once, and it looks designed only when they were chosen together. This part teaches that choice. In those parts a look meant one material. From here on it means the whole frame: what the molecule is made of, what it sits on, where the camera stands, how it is lit, which colors are allowed, what the surface feels like, and what hangs in the air. Chapters 1 to 3 are the method: the seven decisions, one look built and taken apart, and how to tell whether a look fits your molecule. Chapters 4 to 8 take the five decisions the earlier parts leave open, one at a time. Chapters 9 to 11 build three more complete looks. Chapters 12 and 13 are about inventing a look of your own and practicing without a recipe. Chapter 14 is a chooser, a glossary and the sources. Notation. W is the width of your model in meters, read from N › Item › Dimensions. Sizes are given as multiples of W because Molecular Nodes' scale has changed between versions (Chapter 2 of the Geometry Nodes part ). Colors are six-character codes for the Hex field of the color picker. Numbers marked tested come from renders made in Blender 5.2 with Molecular Nodes, on models 5 to 12 m wide."},{"t":"One sentence, seven decisions","w":"Art direction, Chapter 1","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-1","x":"Name the seven decisions that make up a look, and the answer each one falls back to when nobody decides. Write a one-sentence brief that every decision can be tested against. Read the seven decisions off a finished picture."},{"t":"Why a render looks regular","w":"Art direction, Chapter 1","u":"art-direction.html#why-a-render-looks-regular","x":"A regular figure is not a badly made one. It is a figure in which nothing was decided. The molecule keeps the style the import gave it and the import's colors. It is lit evenly, made of satin plastic, floating in front of a flat background, centered, and sharp from front to back. Each of those is a sensible default, chosen by someone else to be clear in every situation, and together they are the picture everyone has already seen. A designed picture differs in one way: its maker replaced the defaults with choices, and the choices agree with each other. That is why copying a picture you admire is easy and inventing one is hard. Copying hands you the choices. The skill is in making them."},{"t":"The sentence","w":"Art direction, Chapter 1","u":"art-direction.html#the-sentence","x":"Before any setting, write one sentence with two halves: what the viewer should look at first, and what the picture should feel like. \"The antibody, on a receptor carved from pale stone under museum light.\" \"The small subunits of a heavy, quiet enzyme resting in still water.\" The first half is the focus and the second is the mood. The sentence is the test for every decision that follows. A choice that serves neither half goes back to something quiet. It also tells you when to stop, which settings never do. The mood half works like the three words in Chapter 5 of the Designing a look part , one level up: those words described a surface, and these describe the room it is in."},{"t":"The seven decisions","w":"Art direction, Chapter 1","u":"art-direction.html#the-seven-decisions","x":"Decision The question A regular figure's answer Where the controls are 1. Form What is the molecule made of, and how much of its detail survives? The style the import gave it, every helix and strand Chapter 4 , and the Molecular Nodes part , Chapter 6 2. Stage What does the subject sit in or on? Nothing: it floats in front of a flat color Chapter 6 3. Camera Which lens, from how high, with the subject where in the frame, and what is sharp? 50 mm, level, dead center, everything sharp Chapter 7 , and the Objects part , Chapter 6 4. Light Where does the key come from, how soft is it, and how much contrast is there? Even light from everywhere Lighting part , Chapter 3 5. Palette How many colors, which area is lightest, and what color is everything that is not the molecule? One saturated color per chain, on white or black Chapter 5 6. Surface What would it feel like to touch? Satin plastic Recipes part and Designing a look part 7. Air and finish What hangs between the camera and the subject, and what is done to the image afterwards? Nothing Chapter 8 The earlier parts cover light and surface in full, and they hold the controls for form and the camera. Chapters 4 to 8 take the five decisions those parts leave open as choices. The whole part is about making all seven for one reason."},{"t":"Why this order","w":"Art direction, Chapter 1","u":"art-direction.html#why-this-order","x":"The first three decisions settle what is in the frame, and they are made in gray, in Solid view, before any light exists: form changes the outline, the stage adds a floor or leaves a void, and the camera crops both. Light comes fourth because it can only be judged on shapes that have stopped moving. Palette comes fifth because the same color is a different color under a different light. Surface comes sixth for the reason given in Chapter 5 of the Designing a look part : a material and its light are one thing, and the light has to be there first. Air and finish come last because blur, haze and glow hide problems in everything before them. A finish laid over six defaults is a regular figure in fog. The order is for deciding, and you will loop back. What it rules out is polishing a late decision while an early one is still a default."},{"t":"Reading a picture","w":"Art direction, Chapter 1","u":"art-direction.html#reading-a-picture","x":"Each decision leaves evidence in a finished picture, and reading that evidence is how other people's pictures become your vocabulary. Form. Squint until the detail goes and look at the outline. Count the kinds of shape: tubes only, or helices and arrows, or a skin with nothing inside. Compare their thickness with the size of the molecule. Stage. Look under the subject: a shadow, a reflection, or nothing. Then look at the corners of the frame: the same color as the middle, darker, or a different color. Camera. Say where the subject's center is in the frame and which side the empty space is on. Compare the near and far ends of the molecule: about the same size means a long lens. Find what is blurred. Light. Find the brightest side and the edge of the shadow, as in Chapter 3 of the Lighting part : direction, softness, contrast. Palette. Count the hues, ignoring grays. Find the lightest area and the darkest. Say what color the background is relative to the subject: the same hue grayed down, its opposite, or none. Surface. Find a highlight and look at its edge, as in Chapter 2 of the Designing a look part . Air and finish. Look for specks, haze, a halo around bright parts, grain, dark corners. Two readings, as examples. The first picture is an enzyme of sixteen chains drawn as thick rope, dark green with four cream chains on top, hanging just above a gray-green floor with small specks around it. Decision Reading Form Tubes only, about as thick as a helix is wide. No helices, strands or side chains Stage A floor that fills the frame, with a soft shadow under the subject Camera Long lens, looking down. Subject right of center, the left third empty. The far specks are blurred Light One large soft source from the upper left, low contrast, and a faint glow from inside the central channel Palette One hue, green, in two values, plus cream. The cream chains are the lightest area. The floor is the green with the color taken out Surface Matte, like rubber: broad dim highlights with no edge Air and finish Specks at several depths, nothing else The sentence The cream chains, on a heavy, quiet thing resting in still water The second picture is a small protein drawn as a fat glossy blue cartoon over a black floor that mirrors it. Decision Reading Form Cartoon, but swollen: helices as thick sausages, loops as fat tubes Stage A black void. A mirror floor, whose reflection is the only sign of ground Camera Long lens, level with the subject, centered, everything sharp Light Soft, from the front and above, plus light from behind that makes the edges glow Palette One hue, blue shifting toward teal. Black everywhere else. No second color Surface Glossy and slightly see-through, like colored glass or gel Air and finish Faint vertical streaks, like rain, and a soft glow The sentence One protein as a neon sign on a wet street at night Three things to notice. Each picture makes seven choices and no more. The two disagree on nearly every line and both work, so no line has a correct answer; there is only agreement with the sentence. And neither uses more than one hue and one accent."},{"t":"Exercise 1.1: read three pictures","w":"Art direction, Chapter 1","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-1-1","x":"Collect three pictures you like: one molecular render, one product photograph and one film still. For each, write one line per decision, then the sentence you think its maker had. Mark each line D if it matches the regular figure's answer in the table, or C if it was chosen. What you should find Almost no D on pictures you like, one or two hues in most of them, and a sentence that every C line serves. If a picture you like has several D lines, it is carried by one decision made very well; name it."},{"t":"Exercise 1.2: read your own last render","w":"Art direction, Chapter 1","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-1-2","x":"Open the last figure you rendered and write the seven lines for it. Mark each line D or C, then try to write its sentence. What you should find Mostly D, with C on the decisions a tutorial walked you through, usually surface and light. The sentence is hard to write because there was none. The D lines are a list of what to practice, not a list of faults: a figure for a paper is allowed to be regular."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 1","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-1-check","x":"What makes a render look regular? Answer Every decision was left at its default. Nothing is wrong with any one of them. Which three decisions are made in gray, before any light exists? Answer Form, stage and camera. Why does palette come after light? Answer The same color reads as a different color under a different light, so it can only be judged once the light is there. Why are air and finish decided last? Answer Blur, haze and glow hide problems in every earlier decision. Two pictures disagree on all seven lines and both look good. What do they share? Answer In each, every choice serves that picture's own sentence."},{"t":"A first look, built whole: soft studio","w":"Art direction, Chapter 2","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-2","x":"This chapter builds the first reading from Chapter 1 on your own model. Any model will do if it has a part you want looked at: one chain of a complex, a bound ligand or antibody, a single domain. Sizes are multiples of W, as in the notation at the top of this part. The numbers were found on a two-chain complex with W = 8.4 m. Build a complete look by taking the seven decisions in order. Say what each decision contributes by taking it out again. Change the mood of a finished look by changing one decision."},{"t":"The sentence and the sheet","w":"Art direction, Chapter 2","u":"art-direction.html#the-sentence-and-the-sheet","x":"The sentence: \"The part I care about, on a heavy, quiet thing resting in still water.\" Write the seven answers down before opening Blender. This table is the look; the build is only typing it in. Decision Choice Why it serves the sentence 1. Form Style Ribbon, thick One kind of shape reads as one object, and thickness is what makes it heavy 2. Stage A matte floor that fills the frame Resting needs something to rest on 3. Camera 85 mm, looking down, subject on the right third, shallow focus Looking down takes the horizon out; the empty left side is where the quiet is 4. Light One large soft key from the upper left, the world as fill, no rim Soft light and low contrast are quiet 5. Palette One hue in two values, and one light accent on the part to look at On a dark floor the eye goes to the lightest area first 6. Surface Rubbery Nothing glints anywhere 7. Air and finish Specks at several depths, blurred by a shallow focus They make the water without drawing it, and the blur makes the subject small"},{"t":"The build","w":"Art direction, Chapter 2","u":"art-direction.html#the-build","x":"Work in a new file with the model imported and nothing else. Stay in Solid view (Z › Solid) for steps 1 to 3. Form. In the model's tree, replace the style node with Style Ribbon (Shift + A › Molecular Nodes › Style), fed by the same Atoms link. Set Peptide Radius to 2.4 (it starts at 1.6), Backbone Smoothing to 0.8 (from 0.5) and Quality to 4 (from 3). The model should now look like rope: no helices, no arrows. Stage. Shift + A › Mesh › Plane, scaled until it is 20 W across (the plane starts 2 m wide, so S, then 10 × W, Enter). Move it under the model with G Z, leaving a gap of about a tenth of the model's height. It is large enough that it does not need centering. Give it a new material with Roughness 1. Camera. Add a camera if the file has none (Shift + A › Camera). Orbit to the side of the model you want to show, looking down at about 25°, then View › Align View › Align Active Camera to View. Set Focal Length to 85 mm and move the camera back with G Z Z until the model spans half the width of the frame, which is about 5 W away. Tilt further down if the far edge of the floor is still in the frame: the floor should be the whole background. In the camera's Viewport Display panel turn on Composition Guides › Thirds, then press G in camera view and slide the frame until the model's center sits on the right-hand line. Light. Follow the build procedure in Chapter 7 of the Lighting part as far as the key: Rendered view, World Strength 0, clay override on. Add one Area light, Disk, Size 2 W, with a Track To on an Empty at the model's center. Place it about 1 W toward the camera from the model, 1 W to the camera's left and 2.6 W above. That is the single key of Chapter 4 of the Lighting part , raised to 60° so that the shadow falls under the model where the camera can see it, and made twice as large. Start Power at 170 × W × W watts (12,000 watts for W = 8.4; Chapter 8 of the Lighting part explains the size of the number) and adjust until the top of the model is just under white. Then set the World to a light gray at Strength 0.25. Add no fill and no rim: the world is the fill. Palette. Three colors, typed into the color picker's Hex field. Body 2F5D50 , a dark green. Accent F0E6C0 , cream. Floor 39423F , the body color with most of its saturation taken out. In the model's tree, after its own color node and before the style: Set Color with the body color for everything, then a second Set Color with the accent color and, in Selection, a Select node for the part to look at (Chapter 5 of the Molecular Nodes part ). Put the floor color in the plane's Base Color. Turn the clay override off. Surface. One material for the whole model, chosen in the style node's Material field, with an Attribute node named Color into Base Color so the two Set Color nodes show through (the Recipes part describes this for any Molecular Nodes object). Use the Rubbery row of the table in Chapter 6 of the Designing a look part : Roughness 0.6, IOR Level 0.3, Sheen Weight 0.2. Air and finish. The specks are a second object that gives nothing of its own, as in Chapter 2 of the Geometry Nodes for art part . Give it the same Location as the Empty from step 4, so that the tree's (0, 0, 0) is the model's center. In its tree: Points (Count 180) with Random Value (Vector) → Position, Min (−3 W, −3 W, −0.6 W) and Max (3 W, 3 W, 0.6 W); Points → Instance on Points › Points; Ico Sphere (Radius W/150, Subdivisions 2) → Instance; then Set Material with a material of Base Color 9FB3A8 , the floor color made paler, and Roughness 0.5. On the camera, tick Depth of Field, set Focus on Object to an Empty placed on the part to look at, and set F-Stop to 0.5/W, which is 0.06 for W = 8.4. The F-Stop looks wrong and is not. At Molecular Nodes' scale your model is the size of a house, and a house photographed from fifty meters away is sharp from front to back at any real aperture. The blur in the reference belongs to a subject a few centimeters across. A very small F-Stop is how you tell Blender that the house is small. Chapter 7 returns to this. Render in Cycles with 64 samples and Denoise on, and leave the view transform at AgX. Standard clips the cream accent to a flat patch, because the accent is the one area close to white. Check the palette with numbers before trusting your eye. In the Image Editor, hold the left mouse button over the accent, then the body, then the floor beside the model, and read L, the luminance, in the footer each time. The accent should be the highest by a wide margin and the body the lowest, with the lit floor between them. In the test render the accent read about three times the body."},{"t":"Exercise 2.1: build it","w":"Art direction, Chapter 2","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-2-1","x":"Write your own sentence and sheet for your model, changing only the part to look at. Build steps 1 to 7 in order, rendering once after step 4 with the clay override still on, and once at the end. What you should see After step 4, a gray rope model above its own soft shadow, on a floor that is lightest at the bottom of the frame and falls away to dark at the top. At the end, a dark model with one light part, a few sharp specks near it and soft discs far in front and far behind, and the left third of the frame empty. If the gray render looks dull, the finished one will too: fix form, stage, camera and light before adding color."},{"t":"Exercise 2.2: take it apart","w":"Art direction, Chapter 2","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-2-2","x":"In the Image Editor's header choose Render Result. Render the finished look into the first Slot. For each line below, pick the next empty Slot, change that one decision to the regular figure's answer, render, and change it back. Form: swap Style Ribbon for Style Cartoon. Stage: hide the floor from renders with its camera icon in the Outliner. Camera: duplicate the camera, make the copy active with Ctrl + numpad 0, and give it 50 mm, a level view from 3 W away with the model centered, and Depth of Field off. Light: hide the key and set World Strength to 1. Palette: mute both Set Color nodes (select them, M) so the import's colors return, and make the floor a neutral gray, 4A4A4A . Surface: Roughness 0.45, IOR Level 0.5, Sheen Weight 0. Air and finish: hide the specks and untick Depth of Field. Step through the eight renders with J and Alt + J, and rank the seven by how much was lost. What you should see Seven near misses, each missing in its own way. With the cartoon back, the heaviness goes and the model is thin and busy. Without the floor, the model is pasted on a gray card. From the level 50 mm camera, the horizon cuts the frame in half and the specks are sharp dots that read as dust. Under even light, the form flattens and the shadow is a smudge. With the import's colors it is a regular figure standing in a nice room, and the eye no longer knows which part to go to. With plastic, almost nothing changes. Without the air, the picture is cleaner and emptier but still the same picture. The decision most tutorials teach first, the surface, matters least in this look. That is a fact about this look and not a rule, and Chapter 3 puts it to use."},{"t":"One decision, three moods","w":"Art direction, Chapter 2","u":"art-direction.html#one-decision-three-moods","x":"A finished look is also a starting point. Each of these changes one decision and leaves the other six alone. Hue. Body 5D2F3A , floor 423A3C , specks B3A0A5 . The picture is the same picture in wine red. Hue is the freest choice in a palette once the values are right. Night. Floor 14181A , World Strength 0.05, key Power at two thirds. The floor nearly disappears and the model is alone in the dark. Pale floor. Floor D9DDD8 . The cream is no longer the lightest area, so the dark body becomes the strongest contrast in the frame and the eye goes there instead. The focus has moved without touching the molecule."},{"t":"Exercise 2.3: move the focus on purpose","w":"Art direction, Chapter 2","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-2-3","x":"Render the three variants above into three Slots. On the pale floor, restore the focus without changing the floor back. You may change only the palette. What you should see The first two variants keep the eye on the accent, and the third sends it to the body. One fix is to swap the roles, giving the part to look at the darkest color and the rest a color close to the floor. Whatever you chose, the part to look at ends up as the area that differs most in value from what surrounds it."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 2","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-2-check","x":"Why is the camera tilted down in this look? Answer So the horizon leaves the frame and the floor becomes the whole background, graded by the key's falloff. Why is the F-Stop so far below any real lens? Answer At Molecular Nodes' scale the model is meters across, and only a very wide aperture blurs anything at that size. Which knockout changed the picture least, and what does that tell you? Answer The surface. This look leans on its palette, form, stage and camera, not on its material. On the pale floor the eye went to the dark body. Why? Answer The eye goes to the strongest contrast in value, and against a pale floor that is the dark part. Why keep the view transform at AgX here? Answer The accent is close to white, and Standard clips it to a flat patch."},{"t":"Does this look fit my molecule?","w":"Art direction, Chapter 3","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-3","x":"A reference is a picture of some other subject. Whether its look survives the move to yours depends on three things you can write down: what the look leans on, what that demands, and what your molecule offers. Find the few decisions a look leans on. Say what each of them demands of the subject. List what your own molecule offers, and choose references by that list. Adapt a reference whose demands your molecule cannot meet."},{"t":"What a look leans on","w":"Art direction, Chapter 3","u":"art-direction.html#what-a-look-leans-on","x":"Exercise 2.2 showed that the seven decisions do not carry equal weight. Soft studio broke when its palette, form, stage or camera was put back, and barely noticed the surface. The second reading in Chapter 1 is the opposite case: put satin plastic on that glossy blue protein and half the picture is gone. Every look leans on a few of the seven decisions, usually three or four, and could lose the rest. You do not have to build a look to find them. Run Exercise 2.2 in your head on the reading: set each line to the regular figure's answer in turn and ask how much is lost."},{"t":"What each decision demands","w":"Art direction, Chapter 3","u":"art-direction.html#what-each-decision-demands","x":"A decision that carries a look needs something from the subject. That need is why some references cannot be moved to some molecules. A look that leans on Demands of the subject When the demand is not met Surface: glass, gel, wax, anything wet Each form large in the frame: a few thick, smooth shapes The material has nowhere to show. Thin tubes in a glassy material come out pale and noisy Palette: a dark body and a light accent A part and a whole: something to make light that is small next to the rest The accent lands on an arbitrary region and points at nothing Form: rope, beads, lumps A fold that is not the message, because the form hides it The figure is about a helix or a loop that can no longer be seen Light: a rim, a silhouette, one hard shadow An outline worth reading A globular complex in silhouette is a ball Camera: a centered, symmetric frame An axis of symmetry, or one part that is plainly the middle A subject with no center, centered, looks like nothing was decided Air: deep blur, haze Depth along the line of sight A flat subject seen face-on gives the blur nothing to do Size does not appear in the table by name. A large complex suits a palette-led look because it has many parts, and a small protein suits a surface-led look because its forms are large in the frame. The part and the large form are the demand; size is only one way of meeting it."},{"t":"What your molecule offers","w":"Art direction, Chapter 3","u":"art-direction.html#what-your-molecule-offers","x":"Read the same table from the other side and it becomes a list of what a molecule has to give. Write one line for each before you look for references. Parts. Partners, ligands, domains, glycans: anything that can take the accent. Symmetry. An axis gives a centered frame a center. Outline. A long, branched or ringed shape can be lit from behind. A ball cannot. Large forms. A small protein has them. So does anything drawn as a low-resolution surface. Depth. A long subject turned toward the camera gives blur something to do. States. Two or three conformations make a sequence, a look no single structure has. Context. A membrane, a partner, a substrate: a stage that means something. As an example, here is the list for the extracellular region of the EGF receptor, with the looks it opens and closes. The lines marked tested were rendered with the two looks from Chapter 1 . Offer For this molecule What follows Parts Ligand, antibody, the dimerization arm, the two receptor chains Palette-led looks transfer. Tested on the dimer with TGF-α (PDB 1MOX): soft studio holds with the two ligands as the accent, and again with only the two dimerization arms Symmetry The ligand-bound dimer has a twofold axis, and the arms cross on it. The tethered monomer has none A centered frame suits the dimer only Outline Strong. The tethered form (PDB 1NQL) is nearly a ring Rim and silhouette looks suit it better than they suit a globular enzyme Large forms None as cartoon or tubes: several hundred residues per chain make many small features A surface-led look fails as it stands. Tested: the glassy blue material on tubes reads as pale thread Depth Modest. The dimer is about twice as wide as it is deep Blur has little to do face-on. Turn the model a quarter turn first States Tethered, extended, dimer A sequence across three panels Context A membrane underneath A floor that is part of the biology"},{"t":"When a demand cannot be met","w":"Art direction, Chapter 3","u":"art-direction.html#when-a-demand-cannot-be-met","x":"A reference whose demand your molecule cannot meet is not lost. There are four moves, in the order to try them. Change the form until the subject meets the demand. Style Surface with Scale raised from 1.25 to 2.5, Quality lowered from 3 to 2, Mean Width 4 and Mean Iterations 6 turns each receptor chain into a few smooth lumps. Tested: the glassy material that failed on tubes reads on those lumps, and satin plastic on the same lumps loses the look again, which confirms what it leans on. Chapter 4 has more forms to change to. Crop to a part that meets the demand. One ligand in its site, or the two arms where they cross, is a small subject with large forms. Move the decision to the part that can carry it. Give the glass to the small partner and something quiet to the rest. The result is a different picture from the reference, and often a better figure, because the difficult material now marks the part the figure is about. Drop that decision and keep the others. A glow from the middle of a symmetric enzyme has no place on a monomer. The palette and the floor around it still do. If none of the four works, the reference was about its subject and not about a look. Keep it for a molecule that fits."},{"t":"Finding references","w":"Art direction, Chapter 3","u":"art-direction.html#finding-references","x":"Search by what your molecule offers and by the decision you want, not by the word protein. A molecule with a strong outline and a few distinct parts has more in common with a pair of pliers, a seed pod or a bronze figure than with a globular enzyme, and photographers have already solved those subjects. Use the words for the decision: \"rim light sculpture photography\", \"low key product photography\", \"specimen on black\", \"ceramics studio photography\". Label each picture you keep with what it leans on and what that demands, in the words of this chapter. A folder sorted that way answers the question you will have later, which is never \"what do good pictures look like\" and always \"what can I do with a subject like mine\"."},{"t":"Exercise 3.1: knock out in your head","w":"Art direction, Chapter 3","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-3-1","x":"Take the three readings from Exercise 1.1 . For each, set every line to the regular figure's answer in turn, in your head, and write down the one or two lines whose loss costs the most. Beside each of those lines, write what it demands of the subject, using the table. What you should find A clear answer for each picture, and different lines for different pictures. If every line seems to matter equally, look again at the surface line: on pictures of large complexes it usually matters least, and on pictures of small glossy objects most."},{"t":"Exercise 3.2: what your molecule offers","w":"Art direction, Chapter 3","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-3-2","x":"Write the seven lines for the model you built in Chapter 2 : parts, symmetry, outline, large forms, depth, states, context. Mark each line strong, weak or none. For each strong line, name one kind of photograph, not of a molecule, that depends on the same thing. What you should find Two or three strong lines, and for each a kind of picture to go looking for. The weak lines tell you which references to stop saving."},{"t":"Exercise 3.3: adapt a reference that does not fit","w":"Art direction, Chapter 3","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-3-3","x":"Choose a reference whose main demand your model cannot meet as it stands. Write one sentence for each of the four moves, saying what it would mean for this reference and this model. Try the most promising move in Solid view for ten minutes, without materials. What you should see A gray blocking in which your model now meets the demand, or a clear reason why it cannot. Either one is a result. Most adaptations are decided in gray, long before the material that made the reference attractive is built."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 3","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-3-check","x":"How do you find what a look leans on without building it? Answer Set each line of its reading to the regular figure's answer in turn and ask how much is lost. What does a surface-led look demand of the subject? Answer Forms that are large in the frame: a few thick, smooth shapes. A look puts a light accent on a dark body. What must the molecule have? Answer A part and a whole: something small to make light next to the rest. A glassy look fails on a large complex drawn as tubes. Name two moves that could save it. Answer Change the form to a low-resolution surface, crop to a small part, or give the glass to the small partner only. Why search for references with words for a decision and not with the word protein? Answer Pictures of other subjects that offer the same thing as your molecule have already solved your problem, and a search by subject never finds them."},{"t":"Form: what the molecule is made of","w":"Art direction, Chapter 4","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-4","x":"A style in Molecular Nodes is usually chosen for what it shows about the structure. Here it is chosen for what it makes the picture out of: wire, rope, beads, or a solid thing. Form is the first decision because every later one is judged on it. Chapter 6 of the Molecular Nodes part lists the styles and their sockets; this chapter is about choosing among them. Choose a form for what it does to the outline and the mass of the picture, not out of habit. Judge a form in gray and as an outline before lighting it. Fatten, smooth and mix forms to control how much detail survives."},{"t":"Six forms of one molecule","w":"Art direction, Chapter 4","u":"art-direction.html#six-forms-of-one-molecule","x":"Tested on a receptor dimer 11 m wide, in one gray material under one light. Form Style and settings Reads as Outline Cartoon Style Cartoon, defaults A drawing of the fold: thin and busy A web full of holes that breaks up at small sizes Rope Style Ribbon, Peptide Radius 1.6 to 2.4 One object made of cord, heavier as the radius goes up Nearly solid at 2.4 Beads Style Spheres A solid thing with a grain Solid, with a rough edge Skin Style Surface, defaults A real object, with pits and a hole or two Solid and detailed Lumps Style Surface with Scale 2 to 2.5 (from 1.25), Quality 2, Mean Width 3 to 4, Mean Iterations 4 to 6 A few large smooth masses The simplest: a cloud Swollen cartoon Style Cartoon with Peptide Shape Round, Helix Thickness 2.0 (from 0.6), Helix Width 3.0 (from 2.2), Sheet Thickness 1.6 (from 0.5), Sheet Width 3.0 (from 2.5), Loop Radius 1.2 (from 0.4), Quality 4 A toy of the fold Thick loops with gaps between them The cartoon is at its best on a molecule rich in helices and sheets and at its worst on one that is mostly loops. The test receptor has few helices, so its cartoon came out as a tangle of wire with a few arrows in it. The default form is not automatically the informative one."},{"t":"Mass and detail","w":"Art direction, Chapter 4","u":"art-direction.html#mass-and-detail","x":"A form sets two things. Mass is how much of the frame the molecule's material covers. Detail is how many separate shapes the eye has to sort out. Cartoon and thin rope are low in mass and high in detail: they need a large share of the frame and a calm background. Skin and lumps are high in mass and low in detail: they survive being small, being blurred, and sitting in front of a busy stage. The test is to shrink the picture. Look at a render at the size of a postage stamp, or step back from the screen until it is that small. What still reads at that size is what the form is really showing. A cartoon of a large complex becomes gray fuzz; lumps stay lumps."},{"t":"Outline first","w":"Art direction, Chapter 4","u":"art-direction.html#outline-first","x":"The outline is what a rim light, a hard shadow or a dark-on-light palette will have to work with, so look at it before anything else. In Solid view, open the Viewport Shading popover (the arrow beside the shading buttons) and set Lighting to Flat, Color to Custom with a black swatch, and Background to Custom with a white one. The model is now a black shape on white. Orbit it. A molecule has good and bad sides, and the outline is how you find them. Tested on a receptor in its closed, ring-shaped form, turned in eight steps of 45°: four views showed the hole in the ring, two showed a lump with a notch, and two showed a slab with nothing to read. The choice of view was worth more than any setting made after it."},{"t":"Mixing forms","w":"Art direction, Chapter 4","u":"art-direction.html#mixing-forms","x":"Two style nodes with different selections, joined with Join Geometry, give two forms in one model (Chapter 5 of the Molecular Nodes part ). The eye goes to detail the way it goes to a light value, so the rule is the same as for the accent color: the part to look at gets the more detailed form, and everything else gets the simpler one. Tested: the receptor as lumps and its ligands as beads. The ligands are found at once, even in the same gray. A mix of two forms is a statement that the two parts are different kinds of thing. Use it when that is true for the picture, and use one form for everything when the molecule should read as one object."},{"t":"What a form hides","w":"Art direction, Chapter 4","u":"art-direction.html#what-a-form-hides","x":"Every form throws something away. Rope hides the secondary structure. Lumps hide everything but the envelope. A cartoon hides volume, so two chains that are packed tightly look as if they barely touch. In a figure, the form has to keep whatever the figure claims: a picture about a helix swap cannot be rope, and a picture about a buried interface cannot be a cartoon. When a form simplifies, say so in the legend."},{"t":"Exercise 4.1: six forms in gray","w":"Art direction, Chapter 4","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-4-1","x":"Give your model one gray material with Roughness 0.9, and reuse the floor, camera and key of Chapter 2 . Render the six forms of the table into six Slots, changing only the style node. Step through them at full size, then shrink the Image Editor until each render is the size of a stamp and step through them again. What you should see At full size, the cartoon is the most informative and the thinnest, the rope reads as one object, and the skin looks most like something you could pick up. At stamp size the order reverses: lumps and skin still read, the rope is a blob with texture, and the cartoon has dissolved."},{"t":"Exercise 4.2: choose the side by its outline","w":"Art direction, Chapter 4","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-4-2","x":"Set the viewport to the black-on-white outline described above. Turn the model in eight steps of 45° (R Z 45, Enter) and take a screenshot of each, or use View › Render Viewport Preview. Lay the eight side by side and choose one. What you should see Two or three views in which the outline shows something only this molecule has, and several in which it could be any protein. Turning the view is free, and it is the first thing to settle."},{"t":"Exercise 4.3: two forms, one focus","w":"Art direction, Chapter 4","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-4-3","x":"Draw the part to look at as beads or skin and the rest as lumps, each with its own style node and selection, and join the two with Join Geometry. Render in one gray, then swap the two forms and render again. What you should see In the first render the eye goes to the detailed part. In the second it goes to the rest of the molecule, and the part you care about has become a smooth pebble that is easy to miss."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 4","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-4-check","x":"Which forms survive at stamp size? Answer The ones high in mass and low in detail: skin and lumps. Why look at the outline before placing a light? Answer It is what a rim light, a hard shadow or a dark-on-light palette has to work with, and it decides which side of the molecule to show. In a mixed model, which part gets the more detailed form? Answer The part to look at; the eye goes to detail. A figure is about a helix that changes place. Which forms are ruled out? Answer Rope and lumps, because they hide secondary structure. Why can a cartoon be a poor choice even for an informative figure? Answer On a molecule that is mostly loops it is a tangle of wire, and at small sizes it dissolves."},{"t":"Palette: value first, then hue","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-5","x":"A color has three properties. Value is how light it is, saturation is how far it is from gray, and hue is which color it is. A palette is decided in that order: the first carries the picture and the last is nearly free. Plan a palette in three grays before choosing any hue. Check a palette with the Greyscale look and the luminance readout. Use hue, saturation and the color of the ground for mood once the values are fixed."},{"t":"Value does the work","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#value-does-the-work","x":"Take the three colors of the soft studio look and replace each with the gray of the same lightness: body 555555 , accent E6E6E6 , floor 404040 . Tested: the picture is intact. The eye still goes to the same part, the model still sits on its floor, and the measured brightness of accent, body and floor is 0.67, 0.37 and 0.29 in gray against 0.66, 0.36 and 0.29 in color. A palette that works in gray works in any hue. A palette that fails in gray cannot be rescued by color. So plan three roles in gray first: The ground is everything that is not the molecule, and the largest area. The body is most of the molecule. The accent is the part to look at, and the smallest area. Give each a value so that the accent differs most from what surrounds it. Three plans cover most pictures: a dark ground with a light accent (Chapter 2 ), a pale ground with a dark subject (Chapters 10 and 11 ), and a black ground with one thing glowing (Chapter 9 )."},{"t":"Checking values in Blender","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#checking-values-in-blender","x":"The setting this guide has called View Transform is labeled View in Blender 5.2. It is in Render Properties › Color Management, with Look under it. The Greyscale look. With View at AgX, set Look to Greyscale. The viewport and the render turn gray, and nothing else changes. Tested: it gives the same picture as the three-gray plan above. Set Look back to None afterwards. The luminance readout. In the Image Editor, hold the left mouse button over an area and read L, the luminance, in the footer, as in Chapter 2 . The False Color view. Set View to False Color to see exposure as a heat map: gray and green are the middle, yellow and red are near the top, and white is clipped. Tested on the soft studio look: the floor is green, the shadows blue, and the cream accent yellow with red edges, which is as bright as an accent should go."},{"t":"Hue is nearly free","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#hue-is-nearly-free","x":"Tested with the values held and the hue changed: Hue Body Floor Measured body brightness Green (Chapter 2 ) 2F5D50 39423F 0.36 Wine 814054 483E41 0.36 Navy 3D5389 3D4047 0.37 Ochre 6A502A 43403A 0.35 The four renders are the same picture in four moods. To change a hue yourself without losing the plan, turn the Greyscale look on, pick the new hue, and move the color picker's Value slider until the gray matches what it was. Then turn the look off. Choose the hue from the mood half of your sentence, from a reference, or from a convention your readers already know. Keep to one hue for body and ground, and let the accent be the only other color."},{"t":"One saturated thing","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#one-saturated-thing","x":"Tested: a vivid green body, a vivid yellow accent and a vivid teal floor. The three values collapsed to 0.62, 0.56 and 0.52, everything shouted, and nothing was the focus. Saturated colors are all of middling value, so a palette made only of them has no value plan left. Saturation is a budget for one role. The ground is always the grayest thing in the frame, the body can carry some color, and whatever is most saturated will be looked at."},{"t":"Three ways to make an accent","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#three-ways-to-make-an-accent","x":"Accent Example on the green body In color With the Greyscale look By value Cream F0E6C0 The strongest Unchanged By value and hue Light coral FF7759 Strong, and warmer Still clearly lighter By hue alone Coral darkened to the body's value, 923824 Visible The two parts merge into one gray An accent has to differ in value. A difference in hue is a bonus on top. An accent that exists only in hue disappears in a grayscale print, on a poor projector, and for many color-blind readers."},{"t":"The color of the ground","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#the-color-of-the-ground","x":"Tested under the green body, at the same value each time. The body's own hue grayed down ( 39423F ) is the quietest: subject and ground belong to one world. A neutral gray ( 404040 ) works and is slightly colder. The opposite hue ( 56383B , a dusty red) makes the subject jump forward, and the ground starts to compete for attention. Start with the grayed body hue and move away from it only for a reason in the sentence."},{"t":"Light and the view transform are part of the palette","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#light-and-the-view-transform-are-part-of-the-palette","x":"A colored light tints everything it touches. Tested: a warm key turned the green and gray-green scene olive and brown, a different mood with the same swatches. Set the color of the lights before you fine-tune any swatch (the Lighting part covers light color). The view transform changes colors too. AgX compresses bright values and takes some color out of them, so a pale accent keeps its shading. Standard shows a swatch exactly as typed, and clips: tested, the cream accent rose to 0.77 and flattened into a patch. Use Standard for flat looks (Chapter 10 ) and AgX for everything lit."},{"t":"More than two parts","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#more-than-two-parts","x":"A model with six chains does not need six hues. Sort the chains into the three roles first. Inside a role, separate neighbors by a small step in value or a small step in hue, not by a new color: two receptor chains as 6FA8A0 and 8DB9A2 read as two things of one kind (Chapter 10 uses this pair). One hue per chain is the regular figure's answer, and it tells the reader that every chain matters equally."},{"t":"Exercise 5.1: plan in gray, then color","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-5-1","x":"In the Chapter 2 scene, replace the three colors with the three grays above and render. Now choose a hue you have not used. With the Greyscale look on, set body and floor to that hue and adjust Value until they match the grays. Turn the look off and render. What you should see The gray render already works as a picture. The colored render has the same structure in a mood you chose, and its Greyscale version is hard to tell from the gray plan."},{"t":"Exercise 5.2: spend the saturation","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-5-2","x":"Render the scene three times: only the accent saturated, only the body saturated, and all three saturated. Read Luminance on accent, body and floor in each. What you should see The eye goes to whichever role is saturated, so the first render keeps the focus and the second moves it to the body. In the third the three Luminance values are close together and the picture is loud and flat at once."},{"t":"Exercise 5.3: the accent that disappears","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-5-3","x":"Give the accent a hue opposite the body's, then darken or lighten it until its Luminance matches the body's. Render in color, then with the Greyscale look. What you should see In color the accent is plain to see. In gray the model is one piece. Now move the accent's Value until the gray version separates again, and note how far you had to go."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 5","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-5-check","x":"In what order are value, saturation and hue decided, and why? Answer Value, then saturation, then hue. Value carries the picture, and hue is nearly free once the values hold. How do you check a palette's values without leaving Blender? Answer The Greyscale look in Color Management, or the luminance readout in the Image Editor. Why does a palette of only saturated colors fail? Answer Saturated colors are all of middling value, so the value plan disappears and nothing is the focus. An accent is clear in color and vanishes in gray. What is wrong with it? Answer It differs from the body in hue only. It needs a difference in value. What is the safest color for the ground? Answer The body's hue with most of the saturation taken out."},{"t":"Stage: what the subject sits in or on","w":"Art direction, Chapter 6","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-6","x":"The stage is everything in the frame that is not the molecule. A regular figure has none, which is why its subject looks pasted on. A stage does three jobs: it gives the subject a place, so it has weight and size; it gives the light something to fall on, so there is a shadow and a gradient; and it is the largest area in the frame, so its value sets the palette (Chapter 5 ). Choose among a void, a floor, a sweep, a mirror and a shadow catcher for what each one says. Build each of them. Decide how the subject meets the ground: touching, hovering or reflected."},{"t":"Seven stages","w":"Art direction, Chapter 6","u":"art-direction.html#seven-stages","x":"All seven were rendered with one model and one key light. Stage What it says What it needs Void, one flat color A diagram. No place, no weight Nothing, which is why it is the default Void, black Alone in the dark Light from behind, or the dark parts of the subject merge with the background Backdrop gradient A poster or a studio portrait A level camera; there is still no ground Floor that fills the frame An object on a table A camera that looks down far enough to lose the horizon Sweep A photographer's studio A level camera; the floor curves up into a wall behind the subject Mirror floor Night, a wet street, a showroom A black World, and light from behind Shadow catcher The page itself is the floor A white or pale page to put the render on An eighth stage is a set that means something: a membrane under a receptor, a strand of DNA under a polymerase. Build it as in the membrane exercise in Chapter 9 of the Objects part , and treat it as part of the palette's ground: grayer and quieter than the subject."},{"t":"The horizon","w":"Art direction, Chapter 6","u":"art-direction.html#the-horizon","x":"A floor seen by a level camera ends in a line, with the World above it. Tested: the line runs straight through the subject and the picture splits in two. There are three cures. Look down until the far edge of the floor leaves the frame. How far depends on the lens. Tested at 15° down, same subject size: 85 mm showed floor only, 50 mm showed a strip of World along the top, and 24 mm showed a broad band of it. Build a sweep , so there is no far edge. Use darkness. A near-black floor under a black World has a horizon nobody can see."},{"t":"Building them","w":"Art direction, Chapter 6","u":"art-direction.html#building-them","x":"Flat void. World Properties › Surface: Color and Strength. To keep the World as a light but hide it from the camera, tick Render Properties › Film › Transparent. Backdrop gradient. In the Shader Editor set to World: Texture Coordinate › Window → Separate XYZ, its Y → Color Ramp › Factor, and the Color Ramp → the Color of a second Background node. Join the two Background nodes with a Mix Shader whose Factor is Light Path › Is Camera Ray, the original Background in the first Shader input and the new one in the second. Tested with the ramp running from 1B2421 to 5F6F69 : the camera sees the gradient, dark at the bottom, and the scene is still lit by the plain Background. The gradient belongs to the frame, not the scene, so it does not move when the camera does. Floor. As in Chapter 2 : a plane 20 W across with Roughness 1. Sweep. A plane 40 W across (S, then 20 × W, then Ctrl + A › Scale). In Edit Mode, with edge selection, click the far edge, press E then Z and type 8 × W to raise a wall. Click the edge where floor and wall meet, press Ctrl + B, and set Width to 2 × W and Segments to 16 in the panel at the bottom left. Leave Edit Mode, right-click › Shade Smooth, and move the plane until the fold is about 4 W behind the model. Chapter 4 of the Objects part covers extrude and bevel. Mirror floor. The floor's Base Color near black, 050505 , and World Strength 0. Roughness sets how wet it is. Tested: 0.05 gives a clear upside-down copy of the model, 0.15 a smear of its colors, and 0.3 only a faint glow. Shadow catcher. On the floor, tick Object Properties › Visibility › Shadow Catcher, and tick Film › Transparent. The render now holds the model and its shadow on a transparent background, ready to lay on a page. Tested with World Strength 0.6, so the shadow is gray and not black."},{"t":"How the subject meets the ground","w":"Art direction, Chapter 6","u":"art-direction.html#how-the-subject-meets-the-ground","x":"The gap between model and floor is a decision of its own. Tested at three heights. With no gap, the shadow is dark and tight under the model, and the model rests: it has weight. With a gap of a tenth of its height, as in Chapter 2 , it has just been set down. With a gap of half its height, the shadow comes loose, turns soft and pale, and the model floats. On a mirror floor the gap sets where the reflection begins. With no gap, subject and reflection are one shape. A small gap makes two. The reflection moves down by twice the gap, so a large gap pushes it out of the frame: tested, at half the model's height it was gone."},{"t":"The light on the ground","w":"Art direction, Chapter 6","u":"art-direction.html#the-light-on-the-ground","x":"A floor lit by one key is not one color. Tested in the Chapter 2 scene: the floor measured 0.33 near the camera and 0.18 at the top of the frame. That falloff is a free gradient and a free vignette, and it is why a single key near the subject looks better on a floor than even light does. If the ground looks flat, move the key closer before reaching for a gradient."},{"t":"Exercise 6.1: seven stages, one model","w":"Art direction, Chapter 6","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-6-1","x":"Keep the model, material and key of Chapter 2 . Use an 85 mm camera, looking down 25° for the floor and the shadow catcher, and nearly level for the others. Build and render the seven stages of the table into seven Slots. For the black void and the mirror floor, add an Area light behind the model, aimed back at the camera. What you should see The same model as a diagram, a thing in the dark, a poster, an object on a table, a studio portrait, a thing on a wet street, and a figure on a page. Nothing about the molecule changed. Write the sentence each one would serve."},{"t":"Exercise 6.2: weight","w":"Art direction, Chapter 6","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-6-2","x":"On the floor of Chapter 2 , render the model touching the floor, a tenth of its height above it, and half its height above it. Repeat the three on the mirror floor. What you should see Resting, set down, floating. On the mirror, one shape, then two, then a model with no reflection in the frame at all."},{"t":"Exercise 6.3: lose the horizon three ways","w":"Art direction, Chapter 6","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-6-3","x":"With a level 85 mm camera and the plain floor, render once to see the horizon. Remove it by tilting the camera, then by a sweep, then by making floor and World near black. What you should see Three pictures without a line, each a different mood: seen from above, in a studio, in the dark. The cure you pick is a stage decision, not a repair."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 6","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-6-check","x":"Why does a subject on a flat void look pasted on? Answer Nothing receives its shadow or its light, so it has no place and no weight. Name three ways to get rid of a horizon. Answer Look down until it leaves the frame, build a sweep, or make floor and World dark. Why does a wide lens make the horizon harder to lose? Answer It sees more of the room, so the far edge of the floor stays in the frame at angles where a long lens sees only floor. What does the gap under the model control? Answer Weight: touching reads as resting, a large gap as floating. The floor under one key is lighter near the camera than at the top of the frame. Why does that help? Answer It is a gradient and a vignette for free, and it keeps the eye near the subject."},{"t":"Camera and frame","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-7","x":"Chapter 6 of the Objects part covers the camera's controls. This chapter covers the choices: which lens, from how high, with the subject where, filling how much, and what is sharp. All of it was tested on a receptor dimer 11 m wide on the floor of Chapter 2 . Choose lens, height, placement and crop for what each one says. Use depth of field at Molecular Nodes' scale. Fix the shape of the frame before placing anything in it."},{"t":"Lens","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#lens","x":"To keep the subject the same size while changing the lens, move the camera: its distance is the width of the frame at the subject, times Focal Length, divided by 36. For a subject that spans half the frame, that is 1.3 W at 24 mm, 2.8 W at 50 mm, 4.7 W at 85 mm and 11 W at 200 mm. Tested at those four, looking down 15°. On the molecule itself the difference is small, much smaller than on a face. The large difference is in how much of the room the lens lets in. At 24 mm a broad band of World showed above the floor, at 50 mm a strip, at 85 mm none, and at 200 mm the floor behind the model was one nearly even tone. A long lens simplifies the background. 85 to 135 mm is the default for a molecule as an object. A short lens brings the room in and puts the viewer close. Use it when the stage is part of the subject, such as a receptor standing in a membrane. Orthographic removes distance altogether. It is the lens of a diagram and of panels that must be compared. A level orthographic camera sees a floor edge-on: tested, the floor vanished and the World showed underneath it. Pair it with a void or a backdrop."},{"t":"Height","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#height","x":"Tested at 85 mm. Camera Reads as What happens to the shadow 3° down, on a sweep Face to face: a portrait, a monument A thin dark line under the subject 25° down An object on a table A soft shape beside the subject 50° down A specimen seen from above; the subject looks smaller A large part of the picture 85° down A map It lies beside the subject at full length From straight above, the test dimer was a plain bar that nobody would recognize. Under a hard, low light its shadow carried the familiar outline instead, and Chapter 11 builds a look on that."},{"t":"Placement and crop","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#placement-and-crop","x":"Tested with the camera 25° down. Centered. Formal and still. It suits a subject with a center, such as a symmetric dimer. On a third. Room on one side for the subject to face into, for its shadow to fall into, or for words. Tight , filling the frame and cut by its edges. Pattern and texture; the outline is gone, so the molecule is no longer recognizable as itself. Loose , a quarter of the frame's width. A small thing in a large room: scale, and loneliness. The empty part of the frame is part of the picture. Decide what it is for before deciding where the subject goes. In the camera's Viewport Display panel, Composition Guides draws thirds, a center cross and others over the camera view, and Passepartout at 1 blacks out everything outside the frame so the crop can be judged honestly."},{"t":"The turn","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#the-turn","x":"Tested: the same dimer turned 45° shows a near end and a far end, and looks deeper and more active. Turned 90° it is seen end on and becomes a narrow tower, a different shape altogether. Settle the turn with the outline test of Chapter 4 , before lens and placement."},{"t":"Depth of field","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#depth-of-field","x":"Chapter 2 gave the rule for F-Stop at Molecular Nodes' scale. Tested with specks around the dimer: F-Stop Specks Subject 1/W Slightly soft Sharp 0.5/W Soft discs far in front and far behind Sharp when face on; the far half goes soft when end on 0.25/W Large pale discs The far side softens even face on Blur needs depth along the line of sight. A flat subject seen face on stays sharp at any of these, so if the blur is doing nothing, turn the model. Set Focus on Object to an Empty on the part to look at, and type the F-Stop: dragging the field stops at 0.1. A very low F-Stop makes the subject look like a small model on a desk. That is the right effect for a look about smallness and the wrong one for a look about a monument."},{"t":"The shape of the frame","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#the-shape-of-the-frame","x":"Set Resolution X and Y in Output Properties › Format before placing the subject, because a placement tuned for one shape breaks in another. The tests here are 3:2. Slides are 16:9, a thumbnail is 1:1, and a journal cover is taller than it is wide and keeps its top for the title, so ask for the journal's template first. When words will share the frame, block them in as a plain rectangle while composing, so the picture has a hole of the right size."},{"t":"Exercise 7.1: four lenses","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-7-1","x":"With the floor of Chapter 2 and the camera 15° down, render the model at 24, 50, 85 and 200 mm, moving the camera each time so the model spans half the frame. What you should see Nearly the same molecule four times and four different rooms. The World comes into the frame at the short lenses and the floor turns into one tone at the long one."},{"t":"Exercise 7.2: four heights","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-7-2","x":"At 85 mm, render from 3° (on a sweep), 25°, 50° and 85° down. Write one word for what the subject is in each. What you should see Something like a monument, an object, a specimen and a map. Watch the shadow grow from a line into the main shape."},{"t":"Exercise 7.3: place it four ways","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-7-3","x":"At 25° down, render the model centered, on a third, tight and loose. For each, say what the empty space is for. What you should see Four pictures of the same thing with four different subjects: the molecule, the molecule and its space, its texture, and its smallness. If you cannot say what the empty space is for, the placement is still a default."},{"t":"Exercise 7.4: find the blur","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-7-4","x":"Add the specks of Chapter 2 . Render at F-Stop 1/W, 0.5/W and 0.25/W with the model face on. Turn the model end on and render at 0.5/W. What you should see The specks soften step by step while the face-on model hardly changes. End on, the near end is sharp and the far end is soft, and the model suddenly looks small."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 7","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-7-check","x":"What does a longer lens change most in a picture of a molecule? Answer The background: it lets in less of the room, so the stage becomes simpler. Why does a level orthographic camera not go with a floor? Answer It sees the floor edge on, so the floor disappears. What does a camera 50° above the subject do to the shadow? Answer It makes the shadow a large part of the picture. The blur is set and nothing looks blurred. Why? Answer The subject is flat along the line of sight. Blur needs depth, so turn the model or add things in front and behind. Why set the shape of the frame first? Answer A placement tuned for one shape breaks in another."},{"t":"Air and finish","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-8","x":"Air is what hangs between the camera and the subject. Finish is what is done to the image after it is rendered. Both come last, both are cheap to add, and both are the easiest decisions to overdo. Put something between the camera and the subject: specks or haze. Add glow, a vignette and a grade after the render. Tell when a finish is improving a picture and when it is hiding a problem."},{"t":"Specks","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#specks","x":"Chapter 2 built them: 180 small spheres in a box 6 W wide and deep and 1.2 W tall. They work only together with a shallow focus. Tested in Chapter 2 's knockouts: without blur they are sharp dots that read as dust on the picture, and with it they become soft discs at several depths, which the eye reads as space."},{"t":"Haze","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#haze","x":"Haze is a volume that scatters light. Add a cube large enough to hold the scene and the camera (14 W across in the tests) and give it a material with nothing in Surface and a Volume Scatter in Volume (Chapter 5 of the Shaders part ). Density is per meter, so it has to shrink as the model grows: type the number below divided by W. Density Tested result, camera 5 W from the subject 0.005/W A slight softening. Floor and body each rise a little in brightness 0.02/W The whole picture lifts. Blacks turn gray. The subject is still clear 0.06/W Milk. The subject is a pale ghost 0.15/W The subject is nearly gone The useful range is the top half of the table. Haze lowers contrast everywhere, and more with distance, so it separates near from far. It also eats the dark values a low-key palette depends on, so check the values again with the Greyscale look after adding it. It roughly doubled the render time in the tests. Lit from behind, the haze itself glows. Tested at 0.06/W with an Area light behind the model: the fog turned bright and the subject became a dim shape in it. For a backlit haze, start at the low end."},{"t":"Glow","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#glow","x":"Glow is added after the render, in the Compositor. Open the Compositor editor and click New in its header to give the scene a compositing tree, and check that Output Properties › Post Processing › Compositing is ticked. On the link from the Render Layers node to the output, add a Glare node and set its Type to Bloom. Only parts brighter than the node's Threshold glow. In a matte, softly lit scene nothing is, and the node does nothing. Something has to be bright: an emissive accent, emissive specks, or a hard highlight on a glossy surface. Tested: the accent given its own material with Emission Strength 4, on a near-black floor. Without Glare the accent is a flat white shape. With Bloom at Threshold 1, Size 0.5 and Strength 1 it has a warm halo that spills onto the dark rope beside it, and it reads as a source of light. Strength is the dial: 0.5 is a hint and 1 is obvious. Fog Glow is a softer and slower relative of Bloom; it could not be tested here."},{"t":"Vignette","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#vignette","x":"Dark corners keep the eye in the middle of the frame. The free version is the falloff of a single key across the floor (Chapter 6 ). The Compositor version, tested: an Ellipse Mask node with Size 0.85 by 0.85, into a Blur node with Size about 0.6 times the image width in pixels, into the B input of a Mix node set to Color and Multiply with Factor 0.7, with the image in A. The corners darken and the center is untouched."},{"t":"Grade","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#grade","x":"Render Properties › Color Management › Look spreads or squeezes the values of the finished render. Tested on the soft studio look, as brightness of accent, body and floor: Look Accent Body Floor None 0.66 0.36 0.29 Low Contrast 0.63 0.37 0.32 Medium High Contrast 0.69 0.34 0.26 Very High Contrast 0.74 0.33 0.21 Punchy 0.57 0.25 0.19 A contrast look moves the accent up and the ground down, which strengthens a value plan that was already right. Punchy darkens everything and deepens the colors. A look cannot create a value plan that is not there."},{"t":"Is the finish helping?","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#is-the-finish-helping","x":"Render with and without each finish into two Slots and flip between them with J. If you cannot say within a few seconds which is better, leave it out. If the picture only works with the finish on, the problem is in an earlier decision. Haze that rescues the picture is covering a stage that was never decided. Blur that rescues it is covering a form with too much detail. Glow that rescues it is covering a palette without an accent. In the knockouts of Chapters 2 and 9 , taking the air away cost almost nothing, which is what a healthy picture looks like. A flat picture has its own kind of air: far parts fade toward the color of the paper. Chapter 11 of the Shaders part builds that as depth cueing, and Chapter 10 here uses it."},{"t":"Exercise 8.1: a ladder of haze","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-8-1","x":"In the Chapter 2 scene, add the haze cube and render at Density 0.005/W, 0.02/W and 0.06/W. Read Luminance on accent, body and floor in each, and compare with no haze. What you should see The three readings move toward each other as the density rises. At the lowest step the picture is softer and still has its blacks. At the highest it is a ghost in milk."},{"t":"Exercise 8.2: make one thing glow","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-8-2","x":"Give the accent its own style node and its own material, as in Exercise 4.3 , with Emission Strength 4 and an Emission Color close to its Base Color. Darken the floor to 14181A and the World to Strength 0.05. Render without Glare, then with Bloom at Strength 0.5 and at 1. Set Emission Strength back to 0 and render with Bloom once more. What you should see A white patch, then a hint of a halo, then a light source. In the last render the Glare node does nothing at all, because nothing in the frame is brighter than its Threshold."},{"t":"Exercise 8.3: take the finish away","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-8-3","x":"Open the most finished picture you have made in this part. Render it into one Slot as it is, and into a second with every kind of air and finish off: specks hidden, Depth of Field unticked, haze hidden, Compositing unticked, Look at None. What you should see The same picture, a little plainer. If the second render has fallen apart, list which of the first six decisions is still a default, and fix that one."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 8","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-8-check","x":"Why must the haze Density be divided by W? Answer Density is per meter, and the same fog has to act over a scene that is W meters across. The Glare node is on and nothing glows. Why? Answer Nothing in the frame is brighter than its Threshold. Glow needs an emissive part or a strong highlight. What does a contrast look do to a value plan? Answer It pushes the light role up and the dark role down. It strengthens a plan and cannot make one. A picture only works with haze on. What does that tell you? Answer An earlier decision, most often the stage, is still a default. Why do specks need a shallow focus? Answer Sharp, they read as dust on the image. Blurred at different depths, they read as space."},{"t":"Night glass","w":"Art direction, Chapter 9","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-9","x":"This is the second reading from Chapter 1 , built whole. It was tested on a single chain of 260 residues, W = 4.9 m. Build a look that leans on its surface and its light, the opposite of soft studio. See why it needs large forms, a black stage and light from behind. Decide whether your own model can carry it."},{"t":"The sentence and the sheet","w":"Art direction, Chapter 9","u":"art-direction.html#the-sentence-and-the-sheet-2","x":"The sentence: \"One protein as a neon sign on a wet street at night.\" Decision Choice Why it serves the sentence 1. Form Swollen cartoon The surface needs large smooth shapes to show on 2. Stage Black void, mirror floor Night, and a wet street that doubles the glow 3. Camera 85 mm, nearly level, centered, everything sharp A sign is looked at straight on 4. Light A dim soft key in front, a stronger light behind The light behind is what makes the edges glow 5. Palette One hue, blue turning teal at the edges; black everywhere else Neon is one color 6. Surface Wet wax: light goes in, a sharp highlight sits on top The surface is the look 7. Air and finish A few faint streaks of rain, a little bloom Wet, and lit"},{"t":"The build","w":"Art direction, Chapter 9","u":"art-direction.html#the-build-2","x":"Form. Style Cartoon with the swollen settings of Chapter 4 : Peptide Shape Round, Helix Thickness 2.0, Helix Width 3.0, Sheet Thickness 1.6, Sheet Width 3.0, Loop Radius 1.2, Quality 4. Choose the turn with the outline test, looking for the side where helices show as coils. Stage. A plane 30 W across with Base Color 050505 and Roughness 0.1, leaving a gap of about 6% of the model's height. World Strength 0. Camera. 85 mm, 6° above level, the model centered and spanning 40% of the frame's width. Aim at a point below the model's center, by about a third of its height, so the model sits in the upper half of the frame and its reflection in the lower. No depth of field. Light. Two Area lights, Disk, both 3 W from the model. The key: Size 2 W, 25° to the camera's left, 40° up, Power 45 × W × W watts. The back light: Size 1.5 W, behind the model and a little to one side, 35° up, Power 200 × W × W watts, a pale blue Color (0.75, 0.9, 1.0), and Object Properties › Visibility › Ray Visibility › Glossy unticked so that it does not appear as a bright patch in the floor. Palette. One material with the color built in. Layer Weight (Blend 0.4) › Facing → the Factor of a Mix node set to Color, with A 1B5BD6 and B 37C8D8 , and the Result → Base Color. The model is blue where it faces you and teal where it turns away. The Recipes part drives Alpha with the same Facing output in its edge-glow ghost. Surface. On the Principled BSDF: Roughness 0.3; Subsurface Weight 1, Subsurface Scale 0.03 × W, and Subsurface Radius (0.15, 0.4, 1.0) so that blue travels farthest; Coat Weight 0.5 and Coat Roughness 0.05. In the words of Chapter 6 of the Designing a look part , this is the Waxy row plus the Wet row's coat at half strength. Air and finish. The rain is built like the specks of Chapter 2 , with a Cylinder in place of the Ico Sphere: Points (Count 30) in a box 3 W wide, 5 W deep and 1.8 W tall, and a Cylinder with Vertices 6, Radius W/2500 and Depth 0.12 W. Its material is an Emission shader, Color 9FD8FF , Strength 1.5. Then a Glare node as in Chapter 8 : Bloom, Threshold 1, Size 0.5, Strength 0.5. Render in Cycles with 64 samples. If the blue comes out pale, lower the lights before touching the color: tested, the first attempt with both lights about twice as strong washed the model out to light cyan."},{"t":"What it leans on","w":"Art direction, Chapter 9","u":"art-direction.html#what-it-leans-on","x":"Tested by putting each decision back, as in Exercise 2.2 . Night glass: the finished look, then each decision put back in turn, then the five variants Full size Put back Result Surface: satin plastic An opaque navy toy. The glow is gone Light: the key alone The edges stop glowing and the model sinks into the black Stage: gray floor, gray World A blue object on a table. Nothing glows, because nothing is dark Form: default cartoon Thin strips, washed pale by the same lights Palette: two bright hues Loud, and no longer a sign Camera: 50 mm from above The reflection almost leaves the frame Air and finish: none Cleaner, and hardly different The look leans on its surface, its back light and its black stage, and those three need the form. Compare Exercise 2.2 , where the surface was the one decision that did not matter. Two looks can rank the same seven decisions in opposite orders."},{"t":"What it demands","w":"Art direction, Chapter 9","u":"art-direction.html#what-it-demands","x":"As Chapter 3 showed, a surface-led look demands large forms. A small protein meets that as a swollen cartoon. A large complex does not: change its form to lumps (tested on a receptor dimer in Chapter 3 ), crop to one part, or give the glass to the small partner only. It also demands that the picture needs no accent, because one hue is the palette. If the picture has a part to look at, break the rule on purpose and make that one part warm. The look invented in Chapter 12 is built around one warm part."},{"t":"Variants","w":"Art direction, Chapter 9","u":"art-direction.html#variants","x":"Tested, each changing one setting. Helix Shape Cylinder. Helices become smooth rods: cleaner and more like a toy. Subsurface Scale 0.01 × W. Denser and darker, more like glazed clay. Subsurface Scale 0.08 × W. Pale and milky; the blue washes toward white. No teal. B set to the same blue as A. Flatter: the edges no longer turn. Style Ribbon at its defaults. On this small protein it still reads, as glowing wire. On a large complex it does not."},{"t":"Exercise 9.1: build it","w":"Art direction, Chapter 9","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-9-1","x":"Choose a single chain or a small protein, under about 300 residues, and write its sheet. Build steps 1 to 7, rendering after step 4 with a gray material and again at the end. What you should see In gray, a fat cartoon edged with light from behind, above a blurred copy of itself. At the end, a blue shape that seems lit from inside, teal where it turns away, with its reflection under it and a few pale streaks around it."},{"t":"Exercise 9.2: take it apart","w":"Art direction, Chapter 9","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-9-2","x":"Put each of the seven decisions back in turn, as in the table above, one Slot each. Rank the seven by how much was lost, and lay your ranking beside the one from Exercise 2.2 . What you should see Surface, light and stage at the top of this list, where they were at the bottom or middle of the other. Air is near the bottom of both."},{"t":"Exercise 9.3: will your molecule carry it?","w":"Art direction, Chapter 9","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-9-3","x":"Put your largest model into the finished scene in place of the small one, as a default cartoon. Try the first three moves of Chapter 3 in turn: lumps, a crop, and glass on one part only. What you should see As a cartoon, pale thread. At least one of the three moves brings the look back, and it will not be the same one for every molecule."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 9","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-9-check","x":"Which decisions does night glass lean on? Answer Surface, the light from behind and the black stage, with a form large enough to show them. Why is the back light hidden from Glossy rays? Answer So the mirror floor does not show it as a bright patch. The blue renders pale. What do you change first? Answer The lights. This material washes out when it is lit too strongly. Why does the Subsurface Radius favor blue? Answer The three numbers set how far red, green and blue travel inside the material, and a blue glow needs blue to travel farthest. What does the look cost a picture that needs a focus? Answer It has one hue and no accent, so a focus has to be added by breaking the palette on purpose."},{"t":"Paper cut","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-10","x":"Night glass asked for a small subject with large forms. This look asks for the opposite. It was tested on the receptor dimer of Chapter 3 with its two ligands, about 12 m wide as a surface. Build a look with no light in it: flat color, shaded grooves and an outline. See why it suits the large, many-part complex that a glossy look turns away. Catch an accent that exists only in hue, and repair it."},{"t":"The sentence and the sheet","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#the-sentence-and-the-sheet-3","x":"The sentence: \"The two ligands, on a receptor cut from colored paper and laid on a page.\" Decision Choice Why it serves the sentence 1. Form Lumps: a low-resolution surface Scissors cannot follow a backbone. Each chain becomes one shape with a few lobes 2. Stage None. The World is the page Paper lies on a page, not in a room 3. Camera Orthographic, level, centered No near and no far: the lens of a diagram 4. Light None A light would turn the paper back into an object 5. Palette Pale warm paper, two muted greens a small step apart, one dark coral, a near-black line A pale ground, a middle body, and an accent darker and more saturated than either 6. Surface Flat color, darkened only inside the grooves The grooves keep the lobes readable without a light 7. Air and finish A thin dark outline around every shape The cut edge of the paper"},{"t":"The build","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#the-build-3","x":"Form. Style Surface with Scale 2.0 (from 1.25), Mean Width 3 and Mean Iterations 4 (both from 1), and Quality left at 3. These are the lumps of Chapter 4 at their mildest. Choose the turn with the outline test of Chapter 4 first: in this look the outline is most of the drawing. Stage. No floor. In World Properties › Surface, set Color to the paper color of step 5 and Strength to 1. The World lights nothing here, because the material of step 6 does not respond to light. It is only the color of the page. Camera. In the camera's Lens panel set Type to Orthographic. Bring the chosen side to face the view, level, and use View › Align View › Align Active Camera to View as in Chapter 2 . Center the model and set Orthographic Scale to 1.6 × W, which makes the model span about 60% of the frame's width. An orthographic camera can stand at any distance without changing the picture. Light. None. Hide or delete every light in the file. Nothing in this look responds to them. Palette. Paper F3EEE2 . Receptor chains 6FA8A0 and 8DB9A2 , the pair from Chapter 5 . Ligands C8452D , a dark coral. Line 2B2B2B . In the model's tree: Set Color with the first green for everything, a second Set Color with the other green and a selection of the second receptor chain, and a third with the coral and a selection of the ligands (Chapter 5 of the Molecular Nodes part ). Surface. One material in the style node's Material field, made of four nodes and no Principled BSDF. An Attribute node named Color → A of a Mix node set to Color and Multiply, Factor 1. An Ambient Occlusion node with Distance 0.15 × W, its Color → B. The Mix's Result → the Color of an Emission node with Strength 1, and Emission → Material Output › Surface. This is \"ambient occlusion as the only shading\" from Chapter 11 of the Shaders part : the chain color as typed, darkened only where the surface is enclosed. Air and finish. Tick Render Properties › Freestyle and set Line Thickness Mode to Relative. In View Layer Properties › Freestyle Line Set › Edge Type, leave Silhouette ticked and untick Border and Crease. Set Freestyle Color › Base Color to the line color and leave Freestyle Thickness › Base Thickness at 3. In Relative mode the line is Base Thickness × image height ÷ 480 pixels wide, so it keeps its weight at any resolution. Tested on a 3:2 frame: 2 pixels at 480 wide and 4 pixels at 960 wide. Render in Cycles with 48 samples, and set View to Standard, as Chapter 5 advised for flat looks. Tested: the paper in the render then measures exactly the code that was typed."},{"t":"Check the accent in gray","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#check-the-accent-in-gray","x":"The first version of this look used a lighter coral, E9896A , for the ligands. In color it looked right. Measured, the ligands were 0.54 and the receptors 0.57, which is the same value. With the color taken out the ligands were gone, and only the outline said they were separate shapes. At C8452D the three roles measure 0.93 for the paper, 0.57 for the receptors and 0.33 for the ligands, and the picture survives in gray. A flat look makes this mistake easy, because nothing except the swatches sets the values: there is no light, no shadow and no highlight. To check, read L, the luminance, in the Image Editor, or set View to AgX and Look to Greyscale for one render and then set them back. The grays are darker under AgX than under Standard, and their order is what matters."},{"t":"What it leans on","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#what-it-leans-on-2","x":"Tested by putting each decision back. Paper cut: the finished look, then each decision put back in turn, then the four variants, the accent checked in color and in gray, and the look on one small chain and on a two-chain complex Full size Put back Result Form: default cartoon A black scribble. The outline follows every strand, and the color disappears under the lines Light and surface: a key light and satin plastic A rendered object with a line drawn round it. The paper is gone Palette: one bright color per chain A loud diagram. The ligands are one color among four Finish: no outline Soft shapes, like felt. Where the two receptors meet, they merge Surface: plain Emission, no grooves Flat stencils. The lobes survive only where the outline crosses a shape Stage: a mid-gray background Duller, and still the look Camera: 50 mm, from 25° above Hardly different The look leans on its form, its lack of light, its palette and its line. The camera, which soft studio depended on, matters least here. A flat picture holds almost no information about distance, so the lens has little to change."},{"t":"What it demands","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#what-it-demands-2","x":"Read this against the table in Chapter 3 . A look that leans on form needs a fold that is not the message. A look that leans on a line needs an outline worth reading. A palette with an accent needs a part and a whole. Nothing else is asked for: no large forms, because flat color has no highlight to show, and no depth or symmetry. So it suits the molecule that night glass turned away, a large complex with several parts and a strong outline. Tested the other way round: one small globular chain alone in this look is a single green pebble with nothing to compare it with. A two-chain complex with its smaller partner in the coral works at once. What it gives up is the fold. Nothing inside the outline says helix or strand, and a cartoon cannot be put back in, as the first row of the table shows. A figure about secondary structure needs another look."},{"t":"Variants","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#variants-2","x":"Tested, each changing one thing. Depth cue. Far parts fade toward the paper, which is this look's kind of air (Chapter 8 ). Camera Data › View Z Depth → Map Range › Value, with From Min the distance from the camera to the front of the model, From Max the distance to its back, To Min 0, To Max 0.55, and Clamp ticked. Its Result → the Factor of a second Mix node set to Color, placed between the first Mix and the Emission, with the paper color in B. Tested with the camera 6 W from the model's center and the range from 5.7 W to 6.35 W: the far half of the dimer fades toward the page and the near half comes forward, like two layers of paper. Full-resolution surface. Style Surface at its defaults. Busier: the outline picks up small notches, and short lines appear inside the shapes. Closer to an engraving than to cut paper. Atoms as spheres. Style Spheres with Sphere set to Mesh, and Base Thickness 1.8. Every atom gets its own ring. It works as a texture and no longer as two receptors. Dark paper. Paper 20262B , line 0B0D0F . The value plan flips, so the accent has to become the lightest role. Tested with the ligands in F6C9A8 : paper, receptors and ligands measure 0.14, 0.58 and 0.75. With the dark coral kept, the ligands measure 0.32, between the paper and the receptors, and depend on their hue again. Not tested here: Chapter 10 of the Shaders part describes a much faster relative of this look in the Workbench engine."},{"t":"Exercise 10.1: build it","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-10-1","x":"Choose a complex with at least two parts and a side with a readable outline. Write its sheet. Build steps 1 to 6 and render. Then add step 7 and render into a second Slot. What you should see First, soft colored shapes with darker grooves, like felt, running into each other where two chains overlap. With the line, cut paper: each chain is one shape with a dark edge, short curved lines mark where one lobe lies over another, and the accent is the darkest color on the page."},{"t":"Exercise 10.2: the accent that was not one","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-10-2","x":"Set the accent to E9896A and render. Read Luminance on a ligand, on each receptor and on the paper. Render once more with View at AgX and Look at Greyscale. Darken the accent until its Luminance is about 0.2 below the receptors', and render both versions again. What you should see In step 1, readings for ligand and receptor within a few hundredths of each other. In step 2, a gray dimer in which the ligands cannot be found. After step 3 they are the darkest shapes in the gray version. This is Exercise 5.3 again, met by accident."},{"t":"Exercise 10.3: take it apart","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-10-3","x":"Put each line of the table above back in turn, one Slot each. Rank the seven, and lay the ranking beside those of Exercises 2.2 and 9.2. What you should see Form, light, palette and line at the top, stage and camera at the bottom. That makes three looks and three different orders. The surface mattered least in soft studio and most in night glass, and the camera, which soft studio depended on, hardly matters here."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 10","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-10-check","x":"Why does this look need no light? Answer Its material is an Emission shader, which shows a color exactly and does not respond to light. The only shading comes from the Ambient Occlusion node. Why are the receptors lumps and not a cartoon? Answer An outline around every strand of a cartoon is a scribble. Flat color needs a few large shapes. The ligands are a clear coral in color and vanish in gray. What happened? Answer The accent matched the body in value and differed only in hue. Darken it, or on dark paper lighten it. What does Relative line thickness do? Answer It scales the line with the image height, so the outline keeps its weight at any resolution. Which molecules suit this look? Answer Ones with several parts and a strong outline, at any size. It asks for no large forms, depth or symmetry."},{"t":"Specimen","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-11","x":"Chapter 7 noted that a molecule seen from above can be a plain shape while its shadow carries the outline. This look is built on that. It was tested on the receptor dimer of Chapter 3 as a full surface, W = 11.5 m. Build a look carried by one hard, low light and the shadow it throws. Use the shadow as a second view of the molecule. Set the shadow's length, edge and darkness with three numbers."},{"t":"The sentence and the sheet","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#the-sentence-and-the-sheet-4","x":"The sentence: \"The whole shape of the receptor, as a specimen on a table in low evening sun.\" Decision Choice Why it serves the sentence 1. Form Skin: a full surface A specimen is a solid thing, and a solid thing throws a solid shadow 2. Stage A pale matte floor that fills the frame, with the model standing on it The shadow needs something to fall on, and a specimen rests 3. Camera 85 mm, 55° down, the model a third of the frame wide and left of center High enough to see the whole shadow, with room for it to fall into 4. Light One Sun, 20° above the horizon, from the camera's left. A weak World as fill A low, hard light draws a long, sharp shadow 5. Palette Cream ground, rust subject, and the gray of the shadow between them Three values: pale ground, middle shadow, dark subject 6. Surface Matte clay Nothing glints. The light is the event 7. Air and finish None Hard light wants clean air"},{"t":"The build","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#the-build-4","x":"Form. Style Surface at its defaults. With the outline test of Chapter 4 , choose two sides of the model: one for the camera and one for the sun. The section after this one explains why. Stage. A plane 40 W across with Base Color E9E1D0 and Roughness 1. Raise it until it touches the lowest point of the model. Chapter 6 showed that a model touching its floor has weight. Camera. 85 mm, looking down at 55°. The model spans a third of the frame's width, with its center about an eighth of the frame left of the middle, so the right half is empty floor. No depth of field. Light. A Sun with Strength 4 and Angle 1.5°. Give it a Track To on an Empty at the model's center, as for the key in Chapter 2 , and move it to the camera's left and low: on the ground plan about 1.2 W toward the camera from the model and 2.6 W to the camera's left, and 1 W above the model's center. That is 65° round from the camera and 20° above the horizon. Only a Sun's direction counts, so these distances are just a way of setting the angle. Set the World to a light gray at Strength 0.35. Palette. Ground E9E1D0 , subject A8452E , and no third swatch, because the third value is the shadow. Tested: ground, shadow and subject measure 0.68, 0.48 and 0.28. Under a sun this low the cream floor renders as a warm light gray, and it is still the lightest value in the frame. Surface. Roughness 0.9 and IOR Level 0.2, between the Dry and Chalky rows of Chapter 6 of the Designing a look part . Air and finish. None. Render in Cycles as in Chapter 2 , with View at AgX."},{"t":"The shadow is a second view","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#the-shadow-is-a-second-view","x":"The shadow on the floor is the outline of the model as the sun sees it, stretched along the ground away from the sun. So this look shows two views of the molecule at once: the camera's in color and the sun's in gray. Tested with the sun at four places round the dimer: Sun Shadow Beside the camera Repeats what the camera sees, and most of it hides behind the model 65° to the camera's left (the look) A long band beside the model with a notch at its far end. A stretched outline, readable but plain 90° to the left, where the sun sees the dimer end on A thin streak with nothing in it Behind and to the left, 150° round Falls toward the camera with a clear outline. The side of the model facing the camera is in shade, and the picture turns dark and dramatic One more test: the dimer turned end on to the camera, with the sun where the look has it. The camera sees a narrow tower. The shadow shows the broad outline with both lobes and the gap between the receptors. The shadow shows the side that the camera cannot see, and of all these renders it was the one in which the shadow said most. So choose the sun's direction the way Chapter 4 chose the camera's: with the black-on-white outline, looking from where the sun will be. Specimen: the sun at four places round the dimer, then the dimer end on, one small globular chain, a two-chain complex and the sun 10° up at Strength 8, then World Strength at 0, 0.1, 0.35 and 0.8 Full size"},{"t":"Three numbers","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#three-numbers","x":"Length is the height of the sun. The shadow is as long as the model is tall, divided by the tangent of the sun's height: 1.2 times the model's height at 40°, 2.7 times at 20° and 5.7 times at 10°. Tested: at 40° it ends well inside the frame, at 20° it reaches the edge, and at 10° it runs out of the frame. A lower sun also spreads its light over more ground. The lit floor measured 0.74, 0.68 and 0.61 at the three heights while the shadow stayed at 0.48, so a long shadow is also a weaker one. Raise Strength as the sun comes down: tested, Strength 8 at 10° matched Strength 4 at 20°. Edge is the Sun's Angle. At 1.5° the edge is sharp near the model and slightly soft at the far end. At 10° the far end of the shadow dissolves. Chapter 2 of the Lighting part gives 0.5° for the real sun. Darkness is the World. The shadow gets no sunlight, so World Strength sets its value. Tested: at 0 the shadow is black and the shaded side of the model disappears into it. At 0.1 it measures 0.25, at 0.35 it measures 0.48, and at 0.8 it is a faint stain of 0.65 on a floor of 0.73."},{"t":"What it leans on","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#what-it-leans-on-3","x":"Tested by putting each decision back. Specimen: the finished look, then each decision put back in turn, then three changes to the sun and five variants Full size Put back Result Light: one large soft key A small soft smudge under the model. The look is gone Stage: no floor Nothing receives the shadow. A red object on a gray card Camera: level The shadow is a thin strip behind the model, and a horizon cuts the frame Palette: a dark ground The shadow sinks into the floor. Ground and shadow measure 0.22 and 0.11 Form: default cartoon A wire model with a shadow like lace. A different look, and not a bad one Surface: satin plastic Small glints on the surface. Little changes Air and finish There was none to take away The look leans on its light, its stage and its camera, and on a ground pale enough to show a shadow. Form and surface are nearly free, which makes this the look to reach for when a molecule has no part to accent and no material that suits it."},{"t":"What it demands","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#what-it-demands-3","x":"An outline worth reading, from the sun's side. Tested on one small globular chain: the shadow is a stretched oval that says nothing the model did not. A two-chain complex that is longer than it is wide gave a shadow with more shape in it. The receptor dimer, with the sun on its broad side, gives two lobes and the gap between them. The closed, ring-shaped receptor of Chapter 4 , drawn as thick rope, gives a shadow with the hole of the ring in it. It also demands room. The subject is a third of the frame wide, so nothing small on it will be read: this is a look about the whole shape. A part can still be marked. Tested with the ligands in cream, F3EBD3 : they show as two light patches on the rust."},{"t":"Variants","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#variants-3","x":"Tested, each changing one thing. Ink. Subject 1F3A5F . It works unchanged, and so should any dark color. Glazed. Roughness 0.35 with Coat Weight 1 and Coat Roughness 0.08. Under a hard light every bump of the surface gets its own glint and the model glitters. A hard light and a glossy, detailed surface do not go together. Rope. The closed receptor as Style Ribbon with Peptide Radius 3.0. A ring of rope, and a shadow that keeps the hole. From straight above. The camera 88° down. The dimer is a plain bar and the shadow carries the outline: a map with a drawing beside it. Lace. The cartoon row of the table above, kept on purpose."},{"t":"Exercise 11.1: build it","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-11-1","x":"Write the sheet for your model, naming the side the camera sees and the side the sun sees. Build steps 1 to 4 and render with the clay override on. Then turn the clay override off, build steps 5 to 7 and render again. What you should see The gray render is already the picture: a pale floor, a gray shadow two to three times as long as the model is tall, and a darker model. Color adds little. If the gray render is dull, look at the shape of the shadow, because the sun is seeing a poor side."},{"t":"Exercise 11.2: two views","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-11-2","x":"With the outline view of Chapter 4 , find the side of your model with the best outline and the side with the worst. Render with the camera on the worst side and the sun seeing the best. Swap them and render again. What you should see In step 2, a plain model beside a shadow that explains it. In step 3, a readable model beside a streak that adds nothing. The shadow earns its place when it shows something the camera does not."},{"t":"Exercise 11.3: three numbers","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-11-3","x":"Render with the sun 40°, 20° and 10° up, reading Luminance on the lit floor each time. At 20°, render with Angle 1.5° and 10°. At 20°, render with World Strength 0, 0.35 and 0.8. What you should see A short, a long and a very long shadow, on a floor that darkens as the sun drops. Then a crisp shadow and one whose far end dissolves. Then a black shadow, a gray one and a faint one."},{"t":"Exercise 11.4: take it apart","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-11-4","x":"Put each line of the table above back in turn, one Slot each, and rank them. What you should see Light, stage and camera at the top, with the dark ground close behind. Form and surface are at the bottom, the reverse of night glass."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 11","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-11-check","x":"What does the shadow show? Answer The outline of the model as seen from the sun, stretched along the ground. A model is 9 m tall and the sun is 20° up. How long is the shadow? Answer 9 divided by the tangent of 20°, about 25 m, or 2.7 times the height. What sets how dark the shadow is? Answer The World. The shadow receives no sunlight, so World Strength sets its value. Why does a dark ground spoil this look? Answer A shadow can only be darker than its ground, and a dark ground leaves it no room. Why is the camera high? Answer From a level camera the shadow is a thin strip behind the model. From above it is a shape."},{"t":"Inventing a look","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-12","x":"Chapters 2 , 9 , 10 and 11 handed you four sheets. This chapter is about writing the fifth yourself. It adds no new controls. It is an order of work, and every step uses something from an earlier chapter. Get from a blank file to a look of your own by a fixed order of work. Choose among ideas with small gray pictures before building any of them. Find a number with a ladder of renders. Borrow single decisions, with their partners, in place of whole pictures."},{"t":"Where the first lines come from","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#where-the-first-lines-come-from","x":"A blank sheet is hard to fill because all seven lines are open at once. Two things you already have will close most of them. The strongest offer picks the decision to lean on. Write the list of Chapter 3 for your molecule and take its strongest line. Strongest offer Decision to lean on Built in Parts Palette: an accent on one part Chapters 2 and 10 Outline Light from behind, a hard shadow, or a drawn line Chapters 10 and 11 , and the example below Large forms Surface: glass, wax, anything wet Chapter 9 Depth Air: blur or haze, with the model turned toward the camera Chapters 7 and 8 Symmetry Camera: a centered, formal frame Chapter 7 States One look repeated across panels, with an orthographic camera Chapter 7 Context Stage: a set that means something Chapter 6 The sentence picks the rest. The mood half of the sentence is made of words, and each word lives in particular decisions. These rows collect what the earlier chapters tested. Mood Decisions that carry it Tested in Quiet Soft light, low contrast, one hue, a matte surface, empty space on one side Chapter 2 Heavy A thick form that touches its floor Chapters 2 and 6 Small, precious A very low F-Stop, a loose frame, a high camera Chapter 7 Alone A black void or a wide empty floor, and a loose frame Chapters 6 and 7 Night, electric A black stage, a mirror floor, light from behind, one glowing hue Chapter 9 Clear, explained No light, flat color, an orthographic camera, a pale page Chapter 10 Still, observed A hard low sun, a pale floor, a long shadow, clean air Chapter 11 Monumental A camera near the floor, a centered frame, a sweep Chapters 6 and 7"},{"t":"Borrow decisions, and their partners","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#borrow-decisions-and-their-partners","x":"You will not invent a decision. Every one has been made before, in a reference, in an earlier chapter, or in a photograph of a teapot. What is yours is the combination and the reason for it. So fill each line of the sheet from a source you can name, and write the source beside it. Some decisions do not travel alone. Each row below was tested somewhere in this part. If you borrow Borrow with it Otherwise A mirror floor A black World, light from behind, and lights hidden from Glossy rays A gray table, or white patches in the floor (Chapters 6 and 9 ) Specks A shallow focus Dust on the picture (Chapters 2 and 8 ) Glow Something emissive, or a hard highlight The Glare node does nothing (Chapter 8 ) A long shadow A pale floor and a high camera The shadow sinks into the floor or hides behind the model (Chapter 11 ) Glass or wax Large smooth forms, and light from behind Pale thread (Chapters 3 and 9 ) A drawn outline Lumps or a skin A scribble (Chapter 10 ) A plain floor A camera that looks down, or a sweep A horizon through the subject (Chapter 6 ) Shallow focus Depth along the line of sight Nothing blurs (Chapter 7 ) Haze A palette that can afford to lose its blacks A gray frame (Chapter 8 ) An accent in a new hue A step in value as well It vanishes in gray (Chapters 5 and 10 ) Every look in this part leans on three or four decisions and keeps the others plain. Soft studio leans on palette, form, stage and camera. Night glass leans on surface, light and stage. Paper cut leans on form, the absence of light, palette and line. Specimen leans on light, stage and camera. So borrow one or two strong decisions, give them their partners, and fill the remaining lines with the quietest answer that agrees with the sentence. A sheet in which every line is loud has no subject."},{"t":"Choose in gray, small and fast","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#choose-in-gray-small-and-fast","x":"Three ideas compared for five minutes beat one idea polished for an hour. Make the comparison before any material exists. Outline thumbnails settle the side. Use the black-on-white viewport of Chapter 4 and View › Render Viewport Preview, in eight turns of 45°. Gray thumbnails settle the sheet. For each candidate, set up only form, stage, camera and the main light. Turn on the clay override (Chapter 7 of the Lighting part ), set the % field under Resolution X and Y in Output Properties › Format to 25, and render into a Slot. Judge the thumbnails at the size they are. A candidate that reads at the size of a stamp will read at any size (Chapter 4 ). Choose the one that already looks like the sentence, and set % back to 100 only for that one."},{"t":"Tune one number with a ladder","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#tune-one-number-with-a-ladder","x":"A number such as a light's height or a material's Subsurface Scale is found, not known. Render it at three to five values that run from clearly too little to clearly too much, changing nothing else, and put the renders side by side in Slots. That row is a ladder. If the best render is at one end, extend the ladder that way. If it is in the middle, you are done, or you can run a finer ladder between its two neighbors. Two rules keep this cheap. Render a ladder at a quarter of the resolution. And build one only for a number on a decision the look leans on, because the others will not change the picture enough to be worth it. This part is full of ladders: F-Stop in Chapter 7 , haze Density in Chapter 8 , Subsurface Scale in Chapter 9 , the height of the sun in Chapter 11 ."},{"t":"A worked example","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#a-worked-example","x":"Tested from a blank file, on the tethered receptor of Chapter 3 with its ligand (PDB 1NQL), W = 8.3 m. Offers. Outline: strong, nearly a ring. Parts: one small ligand. Large forms, symmetry, depth: none worth using. Sentence. \"The ligand, on a receptor that waits closed in the dark.\" Outline thumbnails. Eight turns of 45°. Four showed the hole in the ring, two showed a lump with a notch, and two showed a slab. The first of the four was kept. Gray thumbnails. Four candidate sheets, blocked at 240 pixels wide. Each rendered in under fifteen seconds on two processor cores. Candidate What the thumbnail showed A. Black void, level camera, lit from behind A dark figure edged with light, with the hole in the ring black against it B. Pale floor, camera above, low sun A small model and a long shadow with the hole in it C. Floor, camera 25° down, soft key A pale ring of rope on a table. Quiet D. No stage, orthographic, flat with a line A gray cutout with a white hole All four showed the ring. Only A was dark, so the sentence chose A. None of them showed the ligand, which said in advance that the focus would have to come from the palette. The sheet, with its sources. Decision Choice Borrowed from 1. Form Thick rope Soft studio. An outline needs one simple, solid shape 2. Stage Black void, mirror floor Night glass 3. Camera 85 mm, nearly level, loose and left of center Chapter 7 : loose for a thing alone, and off center because this subject has no symmetry 4. Light Two lights behind, nothing in front The rim-only setup in Chapter 4 of the Lighting part 5. Palette A near-black body and one warm glowing part The third plan of Chapter 5 6. Surface Velvety The Velvety row in Chapter 6 of the Designing a look part : dark in the middle, glowing at the rim 7. Air and finish Bloom on the glowing part Chapter 8 The first render, and what was wrong with it. Two things. The two lights appeared in the mirror floor as white patches, which is the missing partner from the table above: unticking Glossy on both lights removed them. And the edge of light was too faint to carry the picture. Raising Sheen Weight from 0.5 to 1 and Roughness from 0.5 to 0.8 widened it a little, and doubling the power of the lights made the outline read. The worked example: the first render and the three fixes that made the look, then the three ladders, the look with one decision changed at a time, and the four gray thumbnails Full size Three ladders. Height of the lights: at 25° the light runs down both sides of the ring, at 40° more of it lands on the tops, and at 60° only the top edges are lit and the sides go dark. Position: at 140° round from the camera the edge is a clear line, and at 160° it is thinner and dimmer. Body color: 2C4656 in place of 16242E shows blue-gray surface between the edges and weakens the outline. Kept: 25°, 140° and the darker body. One decision changed at a time. A key added in front: the body becomes a gray rope and the outline stops being the subject. One light behind in place of two: half the ring is lost in the black. No warm accent: a cold outline with nothing to look at. Gray floor and gray World: a black rope floating over a table. A thin haze of 0.01/W: the lights behind turn the air gray and the black is gone. The look leans on its light, its stage and its one warm part. The finished sheet. Decision Setting 1. Form Style Ribbon, Peptide Radius 3.0, Backbone Smoothing 0.8, Quality 4. One style node for the receptor and one for the ligand, as in Exercise 4.3 2. Stage Floor 050505 , Roughness 0.12, a gap of 4% of the model's height. World Strength 0.01 3. Camera 85 mm, 5° above level, aimed a fifth of the model's height below its center. The model spans 36% of the frame's width, an eighth of the frame left of the middle 4. Light Two Area lights, Disk, Size 0.6 W, 3 W from the model, 140° round from the camera on either side and 25° up. Each 520 × W × W watts, Color (0.75, 0.92, 1.0), Glossy unticked. No key 5. Palette Body 16242E , ligand FFB36B 6. Surface Receptor: Roughness 0.8, Sheen Weight 1. Ligand: Emission Color FFB36B , Emission Strength 3 7. Air and finish Glare: Bloom, Threshold 1, Size 0.5, Strength 0.6 The whole example was about twenty-five small renders. None of its seven lines is new. The picture is."},{"t":"Exercise 12.1: three sheets for one molecule","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-12-1","x":"Write the offers of your molecule and a sentence for it. Write three sheets that lean on three different decisions. Beside every line, name where you borrowed it from and check the partners table. What you should find The first sheet comes easily and resembles a look from this part. The second and third are harder and more your own. If a line has no source you can name, it is probably a default, so replace it or mark it quiet on purpose."},{"t":"Exercise 12.2: choose in gray","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-12-2","x":"Make the eight outline thumbnails and choose a side. Block the three sheets in gray at 25%, one Slot each. Choose one by looking at the thumbnails at their own size. What you should see Three clearly different pictures, in less time than one finished material takes. One will already look like your sentence. If none does, the sentence is asking for a decision that none of the sheets leans on."},{"t":"Exercise 12.3: build, fix, tune","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-12-3","x":"Build the chosen sheet in the order of Chapter 1 . Write down everything that is wrong with the first full render, and beside each fault the decision it belongs to. Run a ladder for each of two numbers on the decisions your look leans on, three to five values each. What you should see A first render with two or three plain faults, each belonging to one decision. Most are a missing partner. After the ladders, numbers that you chose because you saw their neighbors."},{"t":"Exercise 12.4: prove it and keep it","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-12-4","x":"Put each decision back to the regular answer in turn, as in Exercise 2.2 , and rank the seven. Write the finished sheet with sizes as multiples of W, as in the example, and save it with a small render. What you should see Three or four decisions that the look cannot lose, and they should be the ones you meant to lean on. If the ranking surprises you, the look is not the one you thought you were making, and the sentence or the sheet needs changing. The saved sheet is now a look you can put on another molecule."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 12","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-12-check","x":"All seven lines of the sheet are open. Where do you start? Answer With the molecule's strongest offer, which picks the decision to lean on, and the mood words of the sentence, which pick most of the rest. Why do gray thumbnails come before any material? Answer Form, stage, camera and the main light decide whether the picture works, and they can be compared in minutes at a quarter of the size. How do you find a number such as the height of a light? Answer With a ladder: three to five values from too little to too much with nothing else changed, and a pick from the row. A borrowed mirror floor shows white patches. What was left behind? Answer Its partner: the lights have to be hidden from Glossy rays, under a black World. How many decisions should a look lean on? Answer Three or four. The others stay plain and agree with the sentence."},{"t":"Briefs with no recipe, and when it looks wrong","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-13","x":"Every look so far came with a sheet or showed one being written. This one gives only briefs. It also collects in one table what went wrong in the tests behind this part, because your own pictures will go wrong in the same ways. Carry a brief from one sentence to a finished picture with no recipe. Judge your own picture with five tests before anyone else sees it. Trace a fault to the decision it belongs to. Decide how much of a look a scientific figure can carry."},{"t":"Five tests for your own picture","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#five-tests-for-your-own-picture","x":"Run them in this order on anything you are about to show. The sentence. Read your sentence, look away, then look at the picture for two seconds. The first thing you looked at should be the thing the sentence names (Chapter 1 ). The stamp. Shrink the picture to the size of a stamp. It should still read (Chapter 4 ). Gray. Turn on the Greyscale look. The focus should survive (Chapter 5 ). The knockout. Put the decisions back one at a time, in your head or in Slots. The ones the picture cannot lose should be the ones you meant it to lean on (Chapters 2 and 3 ). Bare. Turn off every kind of air and finish. It should be the same picture, a little plainer (Chapter 8 ). A picture that fails one of them has a fault you can name, and the table below says where to look."},{"t":"When it looks wrong","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#when-it-looks-wrong","x":"Faults inside one material are in Chapter 8 of the Designing a look part , and faults inside one rig are in Chapter 9 of the Lighting part . This table is for faults between decisions. Every row happened in the tests for this part. Symptom Likely cause Fix It looks like every other render Nothing was decided: each line of the sheet is at its default Write the sentence, then change the two lines it cares about most (Chapter 1 ) The subject looks pasted on Stage: nothing receives its shadow or its light A floor, a sweep or a shadow catcher (Chapter 6 ) A line cuts the frame behind the subject Stage and camera: a level camera over a plain floor Look down, build a sweep, or make floor and World dark (Chapter 6 ) The model seems to float Stage: the gap under it is too large A tenth of its height, or touching (Chapter 6 ) The eye goes to the wrong part Palette or form: the focus is not the strongest contrast. The ground is too pale, another role is more saturated, or another part has more detail Check the values in gray. Give the focus the largest step in value, the saturation and the detail (Chapters 2 , 4 and 5 ) The focus is clear in color and gone in gray Palette: the accent differs in hue only Darken or lighten it until it separates in gray (Chapters 5 and 10 ) Everything shouts and nothing leads Palette: every role is saturated, so the values have collapsed Gray the ground and keep saturation for one role (Chapter 5 ) Thin and busy, and it dissolves when small Form: a cartoon or thin tubes on a large molecule Rope, skin or lumps (Chapter 4 ) Glass or wax comes out as pale thread Form and surface: a surface-led look on forms too small to show it A swollen cartoon, lumps, a crop, or the material on one part only (Chapters 3 and 9 ) A glowing material washes out to a pale tint Light: too strong for a subsurface material Lower the lights before touching the color (Chapter 9 ) The model glitters Surface and light: a coat or a low Roughness on a detailed surface, worst under a hard light Smooth the form or roughen the surface (Chapter 11 ) The outline is a scribble Form: lines drawn on a cartoon Lumps or a skin (Chapter 10 ) White patches in a mirror floor Light: the lights are visible to Glossy rays Untick Glossy in each light's Ray Visibility (Chapters 9 and 12 ) The mirror floor shows no reflection Stage or camera: the gap is too large, or the camera too high Lower the model or the camera (Chapters 6 and 9 ) An edge light is faint, or half the outline is missing Light: too weak, too far behind, or on one side only More power, a second light, 140° round in place of 160°, a higher Sheen Weight (Chapter 12 ) A long shadow is a plain streak Light: the sun sees a poor side of the model Move the sun round until the shadow has a shape (Chapter 11 ) A long shadow is faint, or solid black Light: the sun is low and weak, or World Strength is too high or at zero Raise Strength as the sun drops, and keep World Strength near 0.35 (Chapter 11 ) Specks look like dust on the image Air: there is no shallow focus Depth of Field on, with F-Stop near 0.5/W (Chapters 2 and 8 ) Depth of Field is on and nothing blurs Camera: an F-Stop from a real lens, or a subject that is flat along the line of sight Type an F-Stop near 0.5/W, and turn the model (Chapters 2 and 7 ) The subject looks like a toy on a desk Camera: the F-Stop is too low for this sentence Raise it toward 1/W, or turn Depth of Field off (Chapter 7 ) The blacks have turned gray Air: haze, most of all with a light behind it Density down to 0.005/W, or no haze (Chapters 8 and 12 ) Glare is on and nothing glows Finish: nothing in the frame is brighter than Threshold Give one part an emissive material (Chapter 8 ) A pale accent is a flat white patch Finish: the Standard view clips colors near white Set View to AgX (Chapters 2 and 5 ) The picture only works with blur, haze or glow on An earlier decision is still a default Turn the finish off and fix what it was hiding (Chapter 8 ) Everything is decided and it is still dull The sheet leans on nothing, or the side shown has a poor outline Choose one decision to lean on, and run the outline test (Chapters 4 and 12 )"},{"t":"Looks and scientific figures","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#looks-and-scientific-figures","x":"A look says what matters in a picture. A figure also makes a scientific claim, and the two must not disagree. Three questions decide how much look a figure can carry. Does the form keep what the figure claims? Rope hides secondary structure, and lumps hide everything except the envelope (Chapter 4 ). If the claim is about something the form hides, change the form. If the form simplifies, say so in the legend. Does the focus survive the page? Printed in gray, shrunk to the width of a column, or seen by a color-blind reader, the accent has to stay an accent. That means a step in value (Chapter 5 ). Could anything be mistaken for data? Specks can be read as particles or solvent, a glow as a signal, haze as density, and a reflection as a second copy. In a figure, leave out any air and finish that the legend does not explain. Where the picture will be seen sets how far to go. Where How much look Why A panel in the main text A chosen form and palette on a pale or transparent ground, even light, and an orthographic camera wherever panels are compared It has to be read closely and trusted. Chapter 6 of the Lighting part gives the rules for its light A summary figure, a graphical abstract, a slide One look for every panel, such as paper cut or soft studio It is seen for seconds, and one look is what makes it one figure A cover, a poster heading, a title slide, a website Any look Its job is to be looked at Wherever it goes, use one look per figure. If two panels differ in look, the reader takes the difference for a result. A regular figure remains a legitimate choice (Chapter 1 ). After this part it should be a choice."},{"t":"Exercise 13.1: a look on a second molecule","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-13-1","x":"Take one of the four built looks and a molecule from a different family from the one you built it on: a nucleic acid, a membrane protein, a capsid. Write what the look leans on, what that demands, and what the new molecule offers (Chapter 3 ). Change at most two lines of the sheet, and build it. What you should see The same look on a subject that had to be met halfway. For each line you changed, you should be able to name the demand that forced it. If you had to change more than two, you have made a different look, which is also a result."},{"t":"Exercise 13.2: one molecule, three moods","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-13-2","x":"Keep one molecule, one side and one focus. Write three sentences that differ only in mood: one quiet, one electric, one explained. Fill the three sheets from the mood table of Chapter 12 and build them. What you should see Three pictures that a stranger would put in three different rooms, with the eye going to the same part in each. If two of them look alike, their sheets lean on the same decisions."},{"t":"Exercise 13.3: borrow from an object","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-13-3","x":"Choose a photograph you admire of something that is not a molecule: a product, a sculpture, a plate of food. Read its seven lines and mark the two it leans on most (Chapters 1 and 3 ). Rebuild those two on your molecule, with their partners, and keep the other five quiet. What you should see A picture that recalls the photograph without copying it. Where a demand could not be met, one of the four moves of Chapter 3 should be visible in the result."},{"t":"Exercise 13.4: a panel that survives print","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-13-4","x":"Take a claim from a paper you know, of the kind \"this part touches that part\". Make a panel for it that will be 4 cm wide on the page and may be printed in gray. Use an orthographic camera, a pale ground, and a form that keeps what the claim is about. Run the stamp test and the gray test at the printed size. What you should see A panel in which the claim can be found in two seconds, at 4 cm, in gray. It will probably be close to paper cut, or to a regular figure with a chosen form and palette. Write the one sentence of legend that says what the form leaves out."},{"t":"Exercise 13.5: a cover","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-13-5","x":"Set the frame to the journal's template, or to 2400 by 3000 pixels if you have none, and block a rectangle across the top quarter for a title (Chapter 7 ). Choose any look and compose it for that frame from the start. Shrink the result to 3 cm tall, the size of a cover in a list of issues. What you should see A picture that still reads at 3 cm, with the subject clear of the title and the empty space doing a job. A picture composed for a wide frame and then cropped to a tall one shows it, so recompose."},{"t":"Exercise 13.6: a sequence","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-13-6","x":"Choose two or three states of one molecule: unbound and bound, closed and open, monomer and dimer. Build one look and put the same sheet on every state, with the same camera, light and palette, and the accent on whatever changes. Lay the panels side by side. What you should see Panels that differ only where the molecule differs, so that the change is the strongest contrast across the row. If anything else differs between panels, such as the light or the size of the model, the eye goes there first. Chapter 6 of the Lighting part shows how to reuse one rig in several files."},{"t":"Exercise 13.7: a look of your own","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#exercise-13-7","x":"Invent a look on one molecule by the procedure of Chapter 12 , and give it a name. Write its sheet with sizes as multiples of W, then what it leans on and what it demands. Put it on a second molecule that meets the demands, and on a third that does not. What you should see On the second molecule the look arrives almost unchanged. On the third it fails in the way your list of demands predicted. A look whose failures you can predict is one you understand."},{"t":"Check yourself","w":"Art direction, Chapter 13","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-13-check","x":"Name the five tests. Answer The sentence, the stamp, gray, the knockout, and bare. A picture only works with haze on. Which test catches that, and what does it mean? Answer Bare. An earlier decision is still a default, and the haze is hiding it. Why should every panel of a figure share one look? Answer A difference in look between panels reads as a difference in the result. What can a reader mistake specks or a glow for? Answer Data: particles, solvent or a signal. Your picture is fully decided and still dull. Where do you look? Answer At whether the sheet leans on anything, and at whether the side shown has an outline worth reading."},{"t":"Chooser, glossary and sources","w":"Art direction, Chapter 14","u":"art-direction.html#chapter-14","x":""},{"t":"I want this, use that","w":"Art direction, Chapter 14","u":"art-direction.html#i-want-this-use-that","x":"I want to Use Know why a render looks regular Mark each of the seven lines D or C (Chapter 1 ) Find what a look depends on Knockouts, built or in your head (Chapters 2 and 3 ) Know whether a reference fits my molecule What it leans on, what that demands, what the molecule offers (Chapter 3 ) Choose the side to show The black-on-white outline in eight turns (Chapter 4 ) A large complex that reads when small Rope, skin or lumps (Chapter 4 ) Send the eye to one part The largest step in value, then saturation, then detail (Chapters 4 and 5 ) Check a palette The Greyscale look and the luminance readout (Chapter 5 ) Change the mood and keep the picture A new hue at the same values (Chapter 5 ) Give the subject a place A floor, a sweep, a mirror or a shadow catcher (Chapter 6 ) Lose the horizon Look down, build a sweep, or use darkness (Chapter 6 ) Make the subject rest, or float The gap under the model (Chapter 6 ) A simpler background A longer lens (Chapter 7 ) Blur at Molecular Nodes' scale An F-Stop near 0.5/W, typed in (Chapters 2 and 7 ) One part that glows An emissive material and a Glare node set to Bloom (Chapter 8 ) Edges that glow Light from behind, on wax or on a high Sheen Weight (Chapters 9 and 12 ) A look for a small protein Night glass (Chapter 9 ) A look for a large complex with parts Soft studio or paper cut (Chapters 2 and 10 ) A look for a good outline with no part to accent Specimen, or light from behind (Chapters 11 and 12 ) A figure that prints in gray Paper cut, with the accent checked in gray (Chapter 10 ) An outline that keeps its weight at any size Freestyle with Line Thickness Mode at Relative (Chapter 10 ) A shadow of a chosen length The height of the sun (Chapter 11 ) Compare ideas before building them Gray thumbnails at 25% (Chapter 12 ) Find a number A ladder of three to five renders (Chapter 12 ) Find what is wrong The five tests, then the table of symptoms (Chapter 13 )"},{"t":"Glossary","w":"Art direction, Chapter 14","u":"art-direction.html#glossary","x":"Accent : the color role of the part to look at. The smallest area, and the one that differs most in value from what surrounds it. Air : what hangs between the camera and the subject: specks, haze, blur. Body : the color role of most of the molecule. Demand : what a decision needs from the subject before it can carry a look. Finish : what is done to the image after it is rendered: glow, a vignette, a grade, an outline. Focus : the first half of the sentence: what the viewer should look at first. Ground : the color role of everything that is not the molecule, and the largest area. Knockout : a render with one decision put back to the regular figure's answer. Ladder : one number rendered at several values with nothing else changed. Lean on : a look leans on the decisions it cannot lose. Look : all seven decisions, made for one sentence. Lumps : a low-resolution surface, a few large smooth masses for each chain. Mood : the second half of the sentence: what the picture should feel like. Offer : something a molecule has that a decision can use: parts, symmetry, outline, large forms, depth, states, context. Regular figure : a render in which every decision was left at its default. Rope : Style Ribbon made thick. Sentence : the one-line brief, focus and mood, that every decision is tested against. Sheet : the seven decisions written down, each with its reason. Skin : Style Surface at full resolution. Stage : everything in the frame that is not the molecule. Value : how light a color is. W : the width of the model in meters."},{"t":"How this part was checked","w":"Art direction, Chapter 14","u":"art-direction.html#how-this-part-was-checked","x":"Every number marked tested comes from a scripted render in Blender 5.2.4 with Molecular Nodes 520.2.0, in Cycles, on three structures: PDB 1ATN (actin with DNase I), PDB 1MOX (the EGF receptor's extracellular region, residues 1 to 501, with TGF-α) and PDB 1NQL (the same region with EGF, in its tethered form). The dimer of 1MOX was reassembled from public copies of its chains, and the copy of 1NQL held backbone atoms only. Treat the receptor renders as tests of a look and not as figures of the receptor. The names of panels, fields and sockets were read from Blender 5.2's own interface definitions and from the manual. The click paths were not clicked through, so a menu path may be off where a number is not. Three things were not tested at all: the Fog Glow glare (Chapter 8 ), the sweep built by hand in Edit Mode (Chapter 6 , where the tests used a scripted one), and the Workbench relative of paper cut (Chapter 10 )."},{"t":"Sources","w":"Art direction, Chapter 14","u":"art-direction.html#sources","x":"Cameras (Blender Manual) Light Objects (Blender Manual) Object settings in Cycles (Blender Manual) Displays and Views (Blender Manual) Viewport Shading (Blender Manual) Image Editor (Blender Manual) Ambient Occlusion Node (Blender Manual) Volume Scatter (Blender Manual) Freestyle: Render Properties (Blender Manual) Freestyle: Line Set (Blender Manual) Compositing: Introduction (Blender Manual) Glare Node (Blender Manual) Style nodes (Molecular Nodes documentation) PDB 1ATN : Kabsch and others, Nature 347, 37 (1990) PDB 1MOX : Garrett and others, Cell 110, 763 (2002) PDB 1NQL : Ferguson and others, Molecular Cell 11, 507 (2003)"}];
